WN.lb(39,{"mt_ahSqW_kK1b":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Solid to liquid is…","choices":["melting","freezing","condensing"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of ice cream on a hot day.","explain":"Solid turning into liquid is called melting, like ice cream on a hot day."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Liquid to gas is…","choices":["evaporating","freezing","melting"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of puddles drying up in the sun.","explain":"Liquid turning into gas is called evaporating, like a puddle drying up."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which separates sand from water?","choices":["filter","magnet","sieve with big holes"],"answer":0,"hint":"Sand does not dissolve, so you need something that lets water through but not sand.","explain":"A filter lets the water through and catches the sand."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which gets salt from salt water?","choices":["evaporate","filter","magnet"],"answer":0,"hint":"Salt is dissolved.","explain":"Evaporate the water."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"{n} g of sand and {m} g of salt are mixed. You remove the sand. How many grams are left?","answer":"m","hint":"Once the sand is gone, only the salt is left.","explain":"Removing the sand leaves only the salt, which is {m} g.","params":{"n":[10,40],"m":[5,30]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"You do {n} separations, {m} with a filter. How many without a filter?","answer":"n-m","hint":"Take the filter ones away from the total.","explain":"{n} − {m} = {=n-m}, so {=n-m} separations did not use a filter.","params":{"n":[5,9],"m":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Liquid to solid is…","choices":["freezing","melting","evaporating"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of water in a freezer turning into ice cubes.","explain":"Liquid turning into solid is called freezing, like water becoming ice cubes."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Gas turning to liquid is…","choices":["condensing","melting","freezing"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the drops that form on a cold window.","explain":"Gas turning into liquid is called condensing, like water vapour making drops on a cold window."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Which separates iron filings from sand?","choices":["a magnet","a sieve","evaporating"],"answer":0,"hint":"Iron is magnetic.","explain":"Use a magnet."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A sieve catches {n} stones out of {=n+m}. How many go through?","answer":"m","hint":"The sieve keeps the stones. Take the caught ones away from the total.","explain":"{=n+m} − {n} = {m}, so {m} stones go through the sieve.","params":{"n":[1,6],"m":[1,6]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"You separate {n} paper clips with a magnet, {m} at a time. How many goes? ({n} is a multiple of {m})","answer":"n/m","hint":"Share the {n} paper clips into groups of {m}. Each group is one go.","explain":"{n} ÷ {m} = {=n/m}, so it takes {=n/m} goes.","params":{"n":[6,12],"m":[2,3]},"where":"n%m==0"}],"explain":{"kid":["Melt: solid to liquid. Freeze: liquid to solid. Evaporate: liquid to gas. Condense: gas to liquid.","A sieve catches big bits. A filter catches tiny bits that do not dissolve. A magnet pulls out iron."],"adult":["The words come in pairs. Melt and freeze. Evaporate and condense. To get a solid back after dissolving, evaporate the water.","To separate a mixture, ask how the parts differ. Size: use a sieve. Does it dissolve? Filter out what does not, then evaporate to get back what does. Is it iron? Use a magnet."]},"familyTip":"Mix dried peas, rice and paper clips. Ask how to separate them, then try it.","keyIdea":"Melt, freeze, evaporate and condense name changes of state; sieve, filter, evaporate and magnet name ways to separate mixtures.","visual":{"type":"separate","caption":"Iron filings, gravel, sand and salt in water. Name each method as you try it.","opts":{"mix":[["iron filings","magnet"],["gravel","sieve"],["sand","filter"],["salt (dissolved)","evaporate"],["water","none"]]}}},"mt_UKmtuAsSLN":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Dissolving sugar in tea is…","choices":["reversible","irreversible"],"answer":0,"hint":"Evaporate the water.","explain":"Sugar is left behind: reversible."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Cooking an egg is…","choices":["irreversible","reversible"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can you uncook it?","explain":"A new material forms."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"A reversible change makes…","choices":["no new material","a new material","only gas"],"answer":0,"hint":"It is still the same stuff afterwards.","explain":"No new material."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Ice is taken out of the freezer and warms up. What happens?","choices":["It melts back to water","It turns into a new material","It stays ice for ever"],"answer":0,"hint":"Melting is a change you can undo.","explain":"It melts back into water: the same material."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"{n} g of wax melts. What mass of wax sets again, in g?","answer":"n","hint":"No wax is lost when it melts.","explain":"Melting is reversible, so all {n} g of wax sets again.","params":{"n":[10,60]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"{n} changes: {m} are reversible. How many are irreversible?","answer":"n-m","hint":"Take the reversible ones away from the total.","explain":"{n} − {m} = {=n-m} changes are irreversible.","params":{"n":[5,9],"m":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Mixing sand and gravel is…","choices":["reversible (sieve them)","irreversible"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can you sort them out?","explain":"A sieve separates them."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Melting chocolate is…","choices":["reversible","irreversible"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think what happens when you cool it.","explain":"It sets again: reversible."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Burning paper is…","choices":["irreversible","reversible"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can you get the paper back?","explain":"Ash cannot become paper: irreversible."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"{n} ice cubes melt, then all refreeze. How many ice cubes?","answer":"n","hint":"Same as before.","explain":"Melting is reversible, so all {n} ice cubes come back.","params":{"n":[3,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"You dissolve {n} g of salt and evaporate the water. How many grams of salt do you get back?","answer":"n","hint":"Dissolving is reversible, so nothing is lost.","explain":"All the salt is left behind: {n} g comes back.","params":{"n":[5,30]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Melt ice and you get water. Freeze it and you get ice back. That change can go back, so it is reversible.","No new material is made. It is still water."],"adult":["Changes of state (melting, freezing, boiling, condensing), dissolving and mixing are reversible: the same material comes back. Burning, rusting and cooking make new materials, so they are irreversible.","A good test: can you get the starting materials back by a simple method such as cooling, heating, filtering or evaporating? If yes, it is reversible."]},"familyTip":"Make ice lollies from juice, then melt one. Is it the same juice? Now toast bread: can you untoast it?","keyIdea":"A reversible change can be undone: melting, freezing, dissolving and mixing make no new material.","visual":{"type":"particles","caption":"Water: cool it to freeze, warm it to melt. The particles are the same water every time.","opts":{"mp":0,"bp":100,"temp":5,"name":"water","min":-20,"max":40}}},"mt_jg2oNKpNw9":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"A neutral atom has atomic number {z}. How many electrons does it have?","answer":"z","hint":"In a neutral atom the charges balance.","explain":"{z} protons need {z} electrons to balance, so {z}.","params":{"z":[2,20]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Carbon-12 and carbon-14 are both carbon. What is different about them?","choices":["The number of neutrons","The number of protons","The number of electrons in a neutral atom"],"answer":0,"hint":"The element stays the same, so the protons stay the same.","explain":"They are isotopes: both have 6 protons, but carbon-14 has 8 neutrons instead of 6."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"The symbol for an atom shows {=z*2+n} on top and {z} at the bottom. How many neutrons?","answer":"z+n","hint":"Top number minus bottom number.","explain":"{=z*2+n} − {z} = {=z+n} neutrons.","params":{"z":[3,20],"n":[0,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Chlorine has atomic number 17. How many electrons are in a chloride ion, Cl⁻?","choices":["18","16","17"],"answer":0,"hint":"A negative charge means the atom has gained electrons.","explain":"17 protons; a −1 charge means one extra electron, so 18."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Why do we ignore electrons when working out the mass number?","choices":["Their mass is tiny compared with protons and neutrons","They have no mass at all","They are not part of the atom"],"answer":0,"hint":"Compare the masses of the three particles.","explain":"An electron is about 1/1840 of the mass of a proton, so it barely changes the total. It does have some mass."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"An ion has {p} protons and a charge of +2. How many electrons does it have?","answer":"p-2","hint":"+2 means two more protons than electrons.","explain":"{p} − 2 = {=p-2} electrons.","params":{"p":[4,20]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"An atom gains a proton. What happens?","choices":["It becomes a different element","It becomes an isotope of the same element","It becomes a negative ion"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which number decides the element?","explain":"The atomic number goes up by one, so it is the next element in the periodic table."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"An atom has 8 protons, 8 neutrons and 8 electrons. Which number tells you which element it is?","choices":["The 8 protons","The 8 neutrons","The 8 electrons"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which particle can never change without the element changing too?","explain":"The protons. 8 protons is always oxygen; the atomic number of oxygen is 8."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"An atom has atomic number {z} and mass number {=z*2+n}. How many neutrons does it have?","answer":"z+n","hint":"Neutrons = mass number − atomic number.","explain":"{=z*2+n} − {z} = {=z+n} neutrons.","params":{"z":[3,20],"n":[0,4]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"An atom has {p} protons and {n} neutrons. What is its mass number?","answer":"p+n","hint":"Add the particles in the nucleus.","explain":"Mass number = protons + neutrons = {p} + {n} = {=p+n}.","params":{"p":[3,20],"n":[3,22]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"A sodium atom (11 protons) loses one electron. What is it now?","choices":["A sodium ion with charge +1","A neon atom","A sodium ion with charge −1"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count the charges: 11 positive protons, and how many negative electrons now?","explain":"It still has 11 protons, so it is still sodium. 11 − 10 = +1, so it is the ion Na⁺."}],"explain":{"kid":["An atom has a tiny centre called the nucleus. It holds protons and neutrons. Electrons move around the outside.","The number of protons is the atomic number. It tells you the element. Every carbon atom has 6 protons. If it had 7, it would be nitrogen.","The mass number is protons plus neutrons. Electrons are so light that we do not count them.","So neutrons = mass number − atomic number. Carbon-14 has 14 − 6 = 8 neutrons."],"adult":["A nuclear symbol such as ²³₁₁Na shows the mass number A (protons + neutrons) on top and the atomic number Z (protons) below. Neutrons = A − Z, so sodium-23 has 12 neutrons.","Z defines the element and its place in the periodic table. Atoms of the same element with different numbers of neutrons are isotopes: same chemistry, different mass. Carbon-12 and carbon-14 are both carbon.","A proton carries +1, an electron −1, and a neutron no charge. A neutral atom has as many electrons as protons. Losing electrons gives a positive ion (Na⁺ has 10), gaining them gives a negative ion (Cl⁻ has 18).","Common slips: taking the mass number as the number of neutrons, swapping the two numbers, and thinking that a charge changes the element. Only the proton count does that."]},"familyTip":"Pick an element from a periodic table poster or a food label (sodium, calcium, iron). Read off its atomic number and ask: how many protons? How many electrons in a neutral atom?","keyIdea":"The atomic number is the number of protons, and it decides the element. The mass number is protons plus neutrons. In a neutral atom the electrons equal the protons; an ion has gained or lost electrons.","visual":{"type":"ptable","caption":"The atomic number orders the periodic table. Find carbon at number 6, then choose other elements.","opts":{"highlight":"","z":6}},"lab":{"type":"flagnuc","caption":"Flagship lab: build an atom. Add protons, neutrons and electrons and watch the name, the symbol and the charge.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_pp9vsZ4wu2":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"An element is in period 2 and group {g}. How many electrons does its atom have?","answer":"2+g","hint":"Period 2 means two shells, and the first one is full.","explain":"The configuration is 2,{g}, so 2 + {g} = {=2+g} electrons.","params":{"g":[1,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which configuration is wrong for a neutral atom?","choices":["2,7,1","2,8,1","2,8,8,2"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is any inner shell left with room while an outer one is used?","explain":"2,7,1 leaves room in the second shell while the third is in use. Ten electrons fill as 2,8."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Why do lithium (2,1) and sodium (2,8,1) react in similar ways?","choices":["Both have one electron in their outer shell","Both have two shells","Both have the same mass"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the last number in each configuration.","explain":"Reactions involve the outer electrons. Both have one to lose, so both are in group 1."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"An element is in period 3 and group {g}. How many electrons does its atom have?","answer":"10+g","hint":"Period 3 means three shells; the first two are full.","explain":"The configuration is 2,8,{g}, so 2 + 8 + {g} = {=10+g}.","params":{"g":[1,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Oxygen is 2,6. Which statement is true?","choices":["It is in group 6 and period 2","It is in group 2 and period 6","It is in group 8 and period 2"],"answer":0,"hint":"Group: outer electrons. Period: number of shells.","explain":"6 outer electrons gives group 6; 2 shells gives period 2."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Helium has 2 outer electrons. Why is it in group 0, not group 2?","choices":["Its only shell is full, like the other noble gases","It has no protons","Its outer shell holds 8"],"answer":0,"hint":"How many electrons can the first shell hold?","explain":"The first shell holds only 2, so helium's shell is full. Full outer shells make the unreactive group 0."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"What is the configuration of calcium (20 electrons)?","choices":["2,8,8,2","2,8,10","2,18"],"answer":0,"hint":"The third shell takes 8 here before the fourth starts.","explain":"2 + 8 + 8 = 18, leaving 2 for the fourth shell."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"What is the electron configuration of magnesium (12 electrons)?","choices":["2,8,2","2,10","8,4","2,2,8"],"answer":0,"hint":"Fill the first shell with 2, the second with up to 8, then carry on.","explain":"2 + 8 = 10, leaving 2 for the third shell: 2,8,2."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"An atom has the configuration 2,8,{k}. How many electrons does it have?","answer":"10+k","hint":"Add up the electrons in every shell.","explain":"Add the shells: 2 + 8 + {k} = {=10+k} electrons, and a neutral atom has the same number of protons.","params":{"k":[1,8]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"An element has the configuration 2,8,{k}. Which group is it in? (Type 0 for group 0.)","answer":"k % 8","hint":"Count the electrons in the outer shell. A full outer shell of 8 means group 0.","explain":"The outer shell holds {k}, so the group is {=k % 8}.","params":{"k":[1,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Potassium has 19 electrons. Where does the 19th electron go?","choices":["Into a new fourth shell","Into the third shell, which can take up to 18","Into the first shell"],"answer":0,"hint":"Argon, with 18, is 2,8,8.","explain":"For the first 20 elements the third shell holds 8 before the fourth starts, so potassium is 2,8,8,1."}],"explain":{"kid":["Electrons live in shells around the nucleus. Each shell can only hold so many.","The first shell holds 2. The next holds 8. For the first 20 elements, the third holds 8 too.","Electrons fill the inside shell first. Sodium has 11 electrons: 2 in shell one, 8 in shell two, and 1 left over in shell three. We write it 2,8,1.","The last number is the outer shell. Sodium has 1 outer electron, so it sits in group 1. It uses 3 shells, so it is in row 3."],"adult":["In the shell model used up to GCSE, shells fill in order with capacities 2, 8, 8 for the first 20 elements, and the 19th and 20th electrons start a fourth shell. Write the configuration as the counts per shell: chlorine (17) is 2,8,7 and calcium (20) is 2,8,8,2.","The number of occupied shells gives the period. The number of electrons in the outer shell gives the group, except for helium and the noble gases, which have full outer shells and form group 0.","Elements in the same group have the same number of outer electrons, which is why lithium, sodium and potassium react in similar ways: each has one electron to lose.","Common slips: putting the third electron in the first shell, filling the third shell to 18 before starting the fourth, and reading the group from the number of shells. The third shell can hold 18 in fuller models, but for potassium and calcium the fourth shell starts first."]},"familyTip":"Pick a number from 1 to 20 and take turns: one person says the element, the other writes its configuration by filling 2, then 8, then 8. Check together which group it lands in.","keyIdea":"Electrons fill shells from the inside out: 2 in the first shell, then 8, then 8 for the first 20 elements. The configuration, such as 2,8,1 for sodium, tells you the period (how many shells) and the group (how many outer electrons).","visual":{"type":"ptable","caption":"The rows are periods (number of shells). The columns are groups (outer electrons). Find sodium, 2,8,1, at period 3, group 1.","opts":{"highlight":"","z":11}},"lab":{"type":"flagshell","caption":"Flagship lab: fill the shells. Tap a ring to add an electron; a warning shows if an inner shell still has room.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_dx_t8RtPt5":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"How many covalent bonds does a hydrogen atom form?","choices":["1","2","7"],"answer":0,"hint":"Hydrogen's only shell is full with 2 electrons.","explain":"It has 1 electron and needs 2, so one shared pair is enough."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which pair of elements is most likely to bond covalently?","choices":["Carbon and hydrogen","Sodium and chlorine","Magnesium and oxygen"],"answer":0,"hint":"Covalent bonds usually form between two non-metals.","explain":"Carbon and hydrogen are both non-metals, so they share. The other pairs are a metal with a non-metal, which form ions."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"{n} carbon atoms are joined in a chain by single bonds. Every other bond goes to a hydrogen atom. How many hydrogen atoms are there?","answer":"2*n+2","hint":"Each carbon makes 4 bonds. The two end carbons use 1 bond on the chain, the middle ones use 2.","explain":"Total carbon bonds 4 × {n}, minus 2 for each of the {=n-1} C–C bonds, leaves {=2*n+2} bonds to hydrogen (methane CH₄, ethane C₂H₆…).","params":{"n":[1,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Ammonia, NH₃: how many lone pairs does the nitrogen atom have?","choices":["1","0","3"],"answer":0,"hint":"Nitrogen has 5 outer electrons and uses 3 in bonds.","explain":"5 − 3 = 2 unshared electrons, which is one lone pair."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"A chain of {n} carbon atoms joined by single bonds (C–C–C…) has how many C–C bonds?","answer":"n-1","hint":"Draw 3 carbons in a row and count the links.","explain":"Each bond joins two neighbours, so {n} atoms in a row have {=n-1} bonds between them.","params":{"n":[2,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Carbon dioxide is O=C=O. How many outer electrons does each oxygen count?","choices":["8","6","10"],"answer":0,"hint":"Each oxygen shares two pairs with carbon.","explain":"6 of its own plus 2 shared from carbon makes 8."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Nitrogen gas is N₂. Which bond holds the two atoms together?","choices":["A triple bond","A single bond","An ionic bond"],"answer":0,"hint":"Each nitrogen has 5 outer electrons and needs 8.","explain":"Each nitrogen needs 3 more, so they share three pairs: N≡N."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"What is a covalent bond?","choices":["A shared pair of electrons between two atoms","An electron given from one atom to another","The attraction between two ions of opposite charge"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about chlorine: neither atom can take an electron from the other.","explain":"Each atom puts one electron into a shared pair. Transferring electrons makes ions, which is ionic bonding."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"An atom of a non-metal has {v} outer electrons. How many covalent bonds does it usually form?","answer":"8-v","hint":"Each bond adds one shared electron to its count. How many does it need to reach 8?","explain":"8 − {v} = {=8-v} bonds fill its outer shell.","params":{"v":[4,7]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A molecule has {s} single bonds and {d} double bonds. How many electrons are shared in total?","answer":"2*s+4*d","hint":"A single bond is one shared pair; a double bond is two shared pairs.","explain":"{s} × 2 + {d} × 4 = {=2*s+4*d} shared electrons.","params":{"s":[1,4],"d":[0,2]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Why does an oxygen molecule, O₂, have a double bond?","choices":["One shared pair leaves each oxygen with only 7, so they share two","Oxygen always gives away 2 electrons","A single bond would give each oxygen 9"],"answer":0,"hint":"Oxygen has 6 outer electrons. Count with one shared pair, then with two.","explain":"6 + 1 = 7 with one pair; 6 + 2 = 8 with two pairs. So O=O."}],"explain":{"kid":["Non-metal atoms want a full outer shell. Two chlorine atoms each have 7 outer electrons and want 8.","Neither one can take an electron from the other. So they share. Each puts in one electron, and the pair counts for both.","That shared pair is a covalent bond. Now both chlorine atoms count 8.","Some atoms share more than one pair. Two oxygen atoms share two pairs. That is a double bond."],"adult":["Covalent bonds form between non-metal atoms. Both atoms attract the shared pair of electrons, and that attraction holds them together. Each atom counts the shared electrons in its own outer shell.","An atom usually forms 8 minus its number of outer electrons bonds: carbon 4, nitrogen 3, oxygen 2, chlorine 1. Hydrogen fills its first shell with 2, so it makes one bond. Unshared outer pairs are called lone pairs.","Two shared pairs make a double bond (O=O, O=C=O) and three a triple bond (N≡N). In dot-and-cross diagrams one atom's electrons are drawn as dots and the other's as crosses, so you can see who gave what.","Common slips: describing a covalent bond as one atom giving an electron to the other (that is ionic bonding, usually metal with non-metal), giving hydrogen 8 outer electrons, and drawing a single bond in O₂ or N₂."]},"familyTip":"Use two hands as atoms and a pair of coins as the shared electrons, held between them. Ask: how many coins does each hand count? Then try water: one hand in the middle sharing with two others.","keyIdea":"A covalent bond is a shared pair of electrons between two non-metal atoms. Each atom shares enough pairs to fill its outer shell, so the number of bonds is usually 8 minus its outer electrons (hydrogen makes 1).","visual":{"type":"atom","caption":"A chlorine atom: 17 electrons, 7 in the outer shell. One more would make 8, so chlorine forms one covalent bond.","opts":{"p":17,"n":18,"e":17}},"lab":{"type":"flagcov","caption":"Flagship lab: share electrons. Tap two atoms to share a pair; rings turn green when an outer shell is full.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_TB2zg6ZjHq":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Why does carbon form four covalent bonds?","choices":["It has 4 outer electrons and needs 4 more","It has 4 shells","It has 4 protons"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at carbon's electron configuration, 2,4.","explain":"Carbon has 4 outer electrons. Sharing 4 pairs gives it 8 in the outer shell, so it makes 4 bonds."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"How many C–C bonds are in a straight chain of {n} carbon atoms?","answer":"n-1","hint":"Draw a short chain and count the links between neighbours.","explain":"Each link joins two neighbours, so {n} carbons in a line have {=n-1} C–C bonds.","params":{"n":[2,10]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Six carbons are joined in a ring. Which formula is right?","choices":["C₆H₁₂","C₆H₁₄","C₆H₂₄"],"answer":0,"hint":"Each carbon in the ring already has two carbon neighbours.","explain":"6 carbons × 2 H = 12. C₆H₁₄ is the open chain, and C₆H₂₄ gives each carbon 4 H."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Ali draws a carbon atom with five bonds. What is wrong?","choices":["Carbon can only form four bonds","Carbon must form eight bonds","Nothing, carbon can form any number"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count carbon's outer electrons and the bonds they can make.","explain":"Carbon has 4 outer electrons to share, so it forms exactly 4 bonds. A fifth would overfill its outer shell."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Silicon also has 4 outer electrons. Why are long silicon chains rare?","choices":["Si–Si bonds are much weaker than C–C bonds","Silicon has 8 outer electrons","Silicon cannot share electrons"],"answer":0,"hint":"Carbon chains last because the bond between two carbons is strong.","explain":"Silicon can make 4 bonds, but the Si–Si bond is weak, so silicon chains break apart. Strong C–C bonds let carbon chains grow long."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"A branched molecule has {n} carbons, single bonds only and no rings. How many hydrogen atoms does it hold?","answer":"2*n+2","hint":"A branch moves a carbon but adds no extra C–C bond. Compare with the straight chain.","explain":"Any chain of {n} carbons, branched or not, has {=n-1} C–C bonds, so it holds 4 × {n} − 2 × {=n-1} = {=2*n+2} H.","params":{"n":[4,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which reason best explains why there are millions of carbon compounds?","choices":["Carbon makes four bonds and joins to itself in chains, branches and rings","Carbon is the most common element on Earth","Carbon atoms are very large"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about what the four bonds and C–C bonds let carbon build.","explain":"Four bonds per atom and strong C–C bonds allow chains, branches and rings of any length. Carbon is not the most common element."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"How many different compounds have the formula C₅H₁₂?","choices":["3","1","5"],"answer":0,"hint":"Try a straight chain, one branch, and a carbon with four carbon neighbours.","explain":"Pentane, methylbutane and dimethylpropane are the three isomers of C₅H₁₂."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"A straight chain of {n} carbons is filled with hydrogen. How many hydrogen atoms does it hold?","answer":"2*n+2","hint":"Each end carbon has 3 H. Each carbon in the middle has 2 H.","explain":"2 end carbons × 3 = 6, plus {=n-2} middle carbons × 2 = {=2*n-4}. That makes {=2*n+2}, so the formula is C{n}H{=2*n+2}.","params":{"n":[3,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"How many covalent bonds does a carbon atom form in its compounds?","choices":["4","8","2"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count carbon's outer electrons. Each one is shared in one bond.","explain":"Carbon is 2,4. It shares its 4 outer electrons in 4 bonds, reaching 8. The 8 counts electrons, not bonds."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"{n} carbons are joined in a ring and filled with hydrogen. How many hydrogen atoms fit?","answer":"2*n","hint":"In a ring every carbon has two carbon neighbours, so each has 2 bonds left.","explain":"{n} carbons × 2 H = {=2*n}. A ring has 2 fewer H than the open chain of {n}, which has {=2*n+2}.","params":{"n":[3,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Why does neon form no ordinary compounds?","choices":["Its outer shell is already full with 8","It has too few electrons","It is a metal"],"answer":0,"hint":"Bonds form when an atom still needs electrons to fill its outer shell.","explain":"Neon is 2,8. Its outer shell is full, so it has nothing to share and makes no bonds."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Butane and methylpropane are both C₄H₁₀. What is different about them?","choices":["The order the carbon atoms are joined in","The number of hydrogen atoms","The number of carbon atoms"],"answer":0,"hint":"Same formula means the same number of each atom. Look at the shape.","explain":"Butane is a straight chain; methylpropane has a branch. Same atoms, different structure: they are isomers."}],"explain":{"kid":["A carbon atom has 6 electrons: 2 in the first shell and 4 in the outer shell. It shares those 4 to reach 8. Each shared pair is one bond, so carbon makes four bonds.","Carbon can bond to other carbon atoms. A carbon in a chain uses one bond on each neighbour. The rest of its four bonds can hold hydrogen. Propane, C₃H₈, has 3 carbons and 8 hydrogens.","The chain can branch, too. Butane and methylpropane are both C₄H₁₀, but the atoms are joined in a different order. They are different compounds with the same formula.","The ends of a chain can join to make a ring. Mix chains, branches and rings, and the number of compounds grows very fast. Neon has a full outer shell, so it makes no bonds at all."],"adult":["Carbon's configuration is 2,4. Sharing four electron pairs gives it a full outer shell, so in its compounds carbon nearly always forms four bonds. Nitrogen forms three, oxygen two, and neon, with a full shell already, forms no ordinary compounds.","Two things make carbon special. It has four bonds to spend, so one atom can link to several others at once. And the C–C bond is strong, so long chains are stable. Silicon also forms four bonds, but Si–Si bonds are much weaker, so long silicon chains are rare.","The same formula can describe more than one structure. These are isomers. C₄H₁₀ has 2, C₅H₁₂ has 3, C₁₀H₂₂ has 75 and C₂₀H₄₂ has 366,319. Add rings, double bonds and atoms such as oxygen and nitrogen, and the total runs into many millions.","Common mistakes: saying carbon makes 8 bonds (8 is the electron count, not the bond count); giving each carbon in a chain 4 hydrogens; drawing a carbon with 3 or 5 bonds; and thinking a molecule with the same formula must be the same compound."]},"familyTip":"Use dried pasta pieces as bonds and blobs of modelling clay as atoms. Every carbon blob must hold exactly four pieces. See how many different shapes you can make from 4 carbons and 10 hydrogens. Then try 5 carbons and 12 hydrogens.","keyIdea":"Carbon has four outer electrons, so every carbon atom makes four covalent bonds. Carbon atoms also bond strongly to each other, in chains, branches and rings, so the same few atoms can be joined in millions of different ways.","worked":{"title":"How many hydrogens does a chain of 4 carbons hold?","steps":["4 carbons have 4 × 4 = 16 bonds between them.","A straight chain of 4 has 3 C–C bonds. Each one uses a bond on two carbons: 3 × 2 = 6.","16 − 6 = 10 bonds are left for hydrogen.","The formula is C₄H₁₀, butane. The branched shape, methylpropane, gives C₄H₁₀ too."]},"visual":{"type":"atom","caption":"A carbon atom: 6 electrons, 2 in the first shell and 4 in the outer shell. Sharing those 4 gives carbon four covalent bonds.","opts":{"p":6,"n":6,"e":6}},"lab":{"type":"flagcarbon","caption":"Flagship lab: bond carbons into chains, branches and rings, then fill the free bonds with hydrogen and watch the formula and the isomer count change.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_f1w6GDJCnF":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What is a hydrocarbon?","choices":["A compound of hydrogen and carbon only","Any compound that contains carbon","A compound of hydrogen and oxygen"],"answer":0,"hint":"The name is built from two element names. The key word is only.","explain":"A hydrocarbon contains hydrogen and carbon atoms and nothing else. Many carbon compounds contain other elements too."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Sam says C₂H₅OH is a hydrocarbon because it contains carbon and hydrogen. What is wrong?","choices":["It also contains oxygen, so it is not carbon and hydrogen only","Nothing, Sam is right","It has too many carbon atoms"],"answer":0,"hint":"Read the formula to the end. Is there a symbol that is not C or H?","explain":"The O in C₂H₅OH is an oxygen atom. A hydrocarbon has C and H only, so ethanol is not one."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which of these is NOT a hydrocarbon?","choices":["CH₃Cl","C₆H₆","C₂H₄"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for a symbol that is neither C nor H.","explain":"CH₃Cl, chloromethane, has a chlorine atom. Benzene, C₆H₆, and ethene, C₂H₄, are carbon and hydrogen only."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Is carbon dioxide, CO₂, a hydrocarbon?","choices":["No, it has no hydrogen and it contains oxygen","Yes, it contains carbon","Yes, all carbon compounds are hydrocarbons"],"answer":0,"hint":"A hydrocarbon needs both carbon and hydrogen, and nothing else.","explain":"CO₂ has no H at all and has O, so it fails the test twice. Containing carbon is not enough."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"A list has {n} formulae. {k} of them contain oxygen and the rest contain only carbon and hydrogen. How many are hydrocarbons?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Any formula with oxygen in it fails the test. Take those away from the total.","explain":"The {k} with oxygen are not hydrocarbons, so {n} − {k} = {=n-k} are.","params":{"n":[6,12],"k":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Cyclohexane, C₆H₁₂, is a ring of six carbons. Is it a hydrocarbon?","choices":["Yes, a ring is fine as long as the atoms are only C and H","No, hydrocarbons must be straight chains","No, rings always contain oxygen"],"answer":0,"hint":"The test is about which elements are present, not the shape.","explain":"Shape does not matter. C₆H₁₂ is carbon and hydrogen only, so it is a hydrocarbon."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Is hydrogen gas, H₂, a hydrocarbon?","choices":["No, it has no carbon, and it is an element","Yes, it is made of hydrogen","Yes, it burns like methane"],"answer":0,"hint":"Check for both elements in the name hydro-carbon.","explain":"H₂ is the element hydrogen. With no carbon it is not a compound of C and H, so it is not a hydrocarbon."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Crude oil is mostly a mixture of what?","choices":["Hydrocarbons","Carbohydrates such as glucose","Carbon dioxide and water"],"answer":0,"hint":"Petrol, diesel and the gas in a camping stove all come from it.","explain":"Crude oil is mostly hydrocarbons of many sizes. Carbohydrates such as glucose contain oxygen, so they are not hydrocarbons."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"Propane, C₃H₈, is a hydrocarbon. How many atoms are there in {n} molecules of propane?","answer":"11*n","hint":"Read the formula: 3 carbon atoms and 8 hydrogen atoms in each molecule, and nothing else.","explain":"Each molecule has 3 + 8 = 11 atoms, so {n} molecules have 11 × {n} = {=11*n} atoms.","params":{"n":[2,9]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which of these is a hydrocarbon?","choices":["C₃H₈","C₂H₅OH","CO₂"],"answer":0,"hint":"List the element symbols in each formula. Which list is only C and H?","explain":"C₃H₈, propane, has only C and H. C₂H₅OH also has O, and CO₂ has no H."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A list has {n} formulae. {a} of them contain only C and H. Each of the rest also contains O or Cl. How many are NOT hydrocarbons?","answer":"n-a","hint":"One extra element is enough to fail the test. Count the formulae that are not C and H only.","explain":"Only the {a} with C and H alone are hydrocarbons, so {n} − {a} = {=n-a} are not.","params":{"n":[8,12],"a":[1,7]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A hydrocarbon has {c} carbon atoms and {h} hydrogen atoms in each molecule. How many atoms are in one molecule?","answer":"c+h","hint":"A hydrocarbon has only two kinds of atom. Add the two counts.","explain":"Carbon and hydrogen are the only atoms, so {c} + {h} = {=c+h} atoms in one molecule.","params":{"c":[1,8],"h":[4,18]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Propene, C₃H₆, has a C=C double bond. Is it a hydrocarbon?","choices":["Yes, its atoms are only carbon and hydrogen","No, hydrocarbons have only single bonds","No, it has too few hydrogen atoms"],"answer":0,"hint":"The definition is about which kinds of atom are present, not how they are bonded.","explain":"Only the elements matter. C₃H₆ is carbon and hydrogen only, so it is a hydrocarbon, double bond and all."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Why is glucose, C₆H₁₂O₆, not a hydrocarbon?","choices":["It contains oxygen as well as carbon and hydrogen","It is too big a molecule","It has no carbon atoms"],"answer":0,"hint":"Read every element symbol in the formula, not just the first two.","explain":"Glucose has 6 O atoms. Any element besides C and H means it is not a hydrocarbon, whatever its size."}],"explain":{"kid":["A hydrocarbon is made of just two kinds of atom: hydrogen and carbon. The name tells you: hydro for hydrogen, carbon for carbon.","Methane, CH₄, is a hydrocarbon. Its formula has only C and H in it. Octane, C₈H₁₈, is one too. It is in petrol.","Ethanol, C₂H₅OH, has C and H, but it also has an O. That one oxygen atom means it is not a hydrocarbon. The key word is only.","A compound needs both C and H to count. CO₂ has no H. H₂ has no C. Neither of them is a hydrocarbon."],"adult":["A hydrocarbon is a compound that contains carbon and hydrogen atoms and nothing else. To test a formula, list its element symbols. If the list is exactly C and H, it is a hydrocarbon.","The test ignores size and shape. Methane, CH₄, and octane, C₈H₁₈, are hydrocarbons. So are propene, C₃H₆, which has a C=C double bond, and benzene, C₆H₆, a ring. Crude oil is mostly a mixture of hydrocarbons.","Many carbon compounds are not hydrocarbons. Ethanol, C₂H₅OH, and glucose, C₆H₁₂O₆, contain oxygen. Chloromethane, CH₃Cl, contains chlorine. Carbon dioxide, CO₂, has no hydrogen at all.","Common mistakes: calling any compound with C and H a hydrocarbon and forgetting the word only; thinking a double bond or a ring rules a molecule out; and treating every carbon compound, or hydrogen gas, H₂, as a hydrocarbon."]},"familyTip":"Read the ingredients or chemical names on a few labels at home, such as a gas bottle (propane or butane), a candle, nail polish remover (propanone) or vinegar (ethanoic acid). Look up each formula and decide together: only C and H, or something else too?","keyIdea":"A hydrocarbon is a compound made of hydrogen and carbon atoms only. Size, double bonds and rings do not matter; one atom of any other element, such as oxygen or chlorine, means it is not a hydrocarbon.","worked":{"title":"Which of CH₄, C₂H₅OH and C₃H₆ are hydrocarbons?","steps":["List the elements in each formula. CH₄: C and H. C₂H₅OH: C, H and O. C₃H₆: C and H.","A hydrocarbon has C and H only, so check each list for anything else.","C₂H₅OH has an O, so it is not a hydrocarbon, even though it has C and H.","CH₄ and C₃H₆ are hydrocarbons. The double bond in C₃H₆ does not matter."]},"visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Hydrocarbon or not? Check every element symbol: carbon and hydrogen only. Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Hydrocarbon","Not a hydrocarbon"],"items":[["CH₄, methane",0],["C₈H₁₈, octane",0],["C₂H₄, ethene",0],["C₆H₆, benzene",0],["C₂H₅OH, ethanol",1],["C₆H₁₂O₆, glucose",1],["CO₂, carbon dioxide",1],["CH₃Cl, chloromethane",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flaghydro","caption":"Flagship lab: scan the atoms in each molecule, watch the element tally build up, then sort it: hydrocarbon or not?","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_UJ2KVmA1tf":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which is the structural formula of butane?","choices":["CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₃","C₄H₁₀","CH₃CH₃CH₃CH₃"],"answer":0,"hint":"The end carbons have one carbon neighbour; the middle ones have two.","explain":"End carbons are CH₃ and middle carbons are CH₂. C₄H₁₀ is the molecular formula, and CH₃ in the middle would give those carbons five bonds."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Butane and methylpropane are both C₄H₁₀. Which statement is true?","choices":["They have different structural formulae","They are the same molecule","They have different numbers of atoms"],"answer":0,"hint":"The molecular formula counts atoms; it does not show how they are joined.","explain":"Butane is CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₃ and methylpropane is CH₃CH(CH₃)CH₃: same atoms, joined differently."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Mia says the molecular formula of ethane is CH₃. What is wrong?","choices":["CH₃ is only one group; ethane has two carbons, C₂H₆","Nothing, CH₃ is right","Ethane has three carbons"],"answer":0,"hint":"Ethane's structural formula is CH₃CH₃. Add up both groups.","explain":"Ethane is CH₃CH₃: two CH₃ groups. Together that is 2 C and 6 H, so C₂H₆."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"The structural formula of a straight chain of {n} carbons is written CH₃CH₂…CH₃. How many CH₂ groups does it contain?","answer":"n-2","hint":"The two end carbons have one carbon neighbour each, so they are CH₃. Every other carbon is CH₂.","explain":"2 of the {n} carbons are CH₃ ends, so {n} − 2 = {=n-2} are CH₂ groups in the middle.","params":{"n":[3,10]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"How many H atoms are in the molecular formula of CH₃(CH₂){k}OH?","answer":"2*k+4","hint":"Add the H in CH₃, in each CH₂, and the one in OH.","explain":"3 + 2 × {k} + 1 = {=2*k+4} H. The molecular formula is C{=k+1}H{=2*k+4}O.","params":{"k":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"What is the molecular formula of chloroethane, CH₃CH₂Cl?","choices":["C₂H₅Cl","C₂H₆Cl","CH₅Cl"],"answer":0,"hint":"Cl takes the place of one H. Count the H in CH₃ and CH₂ only.","explain":"3 + 2 = 5 H, 2 C and 1 Cl: C₂H₅Cl."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which structural formula matches a chain of three carbons with a CH₃ branch on the middle carbon?","choices":["CH₃CH(CH₃)CH₃","CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₃","CH₃CH₂(CH₃)CH₃"],"answer":0,"hint":"The middle carbon bonds to three carbons, so it keeps only one H. The branch goes in brackets after it.","explain":"The middle carbon has 3 carbon neighbours and 1 H, so it is CH, followed by the branch (CH₃): CH₃CH(CH₃)CH₃."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"In a correct displayed formula, how many lines meet at each carbon atom?","choices":["4","3","It depends on the H count only"],"answer":0,"hint":"Carbon makes four covalent bonds, and each bond is one line.","explain":"Every carbon has four bonds, so four lines. A double bond counts as two of them."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"What is the molecular formula of CH₃CH₂CH₃?","choices":["C₃H₈","C₃H₇","CH₈"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count the carbons, then add up the H in every group.","explain":"3 carbons, and 3 + 2 + 3 = 8 hydrogens, so C₃H₈."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A straight chain is written CH₃(CH₂){k}CH₃, which means {k} CH₂ groups in the middle. How many H atoms are in its molecular formula?","answer":"2*k+6","hint":"Each end group CH₃ has 3 H. Each CH₂ in the middle has 2 H.","explain":"3 + 3 = 6 H on the ends, plus 2 × {k} = {=2*k} H in the middle: {=2*k+6} H in all, so C{=k+2}H{=2*k+6}.","params":{"k":[1,6]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A displayed formula shows a straight chain of {n} carbons with H on every free bond. How many lines (bonds) are in the drawing altogether?","answer":"3*n+1","hint":"Count the C–C lines and the C–H lines separately. The chain holds 2n + 2 H.","explain":"There are {=n-1} C–C lines and {=2*n+2} C–H lines: {=n-1} + {=2*n+2} = {=3*n+1} lines.","params":{"n":[2,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"What does a displayed formula show that a molecular formula does not?","choices":["How the atoms are joined, with every bond","How many carbon atoms there are","Which elements are present"],"answer":0,"hint":"Both tell you how many atoms of each kind. Only one tells you which atom is joined to which.","explain":"A molecular formula only counts atoms. The displayed formula shows every bond, so you can see the shape of the chain."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"What is the molecular formula of ethanol, CH₃CH₂OH?","choices":["C₂H₆O","C₂H₅O","C₂H₆"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count every H, including the one on the oxygen.","explain":"3 + 2 + 1 = 6 H, plus 2 C and 1 O, gives C₂H₆O. C₂H₅O misses the H in OH."}],"explain":{"kid":["A displayed formula is a drawing. It shows every atom as a letter and every bond as a line. Each carbon has four lines.","A structural formula goes along the chain one carbon at a time. You write each carbon with the H atoms joined to it. Propane is CH₃CH₂CH₃.","A molecular formula just counts. Add up each kind of atom and write C first, then H. Propane is C₃H₈.","Two molecules can share a molecular formula but have different shapes. Butane and methylpropane are both C₄H₁₀. Their drawings and structural formulae are not the same."],"adult":["A displayed formula shows every atom and every bond. Each bond is a line, so you can check that each carbon has four. It shows which atoms are joined, not the real 3D angles.","A structural formula writes each carbon with its own H atoms, in chain order. Ethane is CH₃CH₃, propane is CH₃CH₂CH₃ and ethanol is CH₃CH₂OH. A branch goes in brackets after the carbon it hangs from: methylpropane is CH₃CH(CH₃)CH₃.","A molecular formula gives the total of each atom. Carbon comes first, then hydrogen, then the rest in A to Z order: C₂H₆, C₃H₈, C₂H₆O. It says nothing about shape, so butane and methylpropane share C₄H₁₀.","Common mistakes: drawing a carbon with three or five bonds; writing one group, such as CH₃, as the formula for ethane; missing the H in an OH group when you count; and thinking that one molecular formula means one molecule."]},"familyTip":"Write CH₃, CH₂ and OH on small cards. Lay them in a row to build CH₃CH₂CH₃ or CH₃CH₂OH, then count the atoms together to get the molecular formula. Finally, draw it out with a line for every bond and check that each C has four lines.","keyIdea":"One molecule can be written three ways. A displayed formula draws every atom and every bond. A structural formula lists the groups along the chain, such as CH₃CH₂CH₃. A molecular formula only counts the atoms, such as C₃H₈, so it loses the shape.","worked":{"title":"Propane: from structural to displayed and molecular","steps":["The structural formula is CH₃CH₂CH₃: three carbons in a row.","Draw the three Cs joined by lines. Add 3 H to each end carbon and 2 H to the middle one.","Check every carbon has four lines: the end ones have 3 H + 1 C, the middle one has 2 H + 2 C.","Count the atoms: 3 C and 3 + 2 + 3 = 8 H. The molecular formula is C₃H₈."]},"visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Molecular or structural? A molecular formula gives each element once; a structural formula lists the groups. Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Molecular formula","Structural formula"],"items":[["C₃H₈",0],["C₂H₆O",0],["C₄H₁₀",0],["C₂H₅Cl",0],["CH₃CH₂CH₃",1],["CH₃CH₂OH",1],["CH₃CH(CH₃)CH₃",1],["CH₃CH₂Cl",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagformula","caption":"Flagship lab: tap each carbon to write its group and watch the structural and molecular formulae fill in; then build a drawing from CH₃CH₂CH₃.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_djL0kvgJ07":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What is the general formula of the alkanes?","choices":["CₙH₂ₙ₊₂","CₙH₂ₙ","CₙH₄ₙ"],"answer":0,"hint":"Methane is CH₄ and ethane is C₂H₆. Which formula gives both?","explain":"CₙH₂ₙ₊₂: n = 1 gives CH₄ and n = 2 gives C₂H₆. CₙH₂ₙ is the alkene formula, and CₙH₄ₙ ignores the C–C bonds."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"What is the formula of butane?","choices":["C₄H₁₀","C₄H₈","C₄H₁₆"],"answer":0,"hint":"But- means 4 carbons. Use 2 × 4 + 2.","explain":"Butane has 4 carbons and 2 × 4 + 2 = 10 hydrogens: C₄H₁₀. C₄H₈ forgets the ends, and C₄H₁₆ gives every carbon four H."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Ali says each carbon in ethane has four hydrogens, so ethane is C₂H₈. What is the slip?","choices":["One bond on each carbon joins the two carbons, so each has only 3 H","Nothing, Ali is right","Ethane has 3 carbons"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count the bonds on one carbon in ethane, including the bond to the other carbon.","explain":"Each carbon makes four bonds, but one is the C–C bond. That leaves 3 H on each carbon, so ethane is C₂H₆."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Propene, C₃H₆, is a hydrocarbon. Why is it not an alkane?","choices":["It has a C=C double bond, so it is unsaturated and does not fit CₙH₂ₙ₊₂","It contains oxygen","It has too many carbon atoms"],"answer":0,"hint":"Compare its hydrogen count with 2 × 3 + 2.","explain":"An alkane with 3 carbons has 8 H. Propene has 6, because its C=C double bond uses two bonds that could hold H. It is an alkene."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"How many hydrogen atoms are in one molecule of the alkane with {n} carbon atoms?","answer":"2*n+2","hint":"Two hydrogens for each carbon, plus two for the ends of the chain.","explain":"2 × {n} + 2 = {=2*n+2} hydrogen atoms, so the formula is C{n}H{=2*n+2}.","params":{"n":[3,15]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"How many atoms in total are in one molecule of the alkane with {n} carbon atoms?","answer":"3*n+2","hint":"Find the hydrogens with 2n + 2, then add the carbons as well.","explain":"{n} carbons + {=2*n+2} hydrogens = {=3*n+2} atoms in each molecule.","params":{"n":[1,10]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"What kind of bonds are found between the carbon atoms in an alkane?","choices":["Single covalent bonds only","At least one double bond","Ionic bonds"],"answer":0,"hint":"Alkanes are saturated. What would a double bond do to the hydrogen count?","explain":"Every C–C bond in an alkane is a single covalent bond. A C=C double bond would make it an alkene instead."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which alkane has 3 carbon atoms?","choices":["Propane","Butane","Ethane"],"answer":0,"hint":"The stems go meth- (1), eth- (2), prop- (3), but- (4).","explain":"Prop- means 3 carbons, so propane, C₃H₈. Ethane has 2 carbons and butane has 4."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"An alkane has {=2*n+2} hydrogen atoms in each molecule. How many carbon atoms does it have?","answer":"n","hint":"Subtract the 2 end hydrogens first, then halve what is left.","explain":"({=2*n+2} − 2) ÷ 2 = {n} carbon atoms.","params":{"n":[3,12]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"An alkane has {n} carbon atoms. How many hydrogen atoms does each molecule have?","answer":"2*n+2","hint":"Use CₙH₂ₙ₊₂: double the number of carbons, then add 2 for the two ends.","explain":"2 × {n} + 2 = {=2*n+2}. Each carbon holds 2 H, and the two end carbons hold 1 extra H each.","params":{"n":[2,12]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Which of these formulae is an alkane?","choices":["C₃H₈","C₃H₆","C₃H₇OH"],"answer":0,"hint":"An alkane has only C and H, and fits CₙH₂ₙ₊₂. Try n = 3.","explain":"With 3 carbons an alkane has 2 × 3 + 2 = 8 hydrogens, so C₃H₈, propane. C₃H₆ is 2 short, and C₃H₇OH contains oxygen."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"An alkane molecule has {=2*n+2} hydrogen atoms. How many carbon atoms does it have?","answer":"n","hint":"Undo CₙH₂ₙ₊₂ step by step: take away the 2 end hydrogens, then halve.","explain":"({=2*n+2} − 2) ÷ 2 = {n}. The alkane is C{n}H{=2*n+2} with {n} carbons.","params":{"n":[2,10]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Why is propane, C₃H₈, called saturated?","choices":["Every carbon already has four bonds, so no more hydrogen can be added","It is dissolved in water","It has a double bond between two carbons"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about whether any carbon in the chain has a free bond left over.","explain":"All its bonds are single and every carbon has four bonds, so it holds as much hydrogen as it can. A double bond would make it unsaturated."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"In a straight-chain alkane with {n} carbons, how many hydrogen atoms sit on the middle carbons (all the carbons except the two ends)?","answer":"2*(n-2)","hint":"There are {n} − 2 middle carbons. Each one uses 2 bonds for its neighbours, leaving 2 for hydrogen.","explain":"{n} − 2 = {=n-2} middle carbons, each with 2 H, gives {=2*(n-2)} hydrogens. The two ends add 3 + 3 = 6 more.","params":{"n":[3,10]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Alkanes are a family of molecules made of carbon and hydrogen. The carbons join up in a chain. Every bond is a single bond.","Each carbon makes four bonds. A carbon in the middle of the chain uses two of them to hold its neighbours. That leaves room for two H. A carbon at an end has one neighbour, so it holds three H.","So the count goes like this. Two H for every carbon, then two more for the ends. Butane has 4 carbons, so it has 2 × 4 + 2 = 10 hydrogens. Its formula is C₄H₁₀.","No carbon in an alkane has a spare bond. You cannot fit in one more H. We say alkanes are saturated: they are full of hydrogen."],"adult":["Alkanes are a family of hydrocarbons in which every bond between carbon atoms is a single covalent bond. The first four are methane, CH₄; ethane, C₂H₆; propane, C₃H₈; and butane, C₄H₁₀. The name ending -ane marks an alkane, and the stem meth-, eth-, prop- or but- gives the number of carbons.","Each carbon forms four bonds. In a straight chain of n carbons, n − 1 bonds link carbon to carbon. The end carbons carry three hydrogens each and the middle ones carry two. That gives the general formula CₙH₂ₙ₊₂: two hydrogens per carbon plus one extra at each end.","Alkanes are saturated. Every carbon already has its four bonds, so no more hydrogen can be added. A hydrocarbon with a C=C double bond has two fewer hydrogens, CₙH₂ₙ, and is unsaturated. Propene, C₃H₆, is an alkene, not an alkane.","Common mistakes: writing CₙH₂ₙ and forgetting the two end hydrogens; giving each carbon four hydrogens, which ignores the C–C bonds; and calling any hydrocarbon an alkane, even one with a double bond. Smaller alkanes such as propane and butane are gases used as fuels."]},"familyTip":"Look at a camping gas canister or a lighter refill: many say propane or butane. Work out each formula together from the number of carbons (prop- is 3, but- is 4) using two hydrogens per carbon plus two, then check: C₃H₈ and C₄H₁₀.","keyIdea":"Alkanes are hydrocarbons with only single bonds. Every carbon makes four bonds and holds as many hydrogens as it can, so alkanes are saturated and all fit one general formula, CₙH₂ₙ₊₂.","worked":{"title":"What is the formula of pentane, the alkane with 5 carbon atoms?","steps":["Alkanes follow CₙH₂ₙ₊₂, where n is the number of carbon atoms. Here n = 5.","Hydrogens: 2 × 5 + 2 = 12. That is 2 H on each carbon and 1 extra H on each end carbon.","Check with the chain: the two end carbons hold 3 H each, the three middle carbons hold 2 H each, and 3 + 2 + 2 + 2 + 3 = 12.","So pentane is C₅H₁₂."]},"visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Alkane or not? An alkane has only C and H, single bonds only, and fits CₙH₂ₙ₊₂. Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Alkane","Not an alkane"],"items":[["CH₄, methane",0],["C₂H₆, ethane",0],["C₄H₁₀, butane",0],["C₆H₁₄, hexane",0],["C₂H₄, ethene",1],["C₃H₆, propene",1],["C₂H₅OH, ethanol",1],["C₄H₈, butene",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagalk","caption":"Flagship lab: grow the carbon chain, watch hydrogens fill every free bond, test CₙH₂ₙ₊₂, then try a double bond.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_ofQRMhETTm":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What decides which homologous series a compound belongs to?","choices":["Its functional group","Its number of carbon atoms","Its boiling point"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which part of the molecule decides how it reacts?","explain":"The functional group, such as –OH or C=C, defines the family. Compounds with the same number of carbons can be in different series."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Propene, C₃H₆, is an alkene. What is the next alkene in the series?","choices":["C₄H₈","C₄H₉","C₄H₁₀"],"answer":0,"hint":"Add one CH₂ to C₃H₆: one more C and two more H.","explain":"C₃H₆ + CH₂ = C₄H₈, butene. C₄H₁₀ has no C=C, so it is an alkane."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Butane boils at −1 °C and hexane at 69 °C. Which is the best estimate for pentane, the alkane between them?","choices":["About 36 °C","About −40 °C","About 120 °C"],"answer":0,"hint":"Boiling point changes gradually along a series. Pentane sits between butane and hexane.","explain":"Pentane must boil between −1 °C and 69 °C; it boils at 36 °C. The trend is gradual, so the middle member lands in between."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Mia says CH₃OH and C₃H₇OH cannot be in the same series because they are not next to each other. What is wrong?","choices":["They differ by 2 × CH₂ and both have –OH, so they are in the same series","Nothing, Mia is right","They have different numbers of oxygen atoms"],"answer":0,"hint":"A series is a whole family, not just a pair of neighbours.","explain":"Methanol and propanol are both alcohols. They differ by C₂H₄, which is two CH₂ steps, so they sit two places apart in one series."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"An alcohol has {n} carbon atoms. Using CₙH₂ₙ₊₁OH, how many hydrogen atoms does one molecule contain in total?","answer":"2*n+2","hint":"Count the hydrogens on the carbon chain, then remember the H in the –OH group.","explain":"2 × {n} + 1 = {=2*n+1} H on the carbons, plus 1 in –OH, gives {=2*n+2} hydrogen atoms.","params":{"n":[1,8]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"The alkenes fit CₙH₂ₙ. How many hydrogen atoms are in the alkene with {n} carbon atoms?","answer":"2*n","hint":"Put n = {n} into CₙH₂ₙ: the H count is twice the C count.","explain":"2 × {n} = {=2*n}, so the alkene is C{n}H{=2*n}.","params":{"n":[2,10]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Why do all the alcohols react with sodium in a similar way?","choices":["They all have the same functional group, –OH","They all have the same boiling point","They all have exactly two carbon atoms"],"answer":0,"hint":"The part of the molecule that reacts is the same in every member.","explain":"Every alcohol carries –OH, and that group does the reacting, so the reactions are similar even though chain lengths differ."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which pair is NOT in the same homologous series?","choices":["C₂H₄ and C₂H₆","CH₄ and C₃H₈","C₂H₅OH and C₄H₉OH"],"answer":0,"hint":"Same carbons does not mean same family. Check the general formula of each.","explain":"C₂H₄ is an alkene (CₙH₂ₙ) and C₂H₆ is an alkane (CₙH₂ₙ₊₂). The other pairs share a formula and differ by CH₂ steps."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Two neighbouring members of a homologous series differ by what?","choices":["CH₂","CH₃","One carbon atom only"],"answer":0,"hint":"Compare CH₄ with C₂H₆: count the extra carbons and the extra hydrogens.","explain":"CH₄ to C₂H₆ adds 1 C and 2 H, which is CH₂. Every neighbouring pair in a series differs by exactly this."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"In a homologous series, one member has {a} carbon atoms. How many CH₂ units must be added to reach the member with {b} carbon atoms?","answer":"b-a","hint":"Each CH₂ unit adds exactly one carbon atom. Count the extra carbons.","explain":"{b} − {a} = {=b-a} extra carbons, so {=b-a} CH₂ units.","params":{"a":[1,4],"b":[5,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A CH₂ unit has a relative mass of 14. Two members of the same series differ by {k} CH₂ units. How much larger is the relative formula mass of the bigger one?","answer":"14*k","hint":"Each CH₂ adds 12 for the carbon and 2 for the hydrogens.","explain":"{k} × 14 = {=14*k}, so the bigger molecule is {=14*k} heavier.","params":{"k":[1,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Which compound is in the same homologous series as ethanol, C₂H₅OH?","choices":["C₃H₇OH","C₂H₆","CH₃COOH"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for the same functional group, not the same number of carbons.","explain":"C₃H₇OH, propanol, has –OH and is one CH₂ bigger. C₂H₆ has no –OH, and CH₃COOH has –COOH, a different group."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Which statement about the members of a homologous series is true?","choices":["They react in similar ways, and their boiling points change gradually","They all have the same boiling point","They all have the same number of carbon atoms"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about what the shared functional group controls, and what changes as the chain grows.","explain":"The shared group gives similar reactions. Boiling point rises steadily with chain length, and the carbon count changes by one each step."}],"explain":{"kid":["Organic compounds come in families. Chemists call each family a homologous series. Alkanes are one family. Alcohols are another.","Members of a family are like steps on a ladder. Each step adds one more CH₂: one carbon and two hydrogens. Methanol is CH₃OH. Ethanol is C₂H₅OH. Propanol is C₃H₇OH.","What makes a family is a special group of atoms called the functional group. Every alcohol has an OH group. Every alkene has a C=C bond. The group is the part that reacts, so members of a family react in a similar way.","They are not all the same, though. Bigger members boil at higher temperatures. Each step up the ladder makes the boiling point rise a bit more."],"adult":["A homologous series is a family of organic compounds. All its members share a functional group and one general formula. Four examples are the alkanes, CₙH₂ₙ₊₂, the alkenes, CₙH₂ₙ, the alcohols, CₙH₂ₙ₊₁OH, and the carboxylic acids.","Each member differs from the next by one CH₂ unit. Going from ethanol, C₂H₅OH, to propanol, C₃H₇OH, adds one carbon and two hydrogens, and the –OH stays. That steady step is why one formula covers the whole family.","The functional group is the atom or group of atoms that decides how a compound reacts. All alcohols burn and react with sodium because they all carry –OH. Physical properties change step by step instead: boiling point rises as the chain grows, from −162 °C for methane to 36 °C for pentane.","Common mistakes: thinking neighbours differ by CH₃; putting two compounds in one series because they have the same number of carbons, such as ethane and ethanol; and expecting members to have identical properties when only their reactions are alike."]},"familyTip":"Find two or three products at home that list an alcohol, such as hand gel (ethanol) or some cleaners (propanol). Write their formulae on a strip of paper as a ladder: CH₃OH, C₂H₅OH, C₃H₇OH. Ask each other what is added at every step and what never changes.","keyIdea":"A homologous series is a family of organic compounds with the same functional group and the same general formula. Neighbouring members differ by CH₂, they react in similar ways, and physical properties such as boiling point change gradually along the series.","worked":{"title":"Is C₄H₉OH in the same homologous series as ethanol, C₂H₅OH? If so, how far apart are they?","steps":["Check the functional group first. Both formulae end in OH, so both are alcohols.","Check the general formula. For alcohols it is CₙH₂ₙ₊₁OH. With n = 4 that gives C₄H₉OH, so it fits.","Find the difference: C₄H₉OH − C₂H₅OH = C₂H₄, which is 2 × CH₂.","So yes, they are in the same series, two members apart: ethanol, then propanol, then butanol."]},"visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Same series as ethanol, C₂H₅OH, or not? Look for the –OH group, not the carbon count. Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Same series as ethanol","Different series"],"items":[["CH₃OH, methanol",0],["C₃H₇OH, propanol",0],["C₄H₉OH, butanol",0],["C₅H₁₁OH, pentanol",0],["C₂H₆, ethane",1],["C₂H₄, ethene",1],["CH₃COOH, ethanoic acid",1],["C₃H₈, propane",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flaghomo","caption":"Flagship lab: open the CH₂ gaps, test each family rule, sort by functional group, then guess boiling points.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_pe4Ehc0koq":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"An atom has the electron arrangement 2, 8, 5. Where is it in the periodic table?","choices":["Group 5, Period 3","Group 3, Period 5","Group 15, Period 3"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count the shells for the period, then read the last number for the group.","explain":"Three numbers means three shells, so Period 3. The outer shell holds 5, so Group 5. This is phosphorus."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"An element is in Group 7, Period 2. How many outer electrons and shells does it have?","choices":["7 outer electrons and 2 shells","2 outer electrons and 7 shells","9 outer electrons and 2 shells"],"answer":0,"hint":"The group gives one number and the period gives the other. Do not swap them.","explain":"Group 7 means 7 outer electrons, and Period 2 means 2 shells: the arrangement 2, 7, which is fluorine."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Sam says sodium and chlorine should react in the same way because both are in Period 3. What is wrong?","choices":["Reactions follow the group, and they have different numbers of outer electrons","Nothing, Sam is right","They are in different periods"],"answer":0,"hint":"What do elements in the same period share, and what do they not share?","explain":"Both have three shells, but sodium has 1 outer electron and chlorine has 7. Outer electrons decide how an element reacts, so they behave very differently."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"An element in Period 3 has the atomic number {z}. Which group is it in?","answer":"z-10","hint":"Period 3 means three shells. Fill 2 and then 8, and see how many electrons are left for the outer shell.","explain":"The arrangement is 2, 8, {=z-10}. That leaves {=z-10} outer electrons, so it is in Group {=z-10}.","params":{"z":[11,17]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"An atom has {n} electrons in its outer shell and is in Groups 1 to 7. What is its group number?","answer":"n","hint":"For the main groups, the group number and the outer-shell electrons match.","explain":"{n} outer electrons puts the element in Group {n}.","params":{"n":[1,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Helium has only 2 electrons. Why is it placed in Group 0 and not Group 2?","choices":["Its only shell is full, like the other noble gases","It has no outer electrons","It has 8 outer electrons"],"answer":0,"hint":"How many electrons can the first shell hold?","explain":"The first shell holds at most 2, so helium's outer shell is full. Like neon and argon, it barely reacts, so it belongs with Group 0."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Potassium is below sodium in Group 1. How does potassium react with water?","choices":["Like sodium, but more vigorously","It does not react, because it is a different element","Slowly, like magnesium"],"answer":0,"hint":"Same group means a similar reaction. What is the trend down Group 1?","explain":"Both have 1 outer electron, so both fizz and make hydrogen. Reactivity rises down Group 1, so potassium reacts more strongly and the hydrogen catches fire."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"What do all the elements in Period 2 have in common?","choices":["They all have two occupied electron shells","They all have two outer electrons","They all react in the same way"],"answer":0,"hint":"Lithium is 2, 1 and neon is 2, 8. What is the same about both?","explain":"Every Period 2 atom has electrons in two shells. The number of outer electrons rises from 1 to 8 across the row, so their reactions differ."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"What does the period number of an element tell you about its atoms?","choices":["The number of occupied electron shells","The number of outer-shell electrons","The total number of electrons"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about sodium, 2, 8, 1. It is in Period 3. What is there three of?","explain":"Sodium has electrons in three shells and sits in Period 3. The period number counts the occupied shells."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"An element in Period 3 has {g} electrons in its outer shell. How many electrons does one of its atoms have in total?","answer":"10+g","hint":"Period 3 means three shells. The first two shells are full with 2 and 8 electrons.","explain":"The arrangement is 2, 8, {g}, so the total is 2 + 8 + {g} = {=10+g} electrons.","params":{"g":[1,7]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"An element is in Group {g}, Period 2. What is its atomic number?","answer":"2+g","hint":"Period 2 means two shells. The first shell is full with 2. The group tells you the outer shell.","explain":"The arrangement is 2, {g}, which is {=2+g} electrons. A neutral atom has as many protons as electrons, so the atomic number is {=2+g}.","params":{"g":[1,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Which element will react most like sodium?","choices":["Potassium, in Group 1","Magnesium, next to sodium in Period 3","Neon, which has one electron fewer"],"answer":0,"hint":"Reactions depend on outer electrons. Which element also has exactly one outer electron?","explain":"Potassium is in Group 1 with sodium, so both have 1 outer electron and react in similar ways. Magnesium has 2 outer electrons and neon has a full outer shell."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Why are the Group 0 elements so unreactive?","choices":["Their outer shells are already full","They have no electrons in their outer shell","They have only one shell each"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at neon, 2, 8, and argon, 2, 8, 8. How full is the last shell?","explain":"Each noble gas has a full outer shell, so it has no need to gain, lose or share electrons. Only helium has just one shell."}],"explain":{"kid":["The periodic table is not a random list. Where an element sits tells you how its electrons are arranged.","Each row is called a period. Count the rows from the top. The row number is how many shells of electrons the atom has. Sodium has three shells, so it is in Period 3.","Each column is called a group. For Groups 1 to 7, the group number is how many electrons are in the outer shell. Sodium has one outer electron, so it is in Group 1.","Elements in the same group act alike, because the outer electrons do the reacting. Lithium, sodium and potassium all fizz in water. The last column is Group 0. Its atoms have full outer shells, so they hardly react at all."],"adult":["Mendeleev ordered the elements by their properties before electrons were known. The electron model now explains why his table works. Each row is a period, and an atom's period number equals its number of occupied shells.","Each column is a group. For the main groups, 1 to 7, the group number equals the number of electrons in the outer shell. Chlorine is 2, 8, 7: three shells, so Period 3, and seven outer electrons, so Group 7.","Reactions involve the outer electrons, so elements in a group react in similar ways. All Group 1 metals react with water to form hydrogen and an alkaline solution. Properties still change gradually down a group: Group 1 metals get more reactive, while Group 7 elements get less reactive. Group 0, the noble gases, have full outer shells and are very unreactive.","Common mistakes: swapping the two rules, so that the group gives the shells; using the total number of electrons instead of the outer ones; and expecting elements in the same period to behave alike. Helium is a careful exception: it is in Group 0 with only 2 outer electrons, because its single shell is full."]},"familyTip":"Draw a 4 by 8 grid on paper. Take turns saying an electron arrangement, such as 2, 8, 3, and racing to point at the box where it belongs. Then ask which element in the grid would react most like it.","keyIdea":"The periodic table is laid out by electrons. For main-group elements the group number equals the number of outer-shell electrons, and the period number equals the number of occupied shells. Elements in the same group have the same number of outer electrons, so they react in similar ways.","worked":{"title":"An unfamiliar element is in Group 2, Period 4. How many outer electrons and shells does it have, and which element will it behave like?","steps":["The group number gives the outer electrons. Group 2 means 2 electrons in the outer shell.","The period number gives the shells. Period 4 means 4 occupied shells, so the arrangement is 2, 8, 8, 2.","That is 20 electrons in total, so the element is calcium.","It will behave most like magnesium, the element above it in Group 2, because both have 2 outer electrons."]},"visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which element will react most like sodium? Sort by outer electrons, not by row. Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Reacts like sodium (1 outer electron)","Does not"],"items":[["Li, lithium: 2, 1",0],["K, potassium: 2, 8, 8, 1",0],["Mg, magnesium: 2, 8, 2",1],["Cl, chlorine: 2, 8, 7",1],["Ar, argon: 2, 8, 8",1],["Ne, neon: 2, 8",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagptab","caption":"Flagship lab: walk down a group and along a period, place mystery atoms, then drop Group 1 metals into water.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_YHKhUliaQh":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"You are shown C₃H₈ and C₃H₆. Which one is an alkene?","choices":["C₃H₆","C₃H₈","Both, as they have 3 carbons"],"answer":0,"hint":"For 3 carbons, an alkene has 2 × 3 hydrogens.","explain":"C₃H₆ fits CₙH₂ₙ, so it is propene. C₃H₈ fits CₙH₂ₙ₊₂, so it is propane, an alkane."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which is the correct displayed structure of propene, written in short?","choices":["CH₂=CH–CH₃","CH₃=CH–CH₃","CH₂=CH₂–CH₃"],"answer":0,"hint":"Every carbon needs exactly four bonds. The double bond counts as two.","explain":"In CH₂=CH–CH₃ each carbon has four bonds. CH₃= would give the first carbon five bonds, and =CH₂– would give the middle carbon five."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Orange bromine water is shaken with a hydrocarbon and turns colourless. What does this show?","choices":["The hydrocarbon has a C=C double bond","The hydrocarbon is an alkane","The hydrocarbon has no hydrogen"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which kind of hydrocarbon can add bromine atoms?","explain":"Bromine adds across a C=C, which removes its colour. Alkanes have no double bond, so they leave bromine water orange."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"An alkene has {=2*n} hydrogen atoms. How many carbon atoms does it have?","answer":"n","hint":"In CₙH₂ₙ the hydrogens are twice the carbons, so work backwards.","explain":"{=2*n} ÷ 2 = {n}, so the alkene is C{n}H{=2*n}.","params":{"n":[2,8]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"A molecule of an alkene with {n} carbons reacts with bromine, Br₂. How many atoms does the product molecule contain in total?","answer":"3*n+2","hint":"Count the carbons and hydrogens of CₙH₂ₙ, then add the two bromine atoms that join across the C=C.","explain":"{n} C + {=2*n} H + 2 Br = {=3*n+2} atoms, for example C₂H₄Br₂ for ethene.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"In ethene, how many hydrogen atoms are bonded to each carbon?","choices":["2","3","1"],"answer":0,"hint":"Each carbon makes 4 bonds, and 2 of them are in the C=C.","explain":"4 − 2 = 2, so each carbon holds 2 H: CH₂=CH₂, which is C₂H₄."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Leo says alkanes are unsaturated because they have lots of hydrogen. What is wrong?","choices":["Alkanes are saturated: every carbon already has four single bonds","Nothing, Leo is right","Alkanes have no hydrogen at all"],"answer":0,"hint":"Saturated means no more atoms can be added. Can more be added to an alkane?","explain":"In an alkane every carbon already has four single bonds, so nothing more can add. That makes alkanes saturated, and alkenes unsaturated."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which name matches the alkene C₄H₈?","choices":["Butene","Butane","Propene"],"answer":0,"hint":"The stem gives the number of carbons and the ending tells you the family.","explain":"But- means 4 carbons and -ene means a C=C, so C₄H₈ is butene. Butane is C₄H₁₀ and propene is C₃H₆."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"What makes a hydrocarbon an alkene?","choices":["It has one carbon–carbon double bond","It has only single bonds","It contains oxygen as well as carbon"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the -ene ending and the bond between two carbons in ethene.","explain":"An alkene has a C=C double bond. Hydrocarbons with only single bonds are alkanes, and a hydrocarbon contains no oxygen."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"An alkene has {n} carbon atoms. How many hydrogen atoms does it have?","answer":"2*n","hint":"Use CₙH₂ₙ: the number of hydrogens is twice the number of carbons.","explain":"2 × {n} = {=2*n}, so the alkene is C{n}H{=2*n}.","params":{"n":[2,8]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"An alkene has {n} carbon atoms. Using C = 12 and H = 1, what is its relative formula mass?","answer":"14*n","hint":"Work out the formula with CₙH₂ₙ first, then add 12 for each C and 1 for each H.","explain":"The alkene is C{n}H{=2*n}: {n} × 12 + {=2*n} × 1 = {=14*n}. Each CH₂ adds 14.","params":{"n":[2,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Which formula belongs to an alkene?","choices":["C₄H₈","C₄H₁₀","C₄H₉"],"answer":0,"hint":"Check whether the number of hydrogens is exactly twice the number of carbons.","explain":"C₄H₈ fits CₙH₂ₙ, so it is butene. C₄H₁₀ fits CₙH₂ₙ₊₂, butane, and no stable hydrocarbon is C₄H₉."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Why is ethene described as unsaturated?","choices":["Its C=C bond can open and add more atoms","It does not dissolve in water","It has fewer carbon atoms than ethane"],"answer":0,"hint":"Saturated means full. What can happen to the double bond?","explain":"Unsaturated means more atoms can still be added. The C=C can open, for example adding bromine to make C₂H₄Br₂. Ethene and ethane both have 2 carbons."}],"explain":{"kid":["Alkenes are a family of hydrocarbons. That means they are made of only carbon and hydrogen. Every alkene has two carbons joined by a double bond, written C=C.","A carbon atom always makes four bonds. The double bond uses up two of them. So the two carbons in the C=C each have room for one hydrogen less than in an alkane.","The smallest alkene is ethene, C₂H₄. Next come propene, C₃H₆, and butene, C₄H₈. Look at the numbers. There are always twice as many hydrogens as carbons.","Alkenes are called unsaturated. The double bond can open up and grab more atoms. You can test for this with orange bromine water. Shake it with an alkene and the colour vanishes. With an alkane it stays orange."],"adult":["An alkene is a hydrocarbon containing one carbon–carbon double bond. The names end in -ene: ethene, propene, butene, pentene. Each name uses the same stem as the alkane with the same number of carbons.","Carbon forms four bonds, and the C=C counts as two of them. Each carbon in the double bond therefore holds one hydrogen fewer than in the matching alkane. That makes two fewer in total, so the general formula is CₙH₂ₙ rather than CₙH₂ₙ₊₂. Propene is CH₂=CH–CH₃.","Alkanes are saturated: every carbon already has four single bonds, so no more atoms can be added. Alkenes are unsaturated, because the C=C can open and bond to new atoms. With bromine, ethene forms C₂H₄Br₂. That addition removes the orange colour of bromine water, which is the standard test for a C=C.","Common mistakes: drawing three hydrogens on a C=C carbon, so each carbon has five bonds; removing only one hydrogen for the double bond; and reversing the bromine water test. Be careful too with ring compounds. A ring of carbons can also fit CₙH₂ₙ, so the formula alone suggests a chain alkene, but the drawing settles it."]},"familyTip":"Use dried pasta for bonds and coins for atoms. Build ethane with single pasta bonds, then rebuild it as ethene with two pieces of pasta between the carbons. Count how many coin hydrogens you had to take away, and check every carbon still has four pieces of pasta.","keyIdea":"Alkenes are hydrocarbons with one carbon–carbon double bond, C=C. Their general formula is CₙH₂ₙ: ethene C₂H₄, propene C₃H₆, butene C₄H₈. Because the double bond can open and take on more atoms, alkenes are unsaturated, and they turn orange bromine water colourless.","worked":{"title":"You are shown C₃H₈ and C₃H₆. Which is the alkene, and how is it drawn?","steps":["Test each formula. For n = 3, an alkane has 2 × 3 + 2 = 8 hydrogens and an alkene has 2 × 3 = 6.","So C₃H₈ is propane, an alkane, and C₃H₆ is propene, the alkene.","Draw three carbons in a row and put a double bond between the first two: C=C–C.","Fill each carbon to four bonds: 2 H on the first carbon, 1 H on the middle one and 3 H on the last. That gives CH₂=CH–CH₃, six hydrogens in total."]},"visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Alkene or alkane? Check whether the H count is 2 × C or 2 × C + 2. Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Alkene, CₙH₂ₙ","Alkane, CₙH₂ₙ₊₂"],"items":[["C₂H₄, ethene",0],["C₃H₆, propene",0],["C₄H₈, butene",0],["C₅H₁₀, pentene",0],["C₂H₆, ethane",1],["C₃H₈, propane",1],["C₄H₁₀, butane",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagalkene","caption":"Flagship lab: add the hydrogens to ethene, propene and butene, then test each molecule with bromine water.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_bXvMmTglF9":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which part of ethanoic acid, CH₃COOH, is its functional group?","choices":["–COOH","–OH only","CH₃"],"answer":0,"hint":"The OH sits on a carbon that also has a C=O. Do they work alone?","explain":"The C=O and the O–H on the same carbon act together as –COOH. CH₃ is part of the chain."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"CH₃COOCH₃ is shown. Which family is it in?","choices":["Ester","Carboxylic acid","Alcohol"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at what is on the far side of the single-bonded O: an H or a carbon?","explain":"A carbon is on the far side of that O, so the group is –COO–. That makes it an ester, methyl ethanoate."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Ethanol reacts with sodium. Why can you predict that butanol will too?","choices":["It has the same –OH group","It has more carbon atoms","It is a liquid"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which part of the molecule does the reacting?","explain":"The –OH group reacts with sodium. Butanol has the same group, so it reacts the same way; the longer chain does not change that."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"A hydrocarbon turns bromine water from orange to colourless. Which group does it have?","choices":["C=C","–OH","–COOH"],"answer":0,"hint":"Bromine adds across one kind of bond.","explain":"Bromine adds across a C=C double bond, which removes the orange colour. A hydrocarbon cannot have –OH or –COOH, which contain oxygen."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"An ester is made from ethanoic acid and an alcohol with {n} carbon atoms. How many carbon atoms does the ester have?","answer":"n+2","hint":"All the carbons of the acid and the alcohol end up in the ester. Only water leaves.","explain":"Water takes no carbon, so the ester keeps 2 from ethanoic acid plus {n} from the alcohol: {=n+2}.","params":{"n":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Sam says CH₃COOH is an alcohol because it contains O–H. What is wrong?","choices":["The O–H is part of –COOH, so it is a carboxylic acid","Nothing, Sam is right","It has no oxygen at all"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the carbon the O–H is joined to. What else is on it?","explain":"That carbon also has a C=O, so the atoms form –COOH. Carboxylic acids react with carbonates, which alcohols do not."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"What leaves when ethanol and ethanoic acid join to form an ester?","choices":["Water","Hydrogen gas","Carbon dioxide"],"answer":0,"hint":"The acid loses OH and the alcohol loses an H. Put them together.","explain":"OH + H makes H₂O. The rest joins up as ethyl ethanoate with a –COO– group."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which pair of compounds will react in the same ways?","choices":["Propanol and pentanol","Propanol and propane","Propanol and propanoic acid"],"answer":0,"hint":"Same reactions need the same functional group, not the same number of carbons.","explain":"Propanol and pentanol both have –OH. Propane has no group, and propanoic acid has –COOH, so they react differently."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"An ester is made from methanol and a carboxylic acid with {n} carbon atoms. How many hydrogen atoms does the ester have?","answer":"2*n+2","hint":"Count the H in the acid and in methanol, then take away the two H atoms that leave in water.","explain":"The acid has 2 × {n} = {=2*n} H and methanol has 4. Water takes 2, leaving {=2*n} + 4 − 2 = {=2*n+2}.","params":{"n":[1,5]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"What is a functional group?","choices":["The atom or group of atoms that gives a compound its typical reactions","The longest carbon chain in the molecule","Any atom that is not carbon"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about which part of ethanol reacts with sodium.","explain":"The functional group, such as –OH, decides how a compound reacts. The chain hardly reacts, and hydrogen is not carbon but is not a group on its own."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"An alcohol has {n} carbon atoms. How many hydrogen atoms does it have in total, counting the one in –OH?","answer":"2*n+2","hint":"Alcohols fit CₙH₂ₙ₊₁OH. Add the H in the chain to the H in –OH.","explain":"The chain has 2 × {n} + 1 = {=2*n+1} H and the –OH adds 1, so {=2*n+2} in total.","params":{"n":[1,6]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A carboxylic acid has {n} carbon atoms, counting the carbon in –COOH. How many hydrogen atoms does it have?","answer":"2*n","hint":"Methanoic acid is HCOOH and ethanoic acid is CH₃COOH. Each extra carbon adds CH₂.","explain":"Carboxylic acids fit CₙH₂ₙO₂, so {n} carbons carry 2 × {n} = {=2*n} hydrogens.","params":{"n":[1,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Which group is in CH₃CH₂CH₂OH?","choices":["–OH, so it is an alcohol","–COOH, so it is a carboxylic acid","C=C, so it is an alkene"],"answer":0,"hint":"Read the end of the formula. Is there a C=O next to the OH?","explain":"The OH is on a plain CH₂ with no C=O, so the group is –OH. This is propanol, an alcohol."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Which test result shows a compound has a –COOH group?","choices":["It fizzes with sodium carbonate","It turns bromine water colourless","It has a fruity smell"],"answer":0,"hint":"Carboxylic acids are acids. What do acids do with carbonates?","explain":"Acids release carbon dioxide from carbonates. Clearing bromine water is the C=C test, and a fruity smell suggests an ester."}],"explain":{"kid":["Most of an organic molecule is a chain of carbon atoms with hydrogens around them. That chain is quite dull. It does not react with much.","Stuck on the chain there is often a small special part. We call it the functional group. It is the part that does the reacting.","Alcohols have an O–H on the end, written –OH. Carboxylic acids have –COOH. Alkenes have a double bond, C=C. Esters have –COO– in the middle of the chain.","Here is the trick. If two molecules have the same group, they react in the same way. Ethanol and butanol both fizz with sodium. Their chains are different, but their –OH is the same."],"adult":["A functional group is the atom or group of atoms responsible for a compound's characteristic reactions. The C–C and C–H bonds of the chain are much less reactive. So every member of a homologous series reacts alike: methanol, propanol and butanol all react with sodium as ethanol does. The group also gives the name ending: -ene, -ol, -oic acid and -oate.","At GCSE there are four groups to spot. Alkenes have C=C and turn bromine water colourless. Alcohols have –OH and give off hydrogen with sodium. Carboxylic acids have –COOH. They are weak acids, so they fizz with carbonates and give carbon dioxide. Esters have –COO– and often smell fruity.","An ester forms when a carboxylic acid reacts with an alcohol, using an acid catalyst. The acid loses its OH, the alcohol loses the H from its OH, and these leave as water. Ethanoic acid and ethanol give ethyl ethanoate, CH₃COOCH₂CH₃.","Common mistakes: circling only the O of –OH and leaving out its H. Calling a carboxylic acid an alcohol because –COOH contains O–H. Mixing up –COOH with –COO–. Thinking that a longer chain changes which reactions happen."]},"familyTip":"Write a few chain lengths on cards (CH₃, CH₃CH₂, CH₃CH₂CH₂) and the groups on others (–OH, –COOH). Take turns snapping a chain to a group and naming the result, then say one reaction it will have. The reaction depends only on which group card you picked.","keyIdea":"A functional group is the atom or small group of atoms that gives an organic compound its typical reactions. The rest of the molecule is a carbon chain that hardly reacts. Four groups to know: C=C in alkenes, –OH in alcohols, –COOH in carboxylic acids and –COO– in esters. Compounds with the same group react in the same ways, so the group also names the family.","worked":{"title":"Name the family of CH₃CH₂COOH and predict one reaction.","steps":["Look past the chain. CH₃CH₂ is the same as in ethane and does not react with the usual tests.","The end is COOH: a carbon with a C=O and an O–H on it. Together these are the –COOH group.","–COOH means a carboxylic acid. Three carbons in all, so the name is propanoic acid.","Prediction: like ethanoic acid, it fizzes with sodium carbonate and gives carbon dioxide, because it has the same group."]},"visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which family? Find the functional group, then drag each formula to its family, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Alcohol, –OH","Carboxylic acid, –COOH","Ester, –COO–"],"items":[["CH₃OH, methanol",0],["CH₃CH₂CH₂OH, propanol",0],["HCOOH, methanoic acid",1],["CH₃CH₂COOH, propanoic acid",1],["CH₃COOCH₂CH₃, ethyl ethanoate",2],["HCOOCH₃, methyl methanoate",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagfgroup","caption":"Flagship lab: circle the functional group, test molecules with bromine water, sodium and sodium carbonate, then make an ester.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_OO8s4anH67":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which electron arrangement has a full outer shell?","choices":["2,8,8","2,8,1","2,8,6"],"answer":0,"hint":"Only the last number counts. Is that shell full?","explain":"2,8,8 is argon: the outer shell holds 8. 2,8,1 and 2,8,6 have gaps in their outer shells."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Sodium is 2,8,1. Its second shell is full. Why is sodium still very reactive?","choices":["Its outer shell has only 1 electron","Its second shell is too full","It has no full shells"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which shell takes part in reactions?","explain":"Only the outer shell matters. Sodium's outer shell has 1 of 8, so it reacts by losing that electron."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"A metal atom has {n} electrons in shell {s}, its outer shell. It loses them to form an ion. What is the size of the positive charge on the ion?","answer":"n","hint":"Each electron lost leaves one more proton than electrons.","explain":"Losing {n} electrons leaves {n} more protons than electrons, so the ion has a {n}+ charge. Its outer shell is now shell {=s-1}, which is full.","params":{"n":[1,3],"s":[2,4]},"where":"!(s==4&&n==3)&&!(s==2&&n==3)"},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"A non-metal atom has {n} electrons in shell {s}, its outer shell. It gains electrons to form an ion. What is the size of the negative charge on the ion?","answer":"8-n","hint":"How many empty places are there in a shell that holds 8? Each electron gained adds one negative charge.","explain":"It gains 8 − {n} = {=8-n} electrons, so the ion has a {=8-n}− charge and a full shell {s}.","params":{"n":[5,7],"s":[2,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Carbon is 2,4. How does it usually get a full outer shell?","choices":["It shares electrons with other atoms","It loses 4 electrons","It already has one"],"answer":0,"hint":"Losing or gaining 4 electrons would both be hard.","explain":"Carbon shares pairs of electrons, as in methane, CH₄. Then it counts 8 in its outer shell."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Chlorine is 2,8,7. What does it do to reach a full outer shell?","choices":["Gains 1 electron","Loses 7 electrons","Loses 1 electron"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which is fewer: 1 move or 7?","explain":"Gaining 1 electron gives 2,8,8, like argon. Losing 7 would take far more, and losing 1 leaves 2,8,6."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Hydrogen is lighter than helium. Why are party balloons filled with helium?","choices":["Helium does not burn because its outer shell is full","Helium is lighter than hydrogen","Hydrogen has a full outer shell"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about what happens if a hydrogen balloon meets a flame.","explain":"Hydrogen's shell is half full, so it burns. Helium's only shell is full, so it is safe."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"After a sodium atom loses one electron, which noble gas has the same arrangement?","choices":["Neon","Argon","Helium"],"answer":0,"hint":"Write 2,8,1 and take 1 away.","explain":"2,8,1 minus 1 is 2,8, which is neon's arrangement."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Why are the noble gases so unreactive?","choices":["Their outer shell of electrons is full","They have no electrons","They are heavier than other gases"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the outer shell of helium, neon and argon.","explain":"Helium is 2, neon 2,8 and argon 2,8,8. With a full outer shell, they have no need to lose, gain or share electrons."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A metal atom has {n} electrons in its outer shell, which is shell {s}. How many electrons does it lose to get a full outer shell?","answer":"n","hint":"Give away every electron in shell {s}. Shell {=s-1} underneath is already full.","explain":"It loses all {n} electrons in shell {s}. Shell {=s-1}, which is full, becomes the outer shell: a noble-gas arrangement.","params":{"n":[1,3],"s":[2,4]},"where":"!(s==4&&n==3)&&!(s==2&&n==3)"},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A non-metal atom has {n} electrons in its outer shell, which is shell {s} and can hold 8. How many electrons does it gain to fill it?","answer":"8-n","hint":"Count the empty places left in shell {s}.","explain":"8 − {n} = {=8-n}, so it gains {=8-n} electrons. Shell {s} is then full, like the noble gas at the end of row {s}.","params":{"n":[5,7],"s":[2,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Why are filament lamps filled with argon instead of air?","choices":["Argon does not react with the hot filament","Argon makes the light brighter","Argon conducts electricity better"],"answer":0,"hint":"What would oxygen in air do to a white-hot metal wire?","explain":"Oxygen would react with the hot filament and burn through it. Argon, 2,8,8, has a full outer shell and does not react."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Which noble gas has only 2 electrons in its outer shell?","choices":["Helium","Neon","Argon"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which noble gas has only one shell?","explain":"Helium's only shell holds 2, so 2 is full. Neon and argon have 8 in their outer shells."}],"explain":{"kid":["Helium, neon and argon are the noble gases. They sit in the last column of the periodic table, Group 0. They almost never react with anything.","The reason is their electrons. Helium has 2, and its only shell holds 2. Neon is 2,8 and argon is 2,8,8. Their outer shells are full, with no gaps.","That makes them safe to use. Balloons are filled with helium, not hydrogen. Hydrogen is lighter, but it burns. Helium will not burn at all.","Other atoms are not full. Sodium is 2,8,1. It can drop its 1 outer electron and look like neon. Chlorine is 2,8,7. It can take 1 more and look like argon. Atoms take the shortest route to a full shell."],"adult":["Group 0 holds the noble gases: helium, neon, argon, krypton, xenon and radon. They exist as single atoms and are very unreactive. Helium's outer shell holds 2 electrons and is full; the others have 8 in their outer shell.","A full outer shell is a stable arrangement, so these atoms have nothing to gain by losing, gaining or sharing electrons. Their uses depend on this. Helium fills balloons because it is light and does not burn. Argon fills filament lamps because the hot metal filament would burn in oxygen but does not react with argon.","Other atoms react in ways that give them a noble-gas arrangement. Metals with 1 to 3 outer electrons lose them: sodium 2,8,1 becomes Na⁺, 2,8, like neon. Non-metals with 5 to 7 gain electrons: chlorine 2,8,7 becomes Cl⁻, 2,8,8, like argon. Atoms halfway, such as carbon 2,4, share pairs of electrons instead, as in methane, CH₄.","Common mistakes: thinking sodium is stable because its inner shell is full, when only the outer shell counts. Saying helium is lighter than hydrogen. Thinking chlorine loses 7 electrons instead of gaining 1. Be careful too: the full-shell rule is a model. Xenon forms a few compounds with fluorine, so 'noble' means very unreactive, not never."]},"familyTip":"Draw an egg box with 8 holes and use dried beans as electrons. Fill it to match an atom, such as 7 beans for chlorine. Ask: is it quicker to add beans or take them away to get a full box? Then try 1 bean for sodium.","keyIdea":"The noble gases in Group 0 (helium, neon, argon and the rest) hardly react, because their outer shell of electrons is already full: 2 for helium, 8 for the others. Other atoms react in ways that give them the same full outer shell. Metals lose electrons, non-metals gain them, and some atoms share them.","worked":{"title":"Why is a balloon filled with helium and not hydrogen, even though hydrogen is lighter?","steps":["Hydrogen has 1 electron. Its only shell holds 2, so it is not full.","So hydrogen reacts: mixed with air and lit, it burns, sometimes with a bang.","Helium has 2 electrons, which fills its only shell. A full outer shell is stable, so helium does not burn or react.","Helium is a little heavier than hydrogen but still far lighter than air, so it floats safely."]},"visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Full outer shell or not? Look at the last number in each arrangement. Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Full outer shell","Not full"],"items":[["He: 2",0],["Ne: 2,8",0],["Ar: 2,8,8",0],["Na: 2,8,1",1],["Cl: 2,8,7",1],["H: 1",1],["Mg: 2,8,2",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagnoble","caption":"Flagship lab: test helium and argon against hydrogen and air, mark the full outer shells, then move electrons to reach a noble-gas arrangement.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_4GNGZwB5AL":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Potassium is in group 1. What is the charge on a potassium ion?","choices":["1+","1−","7−"],"answer":0,"hint":"Group 1 atoms have one outer electron. Is it easier to lose 1 or gain 7?","explain":"Potassium loses its single outer electron. It then has one more proton than electrons, so the ion is K⁺, a 1+ charge."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Why does a sodium ion have a positive charge?","choices":["It lost an electron, so it has more protons than electrons","It gained a proton","It gained an electron"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which particles change when an ion forms: protons or electrons?","explain":"Only electrons move. Sodium loses one, leaving 11 protons and 10 electrons, so one positive charge is left over."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"An ion of an element in group {g} has {=8*r-14+g} protons and {=8*r-14} electrons. What is the size of the positive charge on the ion?","answer":"g","hint":"Charge = protons − electrons. Work out {=8*r-14+g} − {=8*r-14}.","explain":"{=8*r-14+g} − {=8*r-14} = {g}. There are {g} more protons than electrons, so the ion has a charge of {g}+.","params":{"g":[1,3],"r":[2,4]},"where":"!(r==2&&g>1)&&!(r==4&&g==3)"},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"A non-metal atom in group {g}, period {r}, has {=8*r-14+g} protons. How many electrons does its ion have?","answer":"8*r-6","hint":"Start with {=8*r-14+g} electrons, then add the number needed to fill a shell of 8 from {g}.","explain":"It gains 8 − {g} = {=8-g} electrons: {=8*r-14+g} + {=8-g} = {=8*r-6} electrons, a full outer shell.","params":{"g":[5,7],"r":[2,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Aluminium forms Al³⁺ and oxygen forms O²⁻. What is the formula of aluminium oxide?","choices":["Al₂O₃","AlO","Al₃O₂"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find the smallest total positive charge that equals a total negative charge. Try 6.","explain":"Two Al³⁺ give 6+ and three O²⁻ give 6−. They cancel, so the formula is Al₂O₃."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"A sodium ion has the same electron arrangement as neon. Which statement is true?","choices":["It is still sodium, because it has 11 protons","It has turned into neon","It has 11 electrons"],"answer":0,"hint":"What decides which element an atom or ion belongs to?","explain":"The number of protons decides the element. Na⁺ has 11 protons and 10 electrons, while neon has 10 of each."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Magnesium reacts with oxygen to form magnesium oxide. Which way do the electrons move?","choices":["2 electrons move from magnesium to oxygen","2 electrons move from oxygen to magnesium","No electrons move; they stay on each atom"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one is the metal? Metals give electrons away.","explain":"Magnesium, the metal, gives its 2 outer electrons to oxygen. That makes Mg²⁺ and O²⁻, and the charges cancel: MgO."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Oxygen is in group 6. Which ion does it form?","choices":["O²⁻","O⁶⁺","O²⁺"],"answer":0,"hint":"Six outer electrons: is it fewer moves to lose 6 or to gain 2?","explain":"Oxygen gains 2 electrons to fill its outer shell. It then has 2 more electrons than protons, so it is O²⁻."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"A magnesium atom (group 2) forms an ion. What happens?","choices":["It loses 2 electrons and becomes Mg²⁺","It gains 2 electrons and becomes Mg²⁻","It loses 2 electrons and becomes Mg²⁻"],"answer":0,"hint":"Magnesium is a metal. Do metals give electrons away or take them in?","explain":"Magnesium loses its 2 outer electrons. It keeps 12 protons but has only 10 electrons, so it is 2+, Mg²⁺."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A metal in group {g}, period {r}, has {=8*r-14+g} protons. It forms an ion. How many electrons does the ion have?","answer":"8*r-14","hint":"The group number tells you how many outer electrons it loses. Take that away from the {=8*r-14+g} electrons of the atom.","explain":"The atom has {=8*r-14+g} electrons and loses its {g} outer ones: {=8*r-14+g} − {g} = {=8*r-14} electrons. The ion is {g}+.","params":{"g":[1,3],"r":[2,4]},"where":"!(r==2&&g>1)&&!(r==4&&g==3)"},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A non-metal in group {g}, period {r}, has {=8*r-14+g} protons. It forms an ion. What is the size of its negative charge?","answer":"8-g","hint":"A group {g} atom has {g} outer electrons. How many gaps are left in a shell that holds 8?","explain":"It gains 8 − {g} = {=8-g} electrons. That gives {=8-g} more electrons than protons, so the charge is {=8-g}−.","params":{"g":[5,7],"r":[2,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Calcium forms Ca²⁺ and chlorine forms Cl⁻. What is the formula of calcium chloride?","choices":["CaCl₂","CaCl","Ca₂Cl"],"answer":0,"hint":"The charges must add up to zero. How many 1− ions cancel one 2+ ion?","explain":"One Ca²⁺ needs two Cl⁻ to cancel its charge: 2+ and 2 × 1− make 0. So the formula is CaCl₂."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Which is the correct way to draw a chloride ion?","choices":["[Cl]⁻ with 8 electrons in the outer shell","[Cl]⁺ with 8 electrons in the outer shell","Cl⁻ with 7 electrons in the outer shell and no brackets"],"answer":0,"hint":"Chlorine gained one electron. How many are in its outer shell now, and what sign is the charge?","explain":"Chlorine 2,8,7 gains one electron to reach 2,8,8. Draw the 8 outer electrons inside square brackets, with − at the top right."}],"explain":{"kid":["An atom has the same number of protons and electrons. Each proton counts as plus one. Each electron counts as minus one. They cancel out, so the atom has no charge.","If an atom loses or gains electrons, the two numbers stop matching. Now it has a charge. We call it an ion.","Sodium has 11 protons and 11 electrons. It gives away 1 electron. Now there are 11 plus and only 10 minus. One plus is left over, so sodium becomes a plus ion, Na⁺.","Chlorine does the opposite. It takes 1 electron, so it has 18 minus and 17 plus. One minus is left over, so it is Cl⁻. Metals give electrons away. Non-metals take them in."],"adult":["An ion is a charged particle formed when an atom loses or gains electrons. The nucleus does not change, so the number of protons stays the same. The charge is protons minus electrons: fewer electrons than protons gives a positive ion, and more gives a negative ion.","Atoms lose or gain the smallest number of electrons that leaves a full outer shell. Metals in groups 1, 2 and 3 lose 1, 2 or 3 electrons and form 1+, 2+ and 3+ ions, such as Mg²⁺. Non-metals in groups 5, 6 and 7 gain 3, 2 or 1 and form 3−, 2− and 1− ions, such as O²⁻. Carbon and silicon, in group 4, usually share electrons instead.","When a metal reacts with a non-metal, electrons move from the metal atoms to the non-metal atoms. The ions then join in the ratio that makes the charges cancel. Magnesium chloride is MgCl₂ because one Mg²⁺ needs two Cl⁻ ions. To draw an ion, show its outer shell, put square brackets round it and write the charge at the top right, such as [Cl]⁻ with 8 outer electrons.","Common mistakes: giving a metal ion a negative charge because it lost something; writing the number of outer electrons as the charge, such as 6+ for oxygen; and thinking Na⁺ has turned into neon. It has the same electrons as neon, but it still has 11 protons, so it is still sodium."]},"familyTip":"Use red counters for protons and blue counters for electrons. Lay out 12 of each for magnesium, then take 2 blue away. Ask: which colour is left over, and how many? That leftover is the charge, 2+.","keyIdea":"An ion is an atom that has lost or gained electrons, so its protons and electrons no longer balance. Metals lose their outer electrons and become positive ions; non-metals gain electrons and become negative ions. The group number predicts the charge: groups 1, 2 and 3 give 1+, 2+ and 3+; groups 5, 6 and 7 give 3−, 2− and 1−.","worked":{"title":"What ion does a magnesium atom form, and why?","steps":["Magnesium has 12 protons and 12 electrons, arranged 2,8,2. It is in group 2, so it has 2 outer electrons.","Losing 2 electrons leaves 2,8, a full outer shell. Gaining 6 would also fill a shell, but that is far more work.","Now there are 12 protons and 10 electrons. 12 − 10 = 2 extra positive charges.","The ion is Mg²⁺. Draw it as the 2,8 shells inside square brackets, with 2+ at the top right."]},"visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which ions do these elements form? Use the group number. Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Positive ion","Negative ion"],"items":[["Sodium, group 1",0],["Magnesium, group 2",0],["Aluminium, group 3",0],["Potassium, group 1",0],["Oxygen, group 6",1],["Chlorine, group 7",1],["Fluorine, group 7",1],["Sulfur, group 6",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagion","caption":"Flagship lab: move electrons off and onto atoms, watch the charge ledger tip, pass electrons from magnesium to chlorine, then draw the ion.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_BSJTMFUDKP":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What is CH₃CH(OH)CH₃ called?","choices":["Propan-2-ol","Propan-1-ol","Butan-2-ol"],"answer":0,"hint":"How many carbons are there, and is the –OH on an end or in the middle?","explain":"Three carbons give prop-. The –OH is on the middle carbon, which is carbon 2 from either end: propan-2-ol."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which of these names is wrong?","choices":["Butan-3-ol","Butan-2-ol","But-1-ene"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count the same carbon from the other end of a 4-carbon chain.","explain":"Carbon 3 from one end is carbon 2 from the other. Names use the lower number, so it should be butan-2-ol."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"What is CH₃CH₂CH₂COOH called?","choices":["Butanoic acid","Propanoic acid","Butan-4-oic acid"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does the carbon inside COOH count? Do acids need a number?","explain":"Three carbons in the chain plus the COOH carbon make 4, so but-. The COOH carbon is always carbon 1, so no number: butanoic acid."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"What does the ending -ene in but-2-ene tell you?","choices":["The molecule has a C=C double bond","The molecule has an –OH group","The chain has 2 carbons"],"answer":0,"hint":"Endings name the group. Stems and numbers do other jobs.","explain":"-ene means an alkene with a C=C. The but- says 4 carbons and the 2 says the C=C starts at carbon 2."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which formula is pent-2-ene?","choices":["CH₃CH=CHCH₂CH₃","CH₂=CHCH₂CH₂CH₃","CH₃CH=CHCH₃"],"answer":0,"hint":"Pent- means 5 carbons. The C=C starts at carbon 2.","explain":"Five carbons with the C=C between carbons 2 and 3 give CH₃CH=CHCH₂CH₃. The second is pent-1-ene and the third has only 4 carbons."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Sam names CH₃CH₂COOH as ethanoic acid. What slip did he make?","choices":["He did not count the carbon in –COOH","He counted from the wrong end","He used the alcohol ending"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count every C in the formula, including the one in COOH.","explain":"CH₃, CH₂ and the COOH carbon make 3 carbons, so it is propanoic acid. Leaving out the COOH carbon gives the wrong stem."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Why is ethanoic acid never written with a number?","choices":["The –COOH carbon can only be at the end, so it is always carbon 1","Acids have no functional group","Numbers are only used for alkanes"],"answer":0,"hint":"How many bonds does the COOH carbon have left for the chain?","explain":"The COOH carbon uses 3 bonds on oxygen and has 1 left, so it must end the chain. It is always carbon 1, so the number adds nothing."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"In a straight chain of {n} carbons, the C=C joins carbon {k} and carbon {=k+1}, counting from the left. What number goes in the name?","answer":"k*(k<=n-k)+(n-k)*(k>n-k)","hint":"From the right, the C=C starts at a different carbon. Work that out and take the lower one.","explain":"From the left the C=C starts at carbon {k}. From the right it starts at carbon {=n-k}. The name uses {=k*(k<=n-k)+(n-k)*(k>n-k)}.","params":{"n":[6,8],"k":[1,5]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"An –OH is on carbon {k} counting from the left of a chain of {n} carbons. Which carbon is it, counting from the right?","answer":"n+1-k","hint":"Try a short chain first: on 4 carbons, carbon 1 from the left is carbon 4 from the right.","explain":"The two counts always add up to one more than the number of carbons, so it is {n} + 1 − {k} = {=n+1-k}.","params":{"n":[6,8],"k":[1,5]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"What does the stem prop- tell you about a compound?","choices":["Its longest carbon chain has 3 carbons","It is an alcohol","It has 3 hydrogen atoms"],"answer":0,"hint":"The stem comes first in the name. Which thing does the first part count?","explain":"Meth, eth, prop and but stand for 1, 2, 3 and 4 carbons. The group is shown by the ending, not the stem."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A straight chain of {n} carbons has an –OH on carbon {k}, counting from the left. What number goes in its name?","answer":"k*(k<=n+1-k)+(n+1-k)*(k>n+1-k)","hint":"Count the same carbon from the right as well, then pick the lower number.","explain":"From the left it is carbon {k}; from the right it is carbon {=n+1-k}. The name uses the lower one, {=k*(k<=n+1-k)+(n+1-k)*(k>n+1-k)}.","params":{"n":[6,8],"k":[1,4]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A carboxylic acid is CH₃, then {k} CH₂ groups, then COOH, in a line. How many carbons does its stem count?","answer":"k+2","hint":"Count CH₃, every CH₂, and the carbon inside COOH.","explain":"CH₃ gives 1, the CH₂ groups give {k} and COOH gives 1 more, so the stem counts {=k+2} carbons.","params":{"k":[0,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"What is the name of CH₃CH₂CH₂OH?","choices":["Propan-1-ol","Propan-3-ol","Butan-1-ol"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count the carbons, then number from the end that has the –OH.","explain":"There are 3 carbons, so prop-. The –OH is on an end carbon, so counting from that end it is carbon 1: propan-1-ol."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Which formula is but-1-ene?","choices":["CH₂=CHCH₂CH₃","CH₃CH=CHCH₃","CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₃"],"answer":0,"hint":"The 1 says where the C=C starts. Ene says there must be a C=C.","explain":"But- means 4 carbons and -1-ene puts the C=C between carbons 1 and 2. CH₃CH=CHCH₃ is but-2-ene, and the last one is butane with no C=C."}],"explain":{"kid":["A chemical name is a code. You can read it in three parts. The start tells you how many carbons are in the chain. The number tells you where something is. The end tells you which group the molecule has.","The starts are meth for 1 carbon, eth for 2, prop for 3 and but for 4. Then they count like Greek numbers: pent is 5 and hex is 6. So methane has one carbon and butane has four.","The end shows the group. Ane means only single bonds. Ene means there is a C=C. Ol means there is an OH. Oic acid means there is a COOH. So ethanol has two carbons and an OH.","The number says where the group sits. Count from the end that is nearer to the group. Propan-2-ol has its OH on the middle carbon. If you count from the far end, the number comes out too big."],"adult":["A systematic name has a stem, sometimes a position number, and an ending. The stem gives the number of carbons in the longest chain: meth-, eth-, prop-, but-, pent- and hex- for 1 to 6. The ending gives the functional group: -ane for an alkane, -ene for C=C, -ol for –OH and -oic acid for –COOH. Alcohols and acids keep the \"an\" of the alkane, so we say ethanol and ethanoic acid.","The number tells you where the group is. Number the chain from the end that gives the group the lowest number. Butane with –OH on an end carbon is butan-1-ol. With –OH on an inner carbon it is butan-2-ol, whichever end you start from. For an alkene the number is the first carbon of the C=C: CH₂=CHCH₂CH₃ is but-1-ene and CH₃CH=CHCH₃ is but-2-ene.","Some names need no number because only one place is possible, as in ethanol and propene. The –COOH carbon has only one bond left for the chain. So it is always carbon 1, at the end, and acids such as propanoic acid never carry a number. To draw from a name, read it part by part. In propan-2-ol, prop means three carbons, 2 means carbon 2 and ol means –OH. So the structure is CH₃CH(OH)CH₃.","Common mistakes: counting from the wrong end and writing butan-3-ol. Leaving out the carbon in –COOH, so CH₃CH₂COOH gets called ethanoic acid when it is propanoic acid. Dropping the \"an\", as in \"ethol\". Reading the number as a count, so but-2-ene is taken to have two double bonds."]},"familyTip":"Write stems on one set of cards (meth, eth, prop, but) and endings on another (ane, ene, anol, anoic acid). Pick one of each, say the name, then draw the chain with that many carbons and that group. Swap roles: one person draws, the other names it.","keyIdea":"An organic name is read in three parts. The stem counts the carbons in the longest chain: meth-, eth-, prop-, but-. The ending names the functional group: -ane, -ene, -ol or -oic acid. A number, counted from the end nearer the group, says which carbon it is on, as in propan-2-ol and but-1-ene.","worked":{"title":"Name CH₃CH₂CH(OH)CH₃.","steps":["Count the carbons in the chain: CH₃, CH₂, CH and CH₃ make 4. The stem is but-.","The group is –OH, so the ending is -ol, with the \"an\" kept: butan…ol.","Number from the left and the –OH is on carbon 3. Number from the right and it is on carbon 2. Use the lower number.","The name is butan-2-ol."]},"visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Does the name need a number? Drag each compound to a box, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Number needed","No number needed"],"items":[["Propan-2-ol, CH₃CH(OH)CH₃",0],["But-2-ene, CH₃CH=CHCH₃",0],["Butan-1-ol, CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂OH",0],["Ethanol, CH₃CH₂OH",1],["Propene, CH₂=CHCH₃",1],["Propanoic acid, CH₃CH₂COOH",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagname","caption":"Flagship lab: grow a carbon chain, place a group by tapping or dragging, and watch the name build itself from stem, number and ending.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_HHDQEddw9N":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What is the formula of butanol?","choices":["C₄H₉OH","C₄H₁₀OH","C₄H₈OH"],"answer":0,"hint":"Use CₙH₂ₙ₊₁OH with n = 4.","explain":"2 × 4 + 1 = 9 H on the chain, then the –OH: C₄H₉OH. C₄H₁₀OH keeps all of butane's H, which would give one carbon five bonds."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Ethanol contains –OH. Why is its solution not alkaline?","choices":["The –OH is covalently bonded to carbon and does not form OH⁻ ions","Ethanol has no hydrogen atoms","The –OH falls off as water"],"answer":0,"hint":"What makes sodium hydroxide alkaline? Does ethanol do the same?","explain":"Alkalis release OH⁻ ions in water. The O–H in ethanol stays bonded to its carbon, so the solution stays at pH 7."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Ethanol reacts with an oxidising agent. What is made?","choices":["Ethanoic acid","Ethene","Ethane"],"answer":0,"hint":"Oxidation adds oxygen to the –CH₂OH end of the molecule.","explain":"The –CH₂OH end becomes –COOH, giving ethanoic acid, the acid in vinegar. Ethene and ethane have no oxygen at all."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which equation shows ethanol burning completely?","choices":["C₂H₅OH + 3O₂ → 2CO₂ + 3H₂O","C₂H₅OH + 3½O₂ → 2CO₂ + 3H₂O","C₂H₅OH + 3O₂ → 2CO₂ + 3H₂"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count the O on each side, including the one inside ethanol.","explain":"Right side: 4 + 3 = 7 O. Left side: 1 in ethanol + 6 in 3O₂ = 7. The 3½ version forgets ethanol's own O, and the H atoms end up in water, not H₂."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which is a main use of ethanol?","choices":["A fuel and a solvent, for example in hand sanitiser","Neutralising acid spills","The monomer for making poly(ethene)"],"answer":0,"hint":"Alcohols burn cleanly and dissolve many substances.","explain":"Ethanol burns as a fuel and dissolves oils and scents, so it is used as a solvent. It is neutral, so it cannot neutralise acids, and the monomer for poly(ethene) is ethene."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Sodium is added to ethanol and to water. How do they compare?","choices":["Both give hydrogen; ethanol reacts more gently","Only water reacts","Ethanol reacts more violently"],"answer":0,"hint":"Both have an O–H. Which one fizzes faster?","explain":"Sodium replaces the H on O–H in both, giving hydrogen. The reaction with ethanol is slower and gentler than with water."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"How is ethanol made by fermentation?","choices":["Yeast turns sugar solution into ethanol and carbon dioxide","Ethene is burned in air","Ethanoic acid is heated with sodium"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of bread and brewing. What living thing is used?","explain":"Enzymes in yeast turn sugar into ethanol and carbon dioxide in warm conditions without air. Burning ethene gives only carbon dioxide and water."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"{k} molecules of butanol, C₄H₉OH, burn completely. How many H₂O molecules form?","answer":"5*k","hint":"One butanol has 10 H atoms. Each H₂O takes 2.","explain":"Each C₄H₉OH has 9 + 1 = 10 H, which make 5 H₂O. So {k} molecules give 5 × {k} = {=5*k} H₂O.","params":{"k":[1,6]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"Two molecules of an alcohol with {n} carbons burn completely. How many O₂ molecules are needed?","answer":"3*n","hint":"Products: 2 × {n} CO₂ and 2 × ({n} + 1) H₂O. Take off the 2 O the alcohols bring, then halve.","explain":"Products hold 4 × {n} + 2 × ({n} + 1) = {=6*n+2} O. The alcohols bring 2, so O₂ gives {=6*n} O, which is {=3*n} O₂.","params":{"n":[1,6]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which of these is an alcohol?","choices":["CH₃CH₂CH₂OH","CH₃CH₂COOH","CH₃CH₂CH₃"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for an O–H on a carbon that has no C=O.","explain":"CH₃CH₂CH₂OH is propanol. CH₃CH₂COOH has –COOH, so it is a carboxylic acid, and CH₃CH₂CH₃ is propane with no group."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"An alcohol has {n} carbon atoms. How many H atoms are on its carbon chain, not counting the H in –OH?","answer":"2*n+1","hint":"Alcohols fit CₙH₂ₙ₊₁OH. Read the H number before the OH.","explain":"The chain holds 2 × {n} + 1 = {=2*n+1} H, one fewer than the alkane, because the –OH takes one place.","params":{"n":[1,8]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"How many O₂ molecules are needed to burn {k} molecules of ethanol completely?","answer":"3*k","hint":"One C₂H₅OH needs 3O₂: the products hold 7 O and ethanol brings 1.","explain":"Each ethanol needs 3O₂, so {k} molecules need 3 × {k} = {=3*k} O₂.","params":{"k":[1,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Ethanol is mixed with water and universal indicator is added. What does it show?","choices":["Green, pH 7","Purple, pH 13","Red, pH 2"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does the –OH in ethanol come off as an OH⁻ ion?","explain":"The O–H in ethanol is a covalent bond, so no OH⁻ ions form. The solution is neutral, pH 7."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Which gas is given off when sodium is added to ethanol?","choices":["Hydrogen","Carbon dioxide","Oxygen"],"answer":0,"hint":"Sodium swaps places with one atom of the alcohol. Which one?","explain":"Sodium replaces the H on the O–H, and those H atoms pair up as hydrogen gas. Carbon dioxide comes from acids with carbonates."}],"explain":{"kid":["Alcohols are a family of carbon compounds. Each one has a chain of carbons with an OH group on the end. The first four are methanol, ethanol, propanol and butanol. Ethanol is the one in drinks and in hand gel.","To write the formula, count the carbons. The chain holds twice as many H as carbons, plus one. Then add the OH. So ethanol is C₂H₅OH and butanol is C₄H₉OH.","Alcohols mix with water. The mix is neutral, not alkaline. The OH is stuck to a carbon, so it does not float off like the OH in sodium hydroxide.","Alcohols burn well, so they make good fuels. They burn to make carbon dioxide and water. A piece of sodium fizzes in an alcohol and gives off hydrogen. Some chemicals turn an alcohol into an acid, like ethanol into vinegar."],"adult":["Alcohols are the homologous series with the –OH functional group. The first four are methanol CH₃OH, ethanol C₂H₅OH, propanol C₃H₇OH and butanol C₄H₉OH, which fit CₙH₂ₙ₊₁OH. The –OH takes the place of one H of the alkane, so the chain keeps 2n + 1 H.","Methanol, ethanol and propanol mix fully with water, and butanol mixes partly. The solutions are neutral, pH 7, because the O–H is a covalent bond and no OH⁻ ions are released. With sodium, alcohols fizz more gently than water does. The H on the O is replaced by Na and hydrogen is given off: 2C₂H₅OH + 2Na → 2C₂H₅ONa + H₂.","Alcohols burn in air to carbon dioxide and water, so they are used as fuels. Ethanol is also a solvent in perfumes, sanitisers and inks. When you balance a burning equation, count the alcohol's own O: C₂H₅OH + 3O₂ → 2CO₂ + 3H₂O. An oxidising agent turns an alcohol into the carboxylic acid with the same number of carbons, so ethanol gives ethanoic acid. Ethanol for drinks is made by fermenting sugar with yeast.","Common mistakes: writing C₄H₁₀OH by keeping all of butane's H. Thinking the –OH makes alcohols alkaline. Forgetting the O already in the alcohol and writing 3½O₂ for ethanol. Saying sodium gives off carbon dioxide. Mixing up burning, which goes all the way to CO₂ and H₂O, with oxidation to an acid."]},"familyTip":"Read the labels on hand gel, screen wipes and perfume at home. Find the alcohol named on each (ethanol, propan-2-ol or 'alcohol denat.'), then say what job it does there: killing germs, dissolving oils, or carrying the scent and then evaporating.","keyIdea":"Alcohols are the family with an –OH group on a carbon chain: methanol, ethanol, propanol and butanol, CₙH₂ₙ₊₁OH. Their solutions in water are neutral. They fizz gently with sodium to give hydrogen, burn to carbon dioxide and water, and are oxidised to carboxylic acids. That makes them useful as fuels and solvents.","worked":{"title":"Balance the complete burning of propanol, C₃H₇OH.","steps":["Products: 3 carbons give 3CO₂. 8 hydrogens give 4H₂O.","O needed on the right: 3 × 2 + 4 = 10. The propanol brings 1 O, so O₂ must supply 9 O.","9 O is 4½ O₂. Double everything to clear the half.","2C₃H₇OH + 9O₂ → 6CO₂ + 8H₂O. Check O: 2 + 18 = 20 on the left, 12 + 8 = 20 on the right."]},"visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"True of alcohols or not? Drag each statement to a box, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["True of alcohols","Not true of alcohols"],"items":[["Their solution in water has pH 7",0],["They give off hydrogen with sodium",0],["They burn to carbon dioxide and water",0],["They turn universal indicator purple",1],["They fizz with carbonates to give carbon dioxide",1],["They turn bromine water colourless",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagalc","caption":"Flagship lab: build methanol to butanol, test alcohols with indicator, sodium and an oxidiser, then balance their burning by dragging in O₂.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_up0p5UoLl4":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Liquid X turns bromine water from orange to colourless. What does this show?","choices":["X contains a C=C bond, so it is unsaturated","X is saturated","X must be ethene"],"answer":0,"hint":"What does bromine need in order to add on?","explain":"Decolourising bromine water shows a C=C is present: X is unsaturated. Every alkene gives this result, so it cannot say which one X is."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Liquid Y is C₅H₁₀ but leaves bromine water orange. What is Y most likely to be?","choices":["Cyclopentane, a ring with no C=C","Pentene, because it fits CₙH₂ₙ","Pentane, because it has five carbons"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which CₙH₂ₙ compounds have no double bond?","explain":"C₅H₁₀ fits both pentene and cyclopentane. Staying orange rules out a C=C, so Y is the ring. Pentane is C₅H₁₂, so it does not fit."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"Equal amounts are tested. A diene, with two C=C per molecule, uses up {=2*a} drops of bromine water. How many drops does an alkene with one C=C per molecule use up?","answer":"a","hint":"Half as many C=C bonds use up half as much bromine.","explain":"{=2*a} ÷ 2 = {a} drops. Each C=C takes the same amount of bromine.","params":{"a":[3,7]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"A sample of cyclohexene can use up {c} drops of bromine water. {b} drops are added and the tube is shaken. How many drops' worth of bromine are left to colour the tube orange?","answer":"b-c","hint":"The first {c} drops are used up by the C=C bonds. What is left over?","explain":"{b} − {c} = {=b-c} drops' worth is left over, so the tube stays orange. The C=C bonds have all reacted.","params":{"b":[6,9],"c":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Why does bromine water lose its colour when shaken with an alkene?","choices":["Bromine adds across the C=C and is used up","The alkene dilutes the colour until you cannot see it","Bromine swaps places with hydrogen atoms, as it does with any hydrocarbon"],"answer":0,"hint":"Hexane added the same way leaves the colour. What is different about an alkene?","explain":"The C=C opens and bromine bonds to its carbons. The orange bromine is used up. Dilution cannot be the reason, because hexane stays orange."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which of these will decolourise bromine water?","choices":["Propene, CH₂=CHCH₃","Propane, CH₃CH₂CH₃","Cyclopropane, a ring of three carbons with single bonds"],"answer":0,"hint":"Check each formula for a double bond between two carbons.","explain":"Only propene has a C=C, so only propene decolourises bromine water. Propane and cyclopropane have single bonds only."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Equal amounts of sunflower oil, olive oil and butter are tested. They use up 5, 3 and 1 drops of bromine water. Which is the most unsaturated?","choices":["Sunflower oil","Butter","They are all equally unsaturated"],"answer":0,"hint":"More drops used up means more C=C bonds.","explain":"Sunflower oil used up the most bromine, so it has the most C=C bonds. Butter has the fewest, so it is the most saturated of the three."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Hexane and bromine water are left on a sunny windowsill for hours, and the colour slowly fades. Does this show hexane is an alkene?","choices":["No: in strong light alkanes slowly swap H for Br, a different reaction from the quick alkene test","Yes: any colour loss means a C=C","Yes: hexane is an alkene"],"answer":0,"hint":"How fast does an alkene decolourise bromine water, and does it need light?","explain":"Alkenes decolourise bromine water in seconds, light or not. The slow fading with hexane is substitution caused by light. That is why the test is done quickly."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"Equal amounts are tested. Hex-1-ene uses up {a} drops of bromine water before the orange stays. A compound with {n} C=C bonds in each molecule is tested the same way. How many drops does it use up?","answer":"a*n","hint":"Each C=C uses up the same amount of bromine. {n} C=C per molecule means {n} times as much.","explain":"One C=C per molecule used {a} drops, so {n} per molecule use {n} × {a} = {=a*n} drops.","params":{"a":[3,6],"n":[2,3]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Equal amounts of two oils are tested. Oil P uses up {p} drops of bromine water. Oil Q uses up {=p*k} drops. How many times as many C=C bonds does Q have?","answer":"k","hint":"Drops used up are in proportion to the number of C=C bonds. Divide {=p*k} by {p}.","explain":"{=p*k} ÷ {p} = {k}, so oil Q has {k} times as many C=C bonds. It is the more unsaturated oil.","params":{"p":[2,5],"k":[2,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"How should you describe a positive result for the bromine water test?","choices":["Orange to colourless","Orange to clear","Colourless to orange"],"answer":0,"hint":"Bromine water is already see-through. What happens to its colour?","explain":"The orange colour disappears, so the change is orange to colourless. Clear means see-through, which it was all along."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Cyclohexane, C₆H₁₂, has the same formula as hex-1-ene. What happens when it is shaken with bromine water?","choices":["It stays orange, because its ring has only single bonds","It turns colourless, because it is C₆H₁₂","It turns colourless slowly, because it is a ring"],"answer":0,"hint":"The test looks for a C=C bond, not a formula.","explain":"Cyclohexane is saturated: six carbons joined in a ring by single bonds. With no C=C, the bromine has nothing to add to and stays orange."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Why test hexane next to the unknown liquid?","choices":["As a control: it shows that a liquid with no C=C leaves the colour orange","To make the bromine water more orange","Because hexane reacts faster than alkenes"],"answer":0,"hint":"What would you compare the unknown's result with?","explain":"Hexane has no C=C, so it stays orange. If the unknown turns colourless and the hexane does not, the change is down to a C=C."}],"explain":{"kid":["Bromine water is a see-through orange liquid. Add a few drops to a test tube of something, put a bung in and shake.","If the orange goes away and the tube looks colourless, there is a carbon to carbon double bond inside. The bromine joins on at the double bond, so it is used up.","If the tube stays orange, there is no double bond. Hexane stays orange. So does cyclohexane, even though its formula is the same as hexene's.","Keep adding drops and the colour keeps going, until all the double bonds have been used. Something with more double bonds can take more drops."],"adult":["Bromine water is a weak orange solution of bromine. Shake it with an alkene at room temperature and it turns from orange to colourless in seconds. The C=C opens and a bromine atom bonds to one of its carbons. With pure bromine the product is a dibromoalkane, such as 1,2-dibromoethane. In bromine water, water joins in too, and the main product is a bromoalcohol. Either way, the bromine is used up and the colour goes.","An alkane has only single bonds, so nothing can add on and the bromine water stays orange. Test a known alkane next to the unknown as a control: then you know the colour loss came from a C=C and not from mixing.","Because each C=C reacts with one Br₂, the test can compare unsaturation. Equal amounts of hexa-1,5-diene (two C=C per molecule) decolourise twice as much bromine water as hex-1-ene. Vegetable oils such as sunflower oil decolourise more than butter, so they are more unsaturated.","Common mistakes: writing 'orange to clear' (clear means see-through, which bromine water already is; say colourless); swapping the two results; deciding a CₙH₂ₙ compound must be an alkene, when cyclohexane has that formula and no C=C; thinking the test tells you which alkene it is; and saying alkanes react the same way. In bright sunlight alkanes do react, very slowly, by swapping H for Br. That is a different reaction."]},"familyTip":"Find the nutrition labels on a bottle of sunflower oil and a pack of butter. Compare the 'saturates' line with the total fat. Which one has more unsaturated fat, the kind with C=C bonds? That is the one that would decolourise more bromine water.","keyIdea":"Shake a few drops of orange bromine water with a liquid. If the orange turns colourless, the liquid has a C=C bond: bromine adds across it and is used up, so the compound is unsaturated. If it stays orange, there is no C=C. Each C=C uses up the same amount of bromine, so more bromine decolourised means more C=C.","worked":{"title":"Three colourless liquids are hexane, hex-1-ene and cyclohexane. Plan a test to find the alkene.","steps":["Put the same small amount of each liquid in its own test tube.","Add a few drops of bromine water to each, put in a bung and shake.","Hex-1-ene turns the bromine water from orange to colourless: it has a C=C.","Hexane and cyclohexane both stay orange. Cyclohexane is C₆H₁₂ like hex-1-ene, but its six carbons form a ring of single bonds, so it is saturated."]},"visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"What does each one do to bromine water? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Turns it colourless","Leaves it orange"],"items":[["Ethene, C₂H₄",0],["Cyclohexene, a ring with one C=C",0],["Sunflower oil",0],["Hexane, C₆H₁₄",1],["Cyclohexane, C₆H₁₂",1],["Butane, C₄H₁₀",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagbrw","caption":"Flagship lab: drop bromine water into tubes and shake: hexene against hexane and cyclohexane, then count drops for a diene and three oils.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_Nq3jAjCB-M":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"A student draws butane bent into an L and says it is a new isomer. Is she right?","choices":["Yes, the shape is different","No: the atoms bond in the same order, so it is still butane","Yes, it is 1-methylpropane"],"answer":1,"hint":"Follow the bonds from one end. Is each carbon joined to the same carbons as before?","explain":"The longest chain is still four carbons in a row. Chains twist freely, so it is butane. 1-methylpropane is not a real name: it is butane."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Ethanol and methoxymethane are both C₂H₆O. What kind of isomers are they?","choices":["chain","position","functional group"],"answer":2,"hint":"Ethanol has C–O–H. Where is the oxygen in methoxymethane?","explain":"Methoxymethane is C–O–C, an ether. Ethanol is an alcohol. Different functional groups make them functional group isomers."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"What is the correct name for CH₃CH₂CH(CH₃)CH₃?","choices":["3-methylbutane","2-methylbutane","pentane"],"answer":1,"hint":"Find the longest chain, then number from the end nearer the branch.","explain":"The longest chain has four carbons with a methyl on carbon 2 from the right-hand end: 2-methylbutane. 3-methylbutane numbers from the wrong end."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"An alcohol has {n} carbon atoms, one oxygen atom and only single bonds. How many hydrogen atoms does it have?","answer":"2*n+2","hint":"Alcohols are CₙH₂ₙ₊₁OH. Add the H in the –OH.","explain":"2 × {n} + 1 + 1 = {=2*n+2}. Its ether isomers have the same number.","params":{"n":[2,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which has the lowest boiling point?","choices":["pentane","2-methylbutane","2,2-dimethylpropane"],"answer":2,"hint":"More branching gives a more compact molecule with less surface contact.","explain":"2,2-dimethylpropane boils at about 10 °C, 2-methylbutane at 28 °C and pentane at 36 °C. All three are C₅H₁₂."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Why does ethanol boil at 78 °C but methoxymethane at −24 °C?","choices":["Ethanol has more atoms","Ethanol's O–H lets its molecules hydrogen bond to each other","Methoxymethane has a smaller molecular formula"],"answer":1,"hint":"They have exactly the same atoms. What bond does only ethanol have?","explain":"Both are C₂H₆O. Only ethanol has an O–H, so its molecules form hydrogen bonds, which take more energy to break."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Are CH₃CH₂CH₂OH and HOCH₂CH₂CH₃ isomers?","choices":["Yes, position isomers","No, they are the same compound written from the other end","No, they have different formulas"],"answer":1,"hint":"Read the second one backwards.","explain":"Both are propan-1-ol: the –OH is on an end carbon. Writing a formula backwards does not make a new compound."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Are butane, C₄H₁₀, and pentane, C₅H₁₂, isomers?","choices":["Yes, chain isomers","Yes, because both are alkanes","No: their molecular formulas are different"],"answer":2,"hint":"Isomers must share one molecular formula. Count the carbon and hydrogen atoms in each.","explain":"Isomers must have the same molecular formula. These differ by CH₂, so they are neighbours in the alkane series."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"How many structural isomers does C₆H₁₄ have?","answer":5,"tolerance":0.001,"hint":"Straight chain; one methyl on carbon 2 or 3; two methyls on one carbon or on neighbours.","explain":"Hexane, 2-methylpentane, 3-methylpentane, 2,2-dimethylbutane and 2,3-dimethylbutane: 5."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m11","prompt":"A straight chain of {n} carbon atoms has an –OH on carbon {=n-1}, counted from the left. What locant number belongs in its correct name?","answer":"2","hint":"Names number from the end that gives the –OH the lower number.","explain":"Counted from the right, carbon {=n-1} is carbon 2, which is lower. So the name has -2-ol, not -{=n-1}-ol.","params":{"n":[5,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m10","prompt":"Which of these is NOT an isomer of C₄H₁₀O?","choices":["butan-2-ol","propan-1-ol","ethoxyethane"],"answer":1,"hint":"Count the carbon atoms in each name.","explain":"Propan-1-ol is C₃H₈O, with one carbon fewer. Butan-2-ol and ethoxyethane both have 4 C, 10 H and 1 O."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"An alkane has {n} carbon atoms. How many hydrogen atoms does each of its isomers have?","answer":"2*n+2","hint":"Isomers share one molecular formula. Alkanes are CₙH₂ₙ₊₂.","explain":"Every isomer has 2 × {n} + 2 = {=2*n+2} hydrogen atoms. Branching moves atoms; it never adds or removes any.","params":{"n":[4,10]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Which pair are structural isomers?","choices":["butane and pentane","butane and 2-methylpropane","ethanol and ethane"],"answer":1,"hint":"Write the molecular formula of each. Isomers need the same one.","explain":"Butane and 2-methylpropane are both C₄H₁₀ with different skeletons. Butane and pentane differ by CH₂, and ethane has no oxygen."},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"What type of isomers are propan-1-ol and propan-2-ol?","choices":["chain isomers","position isomers","functional group isomers"],"answer":1,"hint":"Is the carbon chain different, or only where the –OH is?","explain":"Both have a straight three-carbon chain and an –OH. Only the carbon carrying the –OH changes, so they are position isomers."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"How many structural isomers does C₅H₁₂ have?","choices":["2","3","5"],"answer":1,"hint":"Try a straight chain, one branch, then two branches on one carbon.","explain":"Pentane, 2-methylbutane and 2,2-dimethylpropane. Other drawings are these bent, flipped or numbered from the wrong end."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"A chain of {n} carbon atoms has an –OH on carbon 2. Counted from the other end, which carbon is the –OH on?","answer":"n-1","hint":"Number the chain backwards: the last carbon becomes carbon 1.","explain":"From the other end it is carbon {=n-1}. Names use the end that gives the lower number, so the –OH is on carbon 2 either way.","params":{"n":[4,8]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Isomers are like words made from the same letters. Stop and pots use the same four letters in a new order, so they are different words.","Butane has 4 carbon atoms and 10 hydrogen atoms. Its carbons sit in a row. Move one carbon so it joins the middle of the row. You still have 4 carbons and 10 hydrogens, but now it is a new compound called 2-methylpropane.","Bending a chain does not count. A chain can twist like a piece of string. If each atom is still joined to the same atoms as before, it is the same thing.","Isomers can act in different ways. Butane boils at about −1 °C. 2-methylpropane boils at about −12 °C. The branched one is more compact, so its molecules touch less and pull on each other less."],"adult":["Structural isomers have the same molecular formula, but their atoms are joined in a different order. Count both formulas first. If they differ, the two are not isomers, however alike they look. Butane and pentane differ by CH₂: they are members of one homologous series, not isomers.","There are three kinds. Chain isomers have different carbon chains. Pentane, 2-methylbutane and 2,2-dimethylpropane are the three isomers of C₅H₁₂. Position isomers keep the chain and the group but move the group, as in propan-1-ol and propan-2-ol. Functional group isomers have different groups: ethanol, an alcohol, and methoxymethane, an ether, are both C₂H₆O.","Isomers have different properties. Branching makes a molecule more compact. Compact molecules touch less, so the forces between them are weaker and the boiling point falls: pentane 36 °C, 2-methylbutane 28 °C, 2,2-dimethylpropane 10 °C. Ethanol boils at 78 °C but methoxymethane at −24 °C. The O–H in ethanol lets its molecules form hydrogen bonds.","Watch for common mistakes. A bent or flipped drawing is not a new isomer. 2-methylbutane is not 3-methylbutane: number from the end nearer the branch. 1-methylpropane is just butane drawn oddly. Propan-3-ol is propan-1-ol counted from the wrong end. Molecules with different formulas are never isomers."]},"familyTip":"Use four coins as carbon atoms. Lay them in a row, then move one to touch the middle coin. Ask: is it a new shape, or just the row bent? Only a coin joined to a different coin makes a new isomer.","keyIdea":"Structural isomers have the same molecular formula but their atoms are bonded in a different order. Chain isomers have different carbon skeletons (butane and 2-methylpropane, both C₄H₁₀). Position isomers have the same group on a different carbon (propan-1-ol and propan-2-ol). Functional group isomers have different groups (ethanol and methoxymethane, both C₂H₆O). Bending or flipping a drawing does not make a new isomer.","worked":{"title":"Draw and name every alcohol with the formula C₄H₁₀O.","steps":["Start with the straight four-carbon chain. –OH on carbon 1 gives butan-1-ol. –OH on carbon 2 gives butan-2-ol. Carbon 3 is carbon 2 counted from the other end, so it adds nothing new.","Now use the branched skeleton, 2-methylpropane. –OH on an end carbon gives 2-methylpropan-1-ol. All three end carbons are the same, so that is one isomer.","–OH on the central carbon gives 2-methylpropan-2-ol.","So there are 4 alcohols. With the 3 ethers (1-methoxypropane, 2-methoxypropane and ethoxyethane), C₄H₁₀O has 7 structural isomers. Check each has 4 C, 10 H and 1 O."]},"visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which formula is each compound? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["C₄H₁₀","C₅H₁₂","C₃H₈O"],"items":[["butane",0],["2-methylpropane",0],["pentane",1],["2,2-dimethylpropane",1],["propan-2-ol",2],["methoxyethane",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagiso","caption":"Flagship lab: move end atoms on a peg board and watch the name, formula and boiling point update, from butane to ethers.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_fTGqpD1Y9y":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Lemon juice is pH 2 and vinegar is pH 3. How many times more H⁺ ions does the lemon juice have?","choices":["1 time more","2 times","10 times"],"answer":2,"hint":"One step on the pH scale is a factor of ten.","explain":"pH 2 is one step below pH 3, so the H⁺ concentration is 10 times higher."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which ion makes potassium hydroxide solution alkaline?","choices":["K⁺","OH⁻","H⁺"],"answer":1,"hint":"Alkalis are sources of one particular ion in water.","explain":"KOH dissolves into K⁺ and OH⁻. The OH⁻ ions make it alkaline; K⁺ is a spectator when it neutralises an acid."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Hydrochloric acid neutralises sodium hydroxide. Which ions are spectators?","choices":["H⁺ and OH⁻","H⁺ and Cl⁻","Na⁺ and Cl⁻"],"answer":2,"hint":"Spectators are dissolved at the start and still dissolved, unchanged, at the end.","explain":"H⁺ and OH⁻ join to make water. Na⁺ and Cl⁻ stay in solution as sodium chloride."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"You keep adding water to a pH 3 acid. Which pH can it never reach?","choices":["pH 4","pH 6","pH 8"],"answer":2,"hint":"Water spreads the H⁺ out. Does it add any OH⁻ beyond its own?","explain":"Dilution moves the pH towards 7 but never past it. To reach pH 8 you need an alkali to remove H⁺ and add OH⁻."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"What does it mean to call an acid strong?","choices":["There is a lot of it in each dm³","It ionises completely in water","Its pH is always 1"],"answer":1,"hint":"Strong and weak are about splitting into ions; concentrated and dilute are about amount.","explain":"A strong acid gives up all its H⁺ in water. A dilute strong acid can still have a pH of 4 or 5."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"Each sulfuric acid unit, H₂SO₄, can release 2 H⁺ ions. How many OH⁻ ions are needed to neutralise {n} units completely?","answer":"2*n","hint":"Count the H⁺ first. Each one needs one OH⁻.","explain":"{n} units give 2 × {n} = {=2*n} H⁺, so {=2*n} OH⁻ are needed to make {=2*n} water molecules.","params":{"n":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"At 0.1 mol/dm³, why is ethanoic acid about pH 2.9 but hydrochloric acid pH 1?","choices":["Ethanoic acid only partly ionises, so fewer H⁺ are free","Ethanoic acid is more dilute","Ethanoic acid contains no hydrogen"],"answer":0,"hint":"The two have the same concentration. What differs is how many molecules split.","explain":"Ethanoic acid is weak: only about 1 molecule in 100 releases its H⁺. Hydrochloric acid is strong and releases all of it."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"How many times more H⁺ does a pH 1 solution have than a pH 4 solution?","choices":["3","30","1000"],"answer":2,"hint":"Three steps down the scale. Multiply by 10 for each step.","explain":"10 × 10 × 10 = 1000. Subtracting the pH numbers gives the number of steps, not the ratio."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"7 H⁺ ions meet 9 OH⁻ ions. After they react, the solution is…","choices":["acidic","neutral","alkaline"],"answer":2,"hint":"Pair them one to one. Which ion has spares?","explain":"7 pairs make 7 water molecules and 2 OH⁻ are left over, so the solution is alkaline."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m10","prompt":"{h} H⁺ ions are mixed with {o} OH⁻ ions. How many water molecules form?","answer":"o","hint":"Each water molecule needs one H⁺ and one OH⁻. Which ion runs out first?","explain":"The {o} OH⁻ ions run out first, so {o} water molecules form and {=h-o} H⁺ are left over.","params":{"h":[8,12],"o":[2,7]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"A beaker has {h} H⁺ ions and {o} OH⁻ ions. After they react, how many H⁺ ions are left?","answer":"h-o","hint":"Each OH⁻ removes exactly one H⁺ by making one water molecule.","explain":"{o} OH⁻ remove {o} H⁺, leaving {h} − {o} = {=h-o} H⁺. The solution is still acidic.","params":{"h":[6,12],"o":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Which is the ionic equation for the reaction of any acid with any alkali?","choices":["Na⁺ + Cl⁻ → NaCl","H⁺ + OH⁻ → H₂O","HCl + NaOH → NaCl + H₂O"],"answer":1,"hint":"Leave out the ions that are still dissolved and unchanged at the end.","explain":"Only H⁺ and OH⁻ change. The third equation is the full equation for one pair; the ionic one works for all acids and alkalis."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A strong acid of pH {p} is diluted with water until its volume is {=10**k} times bigger. What is its new pH?","answer":"p+k","hint":"Each tenfold dilution raises the pH by one. Count the tens in {=10**k}.","explain":"{=10**k} is 10 multiplied {k} times, so the pH rises by {k}, from {p} to {=p+k}. It is still an acid: water alone never passes pH 7.","params":{"p":[1,3],"k":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Hydrochloric acid and ethanoic acid are both 0.1 mol/dm³. Which is true?","choices":["They have the same pH because the concentration is the same","Hydrochloric acid has the lower pH because all of its molecules ionise","Ethanoic acid has the lower pH because its molecules are bigger"],"answer":1,"hint":"pH depends on the H⁺ actually free in the water, not on how much acid was added.","explain":"Every HCl molecule splits, giving pH 1. Only about 1 in 100 ethanoic acid molecules split, giving pH about 2.9."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"A solution is pH {a}. Another has {=10**d} times more H⁺ ions. What is its pH?","answer":"a-d","hint":"More H⁺ means a lower pH. Each factor of 10 is one step.","explain":"{=10**d} times more H⁺ is {d} steps down the scale: pH {a} − {d} = {=a-d}.","params":{"a":[3,7],"d":[1,2]}}],"explain":{"kid":["When an acid dissolves in water, it lets go of tiny charged bits called hydrogen ions. We write them as H⁺. They make lemon juice sour and turn blue litmus paper red.","An alkali does the opposite job. It lets go of hydroxide ions, OH⁻. Oven cleaner has lots of them.","Mix the two and each H⁺ grabs one OH⁻. Together they make a water molecule, H₂O. If every H⁺ finds a partner, the mix is neutral. Any extra H⁺ keeps it acidic.","The pH scale counts in jumps of ten. pH 3 has ten times the H⁺ of pH 4. pH 2 has a hundred times as much. A small change in the number is a big change in the acid."],"adult":["In aqueous solution an acid is a source of H⁺ ions and an alkali is a source of OH⁻ ions. Hydrochloric acid gives H⁺ and Cl⁻; sodium hydroxide gives Na⁺ and OH⁻. Strictly the H⁺ joins a water molecule as H₃O⁺, but GCSE equations write H⁺.","When an acid neutralises an alkali, the only change is H⁺ + OH⁻ → H₂O. The metal ion and the acid's negative ion stay dissolved as spectators; they become the salt left when the water evaporates. A common mistake is to write Na⁺ + Cl⁻ → NaCl as the reaction.","pH is a logarithmic scale: pH = −log₁₀[H⁺]. Going down one unit multiplies the H⁺ concentration by 10, so pH 1 has 1000 times the H⁺ of pH 4. Diluting a strong acid tenfold raises its pH by one, but water alone can never take it past 7.","Strong and weak describe how much an acid ionises, not how much of it there is. Hydrochloric acid ionises completely. Ethanoic acid only partly ionises: at 0.1 mol/dm³ about 1 molecule in 100 is split, so its pH is about 2.9, while hydrochloric acid of the same concentration is pH 1. Be careful with words: concentrated and dilute describe how much acid is in each dm³."]},"familyTip":"Use dried beans for H⁺ and coins for OH⁻. Put 7 beans and 4 coins on a plate. Pair each coin with a bean and push the pair aside as water. Ask: what is left over, and is the plate acidic, neutral or alkaline?","keyIdea":"In water, an acid releases hydrogen ions, H⁺, and an alkali releases hydroxide ions, OH⁻. Neutralisation is H⁺ + OH⁻ → H₂O; the other ions are spectators. Each step down the pH scale means ten times more H⁺, so pH 2 has 100 times the H⁺ of pH 4. A strong acid splits completely into ions; a weak acid only partly. Strong is not the same as concentrated.","worked":{"title":"Lemon juice is pH 2 and black coffee is pH 5. How many times more H⁺ ions does lemon juice have?","steps":["Find the gap on the pH scale: 5 − 2 = 3 steps.","Each step down multiplies the H⁺ by 10, so three steps is 10 × 10 × 10.","That is 1000. Lemon juice has 1000 times the H⁺ concentration of coffee, not 3 times and not 30 times."]},"visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"What does each substance release in water? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["H⁺: an acid","OH⁻: an alkali","Neither"],"items":[["hydrochloric acid",0],["nitric acid",0],["sodium hydroxide",1],["potassium hydroxide",1],["sodium chloride",2],["sugar",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flaghion","caption":"Flagship lab: dissolve acids and alkalis into ions, pair H⁺ with OH⁻ to make water, and watch H⁺ grow tenfold per pH step.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_QFM2Wf3u1Z":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Bromine water is added to potassium chloride solution. It stays orange. Why?","choices":["Bromine has displaced chlorine","There is no reaction: the orange is the bromine that was added","Potassium chloride turns orange when mixed with water"],"answer":1,"hint":"Is bromine more or less reactive than chlorine?","explain":"Bromine is below chlorine, so it cannot displace it. The orange colour is just the bromine water."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which halogen is the most reactive?","choices":["iodine","chlorine","fluorine"],"answer":2,"hint":"Reactivity falls going down the group.","explain":"Fluorine is at the top. Its outer shell is closest to the nucleus, so it gains an electron most readily."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Astatine is below iodine. What does the trend predict about it?","choices":["A pale gas that is more reactive than iodine","A dark solid that is less reactive than iodine","A colourless liquid like water"],"answer":1,"hint":"Follow two trends: state and colour, and reactivity.","explain":"Colours darken and melting points rise down the group, and reactivity falls, so astatine should be a dark solid less reactive than iodine."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"{n} chlorine molecules displace bromine from potassium bromide. How many chloride ions form?","answer":"2*n","hint":"Cl₂ + 2Br⁻ → 2Cl⁻ + Br₂. Each Cl₂ gives two Cl⁻.","explain":"Each Cl₂ molecule becomes two Cl⁻ ions, so {n} molecules give {=2*n} chloride ions.","params":{"n":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which is the correct ionic equation for chlorine reacting with iodide ions?","choices":["Cl₂ + 2I⁻ → 2Cl⁻ + I₂","Cl + I⁻ → Cl⁻ + I","I₂ + 2Cl⁻ → 2I⁻ + Cl₂"],"answer":0,"hint":"Halogens are diatomic, and the more reactive halogen ends up as the ion.","explain":"Chlorine is more reactive, so it becomes chloride ions and iodine is released as I₂. The third equation runs the wrong way."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Down Group 1 reactivity increases, but down Group 7 it decreases. Why the difference?","choices":["Group 7 atoms are smaller","Alkali metals react by losing an electron; halogens react by gaining one","Halogens have fewer shells"],"answer":1,"hint":"A distant outer electron is easy to lose but hard to attract.","explain":"A further-out outer shell makes losing an electron easier (Group 1) but gaining one harder (Group 7)."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"What is the difference between chlorine and chloride?","choices":["They are two names for the same thing","Chlorine is the element, Cl₂; chloride is the ion, Cl⁻, found in salts","Chloride is chlorine gas dissolved in water"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the charge. Which one has gained an electron?","explain":"Chlorine is a toxic gas made of Cl₂ molecules. Chloride ions in table salt have each gained an electron and are harmless."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"How do halogen atoms usually exist as elements?","choices":["As single atoms, like noble gases","As molecules of two atoms, such as Br₂","As ions, such as Br⁻"],"answer":1,"hint":"Two halogen atoms can share electrons to each get 8.","explain":"Two atoms share a pair of electrons in a covalent bond, so the elements are F₂, Cl₂, Br₂ and I₂."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Iodine solution is added to potassium bromide solution. What do you see?","choices":["It turns orange as bromine forms","It stays brown: no reaction","It turns colourless"],"answer":1,"hint":"Can a less reactive halogen displace a more reactive one?","explain":"Iodine is below bromine, so it cannot take electrons from Br⁻. The brown colour is the iodine solution added."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m10","prompt":"Chlorine water is added to excess potassium iodide. If {n} chlorine molecules react, how many iodine molecules form?","answer":"n","hint":"Write Cl₂ + 2I⁻ → 2Cl⁻ + I₂ and compare Cl₂ with I₂.","explain":"One Cl₂ releases one I₂, so {n} chlorine molecules release {n} iodine molecules, turning the solution brown.","params":{"n":[2,9]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Chlorine water is added to potassium iodide solution. What happens?","choices":["Nothing: iodine is a solid","Iodine is made and the solution turns brown","Chlorine is made and the solution turns green"],"answer":1,"hint":"Which is higher in Group 7, chlorine or iodine?","explain":"Chlorine is more reactive, so it takes electrons from I⁻: Cl₂ + 2KI → 2KCl + I₂. Iodine in solution is brown."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"At room temperature, bromine is…","choices":["a gas","a liquid","a solid"],"answer":1,"hint":"Chlorine above it is a gas and iodine below it is a solid.","explain":"Bromine melts at −7 °C and boils at 59 °C, so it is a red-brown liquid at about 20 °C."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"In Cl₂ + 2Br⁻ → 2Cl⁻ + Br₂, how many Br⁻ ions lose an electron when {n} chlorine molecules react?","answer":"2*n","hint":"Each Cl₂ molecule gains two electrons, one from each of two Br⁻ ions.","explain":"Each Cl₂ needs 2 electrons, so {n} molecules take electrons from 2 × {n} = {=2*n} Br⁻ ions.","params":{"n":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Why does reactivity decrease down Group 7?","choices":["The atoms have more protons, which repel electrons","The outer shell is further from the nucleus, so a new electron is attracted less strongly","The atoms lose electrons less easily"],"answer":1,"hint":"Halogens react by gaining an electron. What makes that harder lower down?","explain":"Each element down the group has an extra shell. The outer shell is further out and more shielded, so the pull on an incoming electron is weaker."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"{n} bromine molecules react completely with iodide ions. How many iodine molecules form?","answer":"n","hint":"Use Br₂ + 2I⁻ → 2Br⁻ + I₂ and compare the numbers in front of Br₂ and I₂.","explain":"One Br₂ makes one I₂, so {n} Br₂ molecules make {n} I₂ molecules.","params":{"n":[2,9]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Group 7 is a family of elements called the halogens. Fluorine and chlorine are gases. Bromine is a red-brown liquid. Iodine is a grey-black solid.","Each halogen atom has 7 electrons in its outer shell. It needs just one more to fill it. So halogens grab an electron from other atoms.","Fluorine grabs hardest. Its outer shell sits close to the middle of the atom, so the pull is strong. Further down, the shell is further out and the pull is weaker.","A strong grabber can steal from a weak one. Put chlorine in a salt that has bromine in it. The chlorine takes the electron, and orange bromine appears."],"adult":["The halogens are diatomic: F₂, Cl₂, Br₂, I₂. Down the group the molecules get larger, so the forces between molecules get stronger and melting and boiling points rise. Fluorine is a pale yellow gas, chlorine a yellow-green gas, bromine a red-brown liquid and iodine a grey-black solid with a purple vapour.","Every halogen atom has 7 outer electrons and reacts by gaining one to form a −1 ion: chlorine becomes chloride, Cl⁻. Down the group each atom has an extra shell. The outer shell is further from the nucleus and more shielded by inner electrons, so an incoming electron is attracted less strongly. Reactivity therefore falls from fluorine to iodine.","In a displacement reaction a more reactive halogen takes electrons from the ions of a less reactive one: Cl₂ + 2Br⁻ → 2Cl⁻ + Br₂. Chlorine displaces bromine and iodine, bromine displaces iodine, and iodine displaces neither. The colour shows the product: bromine in water is orange and iodine is brown.","Watch for common mistakes. Group 7 reactivity falls down the group, the opposite of Group 1, because halogens gain electrons while alkali metals lose them. Chloride is not chlorine. Bromine water added to potassium chloride stays orange, but nothing has reacted. Fluorine is the most reactive even though measured electron affinity is slightly higher for chlorine; its weak F–F bond and strong bonds to other atoms explain this."]},"familyTip":"Play 'steal the sock'. A strong player can take a sock from a weaker one, but not the other way round. Rank three players. Who can steal from whom? Halogens work the same way with electrons.","keyIdea":"The halogens are fluorine, chlorine, bromine and iodine. They exist as pairs of atoms (Cl₂, Br₂). Going down the group, melting and boiling points rise and colours darken: two gases, then a liquid, then a solid. Each atom has 7 outer electrons and reacts by gaining one. Reactivity falls down the group because the outer shell is further from the nucleus. A more reactive halogen displaces a less reactive one from its salt: Cl₂ + 2KBr → 2KCl + Br₂.","worked":{"title":"Predict what happens when bromine water is added to potassium iodide solution, and write the ionic equation.","steps":["Find both halogens in Group 7: bromine is above iodine, so bromine is more reactive.","A more reactive halogen displaces a less reactive one from its salt, so a reaction happens.","Each Br₂ takes two electrons, one from each of two I⁻ ions: Br₂ + 2I⁻ → 2Br⁻ + I₂.","The solution turns brown as iodine forms. Potassium ions are spectators and do not appear in the ionic equation."]},"visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"What happens in each tube? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Reacts","No reaction"],"items":[["chlorine + potassium bromide",0],["chlorine + potassium iodide",0],["bromine + potassium iodide",0],["bromine + potassium chloride",1],["iodine + potassium bromide",1],["iodine + potassium chloride",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flaghal","caption":"Flagship lab: slide down Group 7, drop an electron into chlorine, and pour halogens into salt solutions to find which reactions happen.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt__bbsC2SiAg":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which ester forms from methanol and butanoic acid?","choices":["butyl methanoate","methyl butanoate","methanoyl butanol"],"answer":1,"hint":"The alcohol gives the '-yl' word, the acid gives the '-oate' word.","explain":"Methanol becomes 'methyl' and butanoic acid becomes 'butanoate': methyl butanoate, CH₃CH₂CH₂COOCH₃."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"In ethanoic acid, CH₃COOH, which hydrogen is released as H⁺ in water?","choices":["any of the three on the CH₃","the one bonded to oxygen","all four, one after another"],"answer":1,"hint":"Which bond breaks to leave a negative ion with the charge on oxygen?","explain":"Only the O–H hydrogen ionises, leaving the ethanoate ion CH₃COO⁻. C–H bonds do not break in water."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Hydrochloric acid and ethanoic acid are both 0.1 mol/dm³. Which statement is true?","choices":["They have the same pH because the concentration is the same","Ethanoic acid has the higher pH because fewer of its molecules ionise","Ethanoic acid has the lower pH because its molecules are bigger"],"answer":1,"hint":"pH depends on how many H⁺ ions are in solution.","explain":"HCl is fully ionised (pH about 1). Only about 1% of ethanoic acid ionises, so there is less H⁺ and the pH is about 2.9."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Magnesium is added to propanoic acid. What are the products?","choices":["magnesium propanoate and hydrogen","magnesium propanoate, water and carbon dioxide","magnesium oxide and propanol"],"answer":0,"hint":"Metal + acid → salt + which gas? Only carbonates make carbon dioxide.","explain":"A metal and an acid give a salt and hydrogen. The salt of propanoic acid is a propanoate."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"How many carbon atoms are in one molecule of the ester made from {a}-carbon alcohol and a {b}-carbon carboxylic acid?","answer":"a+b","hint":"All the carbons from both molecules stay in the ester; only water leaves.","explain":"Water (H₂O) holds no carbon, so the ester keeps {a} + {b} = {=a+b} carbons.","params":{"a":[1,4],"b":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"What is HCOOH called?","choices":["methanoic acid","ethanoic acid","methanol"],"answer":0,"hint":"How many carbons are there in total?","explain":"HCOOH has one carbon, the carboxyl carbon itself, so it is methanoic acid."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"What else is needed to make ethyl ethanoate from ethanol and ethanoic acid at a useful rate?","choices":["a few drops of concentrated sulfuric acid, and warming","sodium carbonate","limewater"],"answer":0,"hint":"Esterification is slow and needs a catalyst.","explain":"Concentrated sulfuric acid catalyses the reaction, and warming speeds it up. Ester and water form."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"With excess magnesium, {=2*k} molecules of ethanoic acid react completely: 2CH₃COOH + Mg → (CH₃COO)₂Mg + H₂. How many hydrogen molecules form?","answer":"k","hint":"Two acid molecules give one H₂.","explain":"Two molecules of acid give one H₂, so {=2*k} molecules give {=2*k} ÷ 2 = {k} molecules of hydrogen. A weak acid gives the same total in the end, just more slowly.","params":{"k":[1,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Which is the displayed group that makes a molecule a carboxylic acid?","choices":["–OH on its own","–COOH: C=O and O–H on the same carbon","C=C"],"answer":1,"hint":"Alcohols have –OH; alkenes have C=C.","explain":"The carboxyl group has a carbon double-bonded to one oxygen and single-bonded to an O–H."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"What is CH₃CH₂COOH called?","choices":["ethanoic acid","propanoic acid","propanol"],"answer":1,"hint":"Count every carbon, including the one in –COOH.","explain":"There are three carbons, CH₃, CH₂ and the carboxyl carbon, so it is propanoic acid. Propanol is the alcohol with –OH."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Ethanoic acid is added to sodium carbonate. Which gas is given off?","choices":["hydrogen","carbon dioxide","oxygen"],"answer":1,"hint":"Which gas does any acid give with a carbonate?","explain":"Acid + carbonate → salt + water + carbon dioxide. The gas turns limewater milky; the salt is sodium ethanoate."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A carboxylic acid has {n} carbon atoms in total. How many hydrogen atoms are in one molecule?","answer":"2*n","hint":"Ethanoic acid, CH₃COOH, has 2 carbons and 4 hydrogens. The general formula is CₙH₂ₙO₂.","explain":"Carboxylic acids fit CₙH₂ₙO₂, so {n} carbons means 2 × {n} = {=2*n} hydrogens. Only one of them, the O–H hydrogen, is acidic.","params":{"n":[1,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Why is ethanoic acid called a weak acid?","choices":["It is always diluted with water","Only a small fraction of its molecules ionise to give H⁺","It has only one hydrogen atom"],"answer":1,"hint":"Weak and dilute are not the same idea.","explain":"Weak means partly ionised: at 0.1 mol/dm³ about 1 in 100 molecules release H⁺. Dilute means little acid per volume."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"In 2CH₃COOH + Na₂CO₃ → 2CH₃COONa + H₂O + CO₂, how many molecules of ethanoic acid react with {n} formula units of sodium carbonate?","answer":"2*n","hint":"Compare the numbers in front of CH₃COOH and Na₂CO₃.","explain":"Two ethanoic acid molecules react per Na₂CO₃, so {n} units need 2 × {n} = {=2*n} molecules.","params":{"n":[2,9]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Vinegar tastes sharp because it has ethanoic acid in it. Ethanoic acid is one of a family called carboxylic acids.","Each one ends in a group of atoms written COOH. One carbon, two oxygens and a hydrogen. The carbon in that group counts when you name the acid. Ethanoic acid has two carbons in all.","Only one hydrogen can come off, the one stuck to an oxygen. And in water, only about one molecule in a hundred lets it go. So vinegar is a weak acid. It still fizzes with baking soda, but more gently than a strong acid.","Mix an acid like this with an alcohol and a drop of strong acid, then warm it. You get an ester and water. Many esters smell of fruit, like pear drops or bananas."],"adult":["The functional group is the carboxyl group, –COOH: a carbon with a C=O double bond and an O–H. Names use the alkane stem for the total number of carbons, including the carboxyl carbon, plus '-oic acid': methanoic HCOOH, ethanoic CH₃COOH, propanoic CH₃CH₂COOH, butanoic CH₃CH₂CH₂COOH.","In water the O–H hydrogen ionises reversibly: CH₃COOH ⇌ CH₃COO⁻ + H⁺. At 0.1 mol/dm³ only about 1% of ethanoic acid molecules are ionised, so the pH is about 2.9, compared with about 1 for hydrochloric acid at the same concentration. That is what 'weak' means: partly ionised, not dilute.","They show typical acid reactions, only more slowly. With magnesium: 2CH₃COOH + Mg → (CH₃COO)₂Mg + H₂. With sodium carbonate: 2CH₃COOH + Na₂CO₃ → 2CH₃COONa + H₂O + CO₂, which turns limewater milky. The salts are named '-oate', such as sodium ethanoate. With enough time, a weak acid releases as much hydrogen as a strong one of the same concentration and volume, because molecules keep ionising as H⁺ is used up.","With an alcohol, warmed with a concentrated sulfuric acid catalyst, a carboxylic acid forms an ester and water. The name puts the alcohol part first and the acid part second: ethanol + ethanoic acid → ethyl ethanoate. Common mistakes: forgetting to count the carboxyl carbon, thinking all four hydrogens in ethanoic acid are acidic, confusing weak with dilute, and writing the ester name the wrong way round."]},"familyTip":"Put a spoon of baking soda in a glass and add vinegar. Then repeat with the vinegar mixed half and half with water. Both fizz, but which is faster? Talk about why a weak acid still fizzes.","keyIdea":"Carboxylic acids contain the –COOH group: methanoic, ethanoic, propanoic and butanoic acid have 1 to 4 carbons, counting the carbon in –COOH. Only the hydrogen on the oxygen can leave as H⁺, and only a small fraction of molecules do so at any moment, so they are weak acids. They react with metals to give a salt and hydrogen, with carbonates to give a salt, water and carbon dioxide, and with alcohols to make esters.","worked":{"title":"Name the ester made from propanol and ethanoic acid, and give its formula.","steps":["The alcohol gives the first word. Propanol has 3 carbons, so it becomes 'propyl'.","The acid gives the second word. Ethanoic acid becomes 'ethanoate'.","So the ester is propyl ethanoate.","The acid part CH₃COO– joins to the alcohol chain –CH₂CH₂CH₃: CH₃COOCH₂CH₂CH₃. The O–H from the acid and the H from the alcohol's O–H leave as water."]},"visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which family is each molecule in? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Carboxylic acid","Alcohol","Ester"],"items":[["CH₃COOH",0],["HCOOH",0],["CH₃CH₂OH",1],["CH₃OH",1],["CH₃COOCH₃",2],["HCOOCH₂CH₃",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagcooh","caption":"Flagship lab: stretch the chain to name the acid, find the hydrogen that leaves, race magnesium in a strong and a weak acid, and make esters.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_Fe36KGiLwd":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which ion does lithium form?","choices":["Li⁺","Li⁻","Li²⁺"],"answer":0,"hint":"Lithium has one outer electron, and metals lose electrons.","explain":"Losing one electron leaves 3 protons and 2 electrons, so the charge is 1+: Li⁺."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Caesium is near the bottom of Group 1. Compared with potassium, caesium…","choices":["reacts more slowly with water","reacts more violently with water","does not react with water"],"answer":1,"hint":"Which way does reactivity go down Group 1?","explain":"Reactivity increases down the group, so caesium reacts explosively."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Universal indicator is added to water after sodium has reacted in it. What colour does it turn?","choices":["green","red","purple"],"answer":2,"hint":"What does sodium hydroxide do to pH?","explain":"Sodium hydroxide is a strong alkali, so the indicator turns purple (pH about 13–14)."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"A fresh potassium surface tarnishes: 4K + O₂ → 2K₂O. How many O₂ molecules react with {=4*k} potassium atoms?","answer":"k","hint":"Four potassium atoms use one O₂ molecule.","explain":"The ratio K : O₂ is 4 : 1, so {=4*k} potassium atoms use {k} oxygen molecules.","params":{"k":[1,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Why does the melting point matter when sodium is dropped into water?","choices":["The heat of the reaction melts sodium (98 °C) into a ball","Sodium melts at room temperature","The water boils first"],"answer":0,"hint":"Sodium's melting point is below 100 °C.","explain":"The reaction gives out enough heat to melt sodium, so it forms a silvery ball; lithium (181 °C) does not melt."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"{=2*k} lithium atoms react completely with water: 2Li + 2H₂O → 2LiOH + H₂. How many LiOH units form?","answer":"2*k","hint":"Compare the numbers in front of Li and LiOH.","explain":"Each lithium atom becomes one LiOH, so {=2*k} atoms make {=2*k} units of lithium hydroxide.","params":{"k":[1,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which statement about density is true?","choices":["All alkali metals float on water","Lithium, sodium and potassium float, but rubidium and caesium sink","They all sink, like most metals"],"answer":1,"hint":"Rubidium's density is 1.53 g/cm³; water's is 1.00 g/cm³.","explain":"Li, Na and K are less dense than water. Rb and Cs are denser, so they sink."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Sodium's outer electron is in its third shell. What does that mean for how easily it is lost, compared with lithium?","choices":["Harder, because sodium has more protons","Easier, because it is further from the nucleus and more shielded","The same, because both have one outer electron"],"answer":1,"hint":"Think about distance and the inner shells in between.","explain":"The extra shell puts the electron further out, with 10 inner electrons shielding it, so it is lost more easily: 496 kJ/mol against 520."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"A fresh cut of potassium goes dull in seconds. What has formed on the surface?","choices":["potassium oxide","potassium chloride","potassium hydroxide only"],"answer":0,"hint":"What in air reacts with the metal?","explain":"Oxygen reacts with the surface: 4K + O₂ → 2K₂O. Moisture can then turn some of it into hydroxide."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"What are the products when potassium reacts with water?","choices":["potassium oxide and hydrogen","potassium hydroxide and hydrogen","potassium hydroxide and oxygen"],"answer":1,"hint":"The solution turns indicator purple, and the gas gives a squeaky pop.","explain":"2K + 2H₂O → 2KOH + H₂. The hydroxide makes the water alkaline and the gas is hydrogen."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Why are lithium, sodium and potassium stored under oil?","choices":["To stop them reacting with oxygen and water in the air","Because they are liquids","To keep them cold so they do not melt"],"answer":0,"hint":"What happens to a fresh cut surface within minutes?","explain":"Oil keeps air and moisture away. In air the shiny surface quickly tarnishes as oxide forms."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"In 2Na + 2H₂O → 2NaOH + H₂, how many hydrogen molecules form when {=2*k} sodium atoms react?","answer":"k","hint":"Two sodium atoms make one H₂ molecule.","explain":"The ratio Na : H₂ is 2 : 1, so {=2*k} sodium atoms give {k} molecules of hydrogen.","params":{"k":[1,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Why is potassium more reactive than sodium?","choices":["It has more protons, so it pulls harder","Its outer electron is further from the nucleus and more shielded, so it is lost more easily","It has more outer electrons to lose"],"answer":1,"hint":"Both have one outer electron. What is different about where it is?","explain":"Potassium's outer electron is in the fourth shell, not the third. The pull on it is weaker, so it is lost more easily."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"How many sodium ions are needed to balance the charge of {n} oxide ions (O²⁻)?","answer":"2*n","hint":"Each Na⁺ has charge 1+, each O²⁻ has 2−.","explain":"Each oxide ion needs two Na⁺ (Na₂O), so {n} oxide ions need 2 × {n} = {=2*n} sodium ions.","params":{"n":[1,9]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Group 1 is the first column of the periodic table. Lithium, sodium and potassium are metals, but odd ones. You can cut them with a knife, and the first three float on water.","Cut one and the new face shines like silver. Within minutes it goes dull, because air reacts with it. That is why these metals are kept in oil.","Drop them in water and they fizz. The gas is hydrogen. The water left behind turns into an alkali, so they are called the alkali metals.","Each atom has just one electron in its outer shell, and it gives it away. Lower down the group, that electron sits further from the middle of the atom. It is easier to lose, so the metal reacts faster. Potassium beats sodium, and sodium beats lithium."],"adult":["The alkali metals have one electron in their outer shell. They are soft enough to cut with a knife. Their melting points are low and fall down the group: lithium 181 °C, sodium 98 °C, potassium 63 °C. Lithium, sodium and potassium are less dense than water, so they float. They are kept under oil because a fresh cut face tarnishes in seconds to minutes as it reacts with oxygen: 4Na + O₂ → 2Na₂O.","With water each one forms a hydroxide and hydrogen: 2M + 2H₂O → 2MOH + H₂. Lithium fizzes steadily. Sodium melts into a ball and moves about. Potassium sets the hydrogen alight with a lilac flame. The hydroxide is a strong alkali, so universal indicator turns purple. A lit splint held at the gas gives a squeaky pop.","In every reaction the atom loses its one outer electron to form a 1+ ion, such as Na⁺. So the compounds are ionic: NaCl, K₂O. Down the group the outer electron sits in a shell further from the nucleus, with more inner shells shielding it. It is held less strongly. The energy needed to remove it falls from 520 kJ/mol for lithium to 419 kJ/mol for potassium, and reactivity rises.","Common mistakes: thinking reactivity falls down Group 1 as it does in Group 7; saying the product with water is an oxide; giving the ion a negative charge; and assuming every alkali metal floats. Rubidium and caesium are denser than water. They sink and react explosively."]},"familyTip":"Rank three magnets by strength, then hold a paperclip at different distances from the strongest. Further away, the pull is weaker and the clip drops off sooner. An alkali metal's outer electron is like that clip.","keyIdea":"Lithium, sodium and potassium are soft, low-density metals with one outer electron. They tarnish quickly in air and react with water to give a metal hydroxide (an alkali) and hydrogen: 2Na + 2H₂O → 2NaOH + H₂. They react by losing their outer electron to form 1+ ions. Reactivity increases down the group because the outer electron is further from the nucleus and more shielded, so it is lost more easily.","worked":{"title":"Predict how rubidium reacts with water and write the balanced equation.","steps":["Rubidium is below potassium in Group 1, so the trend predicts it is more reactive than potassium.","Its outer electron is in the fifth shell, further from the nucleus and more shielded, so it is lost even more easily.","Like the others it forms a hydroxide and hydrogen: 2Rb + 2H₂O → 2RbOH + H₂.","Expect a violent, explosive reaction. Its density is 1.53 g/cm³, so unlike lithium, sodium and potassium it sinks."]},"visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Float or sink in water? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Floats","Sinks"],"items":[["lithium, 0.53 g/cm³",0],["sodium, 0.97 g/cm³",0],["potassium, 0.86 g/cm³",0],["rubidium, 1.53 g/cm³",1],["caesium, 1.93 g/cm³",1],["iron, 7.87 g/cm³",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flaggp1","caption":"Flagship lab: cut an alkali metal, drop Group 1 metals into water, test the products and pull off each atom's outer electron.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_HDIIwSeemt":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"How does atomic radius change across a period from left to right?","choices":["It decreases","It increases","It stays the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the core charge with the same inner shells.","explain":"The rising nuclear charge pulls the same outer shell closer, so radius decreases across a period."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"First ionisation energy falls down Group 1 even though the number of protons rises. Why?","choices":["The outer electron is further away and more shielded","The core charge falls","The atoms lose protons"],"answer":0,"hint":"What changes as each new shell is added?","explain":"The extra shells put the outer electron further out behind more inner electrons, which outweighs the rising nuclear charge."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Why is the first ionisation energy of aluminium lower than that of magnesium?","choices":["Al's outer electron is in 3p, higher in energy and shielded by 3s","Al has fewer protons","Al's outer electron is in a new shell"],"answer":0,"hint":"Write the outer sub-shells: Mg 3s², Al 3s² 3p¹.","explain":"The 3p electron starts slightly higher in energy and is partly shielded by the 3s pair, so it takes less energy to remove."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Why is the first ionisation energy of sulfur lower than that of phosphorus?","choices":["Two 3p electrons share an orbital and repel","Sulfur has an extra inner shell","Sulfur has fewer protons"],"answer":0,"hint":"Draw the 3p boxes for P (3p³) and S (3p⁴).","explain":"In sulfur one 3p orbital holds a pair, and the repulsion between them makes one easier to remove."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"The first ionisation energy of sodium is 496 kJ/mol. How many kJ are needed to remove one electron from each atom in {n} mol of sodium atoms?","answer":"496*n","hint":"First ionisation energy is given per mole of atoms.","explain":"Energy = 496 × {n} = {=496*n} kJ.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which reacts more vigorously: chlorine or bromine?","choices":["Chlorine","Bromine","They react equally"],"answer":0,"hint":"Halogens react by gaining an electron. Which atom pulls an incoming electron harder?","explain":"Chlorine's outer shell is closer to the nucleus and less shielded, so it attracts an extra electron more strongly and is more reactive."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Element A has core charge +{a} and element B has core charge +{b}; both are in period 3. Which atom is smaller?","choices":["The element with core charge +{b}","The element with core charge +{a}","They are the same size"],"answer":0,"hint":"Same inner shells, so the bigger core charge pulls harder.","explain":"With the same inner shells, core charge +{b} pulls the outer shell in more than +{a}, so that atom is smaller.","params":{"a":[1,3],"b":[5,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Across period 3, what happens to the shielding of the outer electrons?","choices":["It stays about the same","It increases a lot with each element","It falls to zero"],"answer":0,"hint":"How many inner electrons does every period 3 atom have?","explain":"Every period 3 atom has the same 10 inner electrons (2, 8), so shielding stays roughly constant while nuclear charge rises."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Which of these has the highest first ionisation energy?","choices":["Argon","Sodium","Sulfur"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the right-hand end of the period.","explain":"Argon has the highest core charge in period 3 and the smallest outer shell, so its first ionisation energy is 1521 kJ/mol."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Why is a chlorine atom smaller than a sodium atom?","choices":["Chlorine has more protons pulling on the same shells","Chlorine has more electrons, which pack tighter","Chlorine has fewer shells"],"answer":0,"hint":"Both atoms have three shells. What changes from Na to Cl?","explain":"From Na to Cl the nuclear charge rises from 11 to 17 while the inner shells stay 2, 8, so the outer shell is pulled in."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A period 3 atom has {p} protons and 10 inner electrons. What is its core charge? (Give the number only.)","answer":"p-10","hint":"Core charge is the nuclear charge minus the inner electrons.","explain":"Core charge = {p} − 10 = {=p-10}, so the outer electrons feel a pull of about +{=p-10}.","params":{"p":[11,18]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which has the larger atoms: the Group 1 metal in period {a} or the one in period {b}?","choices":["The one in period {b}","The one in period {a}","They are the same size"],"answer":0,"hint":"Each period down adds a shell.","explain":"Period {b} is lower, so its atom has more shells and a larger radius than the atom in period {a}.","params":{"a":[2,4],"b":[5,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Why does potassium react faster with water than sodium?","choices":["Its outer electron is further out and more shielded, so it is lost more easily","It has more protons, so it holds electrons tighter","It has a larger core charge"],"answer":0,"hint":"Metals react by losing their outer electron. Which atom loses it more easily?","explain":"Both have core charge +1, but potassium's outer electron is in shell 4, further out and more shielded, so its first ionisation energy is lower (419 vs 496 kJ/mol)."}],"explain":{"kid":["An atom is a tug of war. The protons in the nucleus pull on the outer electrons. The inner electrons sit in the way and block some of that pull. We call this shielding.","Go across period 3, from sodium to chlorine. Each step adds one proton and one outer electron. The inner shells stay the same, 2 and 8. So the pull on the outer shell grows, and the atom shrinks from 0.186 nm to 0.099 nm.","Go down Group 1, from lithium to caesium. Each atom has one more shell. The outer electron is further out, with more shielding in the way. It is easier to pull off, so the metals get more reactive.","The energy to remove the first outer electron is the first ionisation energy. It rises across a period and falls down a group. Two small dips across period 3, at aluminium and sulfur, come from sub-shells."],"adult":["A useful model is the core charge: the nuclear charge minus the inner electrons. Across period 3 it rises from about +1 at sodium to +7 at chlorine. Electrons in the same shell shield each other only a little, so the outer shell is pulled closer and the radius falls. The data are 0.186 nm for Na and 0.099 nm for Cl; argon has no comparable bonded radius.","First ionisation energy follows the same pull. It rises from 496 kJ/mol for Na to 1521 kJ/mol for Ar. Down Group 1 it falls from 520 kJ/mol for Li to 376 kJ/mol for Cs. The core charge stays near +1, but the outer electron is further out and better shielded, so it is held less tightly.","The dips are real. Al is lower than Mg because its outer electron is in 3p, which is higher in energy than 3s and partly shielded by it. S is lower than P because one 3p orbital now holds a pair, and the paired electrons repel.","Common mistakes: saying chlorine is bigger because it has more electrons; thinking more protons down a group means a tighter hold, when distance and shielding win; using a new shell to explain the Al and S dips, when all period 3 atoms use shell 3; and giving non-metals the metal trend. Halogens react by gaining an electron, so their reactivity falls down the group."]},"familyTip":"With a periodic table, take turns pointing at two elements in the same period or the same group. The other person says which atom is bigger and which loses an electron more easily, and gives the reason using protons, shells and shielding.","keyIdea":"Across a period, protons are added but the inner shells stay the same, so the pull on the outer shell rises: atoms get smaller and their outer electrons get harder to remove. Down a group, each atom has an extra shell, so the outer electrons are further out and more shielded: atoms get bigger and the outer electrons are easier to remove. Metals react by losing electrons, so they get more reactive down a group; non-metals react by gaining them, so they get less reactive.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Does this make the outer electrons easier or harder to remove? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Harder to remove","Easier to remove"],"items":[["One more proton, same shells",0],["Moving right across a period",0],["Smaller atomic radius",0],["An extra shell",1],["More inner electrons shielding",1],["Moving down a group",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagtrend","caption":"Flagship lab: shrink period 3 atoms, pull off outer electrons, explain the dips and judge which atom reacts more.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_0gHvBBJxh3":{"mastery":{"passMark":5,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Where are the carbon atoms in a skeletal formula?","choices":["At every corner and every line end","Only where a C is written","In the middle of each line"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look back at the five carbons you tapped in pentane.","explain":"Each vertex and each line end is a carbon. The lines between them are C–C bonds."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"An unbranched skeleton has {n} carbons and one C=C double bond, no other groups. How many H atoms?","answer":"2*n","hint":"Start from the alkane, CₙH₂ₙ₊₂. Each carbon of the C=C loses one H.","explain":"2 × {n} + 2 − 2 = {=2*n}. Alkenes fit CₙH₂ₙ.","params":{"n":[3,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Two skeletons each show a 4-carbon zig-zag, but one is drawn upside down. What are they?","choices":["The same molecule, butane","Two isomers","Two different alkenes"],"answer":0,"hint":"Do the bonds change when you flip the drawing?","explain":"Flipping a drawing does not change which atoms bond, so both are butane. Isomers have different bonding."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which skeleton is pentane?","choices":["A zig-zag with 4 lines","A zig-zag with 5 lines","A zig-zag with 3 lines"],"answer":0,"hint":"Pentane has 5 carbons. How many lines join 5 points?","explain":"Five points in a chain need four lines. A 5-line zig-zag would be hexane."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"A zig-zag of 4 carbons has OH on an end carbon. Which molecule is it?","choices":["Butan-1-ol, C₄H₁₀O","Butan-2-ol, C₄H₁₀O","Pentan-1-ol, C₅H₁₂O"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count only the corners and ends, not the O. Where is the OH?","explain":"Four carbons with OH on carbon 1 is butan-1-ol. The O is not a carbon, so it is not pentanol."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"A skeleton has a main zig-zag of {n} carbons plus one CH₃ branch on carbon 2. How many H atoms?","answer":"2*(n+1)+2","hint":"A branched alkane still fits CₙH₂ₙ₊₂, with n the total number of carbons.","explain":"There are {=n+1} carbons in total, so 2 × {=n+1} + 2 = {=2*(n+1)+2} H. Branching does not change the formula.","params":{"n":[4,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Why are the hydrogens on carbon left out of a skeletal formula?","choices":["They are implied: each carbon makes 4 bonds","Those carbons have no hydrogens","Hydrogen does not bond to carbon"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can you always work out how many H a carbon has?","explain":"Since carbon makes 4 bonds, the H count is always 4 minus the lines drawn, so drawing them is not needed."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"The structural formula is CH₃CH(CH₃)CH₃. What does its skeleton look like?","choices":["Three lines meeting at one central point","A straight zig-zag with 3 lines","A zig-zag with 2 lines"],"answer":0,"hint":"The middle carbon bonds to three CH₃ groups.","explain":"The central carbon has three lines to three end carbons: 2-methylpropane, a Y shape."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"An unbranched zig-zag skeleton has {n} carbons and only single bonds. How many hydrogen atoms does the molecule have?","answer":"2*n+2","hint":"The two ends carry 3 H each and every middle carbon carries 2 H.","explain":"2 ends × 3 H + ({n} − 2) middles × 2 H = {=2*n+2}. That matches CₙH₂ₙ₊₂ for alkanes.","params":{"n":[3,10]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A skeletal formula is a zig-zag with {n} lines and no branches. How many carbon atoms are there?","answer":"n+1","hint":"Carbons are the points, and a chain always has one more point than lines.","explain":"{n} lines join {=n+1} points, so there are {=n+1} carbons. Counting lines gives one too few.","params":{"n":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"In a skeletal formula, a carbon has three lines drawn to it. How many hydrogens does it carry?","choices":["1","3","0"],"answer":0,"hint":"Carbon makes 4 bonds. Subtract the lines you can see.","explain":"4 − 3 = 1 hydrogen. This is a branch point, like the second carbon of 2-methylbutane."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"A three-carbon zig-zag has OH on the middle carbon. What is the molecular formula?","choices":["C₃H₈O","C₃H₇O","C₄H₁₀O"],"answer":0,"hint":"The middle carbon now has 1 H. Remember the H in OH, and O is not a carbon.","explain":"Carbons carry 3 + 1 + 3 = 7 H, plus 1 H on the O, so C₃H₈O. This is propan-2-ol."}],"explain":{"kid":["Chemists draw big molecules fast with a skeletal formula. No C is written. Each line is a C–C bond, and each corner or line end is a carbon. Pentane is a zig-zag with 4 lines and 5 points.","The H atoms on carbon are left out, but they are still there. Carbon always makes 4 bonds. Count the lines at a carbon and take that from 4. An end carbon has 1 line, so it has 3 H.","A middle carbon in a chain has 2 lines, so 2 H. A carbon where a branch joins has 3 lines, so just 1 H. Add up every carbon to get the formula.","Atoms that are not C or H are drawn in, such as OH at the end of a line. A double line means C=C. Turning or flipping a zig-zag does not change the molecule. Only the bonds matter."],"adult":["A skeletal formula shows the carbon framework as lines. Each vertex and each line end is a carbon. Hydrogens bonded to carbon are implied. Heteroatoms, and any H bonded to them, are drawn. A double line is a double bond. Displayed formulae show every atom and bond, so converting means adding or removing the C and H labels.","The lab works through it in order. Learners find all five carbons in pentane, then give each carbon of 2-methylbutane its hydrogens using 4 minus bonds. They draw a five-carbon zig-zag on a dot lattice and add OH and C=C to butane. Then they match structural formulae to skeletons.","Common mistakes: counting lines instead of points, so pentane looks like four carbons; forgetting the two line-end carbons; thinking unlabelled carbons have no hydrogens, or always 4; drawing a straight run that hides a carbon; counting the O of OH as a carbon; and believing a flipped zig-zag is a different molecule or an isomer.","Check any skeletal formula by writing the molecular formula two ways. Count C and H from the skeleton, then from a general formula such as CₙH₂ₙ₊₂ for alkanes."]},"familyTip":"Draw a zig-zag on paper with 3 to 7 points and ask: how many carbons, how many hydrogens, and what is the formula? Then add an OH at one end and ask what changes.","keyIdea":"A skeletal formula draws only the carbon chain as a zig-zag of lines. Every corner and every line end is a carbon. Hydrogens on carbon are not drawn: each carbon has four bonds, so it carries 4 minus the lines drawn to it. Other atoms, such as O in OH, are written in, and a double line is a C=C bond.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"How many hydrogens does each carbon carry in a skeletal formula? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["3 H","2 H","1 H"],"items":[["A line-end carbon",0],["A middle carbon in a chain",1],["A branch point with 3 lines",2],["A CH₃ branch end",0],["A carbon in C=C at a chain end",1],["An OH carbon in the chain middle",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagskel","caption":"Flagship lab: find the carbons at corners and ends, fill in hidden hydrogens, draw pentane on dots, add OH and C=C, and match formulae to skeletons.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_siNmAa-jQD":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which statement about E/Z isomers is correct?","choices":["They have the same structural formula but a different arrangement in space","They have different molecular formulas","They turn into each other at room temperature"],"answer":0,"hint":"Are any bonds different, or only the shape in space?","explain":"E/Z isomers are stereoisomers: the same atoms bonded in the same order, but locked in different arrangements by the C=C."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"On one carbon of a C=C are a CH₃ group and a CH₂CH₃ group. Which has priority?","choices":["CH₂CH₃: at the first point of difference it has (C, H, H) against (H, H, H)","CH₃: it is smaller","They tie, so the alkene has no E/Z isomers"],"answer":0,"hint":"Both join through carbon. Look at the atoms on that carbon.","explain":"Both start with C, so compare the next atoms: (C, H, H) beats (H, H, H). CH₂CH₃ wins."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"A carbon of a C=C carries Cl and CH₃. Which has priority?","choices":["Cl, atomic number 17","CH₃, because it has more atoms","CH₃, because it is heavier"],"answer":0,"hint":"Compare the atomic numbers of Cl and of the C in CH₃.","explain":"Cl (17) beats C (6). The number of atoms in the group does not matter; CH₃ is also lighter than Cl."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"In (Z)-but-2-ene, where are the two CH₃ groups?","choices":["On the same side of the C=C","On opposite sides of the C=C","Both on the same carbon"],"answer":0,"hint":"Z comes from zusammen, German for together.","explain":"Z means the priority groups, here the CH₃ groups, are on the same side. Z-but-2-ene is also called cis-but-2-ene."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"An alkene has its C=C between carbons {k} and {=k+1} of a straight chain of {n} carbons. Can it show E/Z isomerism?","choices":["Yes: each carbon of the C=C has an H and a different carbon chain","No: alkenes never show E/Z isomerism","No: one carbon has two identical groups"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is the C=C at the very end of the chain?","explain":"The C=C starts at carbon {k}, not at carbon 1, so each carbon has an H and a chain. Two different groups on each carbon means E/Z isomers.","params":{"n":[6,9],"k":[2,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"One carbon of a C=C carries groups joined through atomic numbers {a} and {b}; the other carries two identical groups. How many E/Z isomers are there?","choices":["None: one carbon has two identical groups","Two","Four"],"answer":0,"hint":"Both carbons need two different groups.","explain":"Even though the first carbon has two different groups ({a} and {b}), the second has two identical ones, so swapping them changes nothing.","params":{"a":[1,6],"b":[7,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which change would turn (E)-but-2-ene into (Z)-but-2-ene?","choices":["Breaking the π bond, rotating one end, and re-forming it","Spinning the molecule round in space","Nothing: they are the same compound"],"answer":0,"hint":"What stops the two forms from turning into each other?","explain":"Only breaking the π bond allows rotation, for example with heat or light energy. Spinning the whole molecule changes nothing about which side each group is on."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"BrClC=CHCl has Br and Cl on one carbon and Cl and H on the other. Br and the lone Cl on the other carbon are on the same side. Which isomer is it?","choices":["Z","E","No E/Z isomers"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find the higher-priority group on each carbon, then check their sides.","explain":"Br (35) beats Cl (17) on one carbon; Cl (17) beats H (1) on the other. Br and that Cl share a side, so it is Z."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Why can a C=C bond not rotate at room temperature?","choices":["Turning it would break the sideways overlap of p orbitals that forms the π bond","The two carbon atoms are too heavy to turn","Double bonds are shorter, so the atoms bump into each other"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about where the second bond of C=C comes from and what it needs.","explain":"The π bond is the side-by-side overlap of p orbitals above and below the bond. Rotation pulls them apart, which would need about 270 kJ/mol."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"In a straight chain of {n} carbon atoms, which alkene can show E/Z isomerism?","choices":["C=C between carbons 2 and 3 of the {n}-carbon chain","C=C between carbons 1 and 2 of the {n}-carbon chain","Neither: a C=C can always rotate"],"answer":0,"hint":"Check both carbons of the C=C. Does each carry two different groups?","explain":"With the C=C at carbon 1, the end carbon carries two H atoms, so there is no E/Z pair. With the C=C at carbon 2 in a chain of {n}, each carbon has an H and a carbon group, so it does.","params":{"n":[5,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"On one carbon of a C=C, group X joins through an atom of atomic number {a} and group Y joins through an atom of atomic number {=a+d}. Which group has priority?","choices":["Y, joined by atomic number {=a+d}","X, joined by atomic number {a}","Neither: they tie"],"answer":0,"hint":"Compare only the atomic numbers of the atoms joined straight to the C=C carbon.","explain":"The higher atomic number wins, so Y ({=a+d}) beats X ({a}). Size and mass of the whole group do not count.","params":{"a":[6,9],"d":[1,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"In 2-bromobut-2-ene, CH₃–C(Br)=CH–CH₃, the two CH₃ groups are on the same side. Which isomer is it?","choices":["E: Br and the CH₃ on the other carbon are the priority pair, and they are on opposite sides","Z: the two CH₃ groups are on the same side","Neither: it has no E/Z isomers"],"answer":0,"hint":"On the carbon with Br and CH₃, which group wins by atomic number?","explain":"Br (35) beats CH₃ (C is 6) on one carbon; CH₃ beats H on the other. If the CH₃ groups share a side, Br is opposite the priority CH₃, so it is E."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Which compound has no E/Z isomers?","choices":["2-methylbut-2-ene, (CH₃)₂C=CH–CH₃","Pent-2-ene, CH₃–CH=CH–CH₂CH₃","1,2-dichloroethene, ClCH=CHCl"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for a carbon of the C=C that carries two identical groups.","explain":"In 2-methylbut-2-ene one carbon of the C=C carries two CH₃ groups, so swapping them gives the same molecule."}],"explain":{"kid":["A single C–C bond lets each end spin, like a wheel on an axle. A C=C double bond is different. Its second bond, the π bond, comes from p orbitals that overlap side by side, above and below the bond. Twist one end and that overlap breaks. That costs about 270 kJ/mol, far more than the molecule gets from heat at room temperature. So a C=C is locked.","Look at but-2-ene. Each carbon of the C=C has a CH₃ and an H. The two CH₃ groups can sit on the same side or on opposite sides. These are two real compounds with the same bonds but a different shape in space. One boils at 4 °C and the other at 1 °C. We call them E/Z isomers.","There is a test. Each carbon of the C=C must have two different groups. But-1-ene has two H atoms on its end carbon. Swap them and you get the same molecule, so it has no E/Z pair.","To name the pair, rank the two groups on each carbon. The atom joined to the C=C with the higher atomic number wins, so Br beats Cl, and Cl beats C. If the first atoms tie, compare the atoms one step further out. Winners on the same side give Z, from the German zusammen, together. Winners on opposite sides give E, from entgegen, opposite."],"adult":["E/Z isomers have the same atoms joined in the same order, but a different shape in space. They exist because the π bond in C=C, made by p orbitals that overlap side by side, stops the bond from turning. Each carbon of the C=C must also carry two different groups.","In the lab, learners twist a C=C and watch it spring back while a C–C spins freely. They swap groups to make both but-2-enes, find that but-1-ene has no pair, rank groups by priority (atomic number first, then the next atoms on a tie), and fix a cis-means-Z slip.","A common mistake is to rank groups by size or mass, so that CH₃ is thought to beat Cl. Only the atomic number of the atom joined to the C=C counts. Another is to think cis is always Z. In 2-bromobut-2-ene the two CH₃ groups can sit on the same side while Br and the other CH₃ are the priority pair, which makes it E. Students also forget to check both carbons, not just one.","Exam questions often ask learners to draw and label both isomers of but-2-ene, explain with the π bond why the C=C cannot turn, or decide whether a named alkene can show E/Z isomerism."]},"familyTip":"Hold a pencil between two fingers and spin it: that is a single bond. Now hold it with two pencils taped side by side: it will not spin without tearing the tape, like the π bond in C=C.","keyIdea":"A C=C bond cannot rotate, because turning it would break the π bond. So when each carbon of the C=C carries two different groups, the groups are locked into two arrangements: E/Z isomers. Rank the two groups on each carbon by the atomic number of the atom joined to the C=C (on a tie, compare the next atoms). If the two higher-priority groups are on the same side it is Z; if they are on opposite sides it is E.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"E/Z or not? Check that each carbon of the C=C has two different groups.","opts":{"bins":["Has E/Z isomers","No E/Z isomers"],"items":[["But-2-ene",0],["Pent-2-ene",0],["1,2-dichloroethene",0],["But-1-ene",1],["Propene",1],["2-methylbut-2-ene",1],["Ethene",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagez","caption":"Flagship lab: twist a C=C and watch it lock, swap groups to make E and Z, rank groups by atomic number, and build your own alkenes.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_s5dZ0xzD5I":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Where does the 19th electron of potassium go?","choices":["3d, because shell 3 is not full","4s, because 4s is lower in energy than 3d","3p"],"answer":1,"hint":"Electrons fill the lowest energy sub-shell that still has room.","explain":"After 3p is full, 4s is lower in energy than 3d, so potassium is 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ 4s¹."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which is the configuration of oxygen (atomic number 8)?","choices":["1s² 2s² 2p⁴","1s² 2s² 2p² 3s²","1s² 2s⁶"],"answer":0,"hint":"2s holds only 2, and 2p can hold up to 6.","explain":"1s² 2s² takes 4 electrons and the other 4 go into 2p: 1s² 2s² 2p⁴. A p sub-shell holds 6, so nothing spills into 3s."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"How many unpaired electrons does oxygen (2p⁴) have?","choices":["4","0","2"],"answer":2,"hint":"Put one electron in each of the three 2p orbitals first, then pair the fourth.","explain":"Three electrons go one per 2p orbital, and the fourth pairs with one of them. That leaves 2 unpaired electrons."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"A neutral atom has 1s² 2s², then {n} electrons in 2p. How many electrons are in its second shell?","answer":"2 + n","hint":"Shell 2 is made of the 2s and 2p sub-shells.","explain":"Shell 2 contains the 2 electrons in 2s and the {n} in 2p, so it holds 2 + {n} = {=2+n} electrons. 1s belongs to shell 1.","params":{"n":[1,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"What is the most electrons one orbital can hold?","choices":["2, with opposite spins","8","6"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of one box in the box diagram, not a whole sub-shell.","explain":"Any single orbital, s, p or d, holds at most 2 electrons, and they must have opposite spins. 6 is a whole p sub-shell; 8 is a whole shell 2."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Chlorine is 2, 8, 7 in the shell model. How is its outer shell written with sub-shells?","choices":["3s² 3p⁵","3p⁷","3s⁷"],"answer":0,"hint":"3s holds 2, and the rest go into 3p.","explain":"The 7 electrons in shell 3 are 3s² 3p⁵. An s sub-shell cannot hold more than 2, and 3p holds up to 6."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which statement about an s orbital is correct?","choices":["It is a sphere centred on the nucleus","It is a fixed circular path the electron travels","It is dumbbell-shaped"],"answer":0,"hint":"An orbital is a region of likely position, not a track.","explain":"An s orbital is a spherical region where the electron is likely to be found. It is not a path like a planet's orbit."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"An atom has 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s², then {n} electrons in 3p. How many electrons are in its third shell?","answer":"2 + n","hint":"Shell 3 here is the 3s and 3p sub-shells together.","explain":"Shell 3 holds the 2 electrons in 3s and the {n} in 3p: 2 + {n} = {=2+n}. In the shell model that is the outer-shell number.","params":{"n":[1,6]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"What is the electron configuration of carbon (atomic number 6)?","choices":["1s² 2s² 2p⁶","1s² 2s² 2p²","1s² 2s⁴"],"answer":1,"hint":"Fill 1s, then 2s, then 2p, and make the small numbers add up to 6.","explain":"1s takes 2, 2s takes 2, and the last 2 go into 2p: 1s² 2s² 2p². The superscripts add to 6, the atomic number."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"An element has 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s², then {n} electrons in 3p. What is its atomic number?","answer":"12 + n","hint":"Add up all the electrons: 2 + 2 + 6 + 2, then the 3p electrons.","explain":"Before 3p there are 2 + 2 + 6 + 2 = 12 electrons, so the atomic number is 12 + {n} = {=12+n}. A neutral atom has as many electrons as protons.","params":{"n":[1,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"How many unpaired electrons does nitrogen (1s² 2s² 2p³) have?","choices":["1","3","0"],"answer":1,"hint":"Three 2p orbitals, three electrons. Do they pair up or spread out?","explain":"By Hund's rule the three 2p electrons go one per orbital with parallel spins, so all three are unpaired."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"How many electrons can {k} full p sub-shells hold altogether?","answer":"6 * k","hint":"A p sub-shell has three orbitals, and each orbital holds two electrons.","explain":"Each p sub-shell holds 3 × 2 = 6 electrons, so {k} of them hold 6 × {k} = {=6*k}.","params":{"k":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"What shape is a p orbital?","choices":["A sphere","A ring around the nucleus","A dumbbell, two lobes on either side of the nucleus"],"answer":2,"hint":"Picture the shape that points along the x, y or z axis.","explain":"Each p orbital is dumbbell-shaped, with two lobes on opposite sides of the nucleus. The three p orbitals point along x, y and z. An s orbital is the sphere."}],"explain":{"kid":["The shell model says shells hold 2, 8 and 8 electrons. Each shell is really split into sub-shells. They are called s, p and d. An s sub-shell holds 2 electrons. A p sub-shell holds 6. A d sub-shell holds 10. Shell 2 is 2s and 2p, so it holds 2 + 6 = 8.","A sub-shell is made of orbitals. An orbital is a region where an electron is likely to be found. One orbital holds 2 electrons at most, and they must spin in opposite directions. An s orbital is shaped like a sphere. A p sub-shell has three orbitals. Each one is a dumbbell, and they point along x, y and z.","Electrons fill the lowest energy first: 1s, 2s, 2p, 3s, 3p, then 4s, then 3d. 4s comes before 3d because it is lower in energy. So potassium puts its 19th electron in 4s, not 3d.","In a p sub-shell, electrons go into separate orbitals first, with the same spin. They only pair up when each orbital has one. This is Hund's rule. Electrons repel, so they spread out. Carbon is written 1s² 2s² 2p². The small numbers count the electrons in each sub-shell, and they add up to the atomic number."],"adult":["This topic refines the 2, 8, 8 shell model that students meet at 14 to 16. Each shell n contains sub-shells (s, p, d), each sub-shell contains orbitals (1, 3 or 5), and each orbital holds up to two electrons of opposite spin. Configurations are written as 1s² 2s² 2p², with superscripts that sum to the atomic number.","The lab is an orbital box diagram ordered by energy. Learners build carbon, nitrogen and oxygen by tapping boxes, then add the 19th electron to argon to see 4s fill before 3d, and split chlorine's 2, 8, 7 into sub-shells. The lab never blocks a wrong fill. It flags an Aufbau slip or a Hund slip so the rule is found, not just told.","Common mistakes: thinking a p sub-shell holds 2 electrons like s, rather than 6 across three orbitals; pairing electrons in one p orbital before the others have one; putting the 19th electron in 3d because shell 3 is not yet full; and mixing up the shell number with the number of electrons. Another slip is to picture an orbital as a fixed path, like a planet's orbit, when it is a region of likely position.","To check understanding, ask for the configuration of an element from 1 to 20 and for the number of unpaired electrons in nitrogen or oxygen. Then ask why the sum of the superscripts must equal the atomic number."]},"familyTip":"Use egg boxes as orbitals: each cup takes two eggs, one pointing up and one down. Ask your teenager to fill three cups for 2p with four eggs and explain why no cup gets a second egg until all three have one.","keyIdea":"Each shell splits into sub-shells: s holds 2 electrons, p holds 6 and d holds 10. A sub-shell is made of orbitals, and one orbital holds at most 2 electrons with opposite spins. An s orbital is a sphere; a p sub-shell has three dumbbell-shaped orbitals at right angles. Electrons fill the lowest energy first, in the order 1s 2s 2p 3s 3p 4s 3d, and in a p sub-shell they go one per orbital before any pair. So carbon is 1s² 2s² 2p², and potassium is 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ 4s¹.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which configurations follow the filling order and the capacity of each sub-shell?","opts":{"bins":["Correct ground state","Breaks a rule"],"items":[["Carbon: 1s² 2s² 2p²",0],["Sodium: 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s¹",0],["Potassium: … 3p⁶ 4s¹",0],["Nitrogen: 1s² 2s² 2p² 3s¹",1],["Potassium: … 3p⁶ 3d¹",1],["Lithium: 1s³",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagorb","caption":"Flagship lab: see s and p orbital shapes, fill an orbital box diagram by tapping, find Hund's rule and why 4s fills before 3d.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_KVbGIY9mzv":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Where is the electron density of a π bond?","choices":["On the axis between the nuclei","Above and below the bond axis","Around one atom only"],"answer":1,"hint":"π bonds come from p orbitals side by side.","explain":"Sideways overlap of p orbitals puts the density in two regions, above and below the axis. Both parts are one π bond."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which statement about a C=C double bond is correct?","choices":["It is two σ bonds","It is one σ bond and one π bond","It is two π bonds"],"answer":1,"hint":"Only one bond can lie on the axis.","explain":"Only one end-on overlap fits on the axis, so a double bond is one σ plus one π."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"An alkane has {n} carbon atoms, formula CₙH₂ₙ₊₂. How many σ bonds does it have?","answer":"3*n+1","hint":"Count the C–C links and the C–H bonds; every single bond is one σ.","explain":"{=n-1} C–C bonds plus {=2*n+2} C–H bonds gives {=3*n+1} σ bonds and no π bonds.","params":{"n":[1,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"How many σ and π bonds are in ethyne, HC≡CH?","choices":["3 σ and 2 π","5 σ and 0 π","1 σ and 2 π"],"answer":0,"hint":"Two C–H bonds plus a triple bond. Do not forget the C–H bonds.","explain":"Two C–H σ bonds, plus the triple bond's one σ and two π: 3 σ and 2 π."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"An s orbital overlaps a p orbital that points along the bond axis. Which bond forms?","choices":["A π bond","A σ bond","A hydrogen bond"],"answer":1,"hint":"Is the overlap end-on or sideways?","explain":"s is a sphere, so any overlap with it is end-on: the s–p overlap gives a σ bond."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"A molecule has {a} single bonds and {b} double bonds, and no triple bonds. How many σ bonds does it have?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Every bond between two atoms, single or double, contains exactly one σ.","explain":"Each of the {a} single bonds is one σ, and each of the {b} double bonds also has one σ: {=a+b} σ bonds, plus {b} π.","params":{"a":[3,9],"b":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Why does ethene decolourise bromine water but ethane does not?","choices":["Ethene's π electrons lie off the axis, open to attack by bromine","Ethane has no electrons to share","Ethene's σ bond is weaker than ethane's"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about where the π electrons are.","explain":"The π electrons sit above and below the axis, so the electrophile bromine can reach them and add across C=C. Ethane has only σ bonds, held on the axes."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"One end of ethene is twisted by 90°. What happens to its bonds?","choices":["Both bonds break","The σ bond is unchanged but the π overlap falls to zero","Nothing changes"],"answer":1,"hint":"Which bond looks the same from every angle round the axis?","explain":"σ is symmetric round the axis, so it survives. The p orbitals end up at right angles, so the π bond breaks."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Two orbitals overlap end-on, along the line between the nuclei. What kind of bond forms?","choices":["A σ bond","A π bond","No bond"],"answer":0,"hint":"Where is the shared electron density: on the axis or beside it?","explain":"End-on overlap puts the electron density on the axis between the nuclei: a σ bond."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"An alkene has {n} carbon atoms and one C=C, formula CₙH₂ₙ. How many σ bonds does it have?","answer":"3*n-1","hint":"Count the C–C links (n − 1) and the C–H bonds (2n). Each link has exactly one σ.","explain":"There are {=n-1} carbon–carbon links and {=2*n} C–H bonds, each with one σ: {=3*n-1} σ bonds. The double bond adds one π.","params":{"n":[2,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Why can the C=C bond in but-2-ene not rotate at room temperature?","choices":["Turning it would break the σ bond","Turning it moves the p orbitals out of line and breaks the π bond","The carbon atoms are too heavy to turn"],"answer":1,"hint":"Which bond depends on the p orbitals staying parallel?","explain":"The σ bond is symmetric round the axis, so twisting does not affect it. The π bond needs parallel p orbitals; at 90° its overlap is zero."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A molecule has {a} C=C double bonds and {b} C≡C triple bonds. How many π bonds does it have in total?","answer":"a+2*b","hint":"Each double bond has one π. Each triple bond has two π. Single bonds have none.","explain":"{a} double bonds give {a} π, and {b} triple bonds give {=2*b} π: {=a+2*b} π bonds in total.","params":{"a":[1,4],"b":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"C–C is 347 kJ/mol and C=C is 612 kJ/mol. About how strong is the π part of C=C?","choices":["About 265 kJ/mol, weaker than σ","About 347 kJ/mol, the same as σ","About 612 kJ/mol"],"answer":0,"hint":"Take away the σ part from the whole double bond.","explain":"612 − 347 ≈ 265 kJ/mol. That is less than 347, so the π bond is weaker than the σ bond."}],"explain":{"kid":["A covalent bond is a shared pair of electrons. Each atom lends an orbital. The bond forms where the two orbitals overlap. How they overlap gives two kinds of bond.","If the orbitals meet head on, along the line between the nuclei, you get a σ bond. Its electrons sit on that line. Two s orbitals do this. So do an s and a p, or two p orbitals that point at each other. Every single bond is one σ bond.","Now take two p orbitals that stand side by side, both at right angles to the bond. They overlap above the line and below it. That is a π bond. It is one bond in two parts. A C=C double bond is one σ bond plus one π bond. A triple bond is one σ plus two π.","Try to twist one end of a C=C. The p orbitals turn out of line, and the overlap drops. At 90 degrees it is gone. So C=C cannot turn unless the π bond breaks. The π bond is also weaker than the σ bond, and its electrons are out in the open. That is where alkenes react."],"adult":["This topic, for ages 16 to 18, explains bonds by the way orbitals overlap. End-on (axial) overlap gives a σ bond with electron density on the internuclear axis. Sideways overlap of parallel p orbitals gives a π bond with density in two regions, above and below the axis. Learners use it to explain the C=C bond, its lack of rotation and its reactivity.","The numbers come from mean bond enthalpies: C–C is about 347 kJ/mol and C=C about 612 kJ/mol. Taking the σ part of C=C as roughly a C–C bond, the π part is about 265 kJ/mol, so C=C is less than twice C–C. Sideways overlap is smaller than end-on overlap, which is why the π bond is weaker.","Common mistakes: calling a double bond two σ bonds; drawing a π bond as two separate bonds because it has two lobes; forgetting that every C–H bond is σ when counting; and thinking the σ bond breaks when C=C twists. The σ bond is symmetric round the axis, so rotation leaves it intact. It is the π overlap that is lost.","This idea links to E/Z isomers, which exist because C=C is locked, and to electrophilic addition. The exposed π electrons attract electrophiles such as bromine, so alkenes add across the double bond, while alkanes with only σ bonds do not."]},"familyTip":"Hold two pens tip to tip for a σ bond, then side by side for a π bond. Twist one pen and see how the side-by-side contact is lost while tip-to-tip stays put.","keyIdea":"A σ (sigma) bond forms when two orbitals overlap end-on, so the shared electrons sit on the axis between the nuclei. A π (pi) bond forms when two parallel p orbitals overlap sideways, so its electrons sit above and below the axis. A single bond is one σ; a C=C double bond is one σ plus one π; a triple bond is one σ plus two π. Twisting a C=C pulls the p orbitals out of line and breaks the π bond, so C=C cannot rotate. The π bond is weaker than σ and its electrons are exposed, which is why alkenes react at the double bond.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"σ only, or σ plus π? Ask: is it a single bond, or a double or triple bond?","opts":{"bins":["σ only","Has π bonds too"],"items":[["C–H in methane",0],["C–C in ethane",0],["O–H in water",0],["C=C in ethene",1],["C≡C in ethyne",1],["C=O in carbon dioxide",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagsigma","caption":"Flagship lab: bring orbitals together end-on for σ, twist p orbitals into line for π, twist ethane and ethene, count bonds in propene and weigh π against σ.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_MtGG21CsSZ":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"X-ray data show that every C–C bond in benzene is 0.139 nm long. Why does this not fit a ring of alternating single and double bonds?","choices":["That ring would have three bonds of about 0.154 nm and three of about 0.134 nm","That ring would have all six bonds at 0.154 nm","Bond lengths in rings cannot be predicted"],"answer":0,"hint":"Single and double bonds have different lengths.","explain":"C–C single bonds are about 0.154 nm and C=C about 0.134 nm. Alternating bonds would give two lengths, but benzene has one length, between the two."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"A hydrocarbon has the formula C{n}H{=2*n+2-2*u}. It has no triple bonds. How many rings plus C=C bonds does it have?","answer":"u","params":{"n":[4,8],"u":[1,4]},"hint":"Work out the H count of the alkane with the same carbons (2n + 2), find the missing H, then halve.","explain":"The alkane would have {=2*n+2} H. {=2*u} are missing, and each ring or C=C removes 2, so there are {u} rings plus C=C bonds."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"A ring molecule with {k} C=C bonds is predicted to release {=120*k} kJ/mol on hydrogenation, but it actually releases {=120*k-s} kJ/mol. By how many kJ/mol is it more stable than predicted?","answer":"s","params":{"k":[2,4],"s":[10,40]},"hint":"Subtract the measured heat from the predicted heat.","explain":"{=120*k} − {=120*k-s} = {s} kJ/mol. The molecule starts {s} kJ/mol lower in energy than the separate C=C model."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which fact does a successful structure for benzene NOT need to explain?","choices":["Benzene boils at about 80 °C","Benzene does not decolourise bromine water","All six C–C bonds in benzene are the same length"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which fact clashes with a ring of three plain C=C bonds?","explain":"The boiling point does not clash with any model of the bonding. The missing addition reaction and the equal bonds both clash with three separate C=C, so a structure must explain them."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Cyclohexene turns bromine water colourless, but benzene does not. Why is this surprising?","choices":["Benzene's formula suggests it has even more C=C bonds than cyclohexene","Benzene has no carbon atoms","Cyclohexene is not a hydrocarbon"],"answer":0,"hint":"Compare the hydrogen counts of C₆H₁₀ and C₆H₆.","explain":"Cyclohexene, C₆H₁₀, has one ring and one C=C. Benzene, C₆H₆, looks like it has three C=C, so it should add bromine even more. It does not."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"How many more hydrogen atoms does hexane, C₆H₁₄, have than benzene, C₆H₆?","answer":8,"hint":"Subtract the two hydrogen counts.","explain":"14 − 6 = 8 more hydrogen atoms. That is 4 pairs, so benzene has 4 rings plus C=C bonds."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Why did chemists first draw benzene as a ring with three C=C bonds?","choices":["It fits C₆H₆ while giving every carbon four bonds","It explains why benzene does not react with bromine","X-rays showed three double bonds"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which fact was known first: the formula or the reactions?","explain":"Six carbons, a ring and three C=C give C₆H₆ with four bonds on every carbon. It fits the formula, but not the reactions or the bond lengths."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"A student says: 'Benzene is unreactive, so it must be saturated.' What is the mistake?","choices":["By formula benzene is highly unsaturated; it is just unusually stable","Benzene is saturated, so the student is right","Saturated compounds always react with bromine water"],"answer":0,"hint":"Saturated means it holds the most H possible. Does C₆H₆?","explain":"C₆H₆ holds 8 fewer H than C₆H₁₄, so it is unsaturated. Its low reactivity is the puzzle a structure must explain, not a sign that it is saturated."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Hexane is C₆H₁₄ and benzene is C₆H₆. How many rings plus C=C bonds must benzene have?","choices":["4","8","1"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find how many H atoms are missing, then remember each ring or C=C removes a pair.","explain":"14 − 6 = 8 hydrogen atoms are missing. Each ring or C=C removes 2, so benzene has 8 ÷ 2 = 4 rings plus C=C bonds."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A hydrocarbon has {n} carbon atoms and {u} rings or C=C bonds in total, and no triple bonds. How many hydrogen atoms does it have?","answer":"2*n+2-2*u","params":{"n":[4,8],"u":[1,3]},"hint":"Start from the saturated alkane, which has 2n + 2 hydrogen atoms, then take away 2 for each ring or C=C.","explain":"The alkane with {n} carbons has {=2*n+2} H. Taking away 2 for each of the {u} rings or C=C leaves {=2*n+2-2*u} hydrogen atoms."},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Benzene is shaken with bromine water at room temperature, in the dark. What do you see?","choices":["The orange colour turns colourless at once","The orange colour stays","A white solid forms"],"answer":1,"hint":"Does benzene react like an alkene?","explain":"Benzene does not add bromine across its bonds the way an alkene does, so the bromine water stays orange. This is part of the puzzle."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Hydrogenating one C=C in a ring releases about 120 kJ/mol. If a ring molecule had {k} separate C=C bonds, how much heat (kJ/mol) would full hydrogenation be predicted to release?","answer":"120*k","params":{"k":[1,4]},"hint":"Each separate C=C is expected to release the same amount, so multiply.","explain":"Each of the {k} C=C bonds releases about 120 kJ/mol, so the prediction is {k} × 120 = {=120*k} kJ/mol."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"A ring with three C=C bonds is predicted to release 360 kJ/mol on hydrogenation, but benzene releases only 208 kJ/mol. What does this show?","choices":["Benzene is about 152 kJ/mol more stable than that structure","Benzene has only about two C=C bonds","The measurement must be wrong"],"answer":0,"hint":"Less heat out means the starting molecule was lower in energy.","explain":"Both reactions end at cyclohexane. Benzene gives out 360 − 208 = 152 kJ/mol less, so it starts about 152 kJ/mol lower in energy: it is more stable than three separate C=C."}],"explain":{"kid":["Benzene is a liquid made of carbon and hydrogen. Its formula is C₆H₆. Hexane also has six carbons, but it has 14 hydrogens. Each carbon in hexane has four single bonds, so it holds as many H as it can. We call that saturated.","Benzene is short of 8 hydrogens. Each C=C bond takes away 2 H. So does joining the chain into a ring. So benzene needs 4 of these in total. A ring with three C=C bonds fits the formula. That was the first guess.","Now test the guess. An alkene such as cyclohexene turns orange bromine water colourless. It adds the bromine across its C=C. Benzene does not. The orange stays. A molecule with three C=C bonds should react three times over, not zero times.","There are more clues. Adding hydrogen to benzene gives out much less heat than three C=C bonds would. And X-rays show that all six bonds in the ring are the same length. So the simple guess is wrong. A better structure has to explain every one of these facts."],"adult":["This topic, for ages 16 to 18, sets up the benzene problem before learners meet Kekulé's model or delocalisation. The formula C₆H₆ is 8 hydrogen atoms short of the saturated alkane C₆H₁₄. Each ring or C=C removes two hydrogens, so benzene has four such units, and cyclohexa-1,3,5-triene fits the formula.","The chemistry contradicts that structure. Benzene does not decolourise bromine water at room temperature, so it does not undergo electrophilic addition like an alkene. Hydrogenating cyclohexene releases about 120 kJ/mol, so three separate C=C bonds predict about 360 kJ/mol, but benzene releases only about 208 kJ/mol. Benzene is therefore about 152 kJ/mol more stable than the triene.","X-ray diffraction gives one more fact. A C–C single bond is about 0.154 nm and a C=C double bond about 0.134 nm, but all six bonds in benzene are 0.139 nm. A ring of alternating single and double bonds would show two different lengths.","Common mistakes: counting missing hydrogen atoms instead of pairs, so learners say 8 double bonds; forgetting that a ring also removes two hydrogens; and thinking benzene is barely unsaturated because it is unreactive. Benzene is highly unsaturated by formula. The puzzle is that it behaves as if it were not, so any proposed structure must fit the formula, the missing addition reactions, the extra stability and the equal bond lengths."]},"familyTip":"Write out hexane and benzene side by side and count the hydrogen atoms. Ask: if benzene really had three double bonds like an alkene, what should it do to bromine water, and what does it actually do?","keyIdea":"Benzene, C₆H₆, has 8 fewer hydrogen atoms than hexane, C₆H₁₄, so its formula needs 4 rings or C=C bonds: a ring with three C=C fits. But benzene does not decolourise bromine water, it releases only 208 kJ/mol on hydrogenation instead of the 360 that three C=C predict, and all six of its C–C bonds are the same length, 0.139 nm. Any structure for benzene must explain all of these facts at once.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Does each fact fit a simple ring with three C=C bonds, or clash with it?","opts":{"bins":["Fits three C=C","Clashes with three C=C"],"items":[["Formula C₆H₆",0],["Each carbon makes 4 bonds",0],["Adds 3 H₂ to make cyclohexane",0],["Bromine water stays orange",1],["Releases only 208 kJ/mol with H₂",1],["All six bonds 0.139 nm",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagbenz","caption":"Flagship lab: build C₆H₆, test it with bromine water, stack hydrogenation energies, measure the ring bonds and pick the facts a structure must explain.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_6iKbJmMBwX":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which equation is a propagation step?","choices":["•CH₃ + Cl₂ → CH₃Cl + Cl•","2Cl• → Cl₂","Cl₂ → 2Cl•"],"answer":0,"hint":"Propagation uses up one radical and makes another.","explain":"•CH₃ + Cl₂ → CH₃Cl + Cl• has one radical in and one out, so the chain carries on: propagation."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Why does chlorinating methane give a mixture of products?","choices":["Cl• can take H atoms from chloromethanes as well as from methane","UV light breaks the carbon atom apart","Chlorine reacts with HCl"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which molecules in the flask still have C–H bonds?","explain":"CH₃Cl, CH₂Cl₂ and CHCl₃ still have C–H bonds, so Cl• can substitute them again, giving a mixture up to CCl₄."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"How can a chemist make chloromethane, CH₃Cl, the main product?","choices":["Use a large excess of methane","Use a large excess of chlorine","Do the reaction in the dark"],"answer":0,"hint":"Make it likely that each Cl• meets CH₄ rather than CH₃Cl.","explain":"With far more CH₄ than CH₃Cl in the flask, Cl• almost always hits methane, so further substitution is rare."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"How many moles of Cl₂ are needed to turn {n} mol of CH₄ completely into CHCl₃?","answer":"3*n","params":{"n":[1,8]},"hint":"CHCl₃ has 3 Cl atoms in place of 3 H atoms. Each Cl₂ replaces one H.","explain":"CH₄ + 3Cl₂ → CHCl₃ + 3HCl, so {n} mol of CH₄ needs 3 × {n} = {=3*n} mol of Cl₂."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Ethane, C₂H₆, is found in small amounts after methane reacts with chlorine. What does this show?","choices":["Methyl radicals, •CH₃, formed and two of them joined","Chlorine contains carbon","The UV light made new carbon atoms"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which termination step gives a two-carbon product?","explain":"•CH₃ + •CH₃ → C₂H₆. Finding ethane is evidence that methyl radicals form during the reaction."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"What kind of bond breaking happens when UV light hits Cl₂?","choices":["Homolytic: each Cl atom keeps one electron","Heterolytic: one Cl takes both electrons","No bond breaks"],"answer":0,"hint":"The products are two identical radicals.","explain":"The bond splits evenly, one electron to each atom, giving two Cl• radicals. That is homolytic fission."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"Ethane, C₂H₆, reacts with chlorine in UV light until {k} hydrogen atoms have been replaced. How many hydrogen atoms are left on the product?","answer":"6-k","params":{"k":[1,5]},"hint":"Ethane starts with 6 hydrogen atoms. Each substitution removes one.","explain":"6 − {k} = {=6-k} hydrogen atoms are left, and {k} chlorine atoms have taken their places."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"A student writes CH₄ + Cl₂ → CH₃Cl + H₂. What is wrong?","choices":["The H atom leaves with the other Cl atom as HCl","Nothing is wrong","Methane cannot react with chlorine at all"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count the atoms on each side.","explain":"The left side has 4 H and 2 Cl. CH₃Cl + HCl also has 4 H and 2 Cl. CH₃Cl + H₂ has 5 H and 1 Cl, so it does not balance."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Methane reacts with chlorine in UV light. What are the products of the first substitution?","choices":["CH₃Cl and HCl","CH₃Cl and H₂","CH₂Cl₂ only"],"answer":0,"hint":"One H leaves the methane. What does it join up with?","explain":"A Cl atom replaces one H. The H that leaves joins the other Cl atom from Cl₂, making HCl: CH₄ + Cl₂ → CH₃Cl + HCl."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Methane is fully substituted by chlorine to make CCl₄. Starting from {n} mol of CH₄, how many moles of HCl form?","answer":"4*n","params":{"n":[1,6]},"hint":"Each of the 4 hydrogen atoms leaves as one HCl.","explain":"CH₄ + 4Cl₂ → CCl₄ + 4HCl, so {n} mol of CH₄ gives 4 × {n} = {=4*n} mol of HCl."},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Why is UV light needed for methane to react with chlorine?","choices":["It splits Cl–Cl into two Cl• radicals","It splits C–H bonds in methane","It removes oxygen from the flask"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which bond is weaker, Cl–Cl or C–H?","explain":"Cl–Cl (about 242 kJ/mol) is much weaker than C–H (about 413). UV light splits it homolytically into two Cl•, which start the chain."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"An alkane CₙH₂ₙ₊₂ with n = {n} has its hydrogen atoms replaced by chlorine one at a time. How many substitution steps can happen in total?","answer":"2*n+2","params":{"n":[1,6]},"hint":"Each step replaces one H. Count the hydrogens.","explain":"The alkane has 2 × {n} + 2 = {=2*n+2} hydrogen atoms, and each substitution replaces one, so {=2*n+2} steps are possible."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Which equation is a termination step?","choices":["•CH₃ + Cl• → CH₃Cl","Cl• + CH₄ → HCl + •CH₃","Cl₂ → 2Cl•"],"answer":0,"hint":"In termination, radicals go in and none come out.","explain":"Two radicals join and no radical is left: termination. Cl• + CH₄ is propagation and Cl₂ → 2Cl• is initiation."}],"explain":{"kid":["Alkanes such as methane are not very reactive. But mix methane with chlorine and shine UV light on it, and they react. A chlorine atom takes the place of one hydrogen atom. That swap is called substitution. The hydrogen leaves with the other chlorine atom as HCl.","Why is light needed? In the dark, every bond holds its pair of electrons. UV light has enough energy to split the Cl–Cl bond. That bond is weaker than C–H. Each chlorine atom keeps one electron. An atom with one unpaired electron is called a radical, written Cl•.","A radical wants a partner for its lone electron. Cl• pulls an H atom off methane to make HCl. That leaves •CH₃, which is also a radical. •CH₃ pulls a Cl atom off Cl₂. That makes CH₃Cl and a new Cl•. So the steps go round and round, like a chain. The chain only stops when two radicals meet and join.","Here is the catch. Cl• does not choose. It can take an H from CH₃Cl as easily as from CH₄. So you get CH₂Cl₂, CHCl₃ and even CCl₄ as well. The result is a mixture. Using lots of extra methane makes CH₃Cl the main product."],"adult":["This topic, for ages 16 to 18, covers free-radical substitution. An alkane and a halogen in UV light give a haloalkane and a hydrogen halide, for example CH₄ + Cl₂ → CH₃Cl + HCl. It is often the first mechanism learners meet. It shows how alkanes, with only strong, non-polar σ bonds, can react at all.","Initiation: UV light splits Cl–Cl (about 242 kJ/mol) evenly, one electron to each atom. This is homolytic fission, and it gives two Cl• radicals. The light does not break C–H (about 413 kJ/mol). Propagation: Cl• + CH₄ → HCl + •CH₃, then •CH₃ + Cl₂ → CH₃Cl + Cl•. The radical comes back each time, so one split can make thousands of molecules. Termination: two radicals join to give Cl₂, CH₃Cl or C₂H₆.","Further substitution happens because Cl• can take a hydrogen from any C–H bond in the flask, including those in chloromethane. So the product is a mixture of CH₃Cl, CH₂Cl₂, CHCl₃ and CCl₄, which must be separated by fractional distillation. Excess methane makes CH₃Cl the main product. Excess chlorine pushes the mix towards CCl₄.","Common mistakes: writing H₂ instead of HCl as the second product; saying the light breaks the C–H bond; calling Cl₂ → 2Cl• a propagation step; and missing that ethane from termination is evidence for methyl radicals. Take care with curly arrows too: a radical step moves single electrons, so it uses half-headed arrows."]},"familyTip":"Ask: why does the reaction need light, and what does that light actually break? Then count: if every chlorine swaps for one hydrogen, how many HCl molecules come from making CCl₄?","keyIdea":"In UV light, an alkane reacts with chlorine or bromine by substitution: a halogen atom replaces a hydrogen atom, and the hydrogen leaves as a hydrogen halide. For methane, CH₄ + Cl₂ → CH₃Cl + HCl. UV light is needed to split Cl–Cl into two Cl• radicals; a chain of radical steps then repeats until two radicals meet. Because a radical can take any hydrogen, including those in the products, a mixture of CH₃Cl, CH₂Cl₂, CHCl₃ and CCl₄ forms.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Sort each step of the chlorination of methane by its stage.","opts":{"bins":["Initiation","Propagation","Termination"],"items":[["Cl₂ → 2Cl•",0],["Cl• + CH₄ → HCl + •CH₃",1],["•CH₃ + Cl₂ → CH₃Cl + Cl•",1],["Cl• + Cl• → Cl₂",2],["•CH₃ + Cl• → CH₃Cl",2],["•CH₃ + •CH₃ → C₂H₆",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagchlor","caption":"Flagship lab: switch on UV to split Cl₂, run the radical chain, end it three ways, then steer the product mixture with the Cl₂ : CH₄ ratio.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_5H0jB_IQdE":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Propanal and propanone are both C₃H₆O. How do they differ?","choices":["Propanal has C=O at the end; propanone has it in the middle","Propanone has more hydrogens","They are the same compound"],"answer":0,"hint":"Same formula: look at where the C=O sits.","explain":"They are isomers. Propanal is an aldehyde with C=O on carbon 1; propanone is a ketone with C=O on carbon 2."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which is the correct name for CH₃CH₂CHO?","choices":["propan-1-al","propanal","propanone"],"answer":1,"hint":"Aldehydes need no number.","explain":"The C=O of an aldehyde is always carbon 1, so no number is written: propanal."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Name CH₃COCH₂CH₂CH₃.","choices":["pentan-4-one","pentan-2-one","pentanal"],"answer":1,"hint":"Number from the end nearest the C=O.","explain":"Counting from the left end, the C=O is on carbon 2, so it is pentan-2-one. Counting from the other end gives 4, which is not the lowest number."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which condensed formula shows a ketone?","choices":["CH₃CH₂CHO","CH₃CH₂CH₂OH","CH₃COCH₂CH₃"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look for CO with carbon on both sides.","explain":"CH₃COCH₂CH₃ has CO between two carbon groups: butanone. CHO is an aldehyde and OH is an alcohol."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"A cyanide ion, CN⁻, attacks a C=O. Which atom does it bond to, and why?","choices":["The oxygen, because oxygen is the reactive atom","The carbon, because it is δ+ and attracts the lone pair","Either atom, at random"],"answer":1,"hint":"A nucleophile looks for positive charge.","explain":"CN⁻ is a nucleophile with a lone pair. It is attracted to the δ+ carbon and repelled by the δ− oxygen."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Why is C=C in an alkene not attacked by nucleophiles like C=O is?","choices":["C=C is not polar, so neither carbon is δ+","C=C has no electrons","C=C is a single bond"],"answer":0,"hint":"Compare the two atoms in each bond.","explain":"Both atoms in C=C are carbon, so they pull equally and neither is δ+. Alkenes react with electrophiles instead."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"How many hydrogens are in a straight-chain ketone with {n} carbons?","choices":["{n}","2 × {n}","2 × {n} + 2"],"answer":1,"hint":"The general formula is CₙH₂ₙO.","explain":"Aldehydes and ketones fit CₙH₂ₙO, so a ketone with {n} carbons has 2 × {n} hydrogens.","params":{"n":[3,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Why does butanone need no position number?","choices":["Butanone is an aldehyde","In a 4-carbon chain, carbon 2 is the only place a ketone C=O can go","Numbers are only used for rings"],"answer":1,"hint":"Try putting the C=O on each carbon of a 4-carbon chain.","explain":"Carbons 1 and 4 give butanal, and carbons 2 and 3 are the same position counted from each end. So there is only one butanone."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"An aldehyde has {n} carbons. Which group is at the end of its chain?","choices":["–OH","–COOH","–CHO"],"answer":2,"hint":"The C=O carbon in an aldehyde also holds an H.","explain":"Every aldehyde, whatever its length, ends in –CHO: the C=O carbon is bonded to one H.","params":{"n":[2,6]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"In the C=O bond, which atom is δ+ and why?","choices":["Oxygen, because it has more electrons","Carbon, because oxygen pulls the shared electrons towards itself","Neither, the bond is shared equally"],"answer":1,"hint":"Compare electronegativity: O is 3.44, C is 2.55.","explain":"Oxygen is more electronegative, so it pulls the bonding electrons closer. Oxygen becomes δ− and carbon is left δ+."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"An aldehyde has {n} carbons in a straight chain. Where is its C=O?","choices":["On carbon 1, at the end of the chain","On the middle carbon","On any carbon"],"answer":0,"hint":"Aldehydes have the –CHO group.","explain":"In an aldehyde the C=O is always at the end, so it is carbon 1. With {n} carbons, the other carbons follow on from it.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Name CH₃CH₂COCH₃.","choices":["butanal","butan-3-one","butanone"],"answer":2,"hint":"Count the carbons, then decide if C=O is at the end or inside.","explain":"There are 4 carbons and the C=O is inside the chain, so it is a ketone: butanone. No number is needed because carbon 2 is the only ketone position."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"A straight-chain aldehyde has {n} carbons. What is its formula?","choices":["CₙH₂ₙO with n = {n}","CₙH₂ₙ₊₂O with n = {n}","CₙH₂ₙO₂ with n = {n}"],"answer":0,"hint":"Aldehydes and ketones share the general formula of one C=O on an alkane chain.","explain":"Aldehydes and ketones have the general formula CₙH₂ₙO. With {n} carbons there are twice as many hydrogens and one oxygen.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Which is the smallest possible ketone?","choices":["ethanone, with 2 carbons","propanone, with 3 carbons","methanone, with 1 carbon"],"answer":1,"hint":"A ketone needs a carbon on each side of the C=O.","explain":"The C=O carbon needs a carbon on each side, so a ketone needs at least 3 carbons. Propanone is the smallest; ethanone does not exist."}],"explain":{"kid":["A carbonyl group is a carbon joined to an oxygen by a double bond, C=O. The two atoms share electrons, but not fairly. Oxygen pulls harder. Its electronegativity is 3.44, and carbon's is 2.55. So the electrons sit nearer the oxygen. Oxygen gets a small negative charge, δ−. Carbon is left with a small positive charge, δ+.","Where the C=O sits sets the family. At the end of a chain, the C=O carbon also holds an H. We write this as CHO. That is an aldehyde, and its name ends in al. Inside the chain, there is a carbon on each side. We write this as CO. That is a ketone, and its name ends in one.","Propanal and propanone both have the formula C3H6O. In propanal the C=O is on carbon 1. In propanone it is on carbon 2. Same atoms, different family. The stem gives the number of carbons: meth is 1, eth is 2, prop is 3, but is 4 and pent is 5.","A ketone needs a carbon on each side, so the smallest one has 3 carbons. Butanone needs no number, since carbon 2 is the only place. In a chain of 5 there are two places, so we write pentan-2-one or pentan-3-one. Count from the end nearest the C=O. Last, the δ+ carbon draws in ions with a lone pair, called nucleophiles."],"adult":["This topic introduces the carbonyl group, C=O, found in aldehydes and ketones. Learners explain why the bond is polar, tell the two families apart by where the C=O sits, and name simple examples such as propanal, propanone and butanone. It sets up later work on oxidation of alcohols and nucleophilic addition.","In the lab, learners place δ+ and δ− on C=O, then slide the C=O along a 3-carbon chain to see propanal turn into propanone with the same formula. They build molecules from names, sort condensed formulas, and send a cyanide ion to the atom it bonds to, comparing C=O with the non-polar C=C.","Common mistakes: putting δ− on carbon; calling CH3COCH3 an aldehyde because it has the letters CHO in it; writing propan-1-al or ethanone; numbering from the wrong end, as in pentan-4-one; and thinking a negative ion bonds to the oxygen. Another mistake is to read CHO and COH as the same thing; COH is not a carbonyl.","A quick check at home or in class: write a condensed formula and ask the learner to point to the C=O, say which family it is, and name it. Then ask which atom a nucleophile would bond to, and why."]},"familyTip":"Write CH3CH2CHO and CH3COCH3 on two cards. Ask which is the aldehyde and which is the ketone, and how they can share the formula C3H6O.","keyIdea":"The carbonyl group C=O is polar: oxygen is more electronegative, so carbon is δ+ and oxygen δ−. In an aldehyde the C=O is at the end of the chain (–CHO, name ends -al); in a ketone it is inside the chain (–CO–, name ends -one). The δ+ carbon is where nucleophiles attack.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which family? Drag each name, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Aldehyde (-al)","Ketone (-one)"],"items":[["methanal",0],["ethanal",0],["butanal",0],["propanone",1],["butanone",1],["pentan-3-one",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagcarbonyl","caption":"Flagship lab: place δ+ and δ− on C=O, turn propanal into propanone, build from names and send a nucleophile to the δ+ carbon.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_S5QIKtrF9e":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Name CH₃CHBrCH₂CH₃.","choices":["2-bromobutane","3-bromobutane","2-bromopropane"],"answer":0,"hint":"Four carbons. Which end is nearer the Br?","explain":"Four carbons is butane. Counting from the left end, Br is on carbon 2, which is lower than 3, so it is 2-bromobutane."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Why does a nucleophile attack the carbon in a C–Cl bond?","choices":["Cl pulls the bond electrons, so the carbon is δ+","The carbon is δ− and attracts it","The chlorine has no electrons left"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which atom is more electronegative, C or Cl?","explain":"Chlorine is more electronegative, so the C–Cl bond is polar with a δ+ carbon that attracts the lone pair of a nucleophile."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"Bond enthalpies: C–Cl 346 kJ/mol and C–I 228 kJ/mol. How much more energy is needed to break {n} mol of C–Cl bonds than {n} mol of C–I bonds, in kJ?","answer":"118*n","params":{"n":[1,6]},"hint":"Find the difference for 1 mol, then multiply.","explain":"346 − 228 = 118 kJ per mol, so for {n} mol it is 118 × {n} = {=118*n} kJ."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"In the silver nitrate test, which haloalkane gives a cream precipitate?","choices":["A bromoalkane (AgBr)","A chloroalkane (AgCl)","An iodoalkane (AgI)"],"answer":0,"hint":"AgCl is white and AgI is yellow.","explain":"AgBr is cream. It forms after the yellow AgI but before the white AgCl."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Why does UV break C–Cl rather than C–F in CCl₂F₂?","choices":["C–Cl is a much weaker bond","C–F is not a covalent bond","Fluorine has no electrons to give"],"answer":0,"hint":"Compare the bond enthalpies: 346 and 467 kJ/mol.","explain":"C–Cl (346 kJ/mol) is weaker than C–F (467 kJ/mol), so stratospheric UV breaks C–Cl and releases Cl•."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"A haloalkane CₙH₂ₙ₊₁Br has n = {n}. Find its relative formula mass. (C = 12, H = 1, Br = 80)","answer":"14*n+81","params":{"n":[1,6]},"hint":"Count C, H and Br atoms, then add their masses.","explain":"12 × {n} + 1 × (2 × {n} + 1) + 80 = {=14*n+81}."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Bromoethane is heated with excess ammonia in ethanol in a sealed tube. What is the main organic product?","choices":["Ethylamine, CH₃CH₂NH₂","Ethanol, CH₃CH₂OH","Ethene, CH₂=CH₂"],"answer":0,"hint":"NH₃ is the nucleophile here. What group replaces Br?","explain":"NH₃ replaces Br, then loses H⁺, giving the amine CH₃CH₂NH₂; excess ammonia mops up the HBr as NH₄Br."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"What is the role of Cl• in the destruction of ozone?","choices":["A catalyst: it is regenerated after each cycle","A reactant that is used up by one O₃","A greenhouse gas that warms the ozone"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at what comes out of the second step: ClO• + O₃ → ?","explain":"ClO• + O₃ → Cl• + 2O₂ gives back Cl•, so it is a catalyst and can destroy many thousands of O₃."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Why were CFCs once used in fridges and spray cans?","choices":["They are unreactive, non-toxic and non-flammable","They react quickly with ozone","They dissolve easily in water"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about what makes a gas safe to use at home.","explain":"CFCs are very stable, not toxic and do not burn. That same stability lets them survive to reach the stratosphere."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Name CH₃CH₂CH₂Br.","choices":["1-bromopropane","3-bromopropane","1-bromobutane"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count the carbons, then number from the end nearer the Br.","explain":"Three carbons gives propane. The Br is on carbon 1 from the nearer end, so it is 1-bromopropane."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A straight-chain haloalkane has {n} carbons and one Cl atom. How many hydrogen atoms does it have?","answer":"2*n+1","params":{"n":[1,8]},"hint":"The alkane has 2n + 2 hydrogens. The Cl takes the place of one.","explain":"The alkane has 2 × {n} + 2 hydrogens; one is replaced by Cl, leaving {=2*n+1}."},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Warm aqueous NaOH reacts with 1-bromobutane. What attacks, and what forms?","choices":["OH⁻ attacks the δ+ carbon; butan-1-ol forms","Na⁺ attacks the bromine; sodium butane forms","OH⁻ attacks the bromine; bromobutanol forms"],"answer":0,"hint":"The nucleophile has a lone pair and a negative charge.","explain":"OH⁻ is the nucleophile. It bonds to the δ+ carbon, Br⁻ leaves, and the product is the alcohol butan-1-ol."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"One Cl• goes round the ozone cycle {k} times. Each cycle destroys 2 O₃. How many O₃ molecules does it destroy?","answer":"2*k","params":{"k":[2,50]},"hint":"Cl• + O₃ → ClO• + O₂, then ClO• + O₃ → Cl• + 2O₂. Count the O₃ in one lap.","explain":"Each lap uses 2 O₃ and gives back the Cl•, so {k} laps destroy 2 × {k} = {=2*k} O₃."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Which order shows the fastest reaction with silver nitrate first?","choices":["iodoalkane, bromoalkane, chloroalkane","chloroalkane, bromoalkane, iodoalkane","They all react at the same rate"],"answer":0,"hint":"The C–X bond has to break. Which one is weakest?","explain":"C–I (228 kJ/mol) is weakest, then C–Br (290), then C–Cl (346), so iodo reacts first and chloro last."}],"explain":{"kid":["Take an alkane and swap one H for a halogen atom. You get a haloalkane. Its name has a prefix for the halogen: fluoro, chloro, bromo or iodo. A number says which carbon it is on. Count from the end that gives the smaller number. So CH₃CHBrCH₃ is 2-bromopropane.","The halogen pulls the shared electrons in the C–X bond towards itself. That leaves the carbon slightly positive, δ+. A nucleophile is a particle with a lone pair, such as OH⁻ or NH₃. It attacks the δ+ carbon, and the halide ion leaves. With warm NaOH, bromoethane turns into ethanol. With ammonia, it turns into ethylamine.","Which haloalkane reacts fastest? You might guess the one with the most polar bond. But the C–X bond has to break, so the weakest bond wins. C–I is the weakest, then C–Br, then C–Cl. With silver nitrate, an iodoalkane gives a yellow solid first, a bromoalkane a cream one next, and a chloroalkane a white one last.","CFCs, such as CCl₂F₂, are haloalkanes with chlorine and fluorine. They do not burn and are not toxic, so they were used in fridges and spray cans. But they are so stable that they last long enough to reach the ozone layer. There, UV light breaks a C–Cl bond and frees a Cl• radical. One Cl• destroys an O₃, then gets free again, over and over. That is why CFCs were banned."],"adult":["This topic, for ages 16 to 18, covers naming haloalkanes, their nucleophilic substitution reactions and the chemistry behind the ban on CFCs. Names follow IUPAC rules: a prefix for each halogen (fluoro, chloro, bromo, iodo), the lowest position numbers, and prefixes in alphabetical order when there are several, as in 1-bromo-2-chloroethane.","Halogens are more electronegative than carbon, so the C–X bond is polar with a δ+ carbon. A nucleophile donates a lone pair to that carbon and the halide leaves. Warm aqueous OH⁻ gives an alcohol, and excess ammonia in ethanol, heated in a sealed tube, gives a primary amine. Hot OH⁻ in ethanol instead favours elimination to an alkene.","Rate is set by bond enthalpy, not polarity. Typical mean values are C–F 467, C–Cl 346, C–Br 290 and C–I 228 kJ/mol, and sources vary a little. Warming each haloalkane with aqueous silver nitrate in ethanol shows the order: AgI (yellow) forms first, then AgBr (cream), then AgCl (white).","In the stratosphere, UV breaks C–Cl, not the stronger C–F, giving Cl•. Then Cl• + O₃ → ClO• + O₂ and ClO• + O₃ → Cl• + 2O₂, so the net change is 2O₃ → 3O₂ with Cl• as a catalyst. The Montreal Protocol (1987) phased CFCs out. Common mistakes: numbering from the wrong end, saying polarity sets the rate, thinking Cl• is used up, and linking the ozone hole to global warming."]},"familyTip":"Look at the label on an old fridge or an aerosol can and see what gas it uses. Then ask: why would a gas that is safe in a kitchen be a problem 20 km up? The answer is the C–Cl bond and UV light.","keyIdea":"A haloalkane is an alkane with a halogen atom in place of a hydrogen, named with a prefix and a position number, as in 2-bromopropane. The C–X bond is polar, so nucleophiles such as OH⁻ and NH₃ attack the δ+ carbon and replace the halogen. The weaker the C–X bond, the faster this happens: iodo > bromo > chloro. In CFCs, UV breaks the weaker C–Cl bond, and each Cl• radical frees itself again and again to destroy ozone, which is why CFCs were banned.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Sort each haloalkane by the number its name needs.","opts":{"bins":["1-…","2-…","No number needed"],"items":[["CH₃CH₂CH₂Cl",0],["CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂Br",0],["CH₃CHICH₃",1],["CH₃CHBrCH₂CH₃",1],["CH₃CH₂CHClCH₃",1],["CH₃Cl",2],["CH₃CH₂Br",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flaghalo","caption":"Flagship lab: name haloalkanes, swap the halogen, race them with silver nitrate, then chase a CFC's Cl• through ozone.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_LYuA6D8Ca5":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which one floats?","choices":["An apple","A key","A stone"],"answer":0,"hint":"Two of these are heavy for their size. One bobs on top.","explain":"An apple floats. A key and a stone sink to the bottom."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which one sinks?","choices":["A small stone","A big beach ball","A leaf"],"answer":0,"hint":"Small things can sink. Which one is heavy for its size?","explain":"The small stone sinks. The big beach ball and the leaf float on top."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Ben says, \"Big things sink.\" Is he right?","choices":["No, a big log floats","Yes, big things always sink","Yes, and small things always float"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of a big log in a river.","explain":"Ben is not right. A big log floats and a tiny coin sinks, so size does not tell you."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"How can you make a clay ball float?","choices":["Squash it into a boat shape","Make it a smaller ball","Paint it"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which change makes a new shape that holds the water out?","explain":"Make it into a boat shape. A smaller ball still sinks, and paint does not change it."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"A toy boat floats. You put in {n} marbles, then lots more, and water comes in. What happens?","choices":["It sinks","It floats, boats always float","It gets bigger"],"answer":0,"hint":"What happens when water comes over the side?","explain":"It sinks. Each marble pushes the boat lower, and once water comes in, the boat goes down.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"You have {n} toys to test. What do you do before each one goes in the water?","choices":["Guess: float or sink?","Throw them all in at once","Ask the water"],"answer":0,"hint":"What do scientists do first, before a test?","explain":"Guess first, then test. Then you can see if your guess was right.","params":{"n":[3,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"A metal spoon goes in the bath. What does it do?","choices":["It sinks","It floats","It swims"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is metal heavy for its size, like a key?","explain":"The metal spoon sinks to the bottom, just like a key or a coin."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Where does a thing that floats stay?","choices":["On top of the water","At the bottom","Under the bath"],"answer":0,"hint":"Float means it does not go down.","explain":"A thing that floats stays on top of the water. A thing that sinks goes to the bottom."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"You drop a rubber duck in the bath. What does it do?","choices":["It floats on top","It sinks to the bottom","It melts"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of bath time. Where do toy ducks stay?","explain":"A rubber duck floats. It stays on top of the water, so you can push it along."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Sam has {n} coins. He drops them all in the water. What do the coins do?","choices":["They sink","They float","Big ones float, small ones sink"],"answer":0,"hint":"Coins are small, but are they light for their size?","explain":"Coins sink, even tiny ones. Metal is heavy for its size, so being small does not help it float.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"A big log falls into the pond. What does it do?","choices":["It floats","It sinks because it is big","It sinks, then pops up"],"answer":0,"hint":"Wood is light for its size. Does big mean it will sink?","explain":"The big log floats. Wood floats even when it is very big, so big does not mean sink."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Mia makes a clay ball and it sinks. Then she squashes it into a boat. She puts in {n} tiny stones. The boat stays dry inside. What does it do?","choices":["It floats","It sinks like the ball","It turns back into a ball"],"answer":0,"hint":"Same clay, new shape. Has any water got in?","explain":"The boat shape floats. It is the same clay, but a boat shape can float while the water stays outside it.","params":{"n":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"You guessed the spoon would float. It sinks. What is the best thing to say?","choices":["It sinks! Now I know.","The water is wrong.","I will not try again."],"answer":0,"hint":"Testing is how we find out. Is a wrong guess bad?","explain":"A wrong guess is fine. The test shows what really happens, so now you know the spoon sinks."}],"explain":{"kid":["Drop a duck in the bath. It stays on top. It floats.","Drop a key in. It goes down to the bottom. It sinks.","Big or small does not tell you. A big log floats. A tiny coin sinks!","So guess first. Then put it in the water and see. Make clay into a boat and it floats. Put in too many stones and it sinks."],"adult":["This topic, for ages 3 to 4, is about sorting everyday things into those that float and those that sink by testing them in water. It builds the habit of guessing first and then checking, which is the start of a fair test. It leads on to describing what materials are like, such as wood, metal and plastic.","Whether a thing floats depends on how heavy it is for its size (its density compared with water), not on its size or its weight alone. A wooden log is huge and heavy but floats, because wood is lighter than the same amount of water. A coin is tiny but sinks, because metal is much heavier than the same amount of water. Shape matters too: a boat shape pushes aside lots of water, so a lump of clay that sinks will float once it is shaped into a hollow boat.","Common mistakes: thinking big things sink and small things float, thinking heavy things always sink (ships are very heavy), and thinking a boat floats whatever you put in it. Children also often say a wrong guess was the water's fault. Praise the test, not the guess: \"You thought it would float. It sank. Now we know!\"","In the lab your child drops things into a tub, tests big and small things, guesses before each test, sinks a clay ball, floats the same clay as a boat and then loads the boat with marbles until the water comes in. You do not need to use the word density yet."]},"familyTip":"At bath time, collect five things (a cork or bottle top, a spoon, a toy car, an apple, a sponge). Ask \"Float or sink?\" before each one goes in, then test it together. Try modelling clay as a ball, then as a little boat.","keyIdea":"Put a thing in water and it either floats on top or sinks to the bottom. You cannot tell just from big or small: a big log floats and a tiny coin sinks. Guess first, then test it in water to find out. Shape matters too: a clay ball sinks, but the same clay made into a boat floats, until too much goes in it.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Float or sink? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Floats","Sinks"],"items":[["rubber duck",0],["big log",0],["apple",0],["key",1],["coin",1],["stone",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagfloat","caption":"Flagship lab: drop things in a tub of water, test big and small things, guess then test, float a clay boat and load it until it sinks.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_fHNe665XRv":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which list gives the four stages of a time-of-flight mass spectrometer in order?","choices":["Ionisation, acceleration, flight tube, detection","Acceleration, ionisation, detection, flight tube","Detection, ionisation, flight tube, acceleration"],"answer":0,"hint":"Particles must be charged before they can be pushed.","explain":"Ionise first so the field can accelerate the ions, then they drift down the flight tube and are detected."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Boron is about 80% ¹¹B and 20% ¹⁰B. Which peak reaches the detector first?","choices":["The small ¹⁰B peak","The tall ¹¹B peak","Both at the same time, because they are the same element"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does arrival time depend on how many ions there are, or on m/z?","explain":"¹⁰B⁺ has the smaller m/z, so it is faster and arrives first. Peak height shows abundance, not arrival order."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"What equation describes electron impact ionisation of a particle X?","choices":["X(g) + e⁻ → X⁺(g) + 2e⁻","X(g) + e⁻ → X⁻(g)","X(g) → X⁺(g) + H⁺"],"answer":0,"hint":"The fast electron knocks one electron off and keeps going.","explain":"The incoming electron knocks one electron off X, so two electrons leave and X⁺ is formed."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"How does the detector give the abundance of each ion?","choices":["Ions gain electrons at the detector, and the current is bigger when more ions arrive","It measures how fast each ion moves","It weighs each ion as it lands"],"answer":0,"hint":"What happens to a positive ion when it touches the detector?","explain":"Each ion picks up electrons at the detector. More ions of one m/z give a bigger current, which is the abundance."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"An ion with m/z {a} takes {t} μs. How many μs does an ion with m/z {=4*a} take in the same spectrometer?","answer":"2*t","hint":"Flight time grows with the square root of m/z. What is √4?","explain":"{=4*a} is 4 × {a}, and √4 = 2, so the time is 2 × {t} = {=2*t} μs.","params":{"a":[5,12],"t":[3,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Why is the flight tube kept under a vacuum?","choices":["So the ions do not collide with air molecules","So the ions are pushed harder","So the ions gain electrons"],"answer":0,"hint":"What could slow an ion down or knock it off course?","explain":"Air molecules would collide with the ions and scatter them, spoiling the flight times."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"In electrospray ionisation, what happens to each molecule M?","choices":["It gains a proton to become MH⁺","It loses a proton to become M⁻","It splits into atoms"],"answer":0,"hint":"The sample is sprayed at high voltage in a solvent and picks up an ion.","explain":"In electrospray ionisation each molecule gains an H⁺: M + H⁺ → MH⁺, so its m/z is one more than its relative molecular mass."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"A sample gives ²⁴Mg⁺ at 7.9 μs. Where would ²⁴Mg²⁺ appear on the spectrum?","choices":["Earlier, at m/z 12","At the same time: same mass","Later, at m/z 48"],"answer":0,"hint":"The spectrometer sorts by m/z, not by mass.","explain":"A 2+ ion gets twice the kinetic energy, so it behaves like m/z = 24 ÷ 2 = 12 and arrives first."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"An ion has a relative mass of {=2*h} and a charge of 2+. At what m/z does it show up?","answer":"h","hint":"The spectrometer sorts by mass divided by charge.","explain":"m/z = {=2*h} ÷ 2 = {h}, so this 2+ ion arrives with the 1+ ions of mass {h}, not with mass {=2*h}.","params":{"h":[10,30]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Why must the sample be ionised in a time-of-flight mass spectrometer?","choices":["Only ions are accelerated by the electric field and make a current at the detector","Ionising makes the particles lighter so they fly faster","Ions are easier to see through the flight tube"],"answer":0,"hint":"What does an electric field push, and what does the detector respond to?","explain":"An electric field only pushes charged particles, and the detector works when ions gain electrons. Losing one electron barely changes the mass."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"An ion with m/z 16 takes {t} μs to reach the detector. How many μs does an ion with m/z 64 take in the same spectrometer?","answer":"2*t","hint":"Flight time grows with the square root of m/z. How many times bigger is 64 than 16?","explain":"64 is 4 × 16, and √4 = 2, so the time doubles: 2 × {t} = {=2*t} μs.","params":{"t":[4,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"After the accelerating plates, a ²⁴Mg⁺ ion and a ²⁶Mg⁺ ion have…","choices":["the same kinetic energy, so ²⁴Mg⁺ moves faster","the same speed","the same speed, but ²⁶Mg⁺ has more kinetic energy"],"answer":0,"hint":"The field gives each ion with the same charge the same energy, zeV.","explain":"Both get KE = zeV. With KE = ½mv², the lighter ²⁴Mg⁺ must have the higher speed."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A molecule with relative molecular mass {m} is ionised by electrospray and gains one H⁺. At what m/z does its ion show up?","answer":"m+1","hint":"Electrospray adds a proton, which has a mass of 1, and a charge of 1+.","explain":"M + H⁺ → MH⁺, so the ion has mass {m} + 1 = {=m+1} and charge 1+, giving m/z = {=m+1}.","params":{"m":[46,180]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"An ion with m/z {a} takes 5 μs. How many μs does an ion with m/z {=9*a} take in the same spectrometer?","answer":"15","hint":"How many times bigger is the m/z? Take the square root of that.","explain":"{=9*a} is 9 × {a}, and √9 = 3, so the time is 3 × 5 = 15 μs.","params":{"a":[2,6]}}],"explain":{"kid":["A mass spectrometer sorts particles by mass. It has four stages: ionise, accelerate, fly, detect. The sample must be a gas, or a fine spray, so the particles move on their own.","First, ionise. An electron gun knocks an electron off each particle, so X becomes X⁺. Another method sprays the sample from a needle at high voltage, so each molecule picks up an H⁺ ion. Only charged particles feel the electric field, and only ions make a current at the detector.","Next, accelerate. An electric field gives every ion with the same charge the same kinetic energy. Kinetic energy is ½mv², so a lighter ion must move faster. In the flight tube there is no more push. The fast, light ions pull ahead, and the tube is a vacuum so they do not hit air.","Last, detect. Each ion hits the detector and gains electrons. That makes a current. The time an ion takes tells the computer its mass-to-charge ratio, m/z. The size of the current tells you how many ions had that m/z. A 2+ ion gets twice the push, so it acts like an ion of half the mass."],"adult":["This A-level Chemistry topic (ages 16 to 18) covers the four stages of a time-of-flight (TOF) mass spectrometer. In electron impact ionisation, high-energy electrons from an electron gun knock an electron off each gaseous particle: X(g) + e⁻ → X⁺(g) + 2e⁻. In electrospray ionisation the sample, dissolved in a volatile solvent, is sprayed from a fine needle at high voltage, and each molecule gains a proton: X + H⁺ → XH⁺.","In the accelerating region an electric field gives each ion kinetic energy KE = zeV, so ions with the same charge get the same KE. From ½mv² = zeV, v = √(2zeV ÷ m). The flight tube has no field, so each ion keeps its speed, and the flight time is t = d ÷ v. Together these give t ∝ √(m/z): an ion with four times the m/z takes twice as long. At the detector, ions gain electrons and the current is proportional to the number of ions arriving.","Common mistakes: saying the ions are accelerated to the same speed rather than the same kinetic energy; thinking the tallest peak arrives first; separating by mass instead of mass-to-charge ratio, so ²⁴Mg²⁺ is wrongly placed at 24 instead of 12; and assuming flight time is proportional to mass. Learners also forget why ionising matters: neutral particles are not accelerated and cannot be detected.","In the lab, learners fire atoms with the electron gun off and on, race ²⁴Mg⁺ against ²⁶Mg⁺ with speed labels, build a boron spectrum and pick the first peak, switch on 2+ ions and find m/z 12, and then set an unknown m/z so its ion arrives at twice the time of ¹⁶O⁺. Numbers use d = 1.0 m and V = 2.0 kV, which give about 7.9 μs for ²⁴Mg⁺."]},"familyTip":"Roll a heavy ball and a light ball across a smooth floor with the same flick of a ruler, or the same push from a stretched rubber band. Ask which crosses the line first, then link it to lighter ions arriving first in a mass spectrometer.","keyIdea":"A time-of-flight mass spectrometer has four stages. The sample is ionised, so the electric field can push it and the detector can register it. The ions are accelerated so that all ions with the same charge get the same kinetic energy. In the flight tube, lighter ions move faster, so ions with a smaller mass-to-charge ratio (m/z) arrive first. At the detector each ion gains electrons, and the current shows how many ions of each m/z arrived.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which stage does each event belong to? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Ionisation","Acceleration","Flight tube","Detection"],"items":[["Electron gun knocks off an electron",0],["Molecule gains H⁺ from a charged spray",0],["Ions get the same kinetic energy",1],["Light ions pull ahead",2],["Ions gain electrons and make a current",3]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagtof","caption":"Flagship lab: ionise, accelerate and race ions down a flight tube, read the spectrum, find 2+ ions and identify a mystery m/z.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_ANso-Gni70":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What is the shape of a water molecule, H₂O?","choices":["Bent, 104.5°","Linear, 180°","Tetrahedral, 109.5°"],"answer":0,"hint":"O has 2 bonds and 2 lone pairs. Name the shape from the atoms.","explain":"The 4 regions are roughly tetrahedral, but only 2 hold atoms, so the shape is bent. The 2 lone pairs squeeze the angle to 104.5°."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"BF₃ has 3 bonding pairs and no lone pairs on B. What shape is it?","choices":["Trigonal planar, 120°","Pyramidal, 107°","Tetrahedral, 109.5°"],"answer":0,"hint":"Three regions with no lone pair spread out in one plane.","explain":"Three regions get furthest apart at 120° in a flat triangle. There is no lone pair to push them into a pyramid."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Put these repulsions in order, strongest first.","choices":["lone–lone > lone–bond > bond–bond","bond–bond > lone–bond > lone–lone","They are all the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"Lone pairs sit closer to the central atom and spread wider.","explain":"Lone pairs repel most, so lone–lone is strongest and bond–bond is weakest. That is why each lone pair closes the bond angle."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Sam says NH₃ is trigonal planar because N has three bonds. What did he miss?","choices":["The lone pair on N, which makes 4 regions","A double bond","Nothing, he is right"],"answer":0,"hint":"N has 5 outer electrons. How many are used in bonds?","explain":"N uses 3 electrons in bonds and keeps 2 as a lone pair. With 4 regions the shape is pyramidal, not flat."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"What is the shape of the ammonium ion, NH₄⁺?","choices":["Tetrahedral, 109.5°","Pyramidal, 107°","Square planar, 90°"],"answer":0,"hint":"The lone pair of NH₃ is used to bond the fourth H⁺.","explain":"NH₄⁺ has 4 bonding pairs and no lone pairs, so it is tetrahedral at 109.5°, like CH₄."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"A central atom has {a} regions of electrons, and {b} of them are lone pairs. How many bonding pairs does it have?","answer":"a-b","hint":"Regions = bonding pairs + lone pairs.","explain":"{a} − {b} = {=a-b} bonding pairs. The lone pairs still count as regions, but they hold no atoms.","params":{"a":[2,4],"b":[0,2]},"where":"a-b>=2"},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Why is CH₄ not a flat cross with 90° angles?","choices":["In 3D the pairs can get further apart, at 109.5°","Carbon atoms are always round","The H atoms attract each other"],"answer":0,"hint":"Electron pairs spread as far apart as they can. Are they stuck on the page?","explain":"A flat cross only gives 90°. Spreading in 3D to the corners of a tetrahedron gives 109.5°, which is further apart."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"A central atom has {b} outer electrons and makes {a} single bonds to H atoms. How many regions of electrons are round it?","answer":"a+(b-a)/2","hint":"Each bond is one region. Pair up the electrons not used in bonds to find the lone pairs.","explain":"{a} bonds + {=(b-a)/2} lone pairs = {=a+(b-a)/2} regions of electrons round the central atom.","params":{"a":[2,4],"b":[2,8]},"where":"(b-a)%2==0 && b>=a && a+(b-a)/2<=4"}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"What is the shape of a molecule with 4 bonding pairs and no lone pairs round the central atom?","choices":["Tetrahedral, 109.5°","Square planar, 90°","Pyramidal, 107°"],"answer":0,"hint":"Four pairs spread out as far as they can in 3D.","explain":"Four regions point to the corners of a tetrahedron, 109.5° apart, as in CH₄. A flat square would put them only 90° apart."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A central atom has {a} bonding pairs and {b} lone pairs. How many regions of electrons are round it?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Bonding pairs and lone pairs both count as regions.","explain":"Regions = {a} bonding + {b} lone = {=a+b}. Lone pairs take up space just like bonds do, so both are counted.","params":{"a":[2,4],"b":[0,2]},"where":"a+b<=4"},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Why is the bond angle in NH₃ (107°) smaller than in CH₄ (109.5°)?","choices":["The lone pair repels more strongly than a bonding pair","N is a smaller atom than C","NH₃ has fewer electrons in total"],"answer":0,"hint":"What does N have that C in CH₄ does not?","explain":"N has a lone pair. It repels more than a bonding pair, so it pushes the three N–H bonds a little closer together."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A central atom has {a} outer electrons and makes {b} single bonds to H atoms. How many lone pairs does it have?","answer":"(a-b)/2","hint":"Each bond to H uses one of its outer electrons. Pair up the rest.","explain":"{a} − {b} = {=a-b} electrons are left over. Pair them up: {=(a-b)/2} lone pairs.","params":{"a":[5,8],"b":[1,4]},"where":"(a-b)%2==0 && a>=b && b+(a-b)/2<=4"},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"How many regions of electrons are round the C atom in CO₂ (O=C=O)?","choices":["2","4","0"],"answer":0,"hint":"A double bond points one way. Does it count once or twice?","explain":"Each double bond is one region, so C has 2 regions. They sit 180° apart and CO₂ is linear."}],"explain":{"kid":["Electrons come in pairs round the central atom of a molecule. Pairs repel each other, so they spread out as far as they can. They spread in 3D, not just flat on the page. That is why methane, CH₄, is not a flat cross.","Count the regions of electrons round the central atom. Each bond is one region, and so is each double bond. Two regions sit 180° apart, as in CO₂: a linear shape. Three spread to 120°, as in BF₃: trigonal planar.","Four regions point to the corners of a tetrahedron. The bond angle is 109.5°, as in CH₄. A lone pair is a pair of electrons that is not in a bond. It still takes up a region, but we name the shape from the atoms we can see.","Lone pairs push harder than bonds, so they squeeze the bonds closer. NH₃ has 3 bonds and 1 lone pair: it is pyramidal, at 107°. H₂O has 2 bonds and 2 lone pairs: it is bent, at 104.5°. Each lone pair takes about 2.5° off."],"adult":["This A-level chemistry topic (ages 16 to 18) uses valence shell electron pair repulsion (VSEPR) theory. The shape of a simple molecule or ion follows from the number of electron-pair regions round the central atom: bonding pairs and lone pairs, with a double or triple bond counted as one region.","Learners should know linear (2 regions, 180°), trigonal planar (3, 120°), tetrahedral (4, 109.5°), pyramidal (3 bonding + 1 lone pair, about 107°) and bent (2 bonding + 2 lone pairs, about 104.5°). The order of repulsion is lone pair–lone pair > lone pair–bonding pair > bonding pair–bonding pair, because a lone pair is held by one nucleus and sits closer to it.","Common mistakes: reading the flat drawing of CH₄ as 90°; counting a double bond as two regions; leaving lone pairs out, so NH₃ is called trigonal planar and H₂O linear; naming the shape from the regions rather than the atoms; giving 109.5° for NH₃ and H₂O; and counting lone electrons instead of pairs.","In the lab, learners squeeze a bond and watch it spring back, turn the molecule in 3D, build linear and trigonal planar shapes, add a lone pair to make NH₃, compare CH₄, NH₃ and H₂O, and work out PH₃ and H₂S from outer electrons. The challenge asks for the shape and angle of six species, including CO₂ and NH₄⁺."]},"familyTip":"Tie four balloons together at their knots. They push apart into a tetrahedron by themselves, just like the electron pairs in methane. Try two and three balloons to see the linear and flat triangle shapes.","keyIdea":"Pairs of electrons round a central atom repel, so they spread out in 3D as far apart as they can. Count the regions: each bond counts once, even a double bond, and each lone pair counts too. Two regions give a linear shape (180°), three give trigonal planar (120°) and four give tetrahedral (109.5°). Lone pairs repel more than bonding pairs, so NH₃ is pyramidal (107°) and H₂O is bent (104.5°).","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which shape? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Linear","Tetrahedral","Pyramidal","Bent"],"items":[["CO₂",0],["BeCl₂",0],["CH₄",1],["NH₄⁺",1],["NH₃",2],["H₂O",3]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagvsepr","caption":"Flagship lab: squeeze and turn a 3D molecule, add bonds and lone pairs to build each shape, and watch lone pairs close the bond angle.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_hudKVJpE5p":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which molecule contains a chiral carbon?","choices":["CH₃CHClCH₂CH₃","CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂Cl","CH₃CHClCH₃"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find a carbon holding H, Cl and two different carbon groups.","explain":"In 2-chlorobutane, C2 holds H, Cl, CH₃ and C₂H₅. In 2-chloropropane the CHCl carbon has two CH₃ groups."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Is C3 in pentan-3-ol, CH₃CH₂CH(OH)CH₂CH₃, chiral?","choices":["No, it holds two ethyl groups","Yes, it holds four different atoms","Yes, it holds an OH group"],"answer":0,"hint":"Compare whole groups, not just the atoms attached.","explain":"C3 holds H, OH, C₂H₅ and C₂H₅. Two groups are the same, so it is not chiral."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"The pure (S) form of a compound rotates light {a}° anticlockwise. How many degrees clockwise does the pure (R) form rotate it, at the same concentration?","answer":"a","hint":"The two enantiomers rotate by equal amounts in opposite directions.","explain":"Equal and opposite: {a}° clockwise.","params":{"a":[6,45]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"What is a racemic mixture?","choices":["A 50:50 mix of two enantiomers, with no net rotation","A pure enantiomer that rotates light strongly","A mix of two structural isomers"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about what the polarimeter showed at 50:50.","explain":"Equal amounts of each enantiomer rotate light equally in opposite directions, so the rotations cancel."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"How many chiral carbons are in alanine, CH₃CH(NH₂)COOH?","choices":["1","2","0"],"answer":0,"hint":"The COOH carbon has a C=O double bond. How many groups does that leave it?","explain":"The CH carbon holds H, NH₂, CH₃ and COOH. The COOH carbon has only three groups, and the CH₃ has three H atoms."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"A sample is {a}% (+) form and the rest is (−) form. By how many percentage points does (+) exceed (−)?","answer":"2*a-100","hint":"First find the percentage of the (−) form: 100 − {a}.","explain":"(−) is {=100-a}%, so (+) exceeds it by {a} − {=100-a} = {=2*a-100} points. Only this excess rotates light.","params":{"a":[55,95]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Why can two enantiomers never be superimposed?","choices":["They are mirror images of a carbon with four different groups","They have different molecular formulas","Their bonds are different lengths"],"answer":0,"hint":"Recall the mirror step: at best only two of the four groups lined up.","explain":"With four different groups, no rotation maps the mirror image onto the original. They have the same formula and bonds."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"(R)-carvone smells of spearmint and (S)-carvone of caraway. What explains this?","choices":["Smell receptors are chiral, so each enantiomer fits differently","The two forms have different boiling points","The two forms have different formulas"],"answer":0,"hint":"Enantiomers act differently only with polarised light and with other chiral things.","explain":"Receptor proteins are chiral, like a left glove. Each enantiomer fits them in a different way."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which carbon in butan-2-ol, CH₃CH(OH)CH₂CH₃, is chiral?","choices":["C2, the carbon with the OH","C1, the CH₃ at the end","C3, the CH₂"],"answer":0,"hint":"List the four groups on each carbon and look for four different ones.","explain":"C2 holds H, OH, CH₃ and C₂H₅: four different groups. C1 has three H atoms and C3 has two."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"The pure (+) enantiomer rotates plane-polarised light {a}° clockwise. At the same concentration, how many degrees anticlockwise does the pure (−) enantiomer rotate it?","answer":"a","hint":"Enantiomers rotate light by the same angle in opposite directions.","explain":"Same angle, opposite direction: {a}° anticlockwise.","params":{"a":[5,40]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Is the central carbon in propan-2-ol, CH₃CH(OH)CH₃, chiral?","choices":["No, it has two CH₃ groups","Yes, it has an OH group","Yes, every central carbon is chiral"],"answer":0,"hint":"Write out its four groups: H, OH and what else?","explain":"Its groups are H, OH, CH₃ and CH₃. Two match, so it is not chiral and has no enantiomers."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A sample is {a}% of one enantiomer and {=100-a}% of the other. What percentage of the sample is a racemic 50:50 pair?","answer":"2*(100-a)","hint":"Each molecule of the minor form pairs with one of the major form.","explain":"The {=100-a}% minor form pairs with {=100-a}% of the major form, so {=2*(100-a)}% is racemic. The rest gives the rotation.","params":{"a":[55,95]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"You swap two groups on one enantiomer of a chiral molecule. What do you get?","choices":["The other enantiomer","The same molecule","A molecule that is not chiral"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about what the lab showed when you swapped two groups on the mirror image.","explain":"One swap gives the mirror image, the other enantiomer. A second swap gives the first back."}],"explain":{"kid":["A carbon atom makes four bonds. They point to the corners of a tetrahedron. If all four groups on a carbon are different, the carbon is chiral. In butan-2-ol, CH₃CH(OH)CH₂CH₃, the second carbon holds H, OH, CH₃ and C₂H₅. All four differ, so it is chiral. Compare whole groups, not just the first atom.","A chiral molecule has a mirror image that will not fit on top of it. Your hands work the same way. Turn the mirror image any way you like. At best two of the four groups line up. The two forms are called enantiomers. They are a type of optical isomer.","To draw the pair in 3D, put two bonds in the page. Draw one as a wedge, which points towards you. Draw one as a dashed line, which points away. Then draw the mirror image beside it. If you swap any two groups, you get the other enantiomer.","Enantiomers have the same boiling point and the same formula. One big difference is how they treat plane-polarised light. One form turns it clockwise. The other turns it the same angle anticlockwise. A 50:50 mix is called racemic. The two turns cancel, so the light comes out unturned."],"adult":["This A-level chemistry topic (ages 17 to 18) covers optical isomerism. A carbon bonded to four different groups is a chiral centre, also called an asymmetric carbon or stereocentre. A molecule with one such centre exists as two non-superimposable mirror images, called enantiomers. Students mark the chiral carbon, draw both enantiomers with wedge and dash bonds, and explain why they cannot be superimposed. It builds on tetrahedral shape, structural formulae and isomerism.","The test is about groups, not atoms. In pentan-3-ol, the middle carbon holds H, OH and two ethyl groups. Two groups match, so it is not chiral, even though the atoms next to it look varied. Enantiomers share physical properties such as melting point. They rotate plane-polarised light equally in opposite directions. They can also act differently with other chiral molecules, such as receptors and enzymes. (R)-carvone smells of spearmint; (S)-carvone smells of caraway.","Common mistakes: calling any carbon with an OH or a halogen chiral; comparing only the attached atoms, not whole groups; counting a C=O carbon, which has only three groups; drawing the mirror image with the same wedge pattern, which gives the same molecule; and saying a racemic mixture rotates light. Be careful when you swap groups. One swap gives the other enantiomer, but two swaps give the first one back.","The lab has five parts. You tap the chiral carbon in four molecules, or say there is none. You build a centre, then break its chirality with one change. You turn a 3D mirror image of butan-2-ol and find that only two of its four groups ever line up. You swap two groups and make it fit. Last, you mix two enantiomers in a polarimeter until the rotation cancels."]},"familyTip":"Ask your teenager to hold up both hands, palms down, and try to lay one exactly on the other. Then ask them to name a molecule that works the same way, and to say which carbon makes it so.","keyIdea":"A chiral carbon is bonded to four different groups. A molecule with one chiral carbon has two forms that are mirror images but cannot be superimposed, however you turn them. These two forms are enantiomers, a type of optical isomer. Swapping any two groups turns one enantiomer into the other. Enantiomers rotate plane-polarised light by the same angle in opposite directions, so a 50:50 racemic mixture shows no rotation.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is the marked carbon chiral? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Chiral","Not chiral"],"items":[["C2 of butan-2-ol: H, OH, CH₃, C₂H₅",0],["C2 of propan-2-ol: H, OH, CH₃, CH₃",1],["C2 of lactic acid: H, OH, CH₃, COOH",0],["C3 of pentan-3-ol: H, OH, C₂H₅, C₂H₅",1],["C2 of 2-chlorobutane: H, Cl, CH₃, C₂H₅",0],["CH₂Cl carbon: H, H, Cl, C₃H₇",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagchiral","caption":"Flagship lab: tap the chiral carbon, build and break a chiral centre, turn a 3D mirror image, swap two groups, and cancel rotation in a polarimeter.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_0_F3SZBndK":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What does a full-headed curly arrow represent?","choices":["The movement of a pair of electrons","The movement of an atom","The movement of a single electron"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about the difference between full-headed and half-headed arrows.","explain":"A full-headed arrow moves an electron pair. A single electron uses a half-headed (fish-hook) arrow."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which is the electrophile when HBr adds to ethene?","choices":["HBr, through its δ+ hydrogen","Ethene","The bromide ion"],"answer":0,"hint":"An electrophile accepts an electron pair. What does the π bond attack?","explain":"The π bond donates its pair to the δ+ H of HBr, so HBr is the electrophile; ethene is the electron-pair donor."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"A student draws {n} full-headed arrows and {k} half-headed (fish-hook) arrows. How many electrons move in total?","answer":"2*n+k","hint":"Full-headed arrows move pairs; fish-hook arrows move single electrons.","explain":"{n} full arrows move {=2*n} electrons and {k} fish-hooks move {k}, so {=2*n+k} electrons move in total.","params":{"n":[1,4],"k":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Propene reacts with HBr. Which is the major product?","choices":["2-bromopropane","1-bromopropane","1,2-dibromopropane"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which carbocation, primary or secondary, is more stable?","explain":"H adds to the CH₂ end, leaving a secondary carbocation, which is more stable. Bromide then bonds to the middle carbon: 2-bromopropane."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Why is a tertiary carbocation more stable than a primary one?","choices":["More alkyl groups push electron density towards the C⁺","It has more hydrogen atoms on the C⁺","It has a double bond"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about what alkyl groups do to electron density nearby.","explain":"Alkyl groups have a positive inductive effect. Three of them spread the positive charge better than one."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Br₂ is non-polar. Why can it act as an electrophile with ethene?","choices":["The π electrons induce a dipole, making the near Br δ+","Br₂ always has a permanent dipole","Ethene gives Br₂ a proton"],"answer":0,"hint":"What do the electron-rich π bond's electrons do to the electrons in Br₂?","explain":"The π electrons repel the electrons in Br₂, so the near bromine becomes δ+ and accepts the π pair."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"{n} mol of propene reacts fully with HBr to give only bromopropanes. How many moles of product form in total?","answer":"n","hint":"Each propene molecule adds one HBr and gives one bromopropane, whichever isomer.","explain":"The ratio is 1 : 1, so {n} mol of propene gives {n} mol of bromopropane in total, mostly the 2-isomer.","params":{"n":[1,5]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"In HBr + ethene, where does the first curly arrow start and end?","choices":["From the C=C π bond to the δ+ H","From the H to the C=C bond","From the δ− Br to the C=C bond"],"answer":0,"hint":"The tail goes on electrons. Which end of HBr attracts electrons?","explain":"The π bond holds the electrons, so the tail starts there. The pair is attracted to the δ+ hydrogen, so the head ends on H."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A mechanism step shows {n} full-headed curly arrows. How many electrons move in total?","answer":"2*n","hint":"Each full-headed arrow moves a pair of electrons.","explain":"Each arrow moves 2 electrons, so {n} arrows move {=2*n} electrons.","params":{"n":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"In step two, which arrow forms the C–Br bond?","choices":["From a lone pair on Br⁻ to the C⁺","From the C⁺ to Br⁻","From the C–C bond to Br⁻"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which species has a spare pair of electrons to give?","explain":"Br⁻ has lone pairs; C⁺ has an empty spot. The arrow runs from a lone pair on Br⁻ to the positive carbon."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"{n} mol of ethene reacts completely with excess HBr. How many moles of bromoethane form?","answer":"n","hint":"Write the equation: C₂H₄ + HBr → C₂H₅Br. What is the mole ratio?","explain":"The ratio of ethene to bromoethane is 1 : 1, so {n} mol of ethene gives {n} mol of bromoethane.","params":{"n":[1,6]}}],"explain":{"kid":["A reaction mechanism shows how bonds break and form, one step at a time. Chemists use curly arrows to show it.","Each curly arrow moves one pair of electrons. The tail sits on the pair: a bond or a lone pair. The head shows where the pair ends up. An atom on its own is never the tail.","Ethene has a π bond full of electrons. In HBr, the H end is δ+. The π pair attacks the H, and the H–Br pair moves onto Br. You now have a carbocation, a carbon with a + charge, and a bromide ion, Br⁻.","Next, a lone pair on Br⁻ forms a bond to the C⁺. The product is bromoethane. With propene, H joins the end carbon, because that leaves the more stable carbocation."],"adult":["A full curly arrow shows a pair of electrons on the move. A half arrow, or fish-hook, shows one electron, as in radical reactions. A-level chemistry asks students to draw the steps of electrophilic addition. They must show dipoles, lone pairs, charges and the carbocation.","In step one, the alkene gives a pair of electrons. The arrow runs from the C=C π bond to the δ+ H of HBr. A second arrow runs from the H–Br bond to Br, which leaves as Br⁻. This is heterolytic fission. In step two, the bromide ion is the nucleophile. Its arrow runs from a lone pair to the C⁺. With Br₂, the π electrons push on the Br–Br bond. This makes the near Br δ+.","Common mistakes: the tail starts on an atom or on a + charge, not on a bond or lone pair. The arrow is drawn backwards, from H to the double bond. The dipole or the lone pair on Br⁻ is missing. The head stops short of the atom that gains the pair. With propene, many students pick the primary carbocation.","Tertiary carbocations are more stable than secondary ones, and secondary beat primary. Each alkyl group pushes some electron density toward the C⁺. For propene, H adds to the CH₂ end, so a secondary carbocation forms. The main product is 2-bromopropane. This fits Markovnikov's rule."]},"familyTip":"Ask your teenager to draw the HBr + ethene mechanism from memory on a scrap of paper, then check four things together: tail on electrons, head on the receiving atom, dipole on HBr, and the lone pair and charge on bromide.","keyIdea":"A curly arrow shows a pair of electrons moving. Its tail starts on the electrons, a bond or a lone pair, and its head points to where the pair goes. In electrophilic addition of HBr to ethene, the π bond attacks the δ+ hydrogen while the H–Br bond breaks heterolytically, giving a carbocation and Br⁻; then the bromide lone pair bonds to the carbocation. With unsymmetrical alkenes the more stable carbocation (tertiary > secondary > primary) gives the major product.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Does each species donate an electron pair (nucleophile) or accept one (electrophile)? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Electron-pair donor (nucleophile)","Electron-pair acceptor (electrophile)"],"items":[["Br⁻ ion",0],["OH⁻ ion",0],["Lone pair on H₂O",0],["C=C π bond",0],["H⁺ ion",1],["CH₃CH₂⁺ carbocation",1],["δ+ H in HBr",1],["NO₂⁺ ion",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagcurly","caption":"Flagship lab: drag curly arrows from electron pairs for H–Br fission, HBr + ethene, the carbocation step, propene, and bromine.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_NRCNs8z92N":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which is the best definition of a nucleophile?","choices":["An electron-pair donor","An electron-pair acceptor","Any negative ion"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about what the nucleophile does with its lone pair.","explain":"A nucleophile donates an electron pair to form a new bond. Neutral NH₃ and H₂O are nucleophiles too, so a charge is not required."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which of these species is an electrophile?","choices":["NO₂⁺","CN⁻","H₂O"],"answer":0,"hint":"An electrophile accepts a pair. Which one has none to give?","explain":"NO₂⁺ is positive with no lone pair to donate, so it accepts a pair. CN⁻ and H₂O donate pairs."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"A ketone with {n} carbon atoms is reduced by NaBH₄. How many carbon atoms does the alcohol product have?","answer":"n","hint":"H⁻ adds a hydrogen, not a carbon.","explain":"H⁻ adds to the δ+ carbon and O gains H, so the chain is unchanged: {n} carbon atoms.","params":{"n":[3,7]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"{n} mol of 1-bromopropane reacts fully with excess KCN. How many moles of new C–C bonds form?","answer":"n","hint":"Each CN⁻ makes one new bond from its carbon to the δ+ carbon.","explain":"One new C–C bond forms per molecule, so {n} mol of new C–C bonds.","params":{"n":[1,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Why can water act as a nucleophile?","choices":["Its oxygen has lone pairs","Its hydrogen atoms are δ+","It is neutral"],"answer":0,"hint":"A nucleophile needs something to donate.","explain":"The oxygen has two lone pairs it can donate. δ+ hydrogens attract pairs rather than give them."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"CN⁻ reacts with ethanal. What is the mechanism?","choices":["Nucleophilic addition","Nucleophilic substitution","Electrophilic addition"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does anything leave when the C=O opens?","explain":"CN⁻ donates a pair to the δ+ carbonyl carbon and the C=O π bond breaks. Nothing leaves, so it is nucleophilic addition."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"NaBH₄ reduces propanal. What is the nucleophile?","choices":["The hydride ion, H⁻","The Na⁺ ion","The carbonyl oxygen"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which part of NaBH₄ carries the electron pair to the carbon?","explain":"BH₄⁻ delivers H⁻, which donates its pair to the δ+ carbon. Na⁺ is a spectator ion."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"In bromoethane, why does OH⁻ attack carbon rather than bromine?","choices":["Br pulls electrons, leaving C δ+","C has a lone pair","Br is δ+"],"answer":0,"hint":"Compare the electronegativity of C and Br.","explain":"Br is more electronegative, so the C–Br carbon is δ+ and attracts the lone pair. Br is δ− and leaves as Br⁻."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which of these species is a nucleophile?","choices":["NH₃","NO₂⁺","H⁺"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for a lone pair that could be donated, not for a charge.","explain":"NH₃ has a lone pair on N, so it can donate a pair. NO₂⁺ and H⁺ can only accept a pair: they are electrophiles."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A 1-bromoalkane with {n} carbon atoms is warmed with KCN in ethanol. How many carbon atoms does the nitrile have?","answer":"n+1","hint":"Cyanide attacks through its carbon atom. Does that carbon join the chain?","explain":"CN⁻ bonds through C, so its carbon joins the chain: {n} + 1 = {=n+1} carbon atoms.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"In chloroethane, CH₃CH₂Cl, which atom does OH⁻ attack?","choices":["The carbon bonded to Cl","The Cl atom","The carbon of the CH₃ group"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which atom is left δ+ by the polar bond?","explain":"Cl is more electronegative, so the carbon bonded to it is δ+. The OH⁻ lone pair attacks that carbon and Cl leaves as Cl⁻."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Each water molecule has 2 lone pairs on its oxygen. How many lone pairs are there in total on {n} water molecules?","answer":"2*n","hint":"Multiply the lone pairs per molecule by the number of molecules.","explain":"2 lone pairs × {n} molecules = {=2*n} lone pairs. Any one of them can be donated, which is why water is a weak nucleophile.","params":{"n":[2,9]}}],"explain":{"kid":["A nucleophile gives away a pair of electrons to make a new bond. The name means nucleus-loving: it is drawn to a positive centre.","To give a pair, it needs a lone pair. OH⁻, CN⁻ and H⁻ have one and a minus charge. NH₃ and H₂O are neutral, but N and O still have lone pairs. So a charge is not needed. A lone pair is.","Where does it attack? A halogen or an oxygen pulls electrons out of its bond to carbon. That leaves the carbon δ+. The pair goes to that δ+ carbon, never to the δ− atom.","An electrophile does the reverse. It takes a pair. H⁺ and NO₂⁺ have no pair to give. Br₂ turns δ+ at one end when it nears a C=C bond."],"adult":["A-level chemistry calls a nucleophile an electron-pair donor. An electrophile is an electron-pair acceptor. Students must know OH⁻, CN⁻, NH₃ and H₂O as the nucleophiles that react with haloalkanes. They also meet H⁻ from NaBH₄, and CN⁻, adding to aldehydes and ketones. In each case the curly arrow starts at the lone pair and ends at the δ+ carbon.","The δ+ site comes from bond polarity. Halogens and oxygen pull electrons more than carbon does, so the carbon in C–X or C=O is δ+. With a haloalkane, the halide ion leaves: this is nucleophilic substitution. With C=O, the π bond breaks and nothing leaves: this is nucleophilic addition. With an alkene, the π bond gives the pair, so the other reagent is an electrophile.","Common mistakes: thinking a nucleophile must be negative, so missing NH₃ and H₂O. Saying cyanide attacks through N; in fact the C attacks, so the chain gains a carbon (1-bromopropane gives butanenitrile). Aiming the arrow at the δ− atom. Naming Na⁺ as the nucleophile in NaBH₄. Mixing up substitution, where something leaves, with addition, where nothing leaves.","In the lab you tap the donor atom in five species and sort eight species into donors, acceptors or neither. Then you find the δ+ target in three molecules, predict five products and name six mechanisms."]},"familyTip":"Ask your teenager to sort OH⁻, NO₂⁺, NH₃ and Br₂ into donors and acceptors, and to explain why NH₃ counts even though it has no charge.","keyIdea":"A nucleophile is an electron-pair donor. It uses a lone pair, often with a negative charge, to attack a δ+ atom and form a new covalent bond. Common nucleophiles are OH⁻, CN⁻ (attacking through carbon), NH₃, H₂O and the hydride ion H⁻ from NaBH₄. An electrophile is the opposite: an electron-pair acceptor such as H⁺, NO₂⁺ or a polarised Br₂. Nucleophiles attack the δ+ carbon of a C–halogen bond (substitution) or of a C=O bond (addition).","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Does the nucleophile replace an atom (substitution) or add across C=O (addition)? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Nucleophilic substitution","Nucleophilic addition"],"items":[["OH⁻ + bromoethane",0],["NH₃ + 1-chloropropane",0],["CN⁻ + 1-iodobutane",0],["H₂O + 2-bromopropane",0],["CN⁻ + ethanal",1],["H⁻ (NaBH₄) + propanone",1],["H⁻ (NaBH₄) + butanal",1],["CN⁻ + propanone",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagnucphile","caption":"Flagship lab: find the donor pair, sort donors from acceptors, aim at δ+, predict products and name the mechanism.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_9Y7in0rRlH":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which is soft?","choices":["A pillow","A brick","A spoon"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one squashes when you press it?","explain":"A pillow squashes when you press it, so it is soft. A brick and a spoon are hard."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"A glass marble is smooth. Is it soft too?","choices":["No, it is hard and smooth","Yes, smooth means soft","It is rough"],"answer":0,"hint":"Press it. Does it squash?","explain":"A marble does not squash, so it is hard. It is also smooth. Smooth and soft are different words."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"There are {n} socks and 1 pencil. You pull each one. How many things stretch?","answer":"n","hint":"Socks stretch. Does a pencil?","explain":"The {n} socks stretch and spring back. The pencil does not stretch, so {n} things stretch.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which is best for drying wet hands?","choices":["A towel","A spoon","A stone"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one soaks up water?","explain":"A towel soaks up water. A spoon and a stone stay wet on top, so they cannot dry you."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"How do you find out if a thing is rough or smooth?","choices":["Stroke it with your finger","Look at its colour","Press it hard"],"answer":0,"hint":"Rough and smooth are about how it feels when you move your finger.","explain":"Stroke it: bumps mean rough, a glide means smooth. Pressing tells you hard or soft."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which is best for a teddy's bed?","choices":["A soft blanket","A hard plank","A rough rock"],"answer":0,"hint":"Teddy wants to lie on something that squashes a little.","explain":"A soft blanket squashes, so teddy is comfy. A plank and a rock are hard."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"You press {n} stones and 3 sponges. How many things do not squash?","answer":"n","hint":"Hard things do not squash. Which ones are hard?","explain":"The {n} stones are hard and do not squash. The 3 sponges are soft.","params":{"n":[2,6]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"You press a sponge and it squashes. You stroke bark and it is bumpy. Which words fit: hard, soft, rough or smooth?","choices":["The sponge is soft and the bark is rough","The sponge is hard and the bark is smooth","Both are smooth"],"answer":0,"hint":"Squash means soft. Bumpy means rough.","explain":"The sponge squashed, so it is soft. The bark felt bumpy, so it is rough."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"In the feely bag there are {n} soft things and 1 hard stone. How many soft things are in the bag?","answer":"n","hint":"Only count the things that squash when you press them.","explain":"The stone is hard. The other {n} squash, so there are {n} soft things.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"A spoon is hard. Is it rough or smooth?","choices":["Smooth","Rough","Soft"],"answer":0,"hint":"Stroke a spoon. Does your finger slide or bump?","explain":"Your finger slides along a spoon, so it is smooth. Hard things can be smooth."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"You stroke {n} rough things and 2 smooth things. How many things feel bumpy?","answer":"n","hint":"Rough things are the bumpy ones.","explain":"The {n} rough things feel bumpy. The 2 smooth ones let your finger slide.","params":{"n":[2,5]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Press a teddy. It squashes, so it is soft. Press a stone. Your finger stops, so it is hard.","Now stroke them. Tree bark feels bumpy. It is rough. A spoon lets your finger slide. It is smooth.","A thing can have two feel words. A ball is hard and smooth. Smooth is not the same as soft.","Pull a sock. It gets long, then springs back, so it is stretchy. Pick things by how they feel: teddy needs a soft bed, and a wall must be hard."],"adult":["This is early science about materials. Development Matters for 3 and 4 year olds asks children to use all their senses in hands-on exploration of natural materials, to explore collections of materials with similar and different properties, and to talk about the differences between materials. In Year 1 the National Curriculum asks children to describe the simple physical properties of everyday materials and to compare and group materials on the basis of those properties.","Each feel word goes with a test. Hard or soft comes from pressing (does it squash?). Rough or smooth comes from stroking (bumps or a glide?). Stretchy comes from pulling and letting go. Absorbent, like a towel, comes from seeing whether it soaks up water. Naming the test helps your child explain why, not just guess.","Common mistakes: using soft and smooth as if they mean the same (a marble is smooth but hard), thinking every hard thing is rough, judging by colour or looks instead of by touch, and mixing up the object with what it is made of. Keep asking 'what did your finger feel?' so the answer comes from the test.","In the lab your child presses things to sort them into hard and soft, strokes them to sort rough and smooth, gives one thing two feel words, pulls things to find the stretchy ones, and picks the right thing for a teddy's bed, a strong wall and drying wet hands."]},"familyTip":"Make a feely bag with a sponge, a spoon, a pine cone, a sock and a stone. Your child puts a hand in, says one feel word, then guesses the thing. Ask 'did you press it or stroke it to find out?'","keyIdea":"You find out how a thing feels by touching it. Press it: if it squashes it is soft, if your finger stops it is hard. Stroke it: if it feels bumpy it is rough, if your finger slides it is smooth. One thing can have two feel words, like a ball that is hard and smooth. Pull it to see if it is stretchy. Then pick things by how they feel: a soft bed for teddy, a hard wall, a towel that soaks up water.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Rough or smooth? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["🍍 Rough","🥄 Smooth"],"items":[["tree bark",0],["sandpaper",0],["brick",0],["pine cone",0],["spoon",1],["apple",1],["glass",1],["silk ribbon",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagtouch","caption":"Flagship lab: press, stroke and pull things to sort hard and soft, rough and smooth, and stretchy, then pick things for a job.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_zV5l7tVnMH":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which list is in order of increasing base strength?","choices":["Phenylamine < ammonia < ethylamine","Ethylamine < ammonia < phenylamine","Ammonia < phenylamine < ethylamine"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which lone pair is least available, and which is pushed out by an alkyl group?","explain":"In phenylamine the pair is delocalised into the ring, so it is weakest. The ethyl group's inductive effect makes ethylamine strongest."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Why does ammonia with bromoethane give a mixture of amines?","choices":["Ethylamine formed also has a lone pair and attacks bromoethane","Bromoethane breaks down when heated","Ammonia is a very weak nucleophile"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does the product still have a lone pair on nitrogen?","explain":"Each amine product is still a nucleophile, so it competes with ammonia. That gives secondary, tertiary and quaternary products."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"How can you make ethylamine the main product?","choices":["Use a large excess of ammonia","Use a large excess of bromoethane","Add dilute acid first"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which molecule should bromoethane usually bump into?","explain":"With excess ammonia, bromoethane nearly always meets NH₃, not ethylamine. Excess bromoethane pushes on to the quaternary salt."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Classify CH₃CH₂N(CH₃)₂.","choices":["Tertiary","Secondary","Quaternary"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count every carbon group joined directly to the nitrogen atom.","explain":"N carries an ethyl and two methyl groups, so three carbons are bonded to it. It is tertiary, not quaternary, as it has no charge."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"Bromoethane reacts with {n} mol of ammonia until all of it becomes the quaternary salt (CH₃CH₂)₄N⁺ Br⁻. How many moles of bromoethane are needed?","answer":"4*n","hint":"Each ethyl group on N comes from one bromoethane.","explain":"Four ethyl groups end up on each N, so {n} mol of ammonia needs {=4*n} mol of bromoethane.","params":{"n":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"What is the by-product in CH₃CH₂Br + 2NH₃ → CH₃CH₂NH₂ + ?","choices":["NH₄Br","HBr","Br₂"],"answer":0,"hint":"What happens to HBr when there is extra ammonia present?","explain":"Any HBr formed reacts with the second NH₃, so the by-product is ammonium bromide, NH₄Br."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"Ethylamine has Mr = 45. What mass in grams is {n} mol of ethylamine?","answer":"45*n","hint":"Mass equals moles multiplied by the relative molecular mass.","explain":"{n} mol × 45 g/mol = {=45*n} g of ethylamine.","params":{"n":[2,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which route gives a primary amine with no mixture of products?","choices":["Reduce a nitrile with LiAlH₄","Heat a haloalkane with a little ammonia","Mix ammonia and haloalkane in water at room temperature"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which route cannot add a second carbon group to nitrogen?","explain":"Nitrile reduction turns C≡N into CH₂NH₂ and nothing else, so no secondary or tertiary amines form."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Why is the reaction of ammonia with a haloalkane done in a sealed tube?","choices":["Ammonia is a gas and would escape on heating","To keep oxygen out","To stop the haloalkane freezing"],"answer":0,"hint":"What state is ammonia in at the reaction temperature?","explain":"Heating drives ammonia out of solution. A sealed tube keeps it in contact with the haloalkane."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Classify (CH₃)₃C–NH₂.","choices":["Primary","Secondary","Tertiary"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count only the carbons bonded directly to the nitrogen atom.","explain":"Only one carbon is bonded to N, so it is a primary amine. The three methyls are on that carbon, not on N."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Using CH₃CH₂Br + 2NH₃ → CH₃CH₂NH₂ + NH₄Br, how many moles of ammonia are used up to make {n} mol of ethylamine?","answer":"2*n","hint":"Read the mole ratio of NH₃ to ethylamine from the balanced equation.","explain":"Two NH₃ react for each ethylamine, so {n} mol of ethylamine uses {=2*n} mol of ammonia.","params":{"n":[1,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"What forms when methylamine reacts with hydrochloric acid?","choices":["CH₃NH₃⁺ Cl⁻, methylammonium chloride","CH₃Cl and NH₃","No reaction, amines are neutral"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which atom has a lone pair that can accept H⁺ from the acid?","explain":"The lone pair on N accepts H⁺, giving the methylammonium ion. With Cl⁻ this is the salt methylammonium chloride."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Ethane-1,2-diamine, H₂NCH₂CH₂NH₂, has two NH₂ groups. How many moles of HCl react with {n} mol of it?","answer":"2*n","hint":"Each NH₂ group has one lone pair that can accept one H⁺.","explain":"Each molecule takes two H⁺, so {n} mol of the diamine reacts with {=2*n} mol of HCl.","params":{"n":[1,5]}}],"explain":{"kid":["An amine is like ammonia, NH₃, with carbon groups in place of some H atoms. To class it, count the carbons joined to the N. One carbon means primary. Two means secondary. Three means tertiary.","Look only at the N. In (CH₃)₃C–NH₂ the N has just one carbon on it, so it is primary. Do not count carbons further out, as you would for an alcohol.","The N has a lone pair of electrons. It can grab an H⁺ from an acid, so amines are bases. Ethylamine and HCl make ethylammonium chloride, CH₃CH₂NH₃⁺ Cl⁻. A spare pair on N makes a stronger base. An ethyl group pushes electrons onto N. In phenylamine the pair spreads into the ring. So phenylamine is weaker than ammonia, and ethylamine is stronger.","The lone pair also lets amines attack the δ+ carbon in a haloalkane. Ammonia and bromoethane make ethylamine. But ethylamine has a lone pair too, so it attacks again. You get a mix of amines and even a salt with four groups on N. Use lots of ammonia to get mostly ethylamine."],"adult":["This is A-level organic chemistry (AQA 3.3.11, OCR A 6.2.1, Edexcel Topic 18). Students classify amines as primary, secondary or tertiary. They explain basicity with the nitrogen lone pair and write equations with acids. They describe making amines from haloalkanes and ammonia, including why mixtures form.","Basicity depends on how available the lone pair is to accept a proton. Alkyl groups have a positive inductive effect, so primary aliphatic amines are stronger bases than ammonia. In phenylamine the lone pair is delocalised into the benzene ring, so it is a weaker base than ammonia. The pKa values of the conjugate acids are about 10.7, 9.3 and 4.6.","Common mistakes: classing amines by the carbon on the C–N carbon, as for alcohols, so (CH₃)₃CNH₂ is wrongly called tertiary. Students also write HBr instead of NH₄Br as the by-product. The balanced equation is CH₃CH₂Br + 2NH₃ → CH₃CH₂NH₂ + NH₄Br. Be careful not to claim tertiary amines are always strongest in water. Solvation of the ion also matters.","The reaction uses excess ethanolic ammonia heated in a sealed tube. Each amine formed is still a nucleophile, so further substitution gives secondary and tertiary amines and a quaternary ammonium salt. A large excess of ammonia favours the primary amine. Reducing a nitrile with LiAlH₄ gives the primary amine with no mixture. In the lab your teenager classifies amines, forms ammonium salts, ranks bases and controls the mixture with the mole ratio."]},"familyTip":"Ask your teenager to explain, without notes, why heating bromoethane with ammonia gives more than one product, and what they would change to get mostly ethylamine.","keyIdea":"An amine is ammonia with one or more hydrogens replaced by carbon groups. Count the carbons bonded directly to N: one is primary, two secondary, three tertiary. The lone pair on N makes amines bases (RNH₂ + H⁺ → RNH₃⁺, an ammonium salt) and nucleophiles. Alkyl groups push electron density onto N, so ethylamine is a stronger base than ammonia; in phenylamine the pair is delocalised into the ring, so it is weaker. Ammonia and a haloalkane give a mixture, because each amine formed is also a nucleophile. A large excess of ammonia favours the primary amine.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Stronger or weaker base than ammonia? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Stronger than NH₃","Weaker than NH₃"],"items":[["Methylamine, CH₃NH₂",0],["Ethylamine, CH₃CH₂NH₂",0],["Propylamine, CH₃CH₂CH₂NH₂",0],["Dimethylamine, (CH₃)₂NH",0],["Phenylamine, C₆H₅NH₂",1],["4-nitrophenylamine",1],["4-methylphenylamine",1],["Ethanamide, CH₃CONH₂",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagamine","caption":"Flagship lab: classify amines by carbons on N, protonate the lone pair, rank bases, control the bromoethane mixture and balance the equation.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_swk_xNASmH":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"In alanine, CH₃CH(NH₂)COOH, which part is the R group?","choices":["CH₃","NH₂","COOH"],"answer":0,"hint":"R is the part that changes from one amino acid to the next.","explain":"NH₂ and COOH are in every amino acid. Alanine's side chain R is CH₃."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which amino acid has no chiral centre?","choices":["Glycine","Alanine","Serine"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for an α-carbon with two identical groups.","explain":"Glycine's α-carbon has two H atoms, so it is not chiral. Alanine and serine have four different groups."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"What is alanine's systematic name?","choices":["2-aminopropanoic acid","2-aminoethanoic acid","3-aminopropanoic acid"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count all three carbons, with C1 the COOH carbon.","explain":"Three carbons gives propanoic acid, and NH₂ is on C2. 3-aminopropanoic acid is β-alanine, a different compound."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"At pH 1, alanine exists mainly as…","choices":["H₃N⁺–CH(CH₃)–COOH","H₃N⁺–CH(CH₃)–COO⁻","H₂N–CH(CH₃)–COO⁻"],"answer":0,"hint":"Strong acid gives H⁺ to every group that can take one.","explain":"At pH 1, below the first pKa of about 2.3, COO⁻ is protonated, so the cation H₃N⁺–CH(CH₃)–COOH dominates."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"A mixture has {n} different amino acids, and glycine is one of them. How many of them have a chiral α-carbon?","answer":"n-1","hint":"Only one common amino acid is not chiral.","explain":"All but glycine are chiral, so {n} take away 1 is {=n-1}.","params":{"n":[3,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"What is a zwitterion?","choices":["A molecule with a + and a − charge, overall neutral","A molecule with no charges at all","An ion with a 2+ charge"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of H₃N⁺ and COO⁻ on one molecule.","explain":"In a zwitterion, H₃N⁺ and COO⁻ are on the same molecule. The charges cancel, so it is neutral overall."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"The R group of an α-amino acid is CₙH₂ₙ₊₁ with n = {n}. How many hydrogens does the whole molecule have?","answer":"2*n+1+4","hint":"R has 2n + 1 H. Add the α-H, two on NH₂ and one on COOH.","explain":"R gives {=2*n+1} H, and the α-H, NH₂ and COOH add 4 more: {=2*n+5} hydrogens.","params":{"n":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which atom is the α-carbon?","choices":["The carbon bonded to both NH₂ and COOH","The carbon in the COOH group","The last carbon of the R group"],"answer":0,"hint":"α means the carbon next to the COOH carbon.","explain":"The α-carbon is next to the COOH carbon and carries the NH₂. The COOH carbon is C1."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which is the general formula of an α-amino acid?","choices":["H₂N–CHR–COOH","H₂N–CH₂–CHR–COOH","HO–CHR–COOH"],"answer":0,"hint":"Both functional groups sit on one carbon.","explain":"In an α-amino acid, NH₂ and COOH share the α-carbon. The second option is a β-amino acid and the third is a hydroxy acid."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"An α-amino acid has a side chain R that is an unbranched alkyl group with {n} carbons. How many carbons are in the whole molecule?","answer":"n+2","hint":"Add the α-carbon and the COOH carbon to the R group.","explain":"{n} carbons in R, plus the α-carbon and the COOH carbon, gives {=n+2} carbons.","params":{"n":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Name H₂N–CH₂–COOH (glycine) systematically.","choices":["2-aminoethanoic acid","aminomethanoic acid","1-aminoethanoic acid"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count the COOH carbon as C1.","explain":"There are two carbons, so the stem is ethanoic acid. The NH₂ is on C2: 2-aminoethanoic acid."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A peptide chain is made from {n} amino acids, none of them glycine. How many chiral centres from α-carbons does it have?","answer":"n","hint":"Every α-carbon except glycine's has four different groups.","explain":"Each of the {n} α-carbons is a chiral centre, so there are {n}.","params":{"n":[2,6]}}],"explain":{"kid":["An amino acid has two groups on one carbon. One is an amine group, NH₂. The other is a carboxylic acid group, COOH. That carbon is called the α-carbon. It also holds one H and a side chain, R.","So the general formula is H₂N–CHR–COOH. Only R changes. In glycine, R is just H. In alanine, R is CH₃. In serine, R is CH₂OH. There are 20 common amino acids in proteins, each with its own R.","To name one, count the chain from the COOH carbon. That carbon is C1, so the α-carbon is C2. Alanine has three carbons, so it is 2-aminopropanoic acid. Glycine has two, so it is 2-aminoethanoic acid.","The α-carbon has four groups on it. If all four are different, it is a chiral centre, with two mirror-image forms. That is true for all of them but glycine, which has two H atoms. In water, the NH₂ gains an H⁺ and the COOH loses one. You get H₃N⁺–CHR–COO⁻, a zwitterion."],"adult":["This is A-level organic chemistry (AQA 3.3.13, OCR A 6.2.2). Students recall the general formula RCH(NH₂)COOH, pick out the amine, carboxylic acid and R groups, and name simple amino acids. They link the α-carbon to optical isomerism and draw the zwitterion and the ions formed in acid and in alkali.","The parent chain includes the carboxyl carbon as C1, so the amino group of an α-amino acid is always at C2. Glycine is 2-aminoethanoic acid and alanine is 2-aminopropanoic acid. Serine is 2-amino-3-hydroxypropanoic acid, with prefixes in alphabetical order. Glycine is the only one of the 20 standard amino acids with no chiral centre.","Common mistakes: leaving the COOH carbon out of the count, giving 'aminoethanoic acid' for alanine. Students also number from the wrong end, and call the NH₂ or COOH 'the R group'. They also draw the neutral form as if it existed in water. Be careful with pH: for alanine the pKa values are about 2.3 and 9.7. It is mainly cationic below 2.3, a zwitterion between, and anionic above 9.7.","In the lab your teenager taps the four parts of alanine and builds it from fragments. OH, CHO and a second H are refused with what they would make. They name glycine, alanine and serine, and decide which α-carbons are chiral. Then they slide the pH to see the cation, zwitterion and anion."]},"familyTip":"Ask your teenager to draw alanine from memory, circle the α-carbon, and explain why its systematic name starts with 2-amino. Then ask what it looks like in a strong acid.","keyIdea":"An α-amino acid has the general formula H₂N–CHR–COOH. The amine group (NH₂) and the carboxylic acid group (COOH) sit on the same carbon, the α-carbon, which also carries an H and a side chain R. Only R differs: H in glycine, CH₃ in alanine. Systematic names count the COOH carbon as C1, so alanine is 2-aminopropanoic acid. Every α-carbon except glycine's is a chiral centre. In water near neutral pH, the molecule is a zwitterion, H₃N⁺–CHR–COO⁻.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"α-amino acid or not? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["α-amino acid","Not an α-amino acid"],"items":[["H₂N–CH₂–COOH (glycine)",0],["CH₃CH(NH₂)COOH (alanine)",0],["HOCH₂CH(NH₂)COOH (serine)",0],["(CH₃)₂CHCH(NH₂)COOH (valine)",0],["H₂N–CH₂CH₂–COOH (3-aminopropanoic acid)",1],["CH₃CH(OH)COOH (lactic acid)",1],["CH₃CH₂NH₂ (ethylamine)",1],["CH₃CH₂COOH (propanoic acid)",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagamino","caption":"Flagship lab: find the groups on alanine, build it, name three amino acids, test chirality and slide pH through cation, zwitterion and anion.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_wlLR3jMwLY":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What is the intermediate in nucleophilic addition to a carbonyl?","choices":["An alkoxide ion with O⁻","A carbocation","A free radical"],"answer":0,"hint":"Where do the π electrons end up after the first step?","explain":"The π pair moves onto oxygen, giving an alkoxide ion. A carbocation belongs to electrophilic addition to C=C."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"What forms when butanone is reduced with NaBH₄?","choices":["Butan-2-ol","Butan-1-ol","Butanoic acid"],"answer":0,"hint":"Butanone is a ketone. Where is its C=O?","explain":"Ketones reduce to secondary alcohols; the OH stays on carbon 2, giving butan-2-ol."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Why does ethanal with KCN then dilute acid give a racemic mixture?","choices":["The planar C=O is attacked equally from both faces","The product has no chiral centre","Cyanide only attacks from one side"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about the shape around the carbonyl carbon.","explain":"The carbonyl carbon is trigonal planar, so attack from each face is equally likely, giving equal amounts of both enantiomers."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which product of KCN, then dilute acid, is NOT chiral?","choices":["The product from propanone","The product from propanal","The product from ethanal"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for a carbon with four different groups.","explain":"Propanone gives 2-hydroxy-2-methylpropanenitrile: the carbon has two CH₃ groups, so it is not a chiral centre."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Why does Br₂ add to ethene but not to the C=O of propanone?","choices":["C=C is electron-rich and attracts electrophiles; the C=O carbon is δ+","C=O has no π bond","Bromine is a nucleophile"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is Br₂ looking for electrons or giving them?","explain":"Br₂ acts as an electrophile, attracted to the electron-rich C=C. The δ+ carbonyl carbon attracts nucleophiles instead."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"{n} molecules of propanal are each attacked once by CN⁻ from a random face. About how many give each enantiomer?","answer":"n/2","hint":"Each face is equally likely.","explain":"Equal chance for each face means about {=n/2} of each enantiomer: a racemic mixture.","params":{"n":[20,100]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"A hydroxynitrile with {n} carbon atoms was made from an aldehyde with KCN, then dilute acid. How many carbon atoms did the aldehyde have?","answer":"n-1","hint":"Cyanide added one carbon.","explain":"Take away the nitrile carbon: the aldehyde had {=n-1} carbons.","params":{"n":[3,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"In the protonation step, where does the curly arrow start?","choices":["On a lone pair of the O⁻","On the H⁺ ion","On the C≡N triple bond"],"answer":0,"hint":"Arrows start on electrons. Which species has a spare pair?","explain":"The O⁻ lone pair forms the new O–H bond, so the arrow runs from that lone pair to H⁺."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which atom does the cyanide ion attack in propanal?","choices":["The δ+ carbonyl carbon","The δ− oxygen","The hydrogen on the carbonyl carbon"],"answer":0,"hint":"Oxygen is more electronegative. Which atom is left electron-poor?","explain":"Oxygen pulls the C=O electrons, so the carbonyl carbon is δ+. The nucleophile's lone pair is attracted to it."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"An aldehyde with {n} carbon atoms reacts with KCN, then dilute acid. How many carbon atoms are in the hydroxynitrile?","answer":"n+1","hint":"The cyanide ion brings its own carbon, and a new C–C bond forms.","explain":"CN⁻ adds one carbon, so {n} carbons become {=n+1}. The nitrile carbon is counted in the name.","params":{"n":[1,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"In the first step, where does the curly arrow from the C=O π bond point?","choices":["To the oxygen atom","To the carbonyl carbon","To the cyanide ion"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which atom can best hold a negative charge?","explain":"The π pair moves onto the more electronegative oxygen, giving the alkoxide O⁻."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A ketone with {n} carbon atoms is reduced with NaBH₄. How many carbon atoms are in the alcohol formed?","answer":"n","hint":"Hydride adds hydrogen, not carbon.","explain":"H⁻ adds only hydrogen, so the secondary alcohol still has {n} carbons.","params":{"n":[3,7]}}],"explain":{"kid":["In C=O, oxygen pulls the shared electrons towards itself. So the carbon is a bit positive, δ+. The oxygen is a bit negative, δ−. A nucleophile has a lone pair it can give. It goes for the δ+ carbon.","Step one has two curly arrows. The lone pair on the carbon of CN⁻ moves to the δ+ carbon. That makes a new C–C bond. At the same time, the π pair of C=O moves onto the oxygen. Now we have an alkoxide ion, with O⁻. Step two: a lone pair on O⁻ grabs H⁺. The O⁻ becomes OH.","KCN, then dilute acid, makes a hydroxynitrile. The chain gains one carbon. Propanal has 3 carbons, so it gives 2-hydroxybutanenitrile, with 4. NaBH₄ gives the hydride ion, H⁻, as the nucleophile. An aldehyde turns into a primary alcohol. A ketone turns into a secondary alcohol.","The C=O carbon is flat. So the nucleophile can hit from above or below, with equal odds. Say the new carbon has four different groups. Then each side gives a different enantiomer. They form 50:50. That is a racemic mixture, and it does not rotate polarised light."],"adult":["This is AQA A-level Chemistry 3.3.8, aldehydes and ketones. OCR A 6.1.2 and Edexcel Topic 17 match it. Students outline the mechanism for reduction by NaBH₄, with the nucleophile shown as H⁻. They outline the mechanism with KCN, then dilute acid. They explain why it can give a mixture of enantiomers. KCN is very toxic, and acid releases HCN gas. So the hazard and the fume cupboard are part of the content too.","Terms to use: carbonyl, polar bond, δ+ and δ−, nucleophile, lone pair and curly arrow. Add π bond, alkoxide intermediate, protonation and hydroxynitrile. In names, the nitrile carbon counts as C1. Then reduction, primary and secondary alcohol, and trigonal planar. Last come chiral centre, enantiomer and racemic mixture.","Common mistakes: sending the nucleophile to the δ− oxygen. Starting an arrow on an atom, not on a lone pair or bond. Drawing the cyanide attack from nitrogen. Moving the π pair onto carbon. Forgetting the protonation step. Naming the product with the old chain length. And saying a racemic product has no chiral centre. In fact both enantiomers form, in equal amounts, so their effects on light cancel.","Contrast this with electrophilic addition to C=C. That bond is non-polar and rich in electrons. So it draws electrophiles such as Br₂, HBr and H₂SO₄. The C=O carbon is poor in electrons, so it draws nucleophiles. In the lab, students attack three carbonyls and draw both arrows. They protonate and name three products. They run attacks on each face and sort attackers by target bond."]},"familyTip":"Ask your teenager to draw the mechanism for propanal with KCN then dilute acid from memory, and to explain why the product does not rotate polarised light even though it has a chiral centre.","keyIdea":"Oxygen is more electronegative than carbon, so the C=O bond is polar: the carbon is δ+ and the oxygen δ−. A nucleophile (CN⁻ from KCN, or H⁻ from NaBH₄) uses its lone pair to attack the δ+ carbon; at the same time the π electrons of C=O move onto oxygen, giving an alkoxide intermediate with O⁻. The O⁻ then takes H⁺ (from dilute acid or water) to form an OH group. CN⁻ gives a hydroxynitrile with one more carbon; H⁻ reduces aldehydes to primary alcohols and ketones to secondary alcohols. The C=O group is planar, so attack from either face is equally likely: aldehydes and unsymmetrical ketones give a racemic mixture of enantiomers.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which product forms? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Hydroxynitrile (KCN, then dilute acid)","Alcohol (NaBH₄)"],"items":[["propanal → 2-hydroxybutanenitrile",0],["ethanal → 2-hydroxypropanenitrile",0],["propanone → 2-hydroxy-2-methylpropanenitrile",0],["methanal → 2-hydroxyethanenitrile",0],["propanal → propan-1-ol",1],["propanone → propan-2-ol",1],["butanone → butan-2-ol",1],["ethanal → ethanol",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagnuadd","caption":"Flagship lab: aim CN⁻ and H⁻ at the δ+ carbon, draw the curly arrows, protonate and name the product, attack the flat C=O from both faces.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_ZOGD-twC3W":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Oxygen melts at −218 °C. What does this low melting point tell you?","choices":["The forces between its molecules are weak","The O=O bond is weak","Oxygen is a giant structure"],"answer":0,"hint":"What has to be overcome when a molecular solid melts?","explain":"Only the weak intermolecular forces are overcome on melting, and little energy does that. The O=O covalent bond is strong."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Why does sugar solution not conduct electricity?","choices":["Sugar molecules have no overall charge","Sugar does not dissolve","The covalent bonds are too strong"],"answer":0,"hint":"What must move to carry a current?","explain":"A current needs charged particles that can move. Sugar dissolves as whole, neutral molecules, so there is nothing to carry the charge."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"A substance melts at {n} °C and does not conduct when solid or liquid. What is it most likely to be?","choices":["A simple molecular substance","A metal","A giant ionic compound"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is that melting point low or very high?","explain":"A low melting point of {n} °C means weak forces between molecules, and no conduction means no ions or free electrons: simple molecular.","params":{"n":[-120,90]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Diamond and iodine both contain covalent bonds. Why does diamond melt at about 3550 °C but iodine at 114 °C?","choices":["Melting diamond breaks millions of covalent bonds; melting iodine only overcomes weak forces","Diamond’s bonds are covalent but iodine’s are ionic","Iodine molecules are bigger than diamond"],"answer":0,"hint":"One is a giant structure and one is made of small molecules.","explain":"Diamond is giant covalent, so melting must break strong bonds. Iodine is simple molecular, so only weak intermolecular forces are overcome."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"A student writes: ‘Methane has a low boiling point because its covalent bonds are weak.’ What is the error?","choices":["The bonds are strong; it is the intermolecular forces that are weak","Methane has a high boiling point","Methane has ionic bonds"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which is weak: the bonds inside or the forces between?","explain":"C–H covalent bonds are strong and stay whole on boiling. The weak intermolecular forces explain the low boiling point."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"An alkane chain has {n} carbon atoms. Each carbon has a mass of 12 and each of its {=2*n+2} hydrogens a mass of 1. What is its Mr?","answer":"14*n+2","hint":"Add 12 for each carbon and 1 for each hydrogen.","explain":"Mr = 12 × {n} + {=2*n+2} = {=14*n+2}. A bigger Mr means stronger forces and a higher boiling point.","params":{"n":[2,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Chlorine is a gas at room temperature but bromine is a liquid. Both are made of X₂ molecules. Why?","choices":["Br₂ molecules are bigger, so the forces between them are stronger","Bromine has stronger covalent bonds","Chlorine is a giant structure"],"answer":0,"hint":"Compare the size (Mr) of the two molecules.","explain":"Br₂ (Mr 160) is bigger than Cl₂ (Mr 71). Bigger molecules have stronger intermolecular forces, so bromine boils at a higher temperature."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"A tray holds {n} substances. {k} of them have giant structures and the rest are simple molecular. How many have low melting points?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Simple molecular substances have the low melting points.","explain":"{n} − {k} = {=n-k} simple molecular substances, each with a low melting point.","params":{"n":[7,12],"k":[2,5]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"When water boils, what is overcome?","choices":["The weak intermolecular forces between water molecules","The covalent bonds between O and H atoms","The ionic bonds in water"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is steam still made of H₂O molecules?","explain":"Steam is still H₂O, so the covalent bonds are not broken. Only the weak forces between the molecules are overcome."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A flask holds {n} water molecules. They all boil to steam. How many whole H₂O molecules are in the steam?","answer":"n","hint":"Boiling moves molecules apart. Does it break any of them?","explain":"No covalent bonds break, so all {n} molecules are still whole H₂O in the steam.","params":{"n":[6,40]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which alkane has the highest boiling point?","choices":["Octane, C₈H₁₈ (Mr 114)","Propane, C₃H₈ (Mr 44)","Methane, CH₄ (Mr 16)"],"answer":0,"hint":"Bigger molecules have stronger forces between them.","explain":"Octane has the biggest molecules, so its intermolecular forces are strongest and it boils highest, at about 126 °C."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Propane boils at −42 °C and pentane boils at 36 °C. How many degrees higher is pentane’s boiling point?","answer":78,"hint":"Count up from −42 to 0, then from 0 to 36.","explain":"From −42 °C to 0 °C is 42 degrees, then 36 more: 42 + 36 = 78 degrees higher."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"A straight-chain alkane has {n} carbon atoms in a row, with one C–C bond between each neighbouring pair. How many C–C covalent bonds are in the chain?","answer":"n-1","hint":"A chain of 3 beads needs 2 links.","explain":"A straight chain of {n} carbons has {n} − 1 = {=n-1} C–C bonds. None of them break when it boils.","params":{"n":[3,9]}}],"explain":{"kid":["A molecule is a small group of atoms joined together. Water is H₂O: one oxygen atom joined to two hydrogen atoms. A glass of water holds a huge number of these tiny molecules.","The atoms in each molecule are held by covalent bonds. A covalent bond is a shared pair of electrons, and it is very strong. The pull between one molecule and the next is much weaker. We call it an intermolecular force.","When you boil water, you pull the molecules apart from each other. You do not break them up. The steam is still H₂O. Only the weak forces had to be beaten, so a kettle can do it at 100 °C.","Bigger molecules pull on each other more. That is why octane, in petrol, is a liquid but methane, in a gas cooker, is a gas. These substances also do not conduct, because their molecules carry no charge."],"adult":["This is GCSE Chemistry, bonding and structure: students explain the properties of small molecules in terms of the forces involved. They must state that intermolecular forces, not covalent bonds, are overcome on melting and boiling. They should link the size of these forces to the size of the molecule, and explain why such substances do not conduct.","Precise terms matter here. Simple molecular substances have covalent bonds within each molecule and weak intermolecular forces between molecules. Melting or boiling point depends on the strength of those forces, which increases with relative formula mass, so the alkanes boil at higher temperatures as the chain gets longer. They are usually gases or liquids at room temperature, or solids with low melting points, like iodine and sulfur.","The most common misconception is that covalent bonds break when a molecular substance boils. Be careful with examiner wording: write that weak intermolecular forces are overcome, not that weak bonds break. A second slip is to call these substances weak overall; the covalent bonds are strong. Students also mix up simple molecular and giant covalent structures such as diamond, or say sugar solution conducts because it dissolves.","In the lab, students heat water and check that every H₂O stays whole as steam. They sort lines into covalent bonds and intermolecular forces. They place four alkanes in order of boiling point. Then they test six samples with a bulb circuit and sort seven substances into simple molecular or giant by melting point."]},"familyTip":"When the kettle boils or a puddle dries up, ask: did the water molecules break up, or just move apart? The answer is just apart. Steam is still H₂O, so the strong bonds inside each molecule are untouched.","keyIdea":"Simple molecular substances, such as water, oxygen, methane and iodine, are made of small molecules. Inside each molecule the atoms are held by strong covalent bonds. Between the molecules there are only weak intermolecular forces. When the substance melts or boils, only these weak forces are overcome; the covalent bonds do not break. So simple molecular substances have low melting and boiling points and are often gases or liquids at room temperature. The forces get stronger as molecules get bigger, so boiling point rises with relative formula mass (Mr). The molecules have no overall charge, so these substances do not conduct electricity.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"What happens to the bonds? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["🔗 Covalent bonds inside a molecule break","↔️ Only weak forces between molecules are overcome"],"items":[["Water boils to steam",1],["Methane burns in oxygen to make CO₂ and water",0],["Ice melts in a drink",1],["Iodine crystals turn to purple vapour",1],["Electric current splits water into hydrogen and oxygen",0],["Petrol evaporates from a spill",1],["Hydrogen and chlorine react to make HCl",0],["Dry ice (solid CO₂) turns straight into gas",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagsimol","caption":"Flagship lab: boil water and count whole molecules, sort bonds from forces, rank alkanes, test conduction and sort simple from giant.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_gjb-JrQ-TN":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"A drop of ink has spread evenly through a beaker of water. What are the ink particles doing?","choices":["Still moving randomly, with no net movement","They have stopped moving","Moving to the bottom"],"answer":0,"hint":"Particles in a liquid never stop moving.","explain":"The particles keep moving, but as many go each way, so the concentration stays even and there is no net movement."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"In which will a smell spread fastest?","choices":["Warm air","Cold air","Cold water"],"answer":0,"hint":"Gas beats liquid, and warmer beats colder.","explain":"Gas particles travel further between collisions than liquid particles, and warmer particles move faster, so warm air is fastest."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Ammonia and hydrogen chloride gas diffuse from opposite ends of a glass tube. A white ring forms nearer the hydrogen chloride end. Why?","choices":["Ammonia particles are lighter, so they move faster","Hydrogen chloride particles are faster","The tube was tilted"],"answer":0,"hint":"The faster gas travels further before they meet.","explain":"Ammonia particles are lighter and move faster on average, so they travel further and meet the hydrogen chloride nearer its end."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Ben says a fan is needed for perfume to spread across a room. What is right?","choices":["It spreads anyway, by random particle motion","Ben is right, it cannot spread in still air","It only spreads if the room is hot"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about the lab with no push at all.","explain":"Air currents can speed it up, but diffusion alone spreads the perfume, because the particles move randomly all the time."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"A tube is split into {n} equal sections. At equilibrium each section holds {k} gas particles. How many particles are in the tube?","answer":"n*k","hint":"At equilibrium every section holds the same number.","explain":"{n} sections of {k} particles make {=n*k} particles.","params":{"n":[3,6],"k":[5,12]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which is NOT an example of diffusion?","choices":["Water boiling in a kettle","A smell of toast spreading through a house","Squash colour spreading through water without stirring"],"answer":0,"hint":"One of these is a change of state.","explain":"Boiling is a change of state from liquid to gas. The other two are particles spreading from high to low concentration."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Why does ink spread more slowly in water than a smell spreads in air?","choices":["Liquid particles are close together and collide more often","Liquid particles do not move","Ink particles are attracted to the glass"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about how far a particle travels between bumps.","explain":"In a liquid the particles are packed close, so each one collides very often and travels only a short way between collisions."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"At equilibrium, in 10 seconds {a} particles cross a line from left to right. About how many cross from right to left in the same time?","answer":"a","hint":"At equilibrium there is no net movement.","explain":"No net movement means the crossings balance, so about {a} cross back the other way.","params":{"a":[12,30]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"What is diffusion?","choices":["The net movement of particles from high to low concentration","Particles moving from low to high concentration","A solid dissolving in a liquid"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about where the particles end up: from crowded to less crowded.","explain":"Diffusion is the net movement of particles from a region of higher concentration to a region of lower concentration."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"In 5 seconds, {a} scent particles cross the middle of a box from left to right and {b} cross from right to left. What is the net number that moved to the right?","answer":"a-b","hint":"Net means take the ones going back away from the ones going forward.","explain":"{a} right minus {b} left gives a net movement of {=a-b} particles to the right.","params":{"a":[20,30],"b":[4,15]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Why does a single scent particle not head straight for the empty side of a room?","choices":["It moves randomly and keeps colliding with other particles","It is attracted back to the bottle","Particles can only move up"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about what happens to a particle each time it hits another one.","explain":"Each particle moves in a random direction and changes direction every time it collides, so its path zigzags."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"After diffusion is complete, each half of a box holds {k} dye particles. How many dye particles are in the whole box?","answer":"2*k","hint":"At equilibrium the two halves hold the same number.","explain":"Two halves of {k} each make {=2*k} particles in total.","params":{"k":[20,40]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Open a bottle of perfume at one end of a still room and, a little later, people at the other end smell it. Nobody fans it. The perfume spreads by diffusion. Diffusion is the net movement of particles from where there are lots of them to where there are few. Scientists say from high concentration to low concentration.","Particles in a gas or a liquid are always moving in random directions. They bump into each other and change direction all the time. One particle does not know where the empty space is. It just zigzags. But more particles start on the crowded side, so more of them happen to cross out of it than cross back.","In the end the particles are spread evenly. They do not stop moving. Just as many cross each way, so there is no net movement. The concentration stays the same everywhere.","Diffusion is faster when it is warmer, because the particles move faster. It is much faster in a gas than in a liquid. Gas particles are far apart and travel a long way between bumps. Liquid particles are packed close, so they bump all the time and spread slowly."],"adult":["This is Key Stage 3 science in the national curriculum for England. Pupils learn the particle model and diffusion in liquids and gases, driven by differences in concentration. They also meet Brownian motion in gases. They should explain diffusion with random particle motion. They should predict that it is faster in gases than in liquids, and faster when it is warmer.","Useful terms are particle, random motion, collision, concentration, concentration gradient, net movement, equilibrium and diffusion. The key word is net. Particles move both ways, and diffusion is the overall result. A bigger difference in concentration gives a faster net movement at first.","Common mistakes: thinking particles move on purpose towards the empty space, or that a draught is needed for a smell to spread. Many think particles stop once the spread is even. Pupils also mix up diffusion with dissolving or evaporating, or say it goes from low to high. A classic test uses ammonia and hydrogen chloride in a tube. The white ring forms nearer the hydrogen chloride end, because the lighter ammonia particles move faster.","In the lab your child lifts a divider and watches purple particles spread with no push. They follow one particle's zigzag path and count crossings both ways until they balance. Then they race ink in water at two temperatures, and scent in air against ink in water. The lab speeds things up so you see it in seconds, but the order of the results is real."]},"familyTip":"Drop a little food colouring into a glass of cold water and another of hot water without stirring. Which spreads first? Ask your child to explain it with moving particles.","keyIdea":"Diffusion is the net movement of particles from a region of higher concentration to a region of lower concentration. It happens because particles in liquids and gases move randomly all the time, not because they aim for the empty space: more particles start where it is crowded, so more cross out of it than back in. When the particles are evenly spread they keep moving, but the crossings balance and there is no net movement. Diffusion is faster in gases than in liquids, and faster when it is warmer.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Speeds diffusion up or slows it down? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["⏩ Faster diffusion","🐢 Slower diffusion"],"items":[["Warmer liquid or gas",0],["Spreading through a gas",0],["A big difference in concentration",0],["Lighter particles",0],["Colder liquid or gas",1],["Spreading through a liquid",1],["Heavier particles",1],["Almost even already",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagdiff","caption":"Flagship lab: lift a divider and watch particles spread, follow one random path, count crossings at equilibrium, and race hot vs cold and gas vs liquid.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_JfMiT_qKq9":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"CO₂ has two polar C=O bonds. Which forces act between CO₂ molecules?","choices":["Only London forces","Permanent dipole–dipole forces","Hydrogen bonds"],"answer":0,"hint":"CO₂ is linear. What happens to the two bond dipoles?","explain":"The two dipoles point in opposite directions and cancel, so CO₂ is non-polar and only London forces act."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which substance has hydrogen bonds between its molecules?","choices":["NH₃","CH₃OCH₃","HCl"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for H bonded directly to N, O or F.","explain":"NH₃ has H on N and a lone pair on N. In CH₃OCH₃ every H is on C, and in HCl the H is on Cl."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Pentane boils at 36 °C and 2,2-dimethylpropane at 9.5 °C. Both are C₅H₁₂. Why the difference?","choices":["The straight chain has more surface contact, so stronger London forces","Pentane has more electrons","2,2-dimethylpropane has hydrogen bonds"],"answer":0,"hint":"Same formula, same number of electrons. What is different about the shape?","explain":"The ball-shaped isomer touches its neighbours less, so its London forces are weaker and it boils lower."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"A molecule X₂ is made of two atoms with {z} electrons each. How many electrons does one X₂ molecule have?","answer":"2*z","hint":"There are two atoms in the molecule.","explain":"2 × {z} = {=2*z} electrons. More electrons mean stronger London forces: from F₂ (18 electrons) to I₂ (106), boiling points rise.","params":{"z":[9,53]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"When water boils, what is overcome?","choices":["Hydrogen bonds and other forces between molecules","The O–H covalent bonds","Ionic bonds"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is steam still made of H₂O molecules?","explain":"Steam is still H₂O. Only the forces between molecules are overcome; the covalent bonds stay."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"In a drawing of a hydrogen bond between two water molecules, where should the dotted line go?","choices":["From a δ+ H on one molecule to a lone pair on O of another","From H on one molecule to H on another","Between O and H inside one molecule"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which parts carry opposite partial charges, on different molecules?","explain":"The δ+ H is attracted to a lone pair on the δ− O of a different molecule, in a nearly straight line O–H···O."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Why does ethanol mix with water, but hexane does not?","choices":["Ethanol can hydrogen bond with water molecules","Hexane is too heavy to mix","Ethanol is ionic"],"answer":0,"hint":"What can ethanol's O–H group do with water?","explain":"Ethanol's O–H group forms hydrogen bonds with water. Hexane only has London forces, so water molecules stay bonded to each other."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"HF boils at 20 °C and HCl at −85 °C. A sample of HF is at {t} °C. How many degrees must it warm to reach its boiling point?","answer":"20-t","hint":"Subtract the temperature from 20.","explain":"20 − {t} = {=20-t} degrees. HF boils much higher than HCl because of hydrogen bonds.","params":{"t":[-60,10]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which forces act between HCl molecules?","choices":["London forces and permanent dipole–dipole forces","Hydrogen bonds only","Permanent dipole–dipole forces only"],"answer":0,"hint":"London forces act between all molecules. Is H bonded to N, O or F?","explain":"HCl is polar, so dipole–dipole forces act, and London forces act between all molecules. H is bonded to Cl, not N, O or F, so there are no hydrogen bonds."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"An alkane has {n} carbon atoms, so its formula is CₙH₂ₙ₊₂. How many electrons does one molecule have? (C has 6, H has 1.)","answer":"8*n+2","hint":"Count 6 for each C and 1 for each of the 2n + 2 H atoms.","explain":"6 × {n} + (2 × {n} + 2) = {=8*n+2} electrons. More electrons mean stronger London forces and a higher boiling point.","params":{"n":[1,10]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Ethanol (Mr 46) boils at 78 °C, but propane (Mr 44) boils at −42 °C. Why?","choices":["Ethanol molecules form hydrogen bonds; propane has only London forces","Ethanol has stronger covalent bonds","Ethanol has many more electrons"],"answer":0,"hint":"The Mr values are close. What does ethanol have that propane does not?","explain":"With similar Mr the London forces are similar. Ethanol's O–H group lets hydrogen bonds form, so more energy is needed to separate the molecules."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"The group 16 trend would put the boiling point of water at about −{t} °C. Water really boils at 100 °C. How many degrees higher is that?","answer":"100+t","hint":"From −{t} °C up to 0 °C, then from 0 °C up to 100 °C.","explain":"{t} + 100 = {=100+t} degrees higher. This big jump is caused by hydrogen bonding between water molecules.","params":{"t":[70,90]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Molecules pull on each other. These pulls are called intermolecular forces. They are much weaker than the covalent bonds inside a molecule. When a liquid boils, the molecules move apart, but each molecule stays whole.","London forces act between all molecules. Electrons are always moving, so for a moment one side of a molecule has more of them. That makes a small dipole, and it pulls on the next molecule. More electrons mean bigger dipoles. So octane, C₈H₁₈, boils at 126 °C, but methane, CH₄, boils at −162 °C.","Some molecules have a dipole all the time. In HCl, the Cl pulls the shared electrons, so Cl is δ− and H is δ+. The δ+ end of one molecule pulls the δ− end of the next. This is a permanent dipole–dipole force. CO₂ has polar bonds too, but it is straight, so the two dipoles cancel.","The strongest of the three is the hydrogen bond. You need H bonded to N, O or F. That H is very δ+, and it is pulled to a lone pair on N, O or F of another molecule. This is why water boils at 100 °C, while H₂S boils at −60 °C. It is also why ethanol mixes with water but hexane does not."],"adult":["This is A-level Chemistry, bonding (for example AQA 3.1.3.7 and OCR A 2.2.2). Students must describe three forces: London (induced dipole) forces, permanent dipole–dipole forces and hydrogen bonds. They use them to explain boiling points and how well things mix. They must also draw a hydrogen bond with the partial charges, the lone pair and a straight line.","Key terms: electronegativity, bond polarity, polar molecule, partial charge (δ+ and δ−), induced dipole, permanent dipole, lone pair, hydrogen bond and anomalous. A molecule with polar bonds is only polar if its shape does not cancel them. So CO₂ and CCl₄ are non-polar. London forces grow with the number of electrons and with how much the molecules touch. That is why a straight chain boils higher than a branched one of the same formula.","Common mistakes: saying covalent bonds break when a liquid boils. Thinking London forces act only in non-polar molecules. Giving HCl hydrogen bonds because it has H and a strong pull on electrons. Giving CH₃OCH₃ hydrogen bonds because it has an O. Drawing the bond to an H, or inside one molecule. Be careful with the word strongest: in HCl the London forces are larger than the dipole forces.","In the lab, students tick the forces for five substances, grow and then branch an alkane, and draw two hydrogen bonds. They tap the three odd hydrides on a boiling point plot and compare pairs with almost the same Mr."]},"familyTip":"Put a drop of water and a drop of rubbing alcohol or hand gel on a plate. Which spreads and dries first? Talk about which molecules hold on to each other more strongly, and why water has a high boiling point for such a small molecule.","keyIdea":"Three kinds of force act between molecules. London (induced dipole) forces act between all molecules and get stronger with more electrons and more surface contact. Permanent dipole–dipole forces add to them when the molecule is polar overall. Hydrogen bonds form only when H is bonded to N, O or F and is attracted to a lone pair on N, O or F of another molecule. Boiling a simple molecular substance overcomes these forces, not the covalent bonds, so stronger forces mean a higher boiling point.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Can these molecules hydrogen bond to each other? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Hydrogen bonds","No hydrogen bonds"],"items":[["H₂O",0],["NH₃",0],["HF",0],["CH₃OH",0],["HCl",1],["CH₄",1],["CH₃OCH₃",1],["H₂S",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagimf","caption":"Flagship lab: tick the forces for five substances, grow and branch an alkane, draw hydrogen bonds, find anomalous hydrides and compare similar Mr.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_RpBKpw_MjT":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which forces act between molecules of propanone?","choices":["London forces and permanent dipole–dipole forces","London forces only","London forces, dipole–dipole forces and hydrogen bonds"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is the C=O polar? Is any H bonded to O?","explain":"The polar C=O gives dipole–dipole forces, and London forces act between all molecules. Every H is on carbon, so there are no hydrogen bonds between propanone molecules."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Put these in order of boiling point, lowest first: propan-1-ol, butane, propanal.","choices":["butane, propanal, propan-1-ol","propanal, butane, propan-1-ol","butane, propan-1-ol, propanal"],"answer":0,"hint":"Similar Mr. Rank by the strongest force between molecules.","explain":"Butane has London forces only (−0.5 °C), propanal adds dipole–dipole forces (48 °C), and propan-1-ol has hydrogen bonds (97 °C)."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Why is hexanal much less soluble in water than ethanal?","choices":["Its long non-polar chain cannot hydrogen bond with water","Hexanal has no C=O group","Hexanal has weaker London forces"],"answer":0,"hint":"Both have one C=O. What is different about the rest of the molecule?","explain":"Both can accept hydrogen bonds at the C=O oxygen, but the long hydrocarbon chain in hexanal cannot, so it breaks up water's hydrogen bonding and dissolves poorly."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"A ketone has {n} carbon atoms, formula CnH2nO. What is its Mr? (C = 12, H = 1, O = 16)","answer":"14*n+16","hint":"Ketones share the general formula CnH2nO with aldehydes.","explain":"12 × {n} + 2 × {n} + 16 = {=14*n+16}. An aldehyde with the same carbons has the same Mr.","params":{"n":[3,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"When propanal boils, what is overcome?","choices":["Forces between propanal molecules","The C=O double bond","The C–H bonds"],"answer":0,"hint":"Propanal vapour is still propanal molecules.","explain":"Boiling separates whole molecules. Covalent bonds inside each molecule, such as C=O, stay intact."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Ethanal (Mr 44) boils at 20 °C. Ethanol (Mr 46) boils at 78 °C. What explains the gap?","choices":["Ethanol has an O–H group, so it forms hydrogen bonds between molecules","Ethanol has a larger Mr","Ethanal has no permanent dipole"],"answer":0,"hint":"The Mr values are almost the same. Look for an H bonded to O.","explain":"Ethanol's O–H lets its molecules hydrogen bond to each other. Ethanal has a dipole but no O–H, so its forces are weaker."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Why do propanal molecules not form hydrogen bonds with each other?","choices":["No hydrogen atom is bonded to the oxygen","Propanal has no lone pairs","Propanal is non-polar"],"answer":0,"hint":"A hydrogen bond needs an H on N, O or F as the donor.","explain":"Every H in propanal is bonded to carbon. The oxygen has lone pairs, so it can accept hydrogen bonds from water, but no propanal H can donate one."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"Going {k} steps up the aldehyde series adds {k} CH2 groups. How many extra electrons is that?","answer":"8*k","hint":"One CH2 has one C (6 electrons) and two H (1 each).","explain":"Each CH2 adds 6 + 1 + 1 = 8 electrons, so {k} steps add {=8*k}. London forces get stronger at each step.","params":{"k":[2,5]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Physical properties of aldehydes and ketones: why does propanal (Mr 58) boil higher than butane (Mr 58)?","choices":["Its polar C=O adds permanent dipole–dipole forces","Propanal molecules hydrogen bond to each other","Its C=O bond must break when it boils"],"answer":0,"hint":"Both have similar London forces. What extra force does a polar C=O give?","explain":"The C=O bond is polar, so dipole–dipole forces act between propanal molecules as well as London forces. Butane has London forces only."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"An aldehyde has {n} carbon atoms, formula CnH2nO. What is its Mr? (C = 12, H = 1, O = 16)","answer":"14*n+16","hint":"Add 12 for each C, 1 for each of the 2n H atoms, and 16 for the O.","explain":"12 × {n} + 2 × {n} + 16 = {=14*n+16}. Each CH2 unit adds 14, so Mr climbs by 14 per carbon.","params":{"n":[2,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which part of propanone accepts a hydrogen bond from a water molecule?","choices":["A lone pair on the C=O oxygen","The δ+ carbonyl carbon","An H atom in a CH3 group"],"answer":0,"hint":"Water's H is δ+. It is attracted to a lone pair on an electronegative atom.","explain":"Water's δ+ H is attracted to a lone pair on the δ− oxygen. The carbonyl carbon is δ+ too, and the CH3 hydrogens have no lone pairs."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"An aldehyde has {n} carbon atoms, formula CnH2nO. How many electrons does one molecule have? (C has 6, H has 1, O has 8)","answer":"8*n+8","hint":"6 for each C, 1 for each of the 2n H atoms, then 8 for the O.","explain":"6 × {n} + 2 × {n} + 8 = {=8*n+8} electrons. More electrons means stronger London forces, so the boiling point rises along the series.","params":{"n":[1,8]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Aldehydes and ketones both have a C=O group. Oxygen pulls the shared electrons harder than carbon does. So the O end is a little negative, δ−, and the C end is a little positive, δ+. The whole molecule has a dipole: one end is slightly plus and the other end is slightly minus.","Opposite ends of nearby molecules attract. These attractions are called permanent dipole–dipole forces. They add to the London forces that act between all molecules. So propanal, with Mr 58, boils at 48 °C. Butane, also Mr 58, has only London forces. It boils at about 0 °C.","But there is a limit. A hydrogen bond needs an H joined to N, O or F. In propanal and propanone every H is on carbon. So their molecules cannot hydrogen bond to each other. Propan-1-ol has an O–H group, so it does. It boils at 97 °C. The order is alkane, then carbonyl, then alcohol.","Water is different. Water has its own δ+ H atoms. They can reach the lone pairs on the C=O oxygen and form hydrogen bonds. That is why ethanal and propanone mix with water. A longer chain has more electrons, so it boils higher. But the long chain cannot bond with water, so it dissolves less."],"adult":["This is A-level chemistry, such as AQA 3.3.9 and OCR A module 6. It builds on the three forces between molecules: London forces, permanent dipole–dipole forces and hydrogen bonds. Learners compare boiling points with alkanes and alcohols of similar Mr. They also explain why short carbonyls mix with water.","The C=O bond is polar, so these molecules have a permanent dipole. No H is bonded to O, so they cannot hydrogen bond to each other. Butane boils at −0.5 °C, propanal at 48 °C and propan-1-ol at 97 °C. In water, lone pairs on the C=O oxygen accept hydrogen bonds from water's δ+ H. Up the series, London forces grow and the long chain cuts solubility.","Common mistakes: saying aldehydes hydrogen bond to each other because they contain oxygen; thinking boiling breaks the C=O bond; giving Mr as the reason propan-1-ol boils higher than propanal; drawing water's H bonded to the δ+ carbon; and saying longer chains dissolve less because they have weaker forces.","In the lab, learners rank compounds of matching Mr by boiling point and pick the forces between molecules. They draw a hydrogen bond from water to a C=O oxygen. They slide from C1 to C6 to see boiling point rise as solubility falls, then pin three compounds on a thermometer."]},"familyTip":"Smell nail-polish remover (it is mostly propanone) and notice how fast a drop dries on a tile, then compare a drop of water. Talk about why the propanone escapes sooner: weaker forces between its molecules than water's hydrogen bonds.","keyIdea":"Aldehydes and ketones have a polar C=O group, so permanent dipole–dipole forces act between their molecules as well as London forces. They have no H on O, so their molecules cannot hydrogen bond to each other. That puts their boiling points above alkanes and below alcohols of similar Mr. Small ones dissolve in water because water's δ+ H forms hydrogen bonds to lone pairs on the C=O oxygen. Longer chains raise the boiling point and lower solubility.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which forces act between molecules of each substance? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["London only","Plus dipole–dipole","Plus hydrogen bonds"],"items":[["butane",0],["propanal",1],["propan-1-ol",2],["propanone",1],["pentane",0],["ethanol",2],["butanone",1],["ethanal",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagcbp","caption":"Flagship lab: rank boiling points at matching Mr, name the forces, bond water to a C=O oxygen and slide along the aldehydes.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_9JWPPZgdLu":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"A swimming pool at 25 °C and a sparkler at 1000 °C. Which has more thermal energy stored in total?","choices":["The pool","The sparkler","They have the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"Total energy depends on how many particles there are, too.","explain":"The sparkler is hotter, but the pool has vastly more particles, so its thermal energy store is far bigger."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"What does temperature measure?","choices":["The average kinetic energy of the particles","The total energy in an object","The size of the particles"],"answer":0,"hint":"Speeds are spread out, so we need a typical value.","explain":"Temperature is a measure of the average kinetic energy of the particles, not the total energy or their size."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"A metal spoon sits in hot soup. Why does the spoon warm up?","choices":["Energy passes from the faster soup particles to the spoon","Cold flows out of the spoon into the soup","The spoon particles get bigger"],"answer":0,"hint":"Energy goes from hotter to cooler.","explain":"Faster particles in the hot soup collide with the spoon and pass on energy until both are at the same temperature."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"A gas is at {k} K. What is its temperature in °C? (Take away 273.)","answer":"k-273","hint":"Degrees Celsius = kelvin − 273.","explain":"Kelvin is 273 more than degrees Celsius, so take 273 away: {k} − 273 = {=k-273} °C.","params":{"k":[280,370]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Food colouring spreads faster in hot water than in cold water. Why?","choices":["The particles move faster in hot water","Hot water pushes the colour","The colour particles are bigger"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about what heating does to particle speed.","explain":"In hot water all the particles move faster, so the colour mixes in sooner. Nothing pushes it."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"In a gas at 20 °C, do all the particles move at the same speed?","choices":["No, there is a spread of speeds","Yes, all exactly the same","Yes, they all stop"],"answer":0,"hint":"Remember the frozen moment in the lab.","explain":"Particles keep colliding and swapping energy, so some are slow and some fast. The temperature reflects the average."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"Two identical blocks touch: one at {a} °C and one at 10 °C. Ignoring losses, what is their final shared temperature in °C?","answer":"(a+10)/2","hint":"Identical blocks end halfway between the two.","explain":"Halfway between {a} °C and 10 °C is {=(a+10)/2} °C.","params":{"a":[30,90]},"where":"a%2==0"},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Ice is cooled from −5 °C to −30 °C. What happens to its particles?","choices":["They vibrate less, but still move","They stop moving completely","They start to flow"],"answer":0,"hint":"Motion is only at its lowest at absolute zero.","explain":"Cooling lowers the average kinetic energy, so the particles vibrate less. −30 °C is far above absolute zero, −273 °C."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"What happens to the particles in a gas when it is heated?","choices":["They move faster on average","They get bigger","They stop moving"],"answer":0,"hint":"Heating gives the particles more kinetic energy.","explain":"Heating raises the average kinetic energy, so the particles move faster. They do not change size."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Water is at {c} °C. What is its temperature in kelvin? (Add 273.)","answer":"c+273","hint":"Kelvin = degrees Celsius + 273.","explain":"The kelvin scale starts at absolute zero, so add 273: {c} + 273 = {=c+273} K.","params":{"c":[10,90]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"How do particles move in a solid?","choices":["They vibrate about fixed places","They do not move at all","They fly about freely"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about the ice in the lab: still there, still jiggling.","explain":"Particles in a solid are held in place, but they still vibrate. Hotter means they vibrate more."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Two identical metal blocks touch. One is at {a} °C and the other at 20 °C. Ignoring energy lost to the air, what temperature do they both end at?","answer":"(a+20)/2","hint":"Identical blocks meet exactly halfway.","explain":"Halfway between {a} °C and 20 °C is {=(a+20)/2} °C.","params":{"a":[40,100]},"where":"a%2==0"}],"explain":{"kid":["Everything is made of tiny particles, and they never sit still. In a solid they jiggle on the spot. In a liquid they slide past each other. In a gas they zoom about and bash into the walls. Even an ice cube has particles that vibrate.","Heat a gas and its particles move faster. Their energy of motion is called kinetic energy. The particles do not swell up or grow. They just move more. Not every particle has the same speed: some are slow and some are very fast. Temperature tells you the average kinetic energy of the particles.","Temperature is not the same as total energy. A cup of tea at 80 °C is hotter than a bath at 40 °C. Its particles are faster on average. But the bath has far more particles, so it stores much more thermal energy. It could melt far more ice.","Put a hot block against a cold block. Fast particles in the hot block bump slow ones in the cold block and pass on energy. The hot one cools and the cold one warms up. When both are the same temperature, the flow stops. That is called thermal equilibrium."],"adult":["This is Key Stage 3 science in the national curriculum for England, under the particle model and energy. It also matches NGSS MS-PS1-4. Pupils link temperature to particle motion in solids, liquids and gases. They learn that heating raises the average kinetic energy of the particles. They also tell temperature apart from thermal energy, and see energy pass from hotter to cooler objects.","Useful terms are particle, kinetic energy, average, temperature, thermal energy store, internal energy, kelvin and thermal equilibrium. Absolute zero is 0 K, or about −273 °C. That is where particle motion is at its lowest. To turn degrees Celsius into kelvin, add 273.","Common mistakes: saying particles expand or melt when heated, rather than move faster. Many pupils think particles in a solid are still. Others think cold flows into things, or that a hotter object always holds more energy. Some say temperature is the speed of every particle, but speeds are spread out and temperature tracks the average.","In the lab your child heats and cools a gas on a thermometer and watches the speed bar. They freeze a moment to spot slow particles in the crowd. They match motion to each state and sort cards between a hot cup and a big warm bath. Then they press hot and cold blocks together and watch them meet in the middle. The lab ignores energy lost to the air."]},"familyTip":"Hold a mug of hot tea and then dip a hand in a warm bath. Which is hotter, and which holds more energy? Ask your child to explain it with particles.","keyIdea":"All particles are moving all the time: they vibrate in a solid, slide past each other in a liquid and fly about freely in a gas. Temperature is a measure of the average kinetic energy of the particles. When something is heated its particles move faster on average, but they do not get bigger. Temperature is not the same as thermal energy: a big cool bath can store far more energy than a small hot cup, because it has far more particles.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is this about temperature or about total thermal energy? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["🌡️ Temperature (average per particle)","🔋 Thermal energy (total store)"],"items":[["Read with a thermometer",0],["Higher when particles move faster on average",0],["Measured in °C or kelvin",0],["Same for a spoonful and a jug of the same soup",0],["Bigger in a bath than in a cup at the same heat",1],["Depends on how many particles there are",1],["Measured in joules",1],["Melts more ice when it is a larger amount",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagtemp","caption":"Flagship lab: heat a gas, freeze it to see a spread of speeds, match each state, split temperature from energy, and reach equilibrium.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_ylruY6VhOf":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Oxygen melts at −218 °C and boils at −183 °C. What state is oxygen in at {t} °C?","choices":["Gas","Liquid","Solid"],"answer":0,"hint":"Compare {t} °C with its boiling point.","explain":"{t} °C is far above oxygen's boiling point of −183 °C, so it is a gas.","params":{"t":[0,30]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"A metal melts at {m} °C. What state is it in at {t} °C?","choices":["Solid","Liquid","Gas"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is it hot enough to melt?","explain":"{t} °C is below its melting point of {m} °C, so it is still solid.","params":{"m":[300,320],"t":[20,40]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which state keeps its own shape?","choices":["Solid","Liquid","Gas"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which particles are fixed in place?","explain":"Solid particles are held in a fixed pattern, so a solid keeps its shape."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"A smell spreads across a room. Why?","choices":["Gas particles move around and spread out","Smells are not made of particles","Only warm air can carry smells"],"answer":0,"hint":"Gas particles move in all directions.","explain":"The smell's gas particles move and mix with the air particles until they spread out."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"What is between the particles in a gas?","choices":["Empty space","Air","Water"],"answer":0,"hint":"Air is itself made of particles.","explain":"Nothing: there is empty space. Air can't be between them, because air is made of particles too."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Ice and liquid water are made of the same particles. What is different?","choices":["How the particles are arranged and move","The size of the particles","What the particles are made of"],"answer":0,"hint":"Same particles, different state.","explain":"Only the arrangement and movement change; the particles are the same."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"When you heat a gas in a sealed box, its particles…","choices":["move faster","get bigger","turn into a liquid"],"answer":0,"hint":"Heating gives particles energy.","explain":"More energy means faster particles. They don't grow."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"In which state are the particles far apart and moving fast in all directions?","choices":["Gas","Liquid","Solid"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which state spreads out to fill a room?","explain":"In a gas the particles are far apart and move quickly."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"A substance melts at {m} °C and boils at {b} °C. What state is it in at {t} °C?","choices":["Liquid, because {t} °C is between its melting and boiling points","Solid, because {t} °C is below its boiling point","Gas, because {t} °C is above its melting point"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is {t} °C above the melting point? Is it below the boiling point?","explain":"{t} °C is above {m} °C (so it has melted) and below {b} °C (so it hasn't boiled). It is a liquid.","params":{"m":[10,30],"b":[80,100],"t":[40,60]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Why can a gas be squashed but a solid can't?","choices":["Gas particles have big spaces between them","Gas particles are smaller","Solid particles are glued together"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about spacing.","explain":"There is space between gas particles to push them into. Solid particles are already touching."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"When ice melts, what happens to its particles?","choices":["They gain energy and start to slide past each other","They melt into smaller particles","They get bigger"],"answer":0,"hint":"The particles themselves don't change.","explain":"The particles gain energy and break out of their fixed pattern. They are still the same particles."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"A sealed syringe holds {v} cm³ of air. You push the plunger until the air takes up half the space. What is the new volume in cm³?","answer":"v/2","hint":"Halve {v}.","explain":"Half of {v} is {=v/2} cm³. The same particles are just pushed closer together.","params":{"v":[10,50]},"where":"v%2==0"}],"explain":{"kid":["All matter is made of particles that are far too small to see. In a solid, the particles are packed tightly in a fixed pattern and can only vibrate. In a liquid, they are close together but can slide past each other. In a gas, they are far apart and move fast in all directions.","This explains what we see. A solid keeps its shape. A liquid takes the shape of its container. A gas spreads out to fill any space."],"adult":["The particle model explains properties we can see. Gases can be squashed because there is empty space between the particles. Solids and liquids hardly squash at all, because their particles are already touching.","Heating gives particles more energy, so they vibrate or move faster. That's why heating can melt a solid or boil a liquid. The particles themselves do not get bigger or melt."]},"familyTip":"Fill a syringe (with no needle) with air, block the end with your finger and push. Then try it with water.","keyIdea":"Everything is made of tiny particles. How they are arranged and how they move explains solids, liquids and gases.","visual":{"type":"particles","caption":"Heat and cool the water. Watch the particles' arrangement and speed.","opts":{"mp":0,"bp":100,"temp":20,"name":"water"}},"lab":{"type":"gaslab","caption":"Real moving particles: heat them, squeeze the box or add more. Watch them collide and the pressure change.","opts":{"n":30,"temp":300,"vol":1}}},"mt_b6kZgqolEd":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"A stone has a mass of {=d*v} g and a volume of {v} cm³. Find its density in g/cm³.","answer":"d","hint":"Mass ÷ volume.","explain":"{=d*v} ÷ {v} = {d} g/cm³.","params":{"d":[2,8],"v":[3,12]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"A metal has a density of {d} g/cm³. What is the mass of {v} cm³ of it, in g?","answer":"d*v","hint":"Density × volume.","explain":"{d} × {v} = {=d*v} g.","params":{"d":[2,11],"v":[2,10]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which is best explained by particles being far apart?","choices":["Gases are easy to squash","Solids keep their shape","Liquids are hard to squash"],"answer":0,"hint":"Squashing needs space.","explain":"Gases squash because there is space between their particles."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"100 g of ice melts. What is the mass of the water?","choices":["100 g","Less than 100 g","More than 100 g"],"answer":0,"hint":"Are any particles lost?","explain":"Still 100 g. The same particles are just arranged differently."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which is a limit of the simple particle model?","choices":["It shows particles as hard balls, which they are not","It can't explain melting","It says gases have no particles"],"answer":0,"hint":"What do the diagrams leave out?","explain":"Real particles aren't hard spheres, and the diagrams don't show the forces between them."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"The gas in a balloon is cooled. Its particles…","choices":["slow down, and the balloon shrinks","shrink in size","stop moving completely"],"answer":0,"hint":"Less energy means less movement.","explain":"The particles slow down and hit the walls less, so the balloon shrinks. The particles stay the same size."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Usually, which state of a substance has the highest density?","choices":["Solid","Liquid","Gas"],"answer":0,"hint":"Where are particles packed closest?","explain":"Usually the solid. Water is a famous exception: ice floats because it is less dense than water."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"A block has a mass of 54 g and a volume of 20 cm³. What is its density in g/cm³?","answer":2.7,"hint":"Mass ÷ volume.","explain":"54 ÷ 20 = 2.7 g/cm³."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"An object has a mass of {=d*v} g and a volume of {v} cm³. What is its density in g/cm³?","answer":"d","hint":"Mass ÷ volume.","explain":"{=d*v} ÷ {v} = {d} g/cm³.","params":{"d":[2,9],"v":[2,15]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Why is steam much less dense than liquid water?","choices":["The same particles are much more spread out","Steam particles are lighter","Steam has no mass"],"answer":0,"hint":"Same particles, different spacing.","explain":"The particles are identical, but in steam they are far apart, so less mass fits in each cm³."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A liquid has a density of {d} g/cm³. What is the mass of {v} cm³ of it, in g?","answer":"d*v","hint":"Mass = density × volume.","explain":"{d} × {v} = {=d*v} g.","params":{"d":[1,3],"v":[10,50]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"When water boils, what is overcome?","choices":["The forces between water particles","The bonds inside each water particle","Nothing: the particles just get bigger"],"answer":0,"hint":"Boiling doesn't make a new substance.","explain":"Boiling overcomes the forces between particles. Each water particle stays whole."}],"explain":{"kid":["The particle model explains things using particles. In a solid, particles are close and fixed. In a liquid they are close but move around. In a gas they are spread out.","Density is how much mass is packed into a space. Most solids are dense, because their particles are packed closely. Gases have a low density, because their particles are far apart."],"adult":["Density = mass ÷ volume, often in g/cm³. A 20 cm³ block with a mass of 54 g has a density of 54 ÷ 20 = 2.7 g/cm³. A change of state changes the spacing, so it changes the density. The mass stays the same.","Every model has limits. Real particles are not hard little balls. Simple diagrams also leave out the forces between them. Even so, the model predicts a lot."]},"familyTip":"Weigh a full glass of water, then work out its density using the volume printed on a measuring jug.","keyIdea":"The arrangement, movement and spacing of particles explain density, squashing, flowing and changes of state.","visual":{"type":"particles","caption":"Same particles, same mass: watch how much space they take up as the state changes.","opts":{"mp":0,"bp":100,"temp":-10,"name":"water","density":true}},"lab":{"type":"gaslab","caption":"Same particles, smaller box: the density and the pressure go up. Try the challenges.","opts":{"n":40,"temp":300,"vol":1}}},"mt_Z_Wu_77ybI":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"How many atoms in total are in {n} molecules of carbon dioxide, CO₂?","answer":"3*n","hint":"Each CO₂ has 1 + 2 atoms.","explain":"3 atoms each, so {n} × 3 = {=3*n}.","params":{"n":[2,12]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"How many oxygen atoms are in {n} molecules of oxygen, O₂?","answer":"2*n","hint":"Each O₂ has 2 atoms.","explain":"{n} × 2 = {=2*n}.","params":{"n":[2,15]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which is a compound?","choices":["CO₂","O₂","Fe"],"answer":0,"hint":"More than one element, joined.","explain":"CO₂ has carbon and oxygen joined together."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"What is the symbol for iron?","choices":["Fe","I","Ir"],"answer":0,"hint":"It comes from the Latin word ferrum.","explain":"Fe. (I is iodine and Ir is iridium.)"},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Where are the protons and neutrons in an atom?","choices":["In the nucleus","In the shells","Spread evenly through the atom"],"answer":0,"hint":"The tiny centre.","explain":"Protons and neutrons are in the nucleus; electrons are in shells."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which statement about water, H₂O, is true?","choices":["Each molecule has 2 hydrogen atoms and 1 oxygen atom","Each molecule has 2 oxygen atoms","It is a mixture of hydrogen and oxygen"],"answer":0,"hint":"The small 2 belongs to H.","explain":"H₂O: 2 hydrogen and 1 oxygen, chemically joined, so it's a compound."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"What is the difference between a carbon atom and the element carbon?","choices":["An atom is one particle; the element is a substance made only of carbon atoms","There is no difference at all","The element contains other atoms too"],"answer":0,"hint":"One particle, or a lot of them?","explain":"An element is made of many atoms, all of the same type."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which of these is an element?","choices":["Oxygen, O₂","Water, H₂O","Carbon dioxide, CO₂"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for only one kind of atom.","explain":"O₂ has only oxygen atoms, so it's an element, even though the atoms are joined in pairs."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"How many atoms are in {n} molecules of water, H₂O?","answer":"3*n","hint":"Each H₂O has 2 + 1 atoms.","explain":"Each H₂O has 3 atoms, so {n} × 3 = {=3*n}.","params":{"n":[2,12]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"What decides which element an atom is?","choices":["The number of protons","The number of neutrons","Its size"],"answer":0,"hint":"It's the atomic number.","explain":"The number of protons is the atomic number, and it decides the element."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"How many hydrogen atoms are in {n} molecules of methane, CH₄?","answer":"4*n","hint":"Each CH₄ has 4 hydrogen atoms.","explain":"{n} × 4 = {=4*n}.","params":{"n":[2,12]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Sodium is a reactive metal and chlorine is a toxic gas. What is sodium chloride like?","choices":["A safe white solid: table salt","A reactive, toxic metal","A mixture you can separate with a magnet"],"answer":0,"hint":"Compounds are new substances.","explain":"Sodium chloride is table salt. Its properties are completely different from its elements."}],"explain":{"kid":["Atoms are the smallest bits of an element. There are about 118 elements. Oxygen, iron and carbon are three of them. Each element has its own kind of atom and a short symbol: O for oxygen, Fe for iron.","A compound forms when atoms of different elements join. Water, H₂O, is a compound. Each water particle has two hydrogen atoms joined to one oxygen atom."],"adult":["An atom has a tiny central nucleus, made of protons and neutrons. Electrons are arranged in shells around it. The number of protons decides which element the atom is.","Compounds can behave very differently from their elements. Sodium is a reactive metal and chlorine is a toxic gas. Together they make sodium chloride, which is table salt. A formula like CO₂ gives the ratio of atoms: one carbon to two oxygen."]},"familyTip":"Read the ingredients on a food or cleaning label. Which names are elements and which are compounds?","keyIdea":"An element has only one type of atom. A compound has two or more elements chemically joined.","visual":{"type":"atom","caption":"Build an atom. The number of protons decides the element.","opts":{"p":6,"n":6,"e":6}},"lab":{"type":"flagatom","caption":"Flagship lab: zoom into gold, sort particle pictures, then build molecules atom by atom.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_MBTVB-E-S7":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"A pure substance melts at {m} °C. An impure sample of it will probably melt…","choices":["below {m} °C, over a range","at exactly {m} °C","above {=m+20} °C"],"answer":0,"hint":"Impurities lower and spread the melting point.","explain":"Impurities lower the melting point and spread it over a range, so below {m} °C.","params":{"m":[50,120]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"A fertiliser is {p}% nitrogen. How many kg of nitrogen are in {=k*10} kg?","answer":"p*k/10","hint":"Find {p}% of {=k*10}.","explain":"{p}% of {=k*10} kg = {=p*k/10} kg.","params":{"p":[10,30],"k":[1,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which is a mixture?","choices":["Air","Oxygen","Carbon dioxide"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which has several substances not joined?","explain":"Air is a mixture of nitrogen, oxygen, argon, carbon dioxide and more."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"A label says 'pure apple juice'. Is it pure in the science sense?","choices":["No: it's a mixture of water, sugars and other substances","Yes, because nothing was added","Yes, because it is all one colour"],"answer":0,"hint":"Science 'pure' means one substance.","explain":"Even with nothing added, juice contains many substances."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which is a formulation?","choices":["Paint","Gold","Sea water"],"answer":0,"hint":"A mixture designed for a purpose, with exact amounts.","explain":"Paint is made to a recipe with exact amounts. Sea water is a mixture, but not designed."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"How could you test whether a white solid is pure?","choices":["Measure its melting point","Look at its colour","Weigh it"],"answer":0,"hint":"Pure substances melt at one temperature.","explain":"A sharp melting point at the expected value shows it is pure."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Salt dissolved in water is…","choices":["a mixture","a compound","an element"],"answer":0,"hint":"Are the salt and water chemically joined?","explain":"The salt and water are not joined, so it is a mixture; you can get the salt back by evaporating."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"In science, which is pure?","choices":["Distilled water","Fresh orange juice","Sea water"],"answer":0,"hint":"One substance only.","explain":"Distilled water contains only water. The others are mixtures."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"A sample melts over a range, from {a} °C to {=a+r} °C. What does this suggest?","choices":["It is a mixture (impure)","It is a pure substance","It is an element"],"answer":0,"hint":"Pure substances melt at one temperature.","explain":"Melting over a {r} °C range suggests impurities.","params":{"a":[40,80],"r":[3,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A formulation is {p}% active ingredient. How many grams of active ingredient are in {=k*100} g?","answer":"p*k","hint":"Find {p}% of {=k*100}.","explain":"{p}% of {=k*100} g = {=p*k} g.","params":{"p":[5,40],"k":[1,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"What is the difference between a mixture and a compound?","choices":["In a compound, the elements are chemically joined in a fixed ratio","A mixture is always a liquid","A compound can be separated by filtering"],"answer":0,"hint":"Joined, or just mixed?","explain":"Compound: elements joined in a fixed ratio. Mixture: substances not joined, in any amounts."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Pure water boils at 100 °C at normal pressure. A water sample boils at {t} °C. What does that suggest?","choices":["It is not pure","It is pure","It has turned into a different compound"],"answer":0,"hint":"Pure water boils at exactly one temperature.","explain":"Boiling at {t} °C suggests something is dissolved in the water.","params":{"t":[101,106]}}],"explain":{"kid":["In science, 'pure' means one substance only, with nothing else mixed in. Pure water is only water particles. 'Pure' orange juice is a mixture of water, sugars and more.","A mixture has two or more substances that are not joined together. Air, sea water and salad are mixtures. You can often separate a mixture, for example by filtering or evaporating."],"adult":["You can test purity by melting or boiling. A pure substance changes state at one fixed temperature. Pure water boils at 100 °C at normal air pressure. Impurities lower the melting point and spread it over a range.","A formulation is a mixture designed for a job, with exact amounts of each part. Paints, medicines and fertilisers are formulations. Compounds are different from mixtures: their elements are chemically joined, in a fixed ratio."]},"familyTip":"Look at three drinks or foods at home. Which are pure in the science sense? (Hint: almost none.)","keyIdea":"A pure substance contains only one element or compound. It melts and boils at exact temperatures, while mixtures melt and boil over a range.","visual":{"type":"particles","caption":"Salt water is a mixture: two kinds of particle, not joined. It freezes below 0 °C and boils above 100 °C.","opts":{"mp":-2,"bp":101,"temp":20,"name":"salt water","mix":true}}},"mt_wfEfQuHOG-":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"A neutral atom has {z} electrons. What is its atomic number?","answer":"z","hint":"Electrons = protons in a neutral atom.","explain":"It has {z} protons, so its atomic number is {z}.","params":{"z":[1,36]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"An element has atomic number {z}. How many electrons does a neutral atom of it have?","answer":"z","hint":"Same as the protons.","explain":"{z} protons, so {z} electrons.","params":{"z":[1,36]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Elements in the same group…","choices":["have similar chemical properties","have the same mass","are all gases"],"answer":0,"hint":"That's why groups are useful.","explain":"Elements in a group react in similar ways."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"What did Mendeleev do that made his table so convincing?","choices":["He left gaps and predicted elements that were found later","He discovered every element","He put the elements in alphabetical order"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about predictions.","explain":"His gaps predicted elements such as gallium and germanium, which were later found."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which group contains the noble gases, which barely react?","choices":["Group 0 (also called Group 18)","Group 1","Group 7"],"answer":0,"hint":"The far right column.","explain":"The noble gases are in Group 0, also numbered Group 18."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"What is the atomic number of an element?","choices":["The number of protons in its atoms","The number of neutrons","Its position in the alphabet"],"answer":0,"hint":"It decides the element.","explain":"It is the number of protons."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which of these is a non-metal?","choices":["Sulfur","Iron","Sodium"],"answer":0,"hint":"Non-metals are on the right.","explain":"Sulfur is a non-metal; iron and sodium are metals."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"What are the columns of the periodic table called?","choices":["Groups","Periods","Shells"],"answer":0,"hint":"Rows are periods.","explain":"Columns are groups; rows are periods."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"An element has atomic number {z}. How many protons are in each of its atoms?","answer":"z","hint":"Atomic number = number of protons.","explain":"The atomic number is the number of protons, so {z}.","params":{"z":[1,30]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Where are the metals in the periodic table?","choices":["On the left and in the middle","Only in the top row","On the far right"],"answer":0,"hint":"Non-metals are on the right.","explain":"Most elements are metals, filling the left and middle."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A neutral atom has {z} protons. How many electrons does it have?","answer":"z","hint":"Neutral means the charges balance.","explain":"A neutral atom has as many electrons as protons: {z}.","params":{"z":[1,30]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Lithium, sodium and potassium are in Group 1. Which is the most reactive?","choices":["Potassium","Lithium","They are all the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"Reactivity increases down Group 1.","explain":"Potassium is lowest of the three, so it is most reactive."}],"explain":{"kid":["The periodic table lists every element. Each box shows a symbol and a number, such as 6 for carbon. That number is the atomic number: how many protons each atom has.","Columns are called groups. Elements in the same group behave in similar ways. Rows are called periods. Metals are on the left and in the middle, and non-metals are on the right."],"adult":["In 1869 Dmitri Mendeleev put the known elements in order. He left gaps for elements no one had found yet and predicted their properties. When gallium and germanium were discovered, his predictions matched.","Patterns run down each group. In Group 1, the alkali metals, reactivity increases as you go down: lithium fizzes in water, while potassium bursts into a lilac flame. Group 0 (also called Group 18) holds the noble gases, which barely react at all."]},"familyTip":"Find a periodic table online and look up five elements you use at home.","keyIdea":"Elements are arranged by atomic number in rows (periods) and columns (groups). Elements in a group have similar properties.","visual":{"type":"ptable","caption":"Highlight groups, periods, metals and non-metals. Choose an element to read about it.","opts":{"highlight":"group1"}}},"mt_t20A6RuTJn":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"How many atoms in total are in {n}CH₄?","answer":"5*n","hint":"Each CH₄ has 1 + 4 atoms.","explain":"Each CH₄ has 5 atoms, so {n} × 5 = {=5*n}.","params":{"n":[2,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"How many oxygen atoms are in {n}Ca(OH)₂?","answer":"2*n","hint":"Each Ca(OH)₂ has 2 oxygen atoms.","explain":"{n} × 2 = {=2*n} oxygen atoms.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"How many hydrogen atoms are in 2NH₃?","answer":6,"hint":"2 molecules × 3 hydrogen atoms.","explain":"2 × 3 = 6."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"How many atoms in total are in Mg(NO₃)₂?","answer":9,"hint":"The bracket doubles N and O₃.","explain":"1 Mg + 2 N + 6 O = 9 atoms."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"How many oxygen atoms are in Al₂(SO₄)₃?","choices":["12","4","7"],"answer":0,"hint":"The bracket is multiplied by 3.","explain":"4 oxygen atoms × 3 = 12. Adding 4 + 3 = 7 is a common slip."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"How many atoms of each element are in H₂SO₄?","choices":["2 H, 1 S, 4 O","2 H, 2 S, 4 O","1 H, 1 S, 1 O"],"answer":0,"hint":"Each small number belongs to the symbol just before it.","explain":"H₂ gives 2 H, S has no number so 1, O₄ gives 4."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"The right-hand side of an equation has {n}H₂O. How many hydrogen atoms must the left-hand side have?","answer":"2*n","hint":"Atoms are not made or destroyed.","explain":"{n} × 2 = {=2*n}, so the left needs {=2*n} hydrogen atoms too.","params":{"n":[2,8]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"How many atoms in total are in one H₂O molecule?","answer":3,"hint":"2 hydrogen + 1 oxygen.","explain":"2 + 1 = 3 atoms."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"How many hydrogen atoms are in {n}H₂O?","answer":"2*n","hint":"{n} molecules, each with 2 hydrogen atoms.","explain":"{n} × 2 = {=2*n} hydrogen atoms.","params":{"n":[2,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"How many oxygen atoms are in Ca(OH)₂?","answer":2,"hint":"The bracket is doubled.","explain":"(OH) × 2 gives 2 oxygen atoms."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"How many oxygen atoms are in {n}CO₂?","answer":"2*n","hint":"Each CO₂ has 2 oxygen atoms.","explain":"{n} × 2 = {=2*n} oxygen atoms.","params":{"n":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"In 3H₂O, what does the 3 mean?","choices":["3 water molecules","3 hydrogen atoms","3 oxygen atoms"],"answer":0,"hint":"A big number in front counts molecules.","explain":"3H₂O is three whole water molecules."}],"explain":{"kid":["In H₂O, the small 2 belongs to the H. So there are 2 hydrogen atoms and 1 oxygen atom: 3 atoms in all.","A big number in front counts molecules. 3H₂O means 3 water molecules. That is 3 × 2 = 6 hydrogen atoms and 3 oxygen atoms."],"adult":["Brackets multiply everything inside them. In Ca(OH)₂ there is 1 calcium, 2 oxygen and 2 hydrogen atoms: 5 atoms in total.","Use both rules together when needed. 2Ca(OH)₂ has 2 calcium, 4 oxygen and 4 hydrogen atoms. In an equation, count each element on each side. A balanced equation has the same totals on both sides."]},"familyTip":"Write formulae on cards (H₂O, CO₂, 2NH₃) and race each other to count the atoms.","keyIdea":"A small number after a symbol counts that atom. A big number in front counts whole molecules. Brackets multiply everything inside.","visual":{"type":"sealed","caption":"Heat calcium hydroxide, Ca(OH)₂, in a sealed flask. Count the atoms before and after: the table checks your count.","opts":{"left":[["Ca(OH)2",1]],"right":[["CaO",1],["H2O",1]],"gas":["H2O"]}}},"mt_ehC1wsdmUz":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"In an open beaker, {m} g of chemicals fizz and give off a gas. Afterwards the beaker's contents have a mass of {=m-g} g. What mass of gas escaped, in g?","answer":"g","hint":"Find the difference.","explain":"{m} − {=m-g} = {g} g of gas escaped.","params":{"m":[100,200],"g":[2,15]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"{=7*k} g of iron reacts with {=4*k} g of sulfur to make iron sulfide. Nothing escapes. What mass of iron sulfide forms, in g?","answer":"11*k","hint":"Add the masses.","explain":"{=7*k} + {=4*k} = {=11*k} g.","params":{"k":[1,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"An iron nail rusts and gets heavier. Where does the extra mass come from?","choices":["Oxygen (and water) from the air","Nowhere: rust is made from nothing","The nail grows"],"answer":0,"hint":"Rust forms when iron reacts with something.","explain":"Iron reacts with oxygen and water from the air, so their mass joins the nail."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"In a sealed container, which change does NOT conserve mass?","choices":["None: mass is always conserved","Melting","Burning"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about every kind of change.","explain":"Mass is conserved in every change if nothing gets in or out."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Sugar dissolves in tea and can't be seen. Where is it?","choices":["Its particles are spread out between the water particles","It has been destroyed","It has turned into water"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can you still taste it?","explain":"The sugar particles are still there, spread through the water."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"40 g of vinegar and 5 g of baking soda react in a sealed bottle. What is the total mass afterwards, in g?","answer":45,"hint":"Sealed: nothing escapes.","explain":"40 + 5 = 45 g."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Why do scientists seal the container when testing conservation of mass?","choices":["So no gas can get in or out","To keep it warm","To speed up the reaction"],"answer":0,"hint":"Gases are easy to lose.","explain":"Sealing stops gases escaping or entering, so the total mass can be compared fairly."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"10 g of sugar dissolves in 200 g of water. What is the mass of the solution, in g?","answer":210,"hint":"Add the masses.","explain":"10 + 200 = 210 g."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"{s} g of salt dissolves in {w} g of water. What is the mass of the salt water, in g?","answer":"s+w","hint":"Nothing is lost when it dissolves.","explain":"{s} + {w} = {=s+w} g. The salt is still there.","params":{"s":[5,30],"w":[100,250]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"An ice cube with a mass of 25 g melts. What is the mass of the water?","choices":["25 g","Less than 25 g","More than 25 g"],"answer":0,"hint":"No particles are lost.","explain":"Still 25 g. Melting doesn't change the mass."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"In a sealed flask, chemicals with a total mass of {m} g react and make a gas. What is the total mass afterwards, in g?","answer":"m","hint":"Can the gas escape?","explain":"The flask is sealed, so nothing escapes. The mass stays {m} g.","params":{"m":[100,300]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Wood burns in an open fire. The ash is lighter than the wood. Why?","choices":["Gases made in burning escape into the air","Some mass is destroyed","The fire uses some mass up as heat"],"answer":0,"hint":"Where do the products go?","explain":"Carbon dioxide and water vapour escape. Their mass plus the ash equals the wood plus the oxygen used."}],"explain":{"kid":["If you dissolve 10 g of sugar in 200 g of water, the solution has a mass of 210 g. The sugar seems to vanish, but its particles are still there, spread out in the water.","This is true for every change: melting, freezing, dissolving and even burning. Nothing is made from nothing, and nothing just disappears. We call this conservation of mass."],"adult":["Sometimes mass seems to change. When wood burns in the open, the ash is lighter. That's because gases, carbon dioxide and water vapour, escape into the air. A rusting nail gets heavier, because it gains oxygen from the air.","If the change happens in a sealed container, the total mass stays exactly the same. Scientists use this to check results, and to work out the mass of a gas that escaped."]},"familyTip":"Weigh a glass of water and a spoonful of sugar, stir the sugar in, then weigh again.","keyIdea":"In any change, the total mass stays the same, as long as nothing gets in or out.","visual":{"type":"sealed","caption":"Iron and sulfur in a sealed flask on a scale. React them: the atoms join up as iron sulfide and the reading stays the same.","opts":{"left":[["Fe",2],["S",2]],"right":[["FeS",2]],"names":{"Fe":"iron","S":"sulfur","FeS":"iron sulfide"}}}},"mt_tFOhzG3wLi":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"{=4*k} g of hydrogen reacts with {=32*k} g of oxygen to make water. What mass of water forms, in g?","answer":"36*k","hint":"Add the masses.","explain":"{=4*k} + {=32*k} = {=36*k} g.","params":{"k":[1,6]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"The products of a reaction contain {n} carbon atoms. How many carbon atoms were in the reactants?","answer":"n","hint":"Atoms are not created or destroyed.","explain":"The same {n} carbon atoms.","params":{"n":[2,20]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Why is mass conserved in a reaction?","choices":["The same atoms are there before and after","The products are always heavier","Energy turns into mass"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count the atoms.","explain":"No atoms are gained or lost, so the mass stays the same."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"In 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O, how many hydrogen atoms are on the right?","choices":["4","2","6"],"answer":0,"hint":"2 molecules × 2 atoms.","explain":"2H₂O has 2 × 2 = 4 hydrogen atoms."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Iron and sulfur are heated and make iron sulfide. Which is true?","choices":["It is a new substance with different properties","It is a mixture you can separate with a magnet","The iron atoms turn into sulfur atoms"],"answer":0,"hint":"A reaction makes a new substance.","explain":"Iron sulfide is a compound. It isn't magnetic like iron, and atoms never change element."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which of these is a chemical reaction rather than a change of state?","choices":["Burning magnesium","Melting ice","Boiling water"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which makes a new substance?","explain":"Burning magnesium makes magnesium oxide, a new substance."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"C + O₂ → CO₂. 12 g of carbon reacts completely with 32 g of oxygen. What mass of carbon dioxide forms, in g?","answer":44,"hint":"Add the masses.","explain":"12 + 32 = 44 g."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"In a chemical reaction, what happens to the atoms?","choices":["They are rearranged into new substances","Some are destroyed","New atoms are created"],"answer":0,"hint":"Atoms swap partners.","explain":"The same atoms join up in new ways."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"{=3*k} g of magnesium reacts completely with {=2*k} g of oxygen to make magnesium oxide. What mass of magnesium oxide forms, in g?","answer":"5*k","hint":"All the atoms end up in the product.","explain":"{=3*k} + {=2*k} = {=5*k} g.","params":{"k":[1,12]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"In C + O₂ → CO₂, how many oxygen atoms are on each side?","choices":["2 on each side","2 on the left and 1 on the right","1 on each side"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count the small numbers.","explain":"O₂ has 2, and CO₂ has 2."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A reaction makes {n}H₂O. How many oxygen atoms must the reactants contain?","answer":"n","hint":"Each H₂O has 1 oxygen atom.","explain":"{n} × 1 = {n} oxygen atoms. Atoms are not created or destroyed.","params":{"n":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"What are the starting substances in a reaction called?","choices":["Reactants","Products","Catalysts"],"answer":0,"hint":"They react.","explain":"Reactants go in; products come out."}],"explain":{"kid":["In a chemical reaction, the starting substances are called reactants. The new substances are called products. The atoms swap partners, but they are all still there.","When carbon burns in oxygen, one carbon atom joins two oxygen atoms. Together they make carbon dioxide, CO₂. There is 1 carbon atom before and after, and 2 oxygen atoms before and after."],"adult":["Because every atom is still there, the total mass of the products equals the total mass of the reactants. This is the particle-level reason for conservation of mass.","Particle diagrams show this well. Draw 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O. On the left there are 4 hydrogen atoms and 2 oxygen atoms. On the right there are 4 hydrogen atoms and 2 oxygen atoms. Only the partners have changed."]},"familyTip":"Use coloured sweets or building bricks as atoms. Build the reactants, then rebuild them into the products without adding or removing any.","keyIdea":"In a chemical reaction, atoms are rearranged into new substances. No atoms are created or destroyed.","visual":{"type":"balancer","caption":"2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O: the atoms are rearranged, not created or destroyed.","opts":{"left":[["H2",2],["O2",1]],"right":[["H2O",2]],"lock":true}}},"mt_dzgK92gM32":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"magnesium carbonate → magnesium oxide + carbon dioxide. {=84*k} g of magnesium carbonate makes {=40*k} g of magnesium oxide. What mass of carbon dioxide is made, in g?","answer":"44*k","hint":"Subtract.","explain":"{=84*k} − {=40*k} = {=44*k} g.","params":{"k":[1,6]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"sodium + chlorine → sodium chloride. {=23*k} g of sodium reacts with {=35.5*k} g of chlorine. What mass of sodium chloride forms, in g?","answer":"58.5*k","hint":"Add the masses.","explain":"{=23*k} + {=35.5*k} = {=58.5*k} g.","params":{"k":[1,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which word equation shows methane burning completely?","choices":["methane + oxygen → carbon dioxide + water","methane → carbon + hydrogen","methane + water → oxygen"],"answer":0,"hint":"Fuel + oxygen → ?","explain":"Complete burning of a hydrocarbon gives carbon dioxide and water."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which elements are in copper sulfate?","choices":["Copper, sulfur and oxygen","Copper and sulfur only","Copper and oxygen only"],"answer":0,"hint":"'-ate' means oxygen is included.","explain":"'-ate' tells you there is oxygen too: copper, sulfur and oxygen."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Complete: hydrochloric acid + sodium hydroxide → sodium chloride + …","choices":["water","hydrogen","carbon dioxide"],"answer":0,"hint":"Acid + alkali → salt + ?","explain":"Acid + alkali → salt + water."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"In 'iron + oxygen → iron oxide', what does the + on the left mean?","choices":["'And': iron and oxygen react together","Add the numbers","The reaction gives off heat"],"answer":0,"hint":"It isn't maths here.","explain":"The + means 'and'."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"What are the reactants in: zinc + copper sulfate → zinc sulfate + copper?","choices":["Zinc and copper sulfate","Zinc sulfate and copper","Only zinc"],"answer":0,"hint":"Reactants are on the left.","explain":"Zinc and copper sulfate are on the left of the arrow."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"In magnesium + oxygen → magnesium oxide, what is the product?","choices":["Magnesium oxide","Magnesium","Oxygen"],"answer":0,"hint":"Products are on the right.","explain":"Magnesium oxide is on the right of the arrow."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"iron + sulfur → iron sulfide. {=7*k} g of iron reacts with {=4*k} g of sulfur. What mass of iron sulfide forms, in g?","answer":"11*k","hint":"Mass is conserved.","explain":"{=7*k} + {=4*k} = {=11*k} g.","params":{"k":[1,12]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"What does the arrow mean in a word equation?","choices":["React to make","Equals","Add together"],"answer":0,"hint":"It shows a change.","explain":"The arrow means the reactants react to make the products."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Complete: zinc + hydrochloric acid → zinc chloride + …","choices":["hydrogen","water","oxygen"],"answer":0,"hint":"Metal + acid → salt + ?","explain":"Metal + acid → salt + hydrogen."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"calcium carbonate → calcium oxide + carbon dioxide. Heating {=100*k} g of calcium carbonate makes {=56*k} g of calcium oxide. What mass of carbon dioxide is made, in g?","answer":"44*k","hint":"Total mass stays the same.","explain":"{=100*k} − {=56*k} = {=44*k} g.","params":{"k":[1,5]}}],"explain":{"kid":["A word equation is a short way to describe a reaction. Reactants go on the left and products go on the right. The arrow means 'react to make'.","For example: magnesium + oxygen → magnesium oxide. The + on the left means 'and'. It doesn't mean adding numbers."],"adult":["Names give clues. A compound ending in '-ide' usually has just two elements: sodium chloride is sodium and chlorine. One ending in '-ate' also has oxygen: copper sulfate has copper, sulfur and oxygen.","Some patterns are worth learning. Metal + acid → salt + hydrogen. Acid + alkali → salt + water. When a fuel like methane burns completely: fuel + oxygen → carbon dioxide + water."]},"familyTip":"Write a word equation for something that happens in your kitchen, such as burning gas on a hob.","keyIdea":"A word equation names the reactants, then an arrow meaning 'react to make', then the products.","visual":{"type":"sealed","caption":"magnesium + oxygen → magnesium oxide. React it, read the word equation, and see what opening the flask does.","opts":{"left":[["Mg",2],["O2",1]],"right":[["MgO",2]],"gas":["O2"],"names":{"Mg":"magnesium","O2":"oxygen","MgO":"magnesium oxide"}}}},"mt_BXj4pfdhxO":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"CH₄ + 2O₂ → CO₂ + 2H₂O. How many molecules of O₂ are needed to burn {k} molecules of CH₄?","answer":"2*k","hint":"2 O₂ for each CH₄.","explain":"{k} × 2 = {=2*k} molecules of O₂.","params":{"k":[2,12]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"N₂ + 3H₂ → 2NH₃. How many H₂ molecules react with {k} N₂ molecules?","answer":"3*k","hint":"3 H₂ for each N₂.","explain":"{k} × 3 = {=3*k} molecules of H₂.","params":{"k":[2,12]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which equation is balanced?","choices":["CH₄ + 2O₂ → CO₂ + 2H₂O","CH₄ + O₂ → CO₂ + H₂O","CH₄ + O₂ → CO₂ + 2H₂O"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count C, H and O on each side.","explain":"CH₄ + 2O₂ → CO₂ + 2H₂O has 1 C, 4 H and 4 O on each side."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"When balancing, what are you allowed to change?","choices":["The big numbers in front of formulae","The small numbers inside formulae","Both"],"answer":0,"hint":"Small numbers define the substance.","explain":"Only the big numbers (coefficients) may change."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"Balance: N₂ + □H₂ → 2NH₃. What number goes in the box?","answer":3,"hint":"2NH₃ has 6 hydrogen atoms.","explain":"6 hydrogen atoms need 3H₂, so the number is 3."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Write 4H₂ + 2O₂ → 4H₂O in its simplest ratio.","choices":["2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O","H₂ + O₂ → H₂O","2H₂ + 2O₂ → 2H₂O"],"answer":0,"hint":"Divide every big number by 2.","explain":"Halving gives 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O, which is still balanced."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"Balance: □Na + Cl₂ → 2NaCl. What number goes in the box?","answer":2,"hint":"2NaCl has 2 sodium atoms.","explain":"2 sodium atoms are needed on the left, so the number is 2."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"In □H₂ + O₂ → □H₂O, both boxes hold the same number. What is it?","answer":2,"hint":"Balance the oxygen first.","explain":"2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O has 4 H and 2 O on each side, so the number is 2."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"N₂ + 3H₂ → 2NH₃. How many molecules of NH₃ can {k} molecules of N₂ make, with plenty of H₂?","answer":"2*k","hint":"Each N₂ makes 2NH₃.","explain":"{k} × 2 = {=2*k} molecules of NH₃.","params":{"k":[2,12]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Why can't you balance H₂ + O₂ → H₂O by writing H₂O₂?","choices":["H₂O₂ is a different substance (hydrogen peroxide)","H₂O₂ has too many hydrogen atoms","You can: that is correct"],"answer":0,"hint":"What do the small numbers decide?","explain":"Changing a small number changes the substance. H₂O₂ is hydrogen peroxide, not water."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Balance: □Mg + O₂ → 2MgO. What number goes in the box?","answer":2,"hint":"How many Mg atoms are on the right?","explain":"2MgO has 2 Mg atoms, so you need 2Mg. Then there are 2 Mg and 2 O on each side."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O. How many molecules of O₂ react with {=2*k} molecules of H₂?","answer":"k","hint":"2 H₂ for every 1 O₂.","explain":"{=2*k} ÷ 2 = {k} molecules of O₂.","params":{"k":[2,12]}}],"explain":{"kid":["H₂ + O₂ → H₂O is not balanced. There are 2 oxygen atoms on the left but only 1 on the right. We can't change H₂O into H₂O₂, because that is a different substance.","Instead, put big numbers in front. 2H₂O gives 2 oxygen atoms, but now 4 hydrogen atoms. So write 2H₂ on the left. Now 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O is balanced: 4 H and 2 O on each side."],"adult":["A good method is to count the atoms of each element on both sides in a table. Balance one element at a time. Leave elements that appear on their own, like O₂, until last. Recount after every change.","Never change the small numbers. That would change the substance itself. Give the final numbers in their simplest ratio: 4H₂ + 2O₂ → 4H₂O should be written as 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O."]},"familyTip":"Use coins or buttons as atoms to model an equation, and check both sides have the same of each.","keyIdea":"Balance an equation by changing only the big numbers in front of formulae, until each element has the same number of atoms on both sides.","visual":{"type":"balancer","caption":"Balance it: change only the big numbers until every row of the table matches.","opts":{"left":[["CH4",1],["O2",1]],"right":[["CO2",1],["H2O",1]]}}},"mt_v0K6GRi4ZL":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Rusting is an example of…","choices":["oxidation","thermal decomposition","displacement"],"answer":0,"hint":"Iron gains something from the air.","explain":"Iron gains oxygen (with water present): oxidation."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which metal can displace copper from copper sulfate solution?","choices":["Zinc","Gold","Silver"],"answer":0,"hint":"It must be more reactive than copper.","explain":"Zinc is more reactive than copper; gold and silver are less reactive."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"What does complete combustion of a fuel like methane produce?","choices":["Carbon dioxide and water","Hydrogen and oxygen","Only smoke"],"answer":0,"hint":"The carbon and hydrogen both join with oxygen.","explain":"Carbon → carbon dioxide; hydrogen → water."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"A candle burns {a} g of wax using {b} g of oxygen. What total mass of products forms, in grams?","answer":"a+b","hint":"No mass is lost or gained.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b} g of products.","params":{"a":[2,9],"b":[5,20]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"Heating {=a+b} g of copper carbonate leaves {a} g of copper oxide. What mass of gas was given off, in grams?","answer":"b","hint":"The difference is the gas.","explain":"{=a+b} − {a} = {b} g.","params":{"a":[10,30],"b":[4,20]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Gold does not react with copper sulfate solution. Why?","choices":["Gold is less reactive than copper","Gold is a gas","Copper sulfate is not a compound"],"answer":0,"hint":"Displacement needs a more reactive metal.","explain":"Only a more reactive metal can push copper out, and gold is less reactive."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which word equation shows thermal decomposition?","choices":["calcium carbonate → calcium oxide + carbon dioxide","magnesium + oxygen → magnesium oxide","zinc + copper sulfate → zinc sulfate + copper"],"answer":0,"hint":"One reactant, two products.","explain":"One substance breaks into two when heated."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which is a displacement reaction?","choices":["iron + copper sulfate → iron sulfate + copper","methane + oxygen → carbon dioxide + water","calcium carbonate → calcium oxide + carbon dioxide"],"answer":0,"hint":"One metal swaps places with another.","explain":"Iron takes copper's place in the compound because iron is more reactive."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Heating {=a+b} g of calcium carbonate leaves {a} g of calcium oxide. How many grams of carbon dioxide escaped?","answer":"b","hint":"Mass is conserved: the missing mass is the gas.","explain":"{=a+b} − {a} = {b} g of carbon dioxide.","params":{"a":[10,30],"b":[5,25]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"{a} g of magnesium is oxidised and gains {b} g of oxygen. What mass of magnesium oxide forms, in grams?","answer":"a+b","hint":"The oxide contains both.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b} g of magnesium oxide.","params":{"a":[2,12],"b":[1,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Which type of reaction uses heat to break one substance into two or more?","choices":["Thermal decomposition","Displacement","Combustion"],"answer":0,"hint":"'Thermal' means heat.","explain":"Thermal decomposition: heat breaks a compound down."}],"explain":{"kid":["Chemists sort reactions into types. Combustion is burning. A fuel reacts with oxygen and gives out heat and light.","Oxidation means a substance gains oxygen, like iron when it rusts. Thermal decomposition means heat breaks one substance into simpler ones. Displacement means a more reactive metal pushes a less reactive metal out of its compound."],"adult":["Combustion is fast oxidation that gives out energy. Methane burns to make carbon dioxide and water. Rusting is slow oxidation of iron. It needs both air and water.","In thermal decomposition, heat splits one compound. Limestone (calcium carbonate) gives lime and carbon dioxide. In displacement, iron pushes copper out of copper sulfate, because iron is more reactive."]},"familyTip":"Spot reactions at home: the gas hob (combustion), a rusty bike chain (oxidation), baking powder in a hot oven (thermal decomposition).","keyIdea":"Combustion is burning in oxygen; oxidation is gaining oxygen; thermal decomposition breaks a substance down with heat; displacement swaps in a more reactive metal.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Sort each everyday reaction into its type. Drag a card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Combustion","Oxidation","Thermal decomposition","Displacement"],"items":[["Methane burns on a gas cooker",0],["A candle burns",0],["An iron nail slowly rusts",1],["Copper turns black when heated in air",1],["Heating limestone makes lime",2],["Heating green copper carbonate gives a black solid",2],["An iron nail in copper sulfate turns pink",3],["Zinc in copper sulfate solution goes coppery",3]]}},"lab":{"type":"reactlab","caption":"Try neutralisation, acid + metal and acid + carbonate. Test each gas.","opts":{"acid":1}}},"mt_1hgck6ucII":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Water boiling is a…","choices":["physical change","chemical change"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is the steam still water?","explain":"Steam is still water, so it is a physical change."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Frying an egg is a…","choices":["chemical change","physical change"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can it be unfried?","explain":"New substances form in the egg, and it can't be undone."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"{a} g of magnesium burns in a closed jar and uses {b} g of oxygen. What mass of magnesium oxide forms, in grams?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Mass is conserved in a closed jar.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b} g.","params":{"a":[2,12],"b":[1,8]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"{a} g of sugar dissolves in {b} g of tea. What does the sweet tea weigh, in grams?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Add the masses.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b} g.","params":{"a":[3,15],"b":[150,300]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Why is dissolving salt a physical change?","choices":["The salt is still there: evaporate the water and it comes back","A new substance forms","It can never be undone"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can you get the salt back?","explain":"Evaporating the water gets the salt back, so no new substance formed."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"When wood burns in an open fire, the ash weighs less than the wood. Why?","choices":["Gases escaped into the air","Mass was destroyed","Ash is lighter than air"],"answer":0,"hint":"Where did the missing mass go?","explain":"Carbon dioxide and water vapour went into the air; the mass still exists."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which change makes a new substance?","choices":["Rusting","Melting","Freezing"],"answer":0,"hint":"Changes of state don't make new substances.","explain":"Rust is a new substance, iron oxide."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which is a chemical change?","choices":["Toast going brown","Butter melting","Sugar dissolving"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is a new substance made?","explain":"Browning makes new substances; the others are physical changes."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"{a} g of salt dissolves in {b} g of water. What is the mass of the solution, in grams?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Dissolving is physical: nothing is lost.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b} g.","params":{"a":[5,40],"b":[50,200]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"{a} g of ice melts. What is the mass of the water, in grams?","answer":"a","hint":"Melting changes state, not mass.","explain":"Melting is a physical change, so the water still weighs {a} g.","params":{"a":[5,90]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Which is a sign of a chemical change?","choices":["A gas is given off and a new colour appears","The substance changes shape","Ice turns into water"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for signs of a new substance.","explain":"Fizzing and a new colour show a new substance has formed."}],"explain":{"kid":["In a physical change, the stuff stays the same. Melted ice is still water. You can usually change it back.","In a chemical change, new substances are made. Burning wood makes ash, smoke and gases. Signs include a new colour, fizzing, light or heat. In both kinds of change, the total mass stays the same."],"adult":["Physical changes alter state, shape or mixing, but the particles themselves are unchanged, so they are usually reversible: melting, dissolving, evaporating.","Chemical changes rearrange atoms into new substances and are usually hard to reverse. Mass is conserved in both. If a gas escapes from an open container, the mass seems to drop, but the gas still has mass."]},"familyTip":"Cook together and name each change: butter melting (physical), toast browning (chemical), sugar dissolving in tea (physical).","keyIdea":"A physical change makes no new substance and is usually reversible; a chemical change makes new substances. Mass is conserved in both.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Sort each change: is a new substance made? Drag a card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Physical change","Chemical change"],"items":[["Ice melting",0],["Salt dissolving in water",0],["Water boiling",0],["Cutting paper",0],["Wood burning",1],["Iron rusting",1],["Baking a cake",1],["Milk going sour",1]]}}},"mt_rbPioPELM1":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"A bike chain rusting is…","choices":["irreversible","reversible"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can rust turn back into shiny iron by itself?","explain":"Rust is a new material, so the change is irreversible."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"What does iron need to rust?","choices":["Water and oxygen","Only heat","Only sunlight"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of a wet nail in air.","explain":"Rusting needs both water and oxygen."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"{a} g of candle wax is melted and then left to set. What mass of wax is there now, in grams?","answer":"a","hint":"Melting and setting are reversible.","explain":"No new material formed, so there is still {a} g.","params":{"a":[10,90]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"{a} g of chalk reacts with {b} g of acid in a sealed bottle. What do the contents weigh afterwards, in grams?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Sealed: nothing escapes.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b} g.","params":{"a":[2,10],"b":[20,60]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"How could you get sugar back after dissolving it in water?","choices":["Evaporate the water","Burn it","You can't"],"answer":0,"hint":"Dissolving is reversible.","explain":"Let the water evaporate and the sugar is left behind."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which is NOT a sign of an irreversible change?","choices":["Changing shape","A new smell","Bubbles of gas"],"answer":0,"hint":"Squashing clay doesn't make a new material.","explain":"A change of shape makes nothing new."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Cooking an egg is irreversible because…","choices":["new substances form in the egg","the egg gets hot","the shell cracks"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can you uncook it?","explain":"Heat changes the egg into new substances that can't change back."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which change is irreversible?","choices":["Burning paper","Melting butter","Freezing juice"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one makes new materials?","explain":"Burning makes ash and gases, which cannot turn back into paper."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A {a} g ice lolly melts. How much does the juice weigh, in grams?","answer":"a","hint":"Melting is reversible: nothing is lost.","explain":"The juice still weighs {a} g.","params":{"a":[20,90]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"{a} g of vinegar and {b} g of baking soda fizz inside a sealed bag. What do the bag's contents weigh afterwards, in grams?","answer":"a+b","hint":"The gas is trapped in the bag.","explain":"Nothing can escape, so it is {a} + {b} = {=a+b} g.","params":{"a":[20,80],"b":[2,10]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Which clue shows a new material has formed?","choices":["Bubbles of a new gas","It changed shape","It got wet"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for something new.","explain":"A new gas means a new material: an irreversible change."}],"explain":{"kid":["Some changes can be undone. Melted chocolate sets again when it cools. These are reversible changes.","Other changes make new materials that cannot turn back. Burnt toast stays burnt. Rust stays rust. Vinegar and baking soda fizz and make a gas. These are irreversible changes."],"adult":["Irreversible changes are chemical: new materials form. Clues include fizzing (a gas forms), a lasting colour change, heat or light given out, or a new smell.","Burning needs oxygen and makes new substances such as carbon dioxide, water and ash. Rusting needs iron, water and oxygen. Acids react with carbonates to release carbon dioxide."]},"familyTip":"Make a mini volcano with vinegar and bicarbonate of soda. Ask: can we get the vinegar back? Why not?","keyIdea":"Some changes make new materials that cannot easily be turned back, like burning, rusting and acid reactions.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Can it be undone? Sort each change into reversible or irreversible.","opts":{"bins":["Reversible","Irreversible"],"items":[["Melting chocolate",0],["Freezing water",0],["Dissolving sugar",0],["Puddles drying up",0],["Burning toast",1],["A bike rusting",1],["Baking soda fizzing in vinegar",1],["Cooking an egg",1]]}}},"mt_Ge4Wtg6QMM":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Why are pan handles often plastic?","choices":["Plastic is a thermal insulator","Plastic conducts heat well","Plastic is magnetic"],"answer":0,"hint":"You hold the handle.","explain":"Plastic doesn't let heat through easily, so the handle stays cool."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which is transparent?","choices":["Clear glass","Wood","Steel"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can you see clearly through it?","explain":"Clear glass lets light straight through."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which dissolves in water?","choices":["Sugar","Sand","Plastic"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one disappears when stirred?","explain":"Sugar is soluble; sand and plastic are not."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Copper is used for wires because it…","choices":["conducts electricity and bends easily","is magnetic","dissolves in water"],"answer":0,"hint":"Two properties a wire needs.","explain":"Good conductor, and flexible enough to bend."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"Of {n} spoons, {k} are steel and the rest are wooden. How many are electrical insulators?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Wood is an insulator.","explain":"{n} − {k} = {=n-k} wooden spoons.","params":{"n":[6,15],"k":[1,5]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"A magnet picks up {a} iron paper clips and {b} steel pins. How many magnetic objects is that?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Iron and steel are both magnetic.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b}.","params":{"a":[2,9],"b":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which test shows if a material conducts electricity?","choices":["Put it in a circuit with a bulb","Hold a magnet near it","Drop it in water"],"answer":0,"hint":"Electricity needs a circuit.","explain":"If the bulb lights, the material conducts."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which material conducts electricity?","choices":["Copper","Plastic","Wood"],"answer":0,"hint":"Wires are made of it.","explain":"Copper is a metal and a good electrical conductor."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Which object is attracted to a magnet?","choices":["An iron nail","An aluminium can","A copper coin"],"answer":0,"hint":"Only some metals are magnetic.","explain":"Iron is magnetic; aluminium and copper are not."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"In a circuit test of {n} materials, {k} made the bulb light. How many are electrical insulators?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Insulators don't light the bulb.","explain":"{n} − {k} = {=n-k} insulators.","params":{"n":[6,12],"k":[1,5]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"{a} g of salt dissolves in some water, and the solution weighs {=a+b} g. What did the water weigh, in grams?","answer":"b","hint":"Dissolving keeps all the mass.","explain":"{=a+b} − {a} = {b} g of water.","params":{"a":[5,30],"b":[50,150]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Materials have properties. Some are hard and some are soft. Some let light through. Some dissolve in water.","Metals let electricity and heat pass through them easily, so we call them conductors. Plastic, wood and rubber do not, so we call them insulators. Iron and steel are pulled by magnets, but most other metals are not."],"adult":["Group materials by tests, not guesses: a simple circuit with a bulb tests electrical conductivity, a magnet tests magnetism, and stirring in water tests solubility.","Properties decide uses. Copper is used for wires because it conducts electricity and bends easily. Pan handles are plastic or wood because they are thermal insulators."]},"familyTip":"Hunt the kitchen with a fridge magnet: which metal things does it stick to? (Steel cans yes, aluminium foil no.)","keyIdea":"Group materials by tested properties: conductivity, magnetism, hardness, solubility and transparency decide what each is used for.","visual":{"type":"circuit","caption":"Put each material in the gap in the circuit. Does the bulb light?","opts":{"items":[["Copper wire",1],["Steel spoon",1],["Aluminium foil",1],["Pencil lead (graphite)",1],["Plastic ruler",0],["Wooden spoon",0],["Glass marble",0],["Rubber band",0]]}}},"mt_htAYR-iCFF":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"In which state are particles in a regular pattern?","choices":["Solid","Liquid","Gas"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of a crystal.","explain":"Solids have a regular arrangement."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which state flows and takes the shape of its container but keeps its volume?","choices":["Liquid","Solid","Gas"],"answer":0,"hint":"Pour it into a jug.","explain":"A liquid flows but its particles stay close together."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Why can a gas be squashed?","choices":["There are big gaps between its particles","Its particles are soft","It has no particles"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the spacing.","explain":"The gaps let the particles be pushed closer together."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"A solid with {r} rows of {c} particles melts. How many particles are in the liquid?","answer":"r*c","hint":"Melting doesn't add or remove particles.","explain":"Still {r} × {c} = {=r*c} particles.","params":{"r":[2,6],"c":[3,8]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"A sealed balloon holds {n} particles. It warms up in the sun. How many particles are there now?","answer":"n","hint":"Nothing gets in or out.","explain":"Still {n} particles; they just move faster.","params":{"n":[10,40]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"What should arrows on a gas diagram show?","choices":["Fast movement in random directions","The particles growing","Particles standing still"],"answer":0,"hint":"Gas particles never stop.","explain":"Arrows show fast, random motion."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"What does heating do to particles?","choices":["Makes them move faster","Makes them bigger","Makes them heavier"],"answer":0,"hint":"Energy goes into movement.","explain":"Hotter particles move faster."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which diagram shows a gas?","choices":["A few particles far apart, with arrows","Particles in neat touching rows","Particles touching but jumbled"],"answer":0,"hint":"Gases have big gaps.","explain":"Gas particles are far apart and move fast."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"When ice melts, do the particles get bigger?","choices":["No: they move more but stay the same size","Yes","They disappear"],"answer":0,"hint":"Heating changes movement, not size.","explain":"The particles are the same; they just move more freely."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A solid diagram has {r} rows of {c} particles. How many particles is that?","answer":"r*c","hint":"Rows × particles in each row.","explain":"{r} × {c} = {=r*c} particles.","params":{"r":[2,6],"c":[3,8]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A sealed box of gas has {n} particles. The box is heated. How many particles are in it now?","answer":"n","hint":"Sealed: none get in or out.","explain":"Heating makes them faster, but there are still {n}.","params":{"n":[8,30]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Everything is made of tiny particles, far too small to see. We draw them as small circles.","In a solid, draw the circles touching, in neat rows. In a liquid, draw them touching but jumbled. In a gas, draw just a few, far apart, with arrows to show they move fast."],"adult":["A good diagram shows arrangement, spacing and movement. Solid: regular and touching, vibrating in place. Liquid: touching but random, sliding past each other. Gas: far apart and random, moving quickly in all directions.","Keep the particles the same size in every state. Heating makes particles move faster; it does not make them bigger."]},"familyTip":"Use coins on a table: neat rows for a solid, a jumbled touching heap for a liquid, a few spread across the table for a gas.","keyIdea":"Draw particles as same-sized circles: neat and touching in solids, touching but jumbled in liquids, far apart and fast in gases.","visual":{"type":"particles","caption":"Change the temperature. Watch how the particles' arrangement, spacing and speed change, then draw what you see.","opts":{"name":"water","mp":0,"bp":100,"temp":-10}}},"mt_MVovx37Xct":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"A tea cosy should be made of a…","choices":["thermal insulator","electrical conductor","transparent material"],"answer":0,"hint":"It keeps the pot warm.","explain":"It must stop heat escaping: a thermal insulator."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Rigid means…","choices":["it does not bend easily","it bends easily","it dissolves"],"answer":0,"hint":"The opposite of flexible.","explain":"Rigid is the opposite of flexible."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which is opaque?","choices":["A wooden door","A clear plastic bottle","A glass of water"],"answer":0,"hint":"No light gets through.","explain":"Wood lets no light through."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"An electrical conductor…","choices":["lets electricity flow through it","blocks electricity","is always magnetic"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of a wire.","explain":"Conductors let electricity flow."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"A test found {k} of {n} metals were magnetic. How many were non-magnetic?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Take the magnetic ones away.","explain":"{n} − {k} = {=n-k}.","params":{"n":[5,10],"k":[1,4]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"{a} g of insoluble sand is stirred into {b} g of water, then filtered out. What mass of sand is caught in the filter, in grams?","answer":"a","hint":"Insoluble: it doesn't dissolve.","explain":"All {a} g of sand is caught.","params":{"a":[5,50],"b":[100,200]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which pair are opposites?","choices":["soluble / insoluble","transparent / translucent","flexible / magnetic"],"answer":0,"hint":"One word means 'not' the other.","explain":"Insoluble means not soluble."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Frosted glass lets light through, but you can't see clearly through it. It is…","choices":["translucent","transparent","opaque"],"answer":0,"hint":"Some light, blurry view.","explain":"Some light gets through but the view is blurred: translucent."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Which word means 'does not dissolve'?","choices":["insoluble","soluble","flexible"],"answer":0,"hint":"'In-' means not.","explain":"Insoluble means it does not dissolve."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"Out of {n} materials, {k} are opaque. How many let at least some light through?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Opaque ones let no light through.","explain":"{n} − {k} = {=n-k}.","params":{"n":[6,12],"k":[1,5]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"{a} g of soluble sugar is stirred into {b} g of water. What is the total mass, in grams?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Dissolving keeps all the mass.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b} g.","params":{"a":[5,30],"b":[100,250]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Scientists use exact words for properties. Transparent means you can see clearly through it. Translucent lets some light through, but things look blurry. Opaque lets no light through.","There are more words in pairs. A conductor lets heat or electricity through, and an insulator does not. Soluble means it dissolves, and insoluble means it does not. Flexible means it bends, and rigid means it does not."],"adult":["Precise words make comparisons clear: 'thermal insulator' says more than 'keeps warm'. Pair each word with its opposite: conductor/insulator, soluble/insoluble, flexible/rigid, magnetic/non-magnetic.","Use the words to justify a choice. A window needs a transparent, rigid, waterproof material, so glass is used. A tea cosy needs a flexible thermal insulator, so wool or fabric works."]},"familyTip":"Shine a torch through things at home: a glass, a tissue, a book. Name each one: transparent, translucent or opaque.","keyIdea":"Use exact words in pairs: transparent/translucent/opaque, conductor/insulator, soluble/insoluble, flexible/rigid, magnetic/non-magnetic.","visual":{"type":"light","caption":"Hold each material in the torch beam. How much light reaches the screen?","opts":{"items":[["Clear window glass",0],["Cling film",0],["Tracing paper",1],["Frosted glass",1],["Tissue paper",1],["Wooden door",2],["Kitchen foil",2],["Cardboard",2]]}}},"mt_P8Da-_zGBG":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What is the relative mass of an electron?","choices":["Almost 0 (about 1/1840)","1","−1"],"answer":0,"hint":"Much lighter than a proton.","explain":"About 1/1840, so almost 0."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"An atom has mass number {m} and {p} protons. How many neutrons does it have?","answer":"m-p","hint":"Neutrons = mass number − protons.","explain":"{m} − {p} = {=m-p} neutrons.","params":{"p":[1,20],"m":[2,45]},"where":"m>=p && m<=2*p+4"},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"An ion has {p} protons and {=p-d} electrons. What is its charge?","answer":"d","hint":"Charge = protons − electrons.","explain":"{p} − {=p-d} = +{d}, so the charge is {d}+.","params":{"p":[3,20],"d":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"An atom has no overall charge because…","choices":["it has equal numbers of protons and electrons","it has no protons","neutrons cancel the charge"],"answer":0,"hint":"+1s and −1s.","explain":"Equal numbers of +1 and −1 charges cancel out."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"What is the relative charge of a proton?","choices":["+1","0","−1"],"answer":0,"hint":"Protons are positive.","explain":"+1."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which two particles are in the nucleus?","choices":["Protons and neutrons","Protons and electrons","Neutrons and electrons"],"answer":0,"hint":"Electrons are in shells.","explain":"Protons and neutrons."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"A nucleus has {p} protons and {n} neutrons. What is the nucleus's total relative charge?","answer":"p","hint":"Only the protons are charged.","explain":"{p} protons × +1 = +{p}, so the charge is {p}.","params":{"p":[1,20],"n":[0,22]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which particle has no charge?","choices":["Neutron","Proton","Electron"],"answer":0,"hint":"'Neutral' sounds like…","explain":"The neutron is neutral."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"An atom has {p} protons and {n} neutrons. What is its mass number?","answer":"p+n","hint":"Mass number = protons + neutrons.","explain":"{p} + {n} = {=p+n}.","params":{"p":[1,20],"n":[0,22]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A neutral atom has {p} protons. How many electrons does it have?","answer":"p","hint":"Neutral means the charges balance.","explain":"It has {p} electrons to balance {p} protons.","params":{"p":[1,20]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Where is almost all of an atom's mass?","choices":["In the nucleus","In the electron shells","Spread out evenly"],"answer":0,"hint":"Electrons are almost massless.","explain":"Protons and neutrons are in the nucleus, and they carry the mass."}],"explain":{"kid":["An atom has a tiny nucleus in the middle. It holds protons and neutrons. Electrons move around the outside in shells.","A proton has a charge of +1. An electron has a charge of −1. A neutron has no charge. Protons and neutrons each have a mass of 1. An electron is so light that we say its mass is almost 0."],"adult":["Relative charges: proton +1, neutron 0, electron −1. Relative masses: proton 1, neutron 1, electron about 1/1840.","Atoms are neutral because they have equal numbers of protons and electrons. Mass number = protons + neutrons. An ion forms when an atom gains or loses electrons: lose one and the charge is +1."]},"familyTip":"Make a table with three rows (proton, neutron, electron) and two columns (charge, mass). Cover it and test each other.","keyIdea":"Protons (+1, mass 1) and neutrons (0, mass 1) sit in the tiny nucleus; electrons (−1, mass almost 0) are outside it.","visual":{"type":"atom","caption":"Add or remove protons, neutrons and electrons. Watch the element, the charge and the mass number change.","opts":{"p":3,"n":4,"e":3}}},"mt_AN2kJE6I0s":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which pH is neutral?","choices":["7","0","14"],"answer":0,"hint":"The middle of the scale.","explain":"pH 7 is neutral."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which ions do acids release in water?","choices":["H⁺","OH⁻","Na⁺"],"answer":0,"hint":"Hydrogen ions.","explain":"Acids release H⁺ ions."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"acid + alkali → ?","choices":["salt + water","gas + oxygen","acid + water"],"answer":0,"hint":"Neutralisation.","explain":"They make a salt and water."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"Solution A is pH {a} and B is pH {=a+d}. How many times more hydrogen ions does A have?","answer":"10**d","hint":"Lower pH means more H⁺; each step is × 10.","explain":"{d} steps: {=10**d} times more.","params":{"a":[1,5],"d":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which is most likely to be pH 13?","choices":["Oven cleaner","Lemon juice","Pure water"],"answer":0,"hint":"Strong alkalis clean grease.","explain":"Oven cleaner is a strong alkali."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"Adding alkali drop by drop, an acid turns green after {a} drops. How many drops would neutralise twice as much of the same acid?","answer":"2*a","hint":"Twice the acid needs twice the alkali.","explain":"2 × {a} = {=2*a} drops.","params":{"a":[5,30]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Litmus paper can tell you…","choices":["only whether it is acid or alkali","the exact pH number","the temperature"],"answer":0,"hint":"Litmus has only two colours.","explain":"Red or blue: acid or alkali, not the number."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"pH 2 is…","choices":["strongly acidic","neutral","alkaline"],"answer":0,"hint":"Far below 7.","explain":"pH 2 is well below 7: a strong acid."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"What colour is universal indicator in pure water?","choices":["Green","Red","Purple"],"answer":0,"hint":"Pure water is pH 7.","explain":"Neutral pH 7 shows green."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A solution changes from pH {a} to pH {=a-d}. How many times more hydrogen ions does it have now?","answer":"10**d","hint":"Each pH step is × 10.","explain":"{d} steps of × 10 make {=10**d} times more.","params":{"a":[5,9],"d":[1,3]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"{a} cm³ of acid is exactly neutralised by {b} cm³ of alkali. What volume of solution is there, in cm³?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Add the volumes.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b} cm³.","params":{"a":[5,50],"b":[5,50]}}],"explain":{"kid":["The pH scale runs from 0 to 14. Below 7 is acid, 7 is neutral, and above 7 is alkali.","An indicator changes colour to show pH. Universal indicator is red in strong acid, green when neutral, and purple in strong alkali. Lemon juice is an acid. Soap is an alkali."],"adult":["Acids release hydrogen ions (H⁺) in water; alkalis release hydroxide ions (OH⁻). The more H⁺, the lower the pH. Each step of 1 on the scale is a tenfold change in H⁺.","Neutralisation: acid + alkali → salt + water, because H⁺ and OH⁻ join to make water. Litmus only says acid or alkali; universal indicator or a pH probe gives a number."]},"familyTip":"Make red-cabbage indicator: boil chopped red cabbage, cool and strain. Test vinegar, soap and baking soda and compare the colours.","keyIdea":"pH runs from 0 to 14: below 7 is acidic, 7 is neutral, above 7 is alkaline; indicators show it with colour.","visual":{"type":"phscale","caption":"Drag the pointer or add acid and alkali. The indicator colour shows the pH.","opts":{"ph":3}},"lab":{"type":"flagacid","caption":"Flagship lab: red cabbage juice. Squeeze, sort, neutralise drop by drop, and see why water is not an alkali.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_UoqUPI_uNz":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"In an exothermic reaction the products have…","choices":["less energy than the reactants","more energy than the reactants","the same energy"],"answer":0,"hint":"Energy went out.","explain":"Energy left, so the products have less."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"A hand warmer uses a reaction that is…","choices":["exothermic","endothermic"],"answer":0,"hint":"It warms your hand.","explain":"It gives out heat: exothermic."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"The reactants have {a} kJ of energy and the products have {=a-d} kJ. How much energy is given out, in kJ?","answer":"d","hint":"Reactants − products.","explain":"{a} − {=a-d} = {d} kJ given out.","params":{"a":[100,200],"d":[10,90]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"A cold pack drops from {a} °C to {b} °C. How many degrees did it fall?","answer":"a-b","hint":"Start − end.","explain":"{a} − {b} = {=a-b} degrees.","params":{"a":[18,25],"b":[2,12]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"What is activation energy?","choices":["The energy needed to start a reaction","The energy given out at the end","The energy of the products"],"answer":0,"hint":"The hump on the profile.","explain":"It is the energy needed to get the reaction going."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Why does sherbet feel cold on your tongue?","choices":["Its reaction takes in heat from your tongue","It is kept in a freezer","It is exothermic"],"answer":0,"hint":"Endo means 'in'.","explain":"The fizzing reaction is endothermic, so it takes in heat."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"On the energy profile of an endothermic reaction, the products are…","choices":["higher than the reactants","lower than the reactants","level with the reactants"],"answer":0,"hint":"Energy was taken in.","explain":"They have gained energy, so they sit higher."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"The temperature rises from 20 °C to 31 °C. The reaction is…","choices":["exothermic","endothermic"],"answer":0,"hint":"Did it get warmer?","explain":"It got warmer, so energy was given out: exothermic."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A reaction starts at {a} °C and ends at {=a+d} °C. By how many degrees did it warm up?","answer":"d","hint":"End − start.","explain":"{=a+d} − {a} = {d} degrees, so it is exothermic.","params":{"a":[15,25],"d":[2,20]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"The temperature falls from {a} °C to {=a-d} °C. By how many degrees did it fall?","answer":"d","hint":"Start − end.","explain":"{a} − {=a-d} = {d} degrees, so it is endothermic.","params":{"a":[18,25],"d":[2,12]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Which is endothermic?","choices":["Heating limestone to break it down","Burning methane","Neutralising an acid"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one needs heat put in all the time?","explain":"Thermal decomposition takes in heat."}],"explain":{"kid":["Some reactions give out heat. They are called exothermic, and the mixture gets warmer. Burning is exothermic.","Some reactions take in heat. They are called endothermic, and the mixture gets colder. Sherbet fizzing on your tongue feels cool because it takes in heat."],"adult":["Exothermic: energy goes out to the surroundings, so the temperature rises. The products have less energy than the reactants. Examples: burning, neutralisation, hand warmers.","Endothermic: energy is taken in, so the temperature falls. The products have more energy. Examples: heating limestone, photosynthesis and cold packs. Every reaction needs some energy to start. That is the hump on the energy profile."]},"familyTip":"Feel a hand warmer, then a cold pack. Which gives energy to your hand, and which takes it away?","keyIdea":"Exothermic reactions give energy out (temperature rises); endothermic reactions take energy in (temperature falls).","visual":{"type":"energy","caption":"Drag the products' energy level up or down. Watch what happens to the temperature of the surroundings.","opts":{"dh":-40,"reactants":"reactants","products":"products"}},"lab":{"type":"reactlab","caption":"Mix acid and alkali with universal indicator. Watch the colour, temperature and bubbles, then test the gas.","opts":{"acid":1,"indicator":true}}},"mt_5r47Pvstyn":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"A window is made of…","choices":["glass","a window","a door"],"answer":0,"hint":"What is it made of?","explain":"Windows are made of glass."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Can a spoon be made of wood?","choices":["Yes","No"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of a cooking spoon.","explain":"Yes, wooden spoons are common."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which pair is (object, material)?","choices":["(chair, wood)","(wood, chair)","(metal, glass)"],"answer":0,"hint":"Thing first.","explain":"A chair is the object; wood is the material."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"There are {n} wooden blocks and {m} plastic blocks. How many blocks?","answer":"n+m","hint":"Add.","explain":"{n} + {m} = {=n+m}.","params":{"n":[2,6],"m":[1,5]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"{n} toys are metal. {m} are not. How many toys?","answer":"n+m","hint":"Add both.","explain":"{=n+m} toys.","params":{"n":[1,6],"m":[1,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Is plastic an object or a material?","choices":["A material","An object"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can you make things from it?","explain":"Plastic is a material."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"A metal spoon and a wooden spoon are both…","choices":["spoons","metal","wood"],"answer":0,"hint":"Same object.","explain":"Both are spoons, made of different materials."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which is a material?","choices":["wood","chair","table"],"answer":0,"hint":"What things are made of.","explain":"Wood is a material; chairs and tables are objects."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Which is an object?","choices":["spoon","metal","plastic"],"answer":0,"hint":"A thing you can use.","explain":"A spoon is an object."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"{n} spoons are made of metal and {m} of wood. How many spoons?","answer":"n+m","hint":"Add them.","explain":"{n} + {m} = {=n+m} spoons.","params":{"n":[1,5],"m":[1,5]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"{n} cups are plastic and {m} are glass. How many glass cups?","answer":"m","hint":"Just the glass ones.","explain":"{m} are glass.","params":{"n":[1,5],"m":[1,5]}}],"explain":{"kid":["A spoon is an object. It can be made of metal, wood or plastic. Those are materials.","It is still a spoon, whatever it is made of."],"adult":["Children often mix up objects (spoon, chair, window) with materials (metal, wood, glass). Ask 'what is it?' for the object and 'what is it made of?' for the material.","The same object can be made of different materials, and the same material can make many objects. That idea leads to choosing materials for a job."]},"familyTip":"Pick three things in the kitchen. For each, say the object and the material: 'a cup, made of china'.","keyIdea":"An object is a thing; a material is what the thing is made of.","visual":{"type":"objects","caption":"Pick an object, then change its material. Is it still the same object?","opts":{"objects":[["spoon",[["metal","#cbd5e1"],["wood","#d6a35c"],["plastic","#f472b6"]]],["cup",[["glass","#bae6fd"],["china","#f8fafc"],["plastic","#86efac"]]],["chair",[["wood","#d6a35c"],["metal","#cbd5e1"],["plastic","#fdba74"]]]]}}},"mt_ZAJvTcroFO":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"A T-shirt is made of…","choices":["fabric","metal","rock"],"answer":0,"hint":"Soft, you wear it.","explain":"Clothes are fabric."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"A book's pages are made of…","choices":["paper","glass","plastic"],"answer":0,"hint":"Thin, you turn them.","explain":"Pages are paper."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"A wall is often made of…","choices":["brick","fabric","paper"],"answer":0,"hint":"Strong blocks.","explain":"Walls are often brick."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"{n} cups are glass and {m} are plastic. How many cups?","answer":"n+m","hint":"Add.","explain":"{=n+m} cups.","params":{"n":[1,5],"m":[1,5]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"A pencil is made of {n} parts. Each part is one material. How many materials?","answer":"n","hint":"One each.","explain":"{n} parts, so {n} materials.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"A spoon can be made of…","choices":["metal, wood or plastic","only glass","only paper"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of your kitchen.","explain":"Spoons come in metal, wood and plastic."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which is NOT a material?","choices":["a table","wood","glass"],"answer":0,"hint":"Things vs stuff.","explain":"A table is an object."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"A key is usually made of…","choices":["metal","glass","paper"],"answer":0,"hint":"Hard and shiny.","explain":"Keys are metal."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"A window is made of…","choices":["glass","wood","fabric"],"answer":0,"hint":"You can see through it.","explain":"Windows are glass."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"You find {n} wooden things and {m} metal things. How many things?","answer":"n+m","hint":"Add.","explain":"{n} + {m} = {=n+m}.","params":{"n":[1,6],"m":[1,6]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"{n} of your {=n+m} toys are plastic. How many are not plastic?","answer":"m","hint":"Take away.","explain":"{=n+m} − {n} = {m}.","params":{"n":[1,5],"m":[1,5]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Look around. A door is wood. A window is glass. A key is metal. A bag can be fabric or plastic.","Each of these is a material."],"adult":["The core list for young children is wood, plastic, glass, metal, water, rock, brick, paper, card and fabric. Naming them gives words for later work on properties.","Point out that one object can use several materials: a pencil is wood with graphite inside and a metal end."]},"familyTip":"Go on a material hunt at home. Find something made of wood, metal, glass, plastic and fabric.","keyIdea":"Everyday things are made of materials such as wood, plastic, glass, metal, rock and fabric.","visual":{"type":"objects","caption":"Choose an object and press Change material to hear the names of materials.","opts":{"objects":[["window",[["glass","#bae6fd"]]],["key",[["metal","#cbd5e1"]]],["T-shirt",[["fabric","#a5b4fc"]]],["wall",[["brick","#f87171"],["rock","#a8a29e"]]],["bag",[["fabric","#a5b4fc"],["plastic","#86efac"],["paper","#fde68a"]]]]}}},"mt_auVZZEuXjs":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"A coat that keeps rain out is…","choices":["waterproof","absorbent","transparent"],"answer":0,"hint":"Water stays out.","explain":"It keeps water out: waterproof."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"A metal spoon is…","choices":["shiny and stiff","soft and bendy","dull and absorbent"],"answer":0,"hint":"Test it in your mind.","explain":"Metal spoons are shiny and stiff."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"A towel is…","choices":["soft and absorbent","hard and shiny","waterproof"],"answer":0,"hint":"It dries you.","explain":"Towels soak up water and feel soft."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"{n} of {=n+m} materials bend. How many do not bend?","answer":"m","hint":"Take away.","explain":"{=n+m} − {n} = {m}.","params":{"n":[1,5],"m":[1,5]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"You did {n} tests on each of {m} materials. How many tests?","answer":"n*m","hint":"Groups of tests.","explain":"{n} × {m} = {=n*m}.","params":{"n":[2,3],"m":[2,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"The opposite of rough is…","choices":["smooth","soft","shiny"],"answer":0,"hint":"Run your hand on it.","explain":"Rough and smooth are opposites."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which test checks if a material is waterproof?","choices":["Drip water on it","Bend it","Scratch it"],"answer":0,"hint":"Water test.","explain":"Drip water and see if it soaks in."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Water soaks into a sponge. The sponge is…","choices":["absorbent","waterproof","shiny"],"answer":0,"hint":"It soaks up water.","explain":"Soaks up water: absorbent."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"The opposite of stiff is…","choices":["bendy","hard","dull"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can it bend?","explain":"Stiff and bendy are opposites."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"You test {n} things. {m} are shiny. How many are dull?","answer":"n-m","hint":"Take away.","explain":"{n} − {m} = {=n-m}.","params":{"n":[5,9],"m":[1,4]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"{n} things are waterproof and {m} are not. How many things?","answer":"n+m","hint":"Add.","explain":"{=n+m}.","params":{"n":[1,5],"m":[1,5]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Is it hard or soft? Is it shiny or dull? Does it bend? Does water soak in?","These words tell us what a material is like."],"adult":["Property words come in opposite pairs: hard/soft, stiff/bendy (flexible), shiny/dull, rough/smooth, waterproof/absorbent, transparent/opaque. Testing is better than guessing.","Let children test: bend it, drip water on it, scratch it with a coin, shine a torch at it. Then name the property in a sentence: 'foil is shiny and bendy'."]},"familyTip":"Test a sock, a spoon and a plastic bag. Is each one bendy? Waterproof? Shiny?","keyIdea":"We describe materials with pairs of words: hard or soft, stiff or bendy, shiny or dull, waterproof or not.","visual":{"type":"matlab","caption":"Pick a material, then do a test. What word describes it?","opts":{"materials":[["metal",{"bend":true,"water":true,"light":false,"scratch":true,"shine":true},"#cbd5e1"],["wood",{"bend":true,"water":false,"light":false,"scratch":true,"shine":false},"#d6a35c"],["glass",{"bend":true,"water":true,"light":true,"scratch":true,"shine":true},"#bae6fd"],["fabric",{"bend":false,"water":false,"light":false,"scratch":false,"shine":false},"#a5b4fc"],["plastic bag",{"bend":false,"water":true,"light":true,"scratch":false,"shine":true},"#86efac"],["paper",{"bend":false,"water":false,"light":false,"scratch":false,"shine":false},"#fde68a"]]}}},"mt_rTn43s8RNX":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Why is a towel made of fabric?","choices":["Fabric soaks up water","Fabric is see-through","Fabric is shiny"],"answer":0,"hint":"A towel dries you.","explain":"Fabric is absorbent."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Why is a saucepan made of metal?","choices":["Metal is strong and lets heat through","Metal is soft","Metal soaks up water"],"answer":0,"hint":"It goes on the cooker.","explain":"Metal is strong and conducts heat."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"A chocolate teapot is a bad idea because…","choices":["chocolate melts when hot","chocolate is see-through","chocolate is too hard"],"answer":0,"hint":"Hot tea!","explain":"It would melt."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"{n} kids want raincoats. {m} have one. How many still need one?","answer":"n-m","hint":"Take away.","explain":"{n} − {m} = {=n-m}.","params":{"n":[5,9],"m":[1,4]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"Each window has {n} panes of glass. How many panes in {m} windows?","answer":"n*m","hint":"Groups.","explain":"{n} × {m} = {=n*m}.","params":{"n":[2,4],"m":[2,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which is best for a wellington boot?","choices":["rubber","paper","fabric"],"answer":0,"hint":"Puddles!","explain":"Rubber is waterproof and bendy."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"A good material for a window is…","choices":["glass","wood","brick"],"answer":0,"hint":"See through.","explain":"Glass is transparent."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"An umbrella should be…","choices":["waterproof","absorbent","see-through"],"answer":0,"hint":"It keeps rain off.","explain":"It must keep water out: waterproof."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"A window needs a material that is…","choices":["see-through","soft","absorbent"],"answer":0,"hint":"You look through it.","explain":"You need to see through it, so glass."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"You try {n} materials for an umbrella. {m} let water through. How many work?","answer":"n-m","hint":"Take away.","explain":"{n} − {m} = {=n-m}.","params":{"n":[5,8],"m":[1,4]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A bag holds {n} books. You need {=n*2}. How many bags?","answer":"2","hint":"Double.","explain":"{=n*2} ÷ {n} = 2 bags.","params":{"n":[2,5]}}],"explain":{"kid":["An umbrella must keep rain off, so we need a waterproof material. Paper would get soggy!","Think about what the job needs, then pick a material that does it."],"adult":["Ask two questions: what does the job need (waterproof, see-through, stiff, soft)? Which material has that property? Then explain the choice with 'because'.","Some materials fail in funny ways: a chocolate teapot melts, a paper umbrella soaks through. These help children reason about properties."]},"familyTip":"Ask: why are windows glass and not wood? Why are towels fabric and not plastic?","keyIdea":"We choose a material because its properties suit the job.","visual":{"type":"matlab","caption":"The job: an umbrella. Try each material. Which ones would keep you dry?","opts":{"materials":[["metal",{"bend":true,"water":true,"light":false,"scratch":true,"shine":true},"#cbd5e1"],["wood",{"bend":true,"water":false,"light":false,"scratch":true,"shine":false},"#d6a35c"],["glass",{"bend":true,"water":true,"light":true,"scratch":true,"shine":true},"#bae6fd"],["fabric",{"bend":false,"water":false,"light":false,"scratch":false,"shine":false},"#a5b4fc"],["plastic bag",{"bend":false,"water":true,"light":true,"scratch":false,"shine":true},"#86efac"],["paper",{"bend":false,"water":false,"light":false,"scratch":false,"shine":false},"#fde68a"]],"job":["An umbrella",["water"]]}}},"mt_SwaNNdm_Ks":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"A sock, a scarf and a teddy are all…","choices":["soft","hard","shiny"],"answer":0,"hint":"Squeeze them.","explain":"They are all soft."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which could be a sorting rule?","choices":["Does it bend?","Do I like it?","Is it Tuesday?"],"answer":0,"hint":"You can test it.","explain":"You can test if it bends."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"{n} things are hard and {m} are soft. How many more hard than soft?","answer":"n-m","hint":"Find the difference.","explain":"{n} − {m} = {=n-m}.","params":{"n":[6,10],"m":[1,5]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"You have {n} shiny and {m} dull things. How many things?","answer":"n+m","hint":"Add.","explain":"{=n+m}.","params":{"n":[2,6],"m":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Can a spoon be in the 'hard' group and the 'shiny' group?","choices":["Yes","No"],"answer":0,"hint":"Materials have many properties.","explain":"A metal spoon is both hard and shiny."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Where does a wooden block go: shiny or dull?","choices":["dull","shiny"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at it.","explain":"Wood is usually dull."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which does NOT belong with glass, metal and foil (shiny)?","choices":["a wool sock","a mirror","a coin"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which is dull?","explain":"Wool is dull."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which goes with a rock, a spoon and a brick?","choices":["a coin (hard)","a sock (soft)","a pillow (soft)"],"answer":0,"hint":"They are all hard.","explain":"A coin is hard too."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"You sort by 'shiny or dull'. Where does a mirror go?","choices":["shiny","dull"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can you see yourself?","explain":"Mirrors are shiny."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"You sort {n} things into 2 groups. One group has {m}. How many in the other?","answer":"n-m","hint":"Take away.","explain":"{n} − {m} = {=n-m}.","params":{"n":[6,10],"m":[1,5]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Group A has {n}, group B has {m}. How many in all?","answer":"n+m","hint":"Add.","explain":"{=n+m}.","params":{"n":[1,6],"m":[1,6]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Put the hard things in one pile and the soft things in another. That is grouping.","You can group the same things a new way, like shiny and dull."],"adult":["Grouping means choosing one property and sorting by it. The same set can be grouped in several ways, which shows that a material has many properties at once.","Ask children to name the rule they used. A good rule is one you can test, like 'does a magnet stick?' or 'does it bend?'."]},"familyTip":"Tip out a box of odds and ends. Sort by one rule, then mix up and sort by another rule.","keyIdea":"We can group materials by a property, such as hard or soft, or shiny or dull.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Pick a rule: is it hard or soft? Sort each thing.","opts":{"bins":["Hard","Soft"],"items":[["Rock",0],["Metal spoon",0],["Brick",0],["Wooden block",0],["Sock",1],["Sponge",1],["Teddy",1],["Scarf",1]]}}},"mt_1JFUNQDwAJ":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which fills the whole room?","choices":["a gas","a solid","a liquid"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of a smell spreading through the whole room.","explain":"A gas spreads out to fill all the space it is in."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which takes the shape of the bottom of its cup?","choices":["a liquid","a solid","nothing"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about what juice does when you pour it into a cup.","explain":"A liquid flows down and takes the shape of the bottom of its cup."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Steam is water as a…","choices":["gas","solid","liquid"],"answer":0,"hint":"It floats up.","explain":"Steam is a gas."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"{n} ice cubes melt. How many puddles of water? (one each)","answer":"n","hint":"Each ice cube melts into one puddle.","explain":"One puddle for each cube makes {n} puddles.","params":{"n":[2,8]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"There are {n} solids and {m} liquids on a table. How many things?","answer":"n+m","hint":"Add the solids and the liquids together.","explain":"{n} + {m} = {=n+m}, so there are {=n+m} things.","params":{"n":[1,6],"m":[1,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"A word for a liquid is…","choices":["runny","rigid","hard"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about how water flows when you pour it.","explain":"Liquids flow easily, so we call them runny."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"A word for a solid is…","choices":["keeps its shape","pours","fills the room"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about an ice cube sitting on a plate.","explain":"Solids keep their own shape, like an ice cube."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Ice is a…","choices":["solid","liquid","gas"],"answer":0,"hint":"It keeps its shape.","explain":"Ice keeps its shape: solid."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Milk is a…","choices":["liquid","solid","gas"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about what milk does when you tip the carton.","explain":"Milk pours and takes the shape of its cup, so it is a liquid."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"You pour {n} cups of juice and {m} cups of milk. How many cups of liquid?","answer":"n+m","hint":"Juice and milk are both liquids, so add the cups together.","explain":"{n} + {m} = {=n+m}, so there are {=n+m} cups of liquid.","params":{"n":[1,5],"m":[1,5]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"{n} balloons are full of air. {m} pop. How many are left?","answer":"n-m","hint":"The popped balloons are gone, so take them away.","explain":"{n} − {m} = {=n-m}, so {=n-m} balloons are left.","params":{"n":[5,9],"m":[1,4]}}],"explain":{"kid":["A block of ice is a solid. It keeps its shape.","Water is a liquid. It runs and fills the bottom of a cup. Air is a gas. It spreads out to fill the room."],"adult":["Solids are hard and keep their shape. Liquids are runny: they flow, pour and take the shape of the container. Gases spread out to fill the space, and you often cannot see them.","Tricky cases help. Sand pours, but each grain is a solid. Honey is a slow liquid. Steam is water as a gas."]},"familyTip":"Find a solid, a liquid and a gas in the kitchen. Tip a cup of water: what does the top of the water do?","keyIdea":"A solid keeps its shape, a liquid flows and takes the shape of its container, and a gas spreads out to fill any space.","visual":{"type":"states","caption":"Choose something, then tilt the jar. Does it keep its shape, flow, or fill the jar?","opts":{"items":[["Ice",0],["Water",1],["Air",2],["Wooden block",0],["Milk",1],["Steam",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagstate","caption":"Flagship lab: ice in a jar. Heat it, tilt it and lift the lid to see how the particles behave.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_YQkUdIHO8L":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Honey flows slowly. It is a…","choices":["liquid","solid","gas"],"answer":0,"hint":"It pours.","explain":"Honey is a slow liquid."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which keeps the same shape in any container?","choices":["a solid","a liquid","a gas"],"answer":0,"hint":"Ice cube in a glass.","explain":"Solids keep their shape."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Bubbles in fizzy drink are…","choices":["gas","liquid","solid"],"answer":0,"hint":"They float up.","explain":"The bubbles are gas."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"You sort {n} solids, {m} liquids and 1 gas. How many things?","answer":"n+m+1","hint":"Add all.","explain":"{n} + {m} + 1 = {=n+m+1}.","params":{"n":[1,5],"m":[1,5]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"{n} of {=n+m} things pour. How many do not pour?","answer":"m","hint":"Take away.","explain":"{m}.","params":{"n":[1,5],"m":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Flour pours. Is flour a solid?","choices":["Yes, it is a powder of tiny solid bits","No, it is a liquid","No, it is a gas"],"answer":0,"hint":"One grain.","explain":"Powders are solids."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which spreads out to fill a whole box?","choices":["a gas","a liquid","a solid"],"answer":0,"hint":"Spray scent.","explain":"Gases fill the space."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Sand pours. Is it a liquid?","choices":["No, each grain is a solid","Yes","It is a gas"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at one grain.","explain":"Each grain keeps its shape: sand is a solid."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Which can be squashed easily?","choices":["a gas","a liquid","a solid"],"answer":0,"hint":"Squeeze a balloon.","explain":"Gas particles are far apart, so gases squash."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A jar holds {n} bubbles of gas. {m} escape. How many are left?","answer":"n-m","hint":"Take away.","explain":"{=n-m} left.","params":{"n":[6,10],"m":[1,5]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"{n} cups of water fill a jug. How many cups fill {m} jugs?","answer":"n*m","hint":"Groups.","explain":"{n} × {m} = {=n*m}.","params":{"n":[2,5],"m":[2,3]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Solids keep their shape. Liquids flow and can be poured. Gases spread out to fill a space.","Sand can pour, but each grain is a tiny solid. It keeps its shape."],"adult":["Grouping by behaviour: does it keep its own shape (solid), flow and keep the same volume (liquid), or spread out to fill any container (gas)? Gases can be squashed; liquids and solids hardly can.","Powders such as sand and flour pour like liquids but are solids, because each grain keeps its shape. Gases are often invisible, so we notice them by smell or by what they push (wind, bubbles)."]},"familyTip":"Squeeze a syringe of air, then a syringe of water (outside, with care). Which one squashes?","keyIdea":"We group materials as solids, liquids or gases by how they behave: keep shape, flow, or spread out.","visual":{"type":"states","caption":"Try each one in the jar and tilt it. Sand is a tricky one!","opts":{"items":[["Sand",0],["Honey",1],["Air",2],["Rock",0],["Juice",1],["Fizzy drink bubbles",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"gaslab","caption":"This is a gas: particles far apart and moving fast. Heat it or cool it and watch.","opts":{"n":25,"temp":300,"vol":1}}},"mt_Pl-nsjYGZ3":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Water boils at…","choices":["100 °C","0 °C","37 °C"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of a kettle bubbling when the water is as hot as it gets.","explain":"Water boils and turns into steam at 100 °C."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Cooling a liquid can make it…","choices":["freeze (turn solid)","boil","vanish"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of what happens to water when you put it in the freezer.","explain":"Cooling a liquid enough makes it freeze and turn solid, like water turning into ice."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"A drink is {t} °C. It cools by {d} °C. What is it now?","answer":"t-d","hint":"Cooling makes the temperature go down, so take {d} away from {t}.","explain":"{t} − {d} = {=t-d}, so the drink is now {=t-d} °C.","params":{"t":[40,80],"d":[5,30]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"Ice is at −{d} °C. How many degrees warmer until it melts at 0 °C?","answer":"d","hint":"Count up from −{d} to zero, one degree at a time.","explain":"From −{d} °C up to 0 °C is {d} degrees, so it needs to warm by {d} degrees.","params":{"d":[2,15]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Why does chocolate melt in your hand?","choices":["Your hand is warmer than its melting point","Hands are wet","It is a gas"],"answer":0,"hint":"Chocolate melts at about 34 °C. Is your hand warmer or cooler than that?","explain":"Your hand is warmer than 34 °C, so it heats the chocolate past its melting point."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"We measure temperature with a…","choices":["thermometer","ruler","scale"],"answer":0,"hint":"It is the tool that shows how hot or cold something is in °C.","explain":"We measure temperature with a thermometer."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Heating water a lot makes…","choices":["steam (a gas)","ice","sand"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the cloud that comes out of a kettle when the water boils.","explain":"Heating water to 100 °C makes it boil and turn into steam, which is a gas."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Ice melts when it is…","choices":["heated","cooled","squashed"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about what happens to an ice cube held in warm hands.","explain":"Heating gives the ice energy, so it melts and turns into liquid water."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Water freezes at…","choices":["0 °C","100 °C","50 °C"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the temperature inside a freezer, where ice cubes form.","explain":"Water freezes and turns into ice at 0 °C."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"Water is at {t} °C. How many degrees warmer until it boils at 100 °C?","answer":"100-t","hint":"Water boils at 100 °C. Take {t} away from 100 to find the gap.","explain":"100 − {t} = {=100-t}, so the water needs to get {=100-t} degrees warmer.","params":{"t":[20,90]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Room is {t} °C. Chocolate melts at 34 °C. How many degrees warmer?","answer":"34-t","hint":"Find the gap between the room temperature and 34 °C by taking away.","explain":"34 − {t} = {=34-t}, so it needs to be {=34-t} degrees warmer for the chocolate to melt.","params":{"t":[15,30]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Heat chocolate and it melts. Put water in the freezer and it turns to ice.","A thermometer tells us how hot or cold something is, in degrees (°C)."],"adult":["Each material changes state at its own temperature. Water melts at 0 °C and boils at 100 °C. Chocolate melts at about 34 °C, which is why it melts in a warm hand.","Heating moves along solid → liquid → gas; cooling goes back. Reading a thermometer at the moment of change is a good first science measurement."]},"familyTip":"Put a square of chocolate in your hand and one on a cold plate. Which melts first? Why?","keyIdea":"Heating can melt a solid and boil a liquid; cooling can freeze a liquid; we measure the temperature in °C.","visual":{"type":"particles","caption":"Chocolate: drag the temperature. Watch it melt near 34 °C.","opts":{"mp":34,"bp":200,"temp":20,"name":"chocolate","min":0,"max":60}},"lab":{"type":"gaslab","caption":"Heat the gas: the particles speed up. Cool it: they slow down.","opts":{"n":30,"temp":250,"vol":1}}},"mt_Mf-T-fYRLX":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"How do you get salt back from salty water?","choices":["Evaporate the water","Use a sieve","Use a magnet"],"answer":0,"hint":"Heat it gently.","explain":"Evaporating leaves the salt."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"How do you get sand out of water?","choices":["Filter it","Evaporate only","Stir it"],"answer":0,"hint":"Sand is insoluble.","explain":"A filter catches the sand."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"A material that dissolves is…","choices":["soluble","insoluble","opaque"],"answer":0,"hint":"Sol-.","explain":"Soluble means it dissolves."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"{n} g of salt in {m} g of water. Mass of the salt water, in g?","answer":"n+m","hint":"Nothing is lost.","explain":"{=n+m} g.","params":{"n":[5,25],"m":[50,150]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"You start with {n} g of sugar and dissolve it. How many grams are still in the water?","answer":"n","hint":"It's still there.","explain":"{n} g.","params":{"n":[5,40]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Is dissolving reversible?","choices":["Yes, evaporate to get the solid back","No, never"],"answer":0,"hint":"Salt from sea water.","explain":"You can get the solid back."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Warm water usually dissolves sugar…","choices":["faster","slower","not at all"],"answer":0,"hint":"Tea!","explain":"Warmer water dissolves sugar faster."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Sugar stirred into water…","choices":["dissolves","melts into a gas","stays as lumps"],"answer":0,"hint":"It seems to vanish.","explain":"It dissolves to make a solution."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Sand in water…","choices":["does not dissolve","dissolves","turns to gas"],"answer":0,"hint":"It sinks.","explain":"Sand is insoluble."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"You dissolve {n} g of salt in water. How much salt is left after it evaporates, in g?","answer":"n","hint":"Salt stays behind.","explain":"All {n} g is left.","params":{"n":[5,30]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"{n} g of sugar goes into {m} g of water. What is the mass of the solution, in g?","answer":"n+m","hint":"Add the masses.","explain":"{n} + {m} = {=n+m} g.","params":{"n":[5,20],"m":[100,200]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Stir sugar into warm water. The sugar seems to vanish. It has dissolved and made a solution.","Leave salty water in a warm place. The water dries up, and the salt is left behind."],"adult":["A soluble solid (salt, sugar) breaks into particles too small to see and spreads through the liquid: a solution. An insoluble solid (sand, chalk) does not, and can be caught by a filter.","Dissolving is reversible: evaporating the water leaves the solid behind. The mass is not lost; the dissolved solid is still there."]},"familyTip":"Try sugar, sand and salt in three cups of warm water. Which dissolve? Leave the salt water on a sunny sill for a few days.","keyIdea":"Some solids dissolve in water to make a solution; we get the solid back by evaporating the water.","visual":{"type":"separate","caption":"A mixture of gravel, sand, salt and water. Try each method: which takes out what?","opts":{"mix":[["gravel","sieve"],["sand","filter"],["salt (dissolved)","evaporate"],["water","none"]]}}}});
